Smart Card Scheme has been introduced as a \nmulti-purpose card for the passengers to pay for tickets.  The \nScheme is intended to reduce the transaction time at the booking \ncounters as it facilitates cashless transaction.  The Scheme has \nbeen launched on pilot basis on two sectors, viz., New Del hi-\nMumbai and  \nNew Delhi -Howrah.  At present, the Go -India Smart Card enables \npassengers to pay for reserved and unreserved tickets.  The Card \ncan be used at nominated Unreserved Ticketing System \n(UTS)/Passenger Reservations System (PRS) counters and in th e \nexisting Automatic Ticket Vending Machines (ATVMs) (for \nunreserved tickets) for issuing tickets on these two sectors.  \nSalient features of the Scheme are as under: \ni. In case of UTS tickets booked using Go -India Smart Card, \nthe cardholders get the UTS-ATVM ticket at 5% less than its \noriginal value.  However, no bonus is permitted in case of \nPRS/reserved tickets booked using Go-India Smart Card.   \nii. Concessional bookings are allowed through Go -India Smart \nCard except military warrants/vouchers and passes .  \nHowever, in such cases, the cardholder shall not get the 5% \ndiscount on purchase of the UTS-ATVM tickets.   \niii. Tatkal and opening day Advance Reservation Period (ARP) \nbooking are allowed against the Go -India Smart Card \nsubject to limitation of one transaction per day.   \n-2/- \n\n \n \n \n-2- \n \n1051 Go-India Smart Cards have been issued and 7999 tickets  \n(83 reserved and 7916 unreserved tickets) have been booked \nagainst these cards up to 23.04.2016. \n \n(d):  Introduction of new schemes and their extension based on t he \nfeedback is a continuous and ongoing process. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |148", "t": "National Urban Livelihood Mission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 148 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nReforms in Education Sector \n \n148. KUMARI SUSHMITA DEV:  \n SHRI JYOTIRADITYA M. SCINDIA:  \n SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:  \n SHRI T. RADHAKRISHNAN: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes a series of structural reforms in education ranging from schools \nto Universities and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether the Government is also considering overhauling of University Grants Commission and All \nIndia Council of Technical Education and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has any proposal for taking the views of eminent edu cationists before \nfinalising any reforms in education and if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(d) the time by which a decision is likely to be taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) and (d): The Government is in the process of framing a New Education Policy which will cover \ncomprehensively various aspects of School and Higher Education. The Government of India  has \nconstituted a ‘Committee for Evolution of the New Education Policy’ for drafting the  New Education \nPolicy (NEP). The Committee consists of Shri T.S.R. Subramanian, Former Cabinet Secretary as the \nChairman and Smt. Shailaja Chandra, former Chief Secretary, NCT of Delhi, Shri Sevaram Sharma, \nformer Home Secretary, NCT of Delhi, Shri Sudhir Mankad, former Chief Secretary, Gujarat & Prof. \nJ.S. Rajput, former Director, NCERT as members of the Committee. All the suggestions received from \nmultiple stakeholders have been forwarded to the Committee. The Committee is mandated to examine \nthe outcome documents, recommendations and suggestions received and formulate a draft National \nEducation Policy as well as a Framework for Action (FFA). \n \n(b): The Ministry has  undertaken external review of the UGC through an Expert Committee and \nprocess reforms have already been initiated in the UGC. Further, AICTE was also reviewed through an \nexternal Review Committee. The Ministry is in the process of examining and implementing the \nrecommendations of the Committee. \n \n(c): MHRD held thematic consultations through Apex level institutions and regulators, such as, UGC, \nAICTE, NCTE, AIU, NCERT, CBSE, IIAS, NLMA, NAAC, NUEPA, IGNOU and Central \nUniversities by inviting all relevant stakeholders including experts, academics, industry \nrepresentatives, civil society etc. during July -October, 2015. Further, the Ministry held thematic \nconsultations at the national level, in which experts having domain expertise as well stakeholders were \ninvited for deliberations. \n \n********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |149", "t": "Investment in Railways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 149 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.05.2016 \n \nINVESTMENT IN RAILWAYS \n \n*149.    SHRI ADHALRAO PATIL SHIVAJIRAO:  \n             SHRI DHARMENDRA YADAV:   \n \n      Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether PHD Chamber of Commerce and Industry and Institute of \nCost Accountants of India in their study have revealed that the \npublic transporter suffered under investment between 1951 -\n2014; \n \n(b) if so, the reaction of the Government thereto;  \n \n(c) the corrective measures taken by the Government to provide \nsufficient investment in the Railways; and \n \n(d) the extent to which the present investment trend is likely to help \nin expanding and improving rail network in the country? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI SURESH PRABHAKAR PRABHU) \n \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( d) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 149 BY SHRI ADHALRAO PATIL \nSHIVAJIRAO AND SHRI DHARMENDRA YADAV TO BE ANSWERED IN \nLOK SABHA ON 04.05.2016 REGARDING INVESTMENT IN RAILWAYS  \n \n(a): Yes, Madam.   \n \n (b) to (d): The need for more investment to create adequate \ntransport capacity has been recognised by the Railways.  \nAccordingly, a five year investment plan of ₹ 8.56 lakh crore, \nbeginning 2015 -16, has been prepared which is significantly higher \nthan the investment in previous periods.  A quantum jump has been \nmade in the plan outlays in 2015 -16 and 2016 -17.  In the current \nfinancial year i.e. 2016 -17, the plan outlay is ₹ 1.21 lakh crore.  The \nfocus of increased capital investment is towards capacity \nenhancement by way of commissioning of New Line/Gauge \nConversion/Doubling and electrification works as also safety, rolling \nstock and other important areas of modernisation.  \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |150", "t": "Indo-Israel Relations", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.150 \nFOR ANSWER ON 25/04/2016 \n \nCHEAP STEEL IMPORT \n \n150. SHRI VINAYAK BHAURAO RAUT: \nSHRI ANANDRAO ADSUL: \nKUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE: \nSHRI P. NAGARAJAN: \nSHRI NAGENDRA KUMAR PRADHAN: \nSHRIMATI RITI PATHAK: \nSHRI RAHUL SHEWALE: \nSHRI S.P. MUDDAHANUME GOWDA: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes  to impose new steel import duty in view  of \ncheap steel import from China; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof including  the extent to which the domes tic steel  \nmanufacturers are likely be benefied therefrom; and \n \n(c) the other steps taken/proposed to  be taken by the Government to encourage  \nand safeguard the interest of the domestic steel manufacturers? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF         (SHRI VISHNU DEO SAI) \nSTEEL AND MINES \n \n(a)&(b): There is no proposal to impose new steel import duty, at present. \n \n(c) In order to protect the interests of country’s steel industry, the Government has \ntaken  the following steps : \n \n(i) To ensure tha t only quality steel is produced or imported, Government has  \nnotified Steel & Steel Products (Quality Control) Orders, 2012 dated \n12.03.2012 and  Steel & Steel Products (Quality Control) Orders, 2015 dated \n15.12.2015. \n   \n(ii) To increase availability of Coal and Iron ore for the domestic steel industry:-  \n \n(a) Notified the Coal Mines (Special Provisions) Amendment Act, 2015 on  \n30.03.2015 to streamline coal block allocations.  \n \n(b) Notified the Mines and Minerals (Development an d Regulation) \nAmendment Act, 2015 on 27.03.2015 to streamline grant of Mining \nLeases.   \n \nContd………..2/- \n  \n\n \n-: 2 :- \n \n \n \n(iii) The Union Budget 2015 -16 had raised peak rate of basic customs duty on \nboth flat and non-flat steel to 15% from 10%. \n \n(iv) Hiked import duty on  ingots & billets, alloy steel (flat & long), stainless steel \n (long) and non-alloy long products from 5 % to 7.5%  and non -alloy and other \n alloy flat products from 7.5% to 10%.  This was further revised in August, \n 2015 on flat steel  from 10% to 12.5%, long steel from 7.5% to 10% and semi -\n finished steel from 7.5% to 10%.  \n \n(v) In November 2014, the import of rebars was ensured strictly as per Steel \nProduct Quality Control Order 2012, to block influx of cheap imports of boron \nadded rebars. \n \n(vi) In June, 2015, an Anti -Dumping Duty for five years on imports of certain \n variety of hot -rolled flat products of stainless steel from China ($ 309 per \ntonne), Korea ($ 180 per tonne) and Malaysia ($ 316 per tonne).  \n \n(vii) Imposed, Safeguard Du ty of 20% in March 2016 on hot -rolled flat products of \n non-alloy and other alloy steel, in coils of width of 600 mm or more.  \n \n(viii) Imposed, vide its notification dated 05.02.2016, the Minimum Import Price \n (MIP)  condition on 173 steel products. Import s of items covered under this \n notification will not be allowed into the country below the notified price.  \n \n(ix) For reducing the stress in steel sector, RBI has extended 5:25 scheme in July \n 2015,  whereby longer amortization period for loans to projects  in \n infrastructure and  core industries sectors, say 25 years, based on the \neconomic life or concession period of the project, with periodic re -financing, \nsay every 5 years, is allowed.  \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |151", "t": "Fare in Special Trains", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 151 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.05.2016 \n \nFARE IN SPECIAL TRAINS \n \n†*151. SHRI DALPAT SINGH PARASTE \nSHRI C.S. PUTTA RAJU : \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Railways has recently assessed the increase in the \nnumber of rail passengers and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether special trains are being arranged to cater to increase \nin passenger load and if so, the details thereof;   \n \n(c) whether special f are is being charged from the passengers \ntraveling in the said trains and if so, the details thereof and the \nreasons therefor; \n \n(d) whether the Government is reviewing the increase in fare; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the corrective steps taken so far in \nthis regard? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI SURESH PRABHAKAR PRABHU) \n \n(a) to (e)  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 151 BY SHRI DALPAT SINGH PARASTE AND SHRI              \nC.S. PUTTA RAJU TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 04.05.2016 \nREGARDING FARE IN SPECIAL TRAINS \n \n(a) Yes, Madam.  As per Budget Estimates 2016 -17, there is an \nincrease in the number of originating passengers from 8152 million \n(provisional) in the year 2015 -16 to 8182 million in the year       \n2016-17, showing an increase of 0.37%.  \n(b) Yes, Madam.  Special trains are run and extra coaches are attached \nfor clearance of extra passeng er traffic during peak seasons, \nfestivals, and special events, keeping in view the pattern of traffic, \ncommercial justification, operational feasibility and availability of \nresources.  This is an ongoing process on Indian Railways.  \n(c) The following type of S pecial train services are run including Suvidha \ntrains and special trains on special charges over Indian Railways:- \n(1) Special trains on normal fare structure (equivalent to regular time \ntabled trains) under the following circumstances:- \n(i) Unreserved special train for all distances including               \nJansadharan Express. \n(ii) To clear passengers due to hold up of traffic on account of    \nincidents like accidents,  floods, breaches, line blocks, \ncancellation of trains etc.  \n(2) Special trains on special charges are run based on pattern of \ndemand. In addition to fare equivalent to regular Time Tabled \ntrains, special charges are also levied at the rate of 10% of basic \nfare for second class (reserved) and 30% of basic fare for all other \nclasses subject to minimum and ma ximum charges. However, \nunreserved class of Special trains on special charges is charged at \npar with second class superfast Mail/Express fares. \n……2/- \n\n- 2 - \n \n(3) Suvidha Trains: -With effect from 01.07.2015, Suvidha  trains are \nintroduced for meeting peaks in demand as may arise from time to \ntime during summer, winter, festivals and on other occasions.  \nMinimum fare of Suvidha trains is equal to Tatkal fare applicable for \nthe class of accommodation.  This is for initi al 20% of the available \nberths.  Thereafter, the fare is increased for subsequent slabs of 20% \nof seats/berths booked subject to maximum of three times of the \nTatkal fare. Other applicable supplementary charges like reservation \ncharge, superfast charge, se rvice tax etc., as applicable are levied \nseparately. However, unreserved class of Suvidha trains is charged at \npar with the second class superfast Mail/Express fares. \n  Special trains on special charges and Suvidha trains are run as \nadditional services ove r and above the regular time -tabled trains \nduring peak seasons to meet surge in demand. They do not  displace \nthe regular services but complement them by providing additional \nfacility for travel at short notice/lesser advance reservation period by \npaying higher fares.  In view of the subsidized fare structure for the \nregular time tabled trains and extra efforts being made by Railways \nfor running of such special trains, the fares of Suvidha trains & \nSpecial trains on special charges have been kept higher. \n(d) At present there is no such proposal. \n(e) Does not arise. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |153", "t": "Cyber Security", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(DEPARTMENT OF ELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY) \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *153 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 04.05.2016 \n \n \nCYBER SECURITY \n \n*153.       SHRI RAMESH BIDHURI: \n       SHRI  NANA PATOLE: \n \nWill the MINISTER of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be \npleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether there is a lack of expertise in handling the cyber security cases in the country and \nif so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government for enhancing the same; \n \n(b) whether the Government has collaborated with other institutions for strengthening the \ncyber security and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether  cyber security mock drills were conducted at National and international level by \nvarious agencies and if so, the details and outcome thereof; and \n \n(d) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to create cyber security awareness in the \ncountry? \n \nANSWER \n \n             MINISTER FOR COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY   \n         (SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a) to (d):   A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.*153 FOR \n04.05.2016 REGARDING CYBER SECURITY \n  \n(a):  In tune with the dynamic nature & growth of Information Technology (IT) and emerging threat landscape, \ncontinuous efforts are required towards enhancing cyber security, capacity building and human resource \ndevelopment. Towards addressing capacity building in the Cyber Security Sector as national strategy, \nGovernment has formulated a framework for Cyber Security architecture for upgrading the security of Indian \nCyber Space.  The architecture addresses the aspects r elating to capacity building and manpower augmentation \nby stakeholders and engagement of private sector in Public Private Partnership mode.  \n \nTowards enhancing the qualified cyber security manpower, the following steps have been taken: \n \n(i) Department of Electronics and Information Technology (DeitY) is implementing the Information Security \nEducation and Awareness (ISEA) Project which aims to generate 1.14 lakhs qualified professionals at \nvarious levels in period of 5 years.   A total of 51 institutions in vario us categories across the country are \nparticipating in the project.  Besides, National Institute of Electronics and Information Technology \n(NIELIT) is conducting certification courses for creation of cyber security professionals.  \n(ii) Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT-In) conducts regular training programme to make the \nnetwork and system administrators aware about securing the IT infrastructure and mitigating cyber attacks. \nCERT-In is regularly conducting Cyber Crisis Management Plan (CCMP) workshops  for Central \nGovernment Ministries/Departments, States & UTs and critical sector organisations to sensitise them about \nthe cyber security threat landscape,  enabling them to prepare and implement the Cyber Crisis Management \nPlan as well as participate in the mock drill exercises. \n(iii)Cyber forensics training lab has been set up at Training Academy of Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI), \nGhaziabad to impart basic and advanced training in cyber forensics and investigation of cybercrimes to \npolice officers. In a ddition, Government has set up cyber forensic training and investigation labs in the \nStates of Kerala, Assam, Mizoram, Nagaland, Arunachal Pradesh, Tripura, Meghalaya, Manipur and \nJammu & Kashmir for training of Law Enforcement and Judiciary in these States. \n(iv) National Security Council Secretariat (NSCS) and the Ministry of Human Resource Development (MHRD) \nare exploring mechanisms to include Cyber Security Capsules as part of skill development at entry level in \nvarious Industrial Training Institutes (ITI’s) in the country. \n \n(b): Government is collaborating with various Research Institutes, Academia and     Industry for strengthening \ncyber security. Specific initiatives under taken are as follows:  \n \ni. Government has undertaken Research and Development programme for development of skills and \nexpertise in areas of cyber security by facilitating basic research, technology demonstration and R&D test \nbed projects. Under these projects, efforts have been made to nurture institutions and capacity \nenhancement. Currently, 37 projects at 27 institutes have been initiated. \n\nii. The academic activities under ISEA Project are carried out by 51 institutions across the country. \niii. National Security Council Secretariat (NSCS) has initiated plans for short term courses at IIT, Delhi and \nother Academic Institutions to train Government officers in various aspects of Cyber Security. \niv. CERT-In is collaborating with Industry for information exchange on cyber threats, incident resolution \nand awareness outreach.   \n \n(c):   i.  Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT -In) carries out cyber security mock drills at "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |154", "t": "Heat Wave", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *154 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, MAY 04, 2016 \n \nHEAT WAVE \n \n*154.       SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \n                SHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of EARTH SCIENCES be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether various parts of the country are experiencing severe heat \nwave conditions during the summer and if so, the details thereof \nincluding the States most affected by the severe heat wave along \nwith the reasons therefor;  \n(b)  whether the Government has conduct ed or proposes to conduct a \ndetailed scientific study on the 'heat wave' phenomenon in various \nparts of the country and if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the Government has also assessed the impact of intensive \nheat wave on human beings as well as f lora and fauna of the \ncountry and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d)  the remedial measures taken/ proposed to be taken by the \nGovernment to address the issue? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER FOR MINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY AND \nMINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES \n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT LAID ON THE TABLE OF THE LOK SABHA IN REPLY (a) to (d) TO \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *154 REGARDING “Heat Waves” TO BE ANSWERED ON \nWEDNESDAY, May 04, 2016. \n \n(a) Yes Madam. Every year India experiences severe heat waves in summer, but in \nthe year 201 6 during March -April, the most affected states include Odisha, \nJharkhand, Gangetic West Bengal, Bihar, Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu \nand  Karnataka  on many days and Himachal Pradesh, Punjab, Haryana, Delhi, \nRajasthan, Madhya Pradesh and Gujarat on some days.  Date -wise details are \ngiven in Annexure-I. \n \nThe reason for severe heat wave during this summer was the El-Nino effect, warm \nsea surface tempera ture over  the pacific ocean and other oceanic basins \nincluding the Indian Ocean  which had  an impact on the Global atmospheric \ncirculation pattern as well. The winds blowing over the Indian sub -continent are \nwarm & dry. During the current season,  the southern peninsular India as well as \nthe coastal belt of India  was under the influence of continental dry air , which \nnormally have the Maritime influence and thus experience moderate \ntemperatures. A deficient southwest monsoon of past year also has contributed to \nthis situation, in terms of depleted soil moisture, dry and parched land surface & \nwater bodies and less vegetation, which in turn resulting in less & less moisture \ncontent from evapo-transpiration. \n \n(b) Using daily maximum temperature data of 103 stations uniformly distributed over \nthe country for the period 1961 -2010 from the Indian main land during the hot \nweather season (March to July) for the last 50 years (1961 -2010), various \nstatistical aspects of heat waves (HWs) and severe heat waves (SHWs) such as \nlong term climatology, decadal variation, and long term trends were examined. It \nwas observed that many areas of the country (north, northwest, central and \nnortheast Peninsula) have experienced HW days of ≥8 HW days on an average per \nseason. The SHW were m ainly experienced over north, northwest and central \nparts of the country. Compared to previous four decades, there was noticeable \nincrease in the HW/SHW days over the country during the recent decade 2001 -\n2010, which is also the warmest decade for the country as well as for the globe.  \n \n(c) Limited studies suggest that heat wave conditions lead to exhaustion and is \nconsidered by experts to be the forerunner of heat stroke with the difference \nbeing that the neurologic function remains intact. Heat exhaustion is marked by \nexcessive dehydration and electrolyte (sodium and potassium salts depletion / \nimbalance). Symptoms may include diarrhea, headache, nausea and vomiting, \ndizziness etc. Definitive therapy includes removing patients from the heat and \nreplenishing th eir fluids. There is an urgent need to quantify much of the \nsubjective and intuitive information that has been published on climate/mortality \nrelationships. Heat wave conditions forces wild life fauna, particularly mammals, \nbirds and reptiles, to shift fro m their habitats to places in the vicinity of water \nbodies unable to bear the scorching heat. \n\n \n \n \n(d) Upon prediction of the heat wave conditions by the India Meteorological \nDepartment (IMD), various state Governments have a system of givin g wide \npublicity of Dos and Don’ ts through advertisements in TV channels, News Papers \nand opening drinking water camps at identified places in rural and urban areas to \nmitigate the impact of heat waves. The schedule of National Rural Employment \nGuarantee Scheme workers is adjusted to avoid exposure to extreme hot weather \nduration periods. Advisories include precautionary measures to avoid heat stroke \ninclude – drinking plenty of water; avoiding going out in open heat during 10AM -\n4PM; wearing light colored clothes; covering hea"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |155", "t": "Advertisement Revenue in Railways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.155 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.05.2016 \n \nADVERTISEMENT REVENUE IN RAILWAYS \n \n*155.  SHRI JYOTIRADITYA M. SCINDIA: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Railways has appointed any global consultancy firm to \nassist them in mopping up advertising revenue in the next few \nyears; \n \n(b)  if so, the terms and conditions of the agreement and the details of \nareas/sectors in which the firm is likely to guide Railways  to \ngenerate revenues; and \n \n(c) the details of views suggested by the consultancy firm and the \naction taken by the Railways on those points/views? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS \n( SHRI SURESH PRABHAKAR PRABHU ) \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( c) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 155 BY SHRI JYOTIRADITYA M. SCINDIA  TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 04.05.2016 REGARDING ADVERTISEMENT \nREVENUE IN RAILWAYS. \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam.  With a view to generate additional revenue through \ncommercial publicity, a Professional Media Agency, has been appointed \nthrough open tender process by Rail India Technical and Economic \nServices (RITES).  Terms and conditions of the appointment include: \n(i)  Assessment of the earning potential/value of static and mobile \nassets of Indian Railway on Pan India basis by selection and survey \nof representative stations and trains; and \n(ii)  Completion of the study within a time frame of six months from the \ndate of appointment i.e. 25.01.2016. \n \n(c): Does not arise. \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |156", "t": "New Flagship Schemes", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.156* \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MAY 04, 2016 \n \nNEW FLAGSHIP SCHEMES  \n \nNo. *156                    DR. K. GOPAL:  \n \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether the new flags hip schemes under hi s Ministry have not \nbeen able to achieve the envisioned targets; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the Government propo ses to take adequate steps \ntowards bridging the country’s infrastructure gap; and \n \n(d) if so, the details of the steps proposed to garner ample funds to \ntide over the urban infrastruct ure deficit during the coming \nyears? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SHRI M. VENKAIAH NAIDU) \n \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n*****\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.156* FOR 04.05.2016 REGARDING NEW FLAGSHIP \nSCHEMES \n******** \n \n(a)&(b): Ministry has launched Flagsh ip schemes for assisting efforts \nof State Government towards urban infrastructure and for improving \ngovernance & capacity building.  T hese schemes are Swachh Bharat \nMission (SBM), Smart City Mission and Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and \nUrban Transformation (AMRUT). The envisioned targets and outcomes of \nthe missions are expected  to be achieved in t he respective mission \nperiod.   \n \n(c)& (d): Urban Development is a St ate subject. The Ministry assists \nthe State and UT Governments by pr oviding technical  and financial \nassistance through its schemes and programmes.  The new flagship \nschemes of the Government env isage a paradigm change in financing \nurban infrastructure in the country to  tide over the urban infrastructure \ndeficit during the coming years.  This paradigm change relates to \naugmenting the grants from government for urban infrastructure through \nincreased use of public private part nerships, user charges, funding from \nbilateral and multilatera l sources of finance, m unicipal bonds, National \nInfrastructure and Investment Fund, etc.   \n  \n******\n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |158", "t": "Training for Women", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *158 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.05.2016 \n \nTRAINING FOR WOMEN \n \n*158.   KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE:                 S HRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Women’s Training Division under his  Ministry conducts Training \nand Employment Programmes through a network of Nati onal and Regional \nVocational Training Institutes exclusively for women;    \n \n(b) if so, the details of funds allocated therein d uring each of the last three years, \nState-wise; \n \n(c) the details of targets fixed and achieved durin g the above period; \n \n(d) whether the Government has achieved the desired  results; and  \n \n(e) if not, the effective steps taken by the Govern ment in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF SKILL \nDEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY) \n \n(a) to (e)   A Statement is laid on the Table of th e House.  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS  (a) to (e)  OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *158 TO BE ANSWERED ON 04.05.2 016 ASKED BY \nKUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE AND SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA  RE GARDING \n“TRAINING FOR WOMEN”  \n \n(a) Yes Madam. The Women’s Training Division under Ministry of Skill \nDevelopment & Entrepreneurship conducts Training an d Employment Programmes \nthrough a network of National and Regional Vocation al Training Institutes exclusively \nfor women. \n \n(b) State-wise details of funds allocated therein d uring the last three years i.e. \n2013-14 to 2015-16 is given below:-  \n(Rs. in Lakhs) \nS. \nNo \n \nState Name of \nNVTI/RVTI \nFunds allocated  \n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \n1. Uttar Pradesh NVTI-Noida 420.03 454.53  454.19 \n Uttar Pradesh RVTI-Allahabad 307.81 224.02 272.52 \n2. Haryana RVTI-Panipat 273.59 279.14 361.54 \n3. Rajasthan RVTI-Jaipur 241.79 195.63 310.29 \n4. Meghalaya RVTI-Tura 194.46 312.18 203.73 \n5. West Bengal RVTI-Kolkata 289.82 252.61 239.95 \n6. Karnataka RVTI-Bengaluru 300.04 313.63 312.39 \n7. Kerala RVTI-Trivandrum 205.81 234.41 236.99 \n8. Madhya Pradesh RVTI-Indore 214.57 238.13 256.87 \n9. Gujarat RVTI-Vadodara 172.52 168.92 168.18 \n10.  Maharashtra RVTI-Mumbai 177.69 199.34 225.67 \n \n(c) The details of targets fixed and achieved durin g the last three years i.e.     \n2013-14 to 2015-16, is given below:-  \nYear Target Fixed Achievement \n2013-14 8068 (Modular System) 8777 \n2014-15 5000 (Semester System) 6697 (including trained in \nModular System backlog) \n2015-16 8260 10,128 \n \n(d) Yes Madam. . \n \n(e) Not applicable in view of (d) above. \n \n*********** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |159", "t": "Security of Indian Students in Ukraine", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.159  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.05.2016 \n \nSECURITY OF INDIAN STUDENTS IN UKRAINE \n \n*159.  SHRI K. ASHOK KUMAR: \nKUNWAR HARIBANSH SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether some Indian students have been killed/injured in a \nrecent attack in Ukraine and if so, the details thereof and the \nreasons therefor; \n \n(b)  whether the Indian Government has taken up t he matter with \nUkraine; \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof and the response of the Ukraine \nGovernment thereto; and \n \n(d)  the steps taken/ being taken by the Government for ensuring \nthe safety and security of the Indian students in Ukraine? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER FOR EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRIMATI SUSHMA SWARAJ) \n \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n***** \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a ) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 159 REGARDING \"SECURITY OF \nINDIAN STUDENTS IN UKRAINE\" FOR ANSWER ON 04.05.2016 \n \n(a) Yes. Three Indian students of Uzhgorod Medical College in Ukraine were \nstabbed by three Ukrainian nationals at around 3 AM in the morning of Sunday, 10 \nApril 2016. Shri Pranav Shaindily a, R/o Muzaffarnagar, UP and Shri Ankur Singh, \nR/o Ghaziabad, UP succumbed to the stabbing. The third student Shri Indrajeet Singh \nChauhan, R/o Agra, U P, however, survived and was discharged on 21 April, 2016 \nfrom the hospital, viz Uzhgorod City Hospital, and is recuperating in college hostel. \nPreliminary investigations suggest robbery could have been the motive of the incident.  \n \n(b) & (c) Yes. Our Embassy in Kyiv has taken up the matter of the safety of \nIndian students strongly with the Foreign Office of U kraine on 11 April, 2016 and \nrequested for expeditious investigation of the issue. In parallel, our Ambassador  has \nalso met the Chief of Police of Ukraine on 14 April, 2016 and requested  that trial of \nthe accused be expedited and the criminals be brought t o justice. Our  Embassy has \nalso taken up the matter with the Prosecutor General Office of Uzhgorod region on 21 \nApril, 2016 seeking speedy trial. \n \n(d)  Embassy regularly visits the universities, interacts with the students and \ndiscusses safety and security  aspects with the university authorities. In addition, our \nEmbassy periodically issues advisories for Indian Nationals; advice on personal safety \nand security is integral part of such advisories. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |16", "t": "Mahila Samakhya Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 16 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nSOCIAL SECURITY TO WORKERS \n \n16.  SHRI J.J.T. NATTERJEE: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether a large percentage of workers in the country is engaged in informal \nemployment and majority of them have low earnings with limited or no social \nprotection; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; \n(c) whether the Government has formulated/proposed to  formulate any specific \nplans for such kind of employees; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a to d): As per the latest results of the labour  force survey on employment and \nunemployment conducted in 2011 -12 by National Sample Survey Office (NSSO), \nMinistry of Statistics and Programme Implementation, the percentage of workers in \nthe country engaged in informal employment was 92.4%.   \n In order t o provide social security to unorganised workers, the Government \nhas enacted the “Unorganised Workers’ Social Security Act, 2008” and has  \nincreased ambit of Rashtriya Swasthya BimaYojana (RSBY) to cover larger cross \nsection of workers and their families. Equal Remuneration Act, 1973 provides for \npayment of equal remuneration to men and women workers for the same work or \nwork of similar nature without any discrimination.  The Act is effectively enforced \nby the Central and State Government by conducting regu lar inspections to detect \nthe violation of provisions of the Act by establishments.  The coverage of \nestablishments under The Employees’ Provident Fund & Miscellaneous Provisions \n(EPF&MP) Act has been enlarged to include construction workers.   \n The Govern ment has also launched Prime Ministers’ Jan Dhan Yojana for \nfinancial inclusion of the poor and also three insurance schemes, namely (i) Prime \nMinisters’ Suraksha Beema Yojana (PMSB Y), which provides insurance cover on \naccount of an accident.  (ii) Atal Pe nsion Yojana (APY), which is a Government \nbacked pension scheme aimed at unorganized sector and (iii) Prime Ministers’ \nJeevan Jyoti Beema Yojana (PMJJBY), a Government backed insurance scheme \nproviding life insurance cover to people in the age group of 18 to 50 and having a \nbank account at a nominal premium.                                                                                                                          \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |160", "t": "Urban Poverty", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 160* \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MAY 04, 2016 \n \nURBAN POVERTY                                       \nNo. 160* SHRI GOPAL SHETTY: \n   DR VIRENDRA KUMAR:   \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION be \npleased to state:- \n \n(a) the estimated population living below poverty line in urban areas; \n(b) whether any survey has been conducted recently to ascertain \nthe number of urban poor and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any comprehensive scheme has been prepared and \nimplemented for urban poverty alleviation; \n(d) if so, whether these schemes have not been successful in \ncontrolling urban poverty; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not the reason therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING & URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n[SHRI M. VENKAIAH NAIDU] \n \n(a)      to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \nContd.-Pg./2 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n-2- \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (e) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 160* REGARDING “Urban Poverty” \nRAISED BY SHRI GOPAL SHETTY AND DR VIRENDRA KUMAR FOR \nANSWER ON MAY 4, 2016. \n \n(a) As per the Poverty Estimates 2011-12 released by erstwhile \nPlanning Commission, the number of persons living below poverty \nline in urban areas in 2011-12 was 531.25 lakhs.   \n(b) A survey to collect data regarding individual and household \nparticulars in urban areas was conducted along with caste \nenumeration and rural BPL survey by respective State \nGovernment/UT Administration as combined Socio-Economic Caste \nCensus (SECC).  \n(c)Yes Madam. This Ministry is implementing “Deendayal Antyodaya \nYojana-National Urban Livelihoods  Mission (DAY-NULM)” to reduce \npoverty and vulnerability of urban poor households by enabling \nthem to access gainful self-employment and skilled wage \nemployment opportunities for improvement in their livelihoods on a \nsustainable basis.  The Scheme also has provision for Shelters for \nurban homeless and infrastructure facilities for street vendors. \n(d) & (e):  Under DAY-NULM, substantial assistance has been provided \nto urban poor for improving their livelihoods. Since 2014-15, \n4,00,514 persons have been provided skill training, 85,502 \nbeneficiaries have been assisted for setting up individual or group \nmicro-enterprises, 1,00,431 Self-Help Groups (SHG) have been \nformed, 52,487 SHGs have been given Revolving Fund and 89,752 \nSHGs have been disbursed loans under SHG Bank Linkage \nProgramme.    \n***** \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |161", "t": "Facilities to Weavers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO. \n161\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n25\n.0\n4\n.2016\n \nVAISAKHA\n \n5, \n1938 (SAKA)\n \n \n \nRESTORATION OF JAGANNATH TEMPLE\n  \n \n \nWill the \nMinister  of \nCULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n \n                                                        \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE, CULTURE\n \nAND \nTOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n \nAND \n \nMINISTER OF STATE, CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n161\n.  \n \nSHRI  ASHOK SHANKARRAO CHAVAN:\n \nKUNWAR HARIBANSH SINGH:\n \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO\n:\n \n(a)\n \n \nwhether the Government\n’s\n \nattention has been drawn to a crack being detected \nin the 12th Century Shri Jagannath Shrine at Puri, Odisha;\n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government \nhas sent\n \nany expert team Arch\na\neological Survey of \nIndia (ASI) to examine and repair/restoration of the temple;\n \n(d)\n \nwhether the ASI has submitted its report to the Government in this regard;\n \n(e)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the corrective measures taken\n \nby the Government \nin this regard; and\n \n(f)\n \nthe steps taken by the Government to \nmaintain its legacy for future \ngenerations?\n \n(a)\n \n&\n \n(b)\n \n \nYes Madam.  During the recent de\n-\nplastering work at the interior of Jagmohana \nof Shri  Jagann\na\nth Temple, Puri, some cracks have been noticed in the stone \nbeams. \n \n(c)\n \nto\n \n(f)\n \nExperts from Archaeological Survey of India (ASI), IIT Madras and Core \nTechnical Committee for the Temple have visited the temple & submitted the \nreports.  The suggestions have been examined and essential conservation  work \nat the temple is attended regula\nrly to maintain its legacy for future generations.\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |164", "t": "Shortage of Water in Thermal Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 164 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nJob-oriented Educational System \n164. SHRI MALYADRI SRIRAM: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Ministry has any plan to create a new job -oriented educational \nsystem across the country; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the fund allocation made by the Government \nin this regard? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a):  Yes, Madam. The Ministry has a broad plan to create a job -oriented \neducational system across the country and has implemented the following skill \ndevelopment programmes.  \n \n(i) Community College Scheme. \n(ii) B. Vocs degree programme. \n(iii) Deen Dayal Upadhyay KAUSHAL Kendras \n(iv) National Skill Qualification Framework (NSQF) Programmes in AICTE          \napproved Colleges/ Polytechnics. \n(v) National Employability Enhancement Mission (NEEM). \n(vi) Employability Enhancement Training Programme (EETP). \n \n Further, this Ministry has  also introduced a new scheme under the umbrella of \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal V ikas Yojana (PMKVY)  through Technic al Institutions \nwith the aim to impart Engineering Skills to 10 Lakh y outh in the next three ye ars \nstarting from the academic year 2016-17.  \n(b): The funds allocated by the Government  for the schemes during the last three \nyears are at Annexure. \n***** \n\n \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 164 FOR 25.0 4.2016 ASKED BY SHRI \nMALYADRI SRIRAM  REGARDING JOB-ORIENTED EDUCATIONAL \nSYSTEM: \n  \nName of Scheme        Grants Allocated (Rs. in Crore) \n 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nCommunity College 73.05 94.92 114.96 \nB. Voc Programme Nil 231.36 241.06 \nDDU Kaushal Kendras Nil Nil 170.65 \nEnrolment under NSQF Programme Nil Nil Nil \nEmployability Enhancement \nTraining Programme (EETP) \nNil 14.53 3.71 \nNational Employability \nEnhancement Mission (NEEM) \nNil Nil Nil \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |165", "t": "Rural Employment", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.165 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \n \n MDMS in Rajasthan \n \n  †165. SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the reasons for delay in releasing the funds by the Government to the tune of Rs. 21.24 \n crores for providing total number of 47000 gas connections under Mid Day Meal \n Scheme (MDMS) in Rajasthan including Jalore-Sirohi district during the year 2014-15; \n \n(b)  the time by which the said amount is likely to be released by the Government; \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to provide full contribution to the tribal areas in \n Rajasthan and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to allocate an additional amount to Rajasthan for \n payment of foodgrain cost to Food Corporation of India and if so, the details thereof ? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b):   The Government of Rajasthan had assured before the meeting of the Programme \nApproval Board held on 28.3.2014 that the State would provide funds for \ninstallation of LPG connections in the schools during 2015- 16.  No separate \nCentral assistance was approved for the purpose. \n(c): The funding pattern for Mid Day Meal Scheme had been revised from 1\nst April, \n2015 and the sharing between Centre and States is 60:40 for Rajasthan. There is \nno provision of separate funding pattern for tribal areas.  \n(d): The Central assistance includes the cost of foodgrains to be paid to FCI.   \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |166", "t": "Modernisation of Major Ports", "head": "��������������������������������������\n�������������������������