ER MEAT  TON  268  0.55  262  0.44  2  0.00 \nPROCESSED MEAT  TON  508  1.29  405  2.29  284  0.96 \nNATURAL RUBBER  TON  10032  27.49  3059  7.06  5621  49.46 \nMARINE PRODUCTS  KGS  1000818636  5016.63  1073965690  5510.49  908135276  4408.14 \nIRON ORE  TON  16301238  1557.63  7490012  526.50  4253793  145.81 \nMICA  KGS  127232858  50.73  140447782  56.02  122033063  47.94 \nCOAL,COKE AND \nBRIQUITTES ETC  TON  2343865  208.14  1343283  136.51  1118974  113.11 \nSULPHER, \nUNROASTED IRON \nPYRITE  TON  577782  66.26  397768  60.57  570596  74.20 \nFINISHED LEATHER  KGS  67163410  1285.15  64503936  1331.24  46710624  957.68 \nLEATHER \nFOOTWEAR \nCOMPONENT  KGS  12073115  320.02  13513575  361.94  8780752  263.16 \nGOLD  KGS  70720  3031.98  70819  2845.15  135648  4965.06 \nSILVER  KGS  34247  18.52  7572  5.68  30253  7.07 \nFERTILEZERS CRUDE  TON  10650  6.42  10952  7.75  22376  10.91 \nFERTILEZERS \nMANUFACTURED  TON  137374  75.40  164707  82.54  220155  83.75 \nAYUSH AND HERBAL \nPRODUCTS  KGS  76720120  366.99  92059222  354.68  87011469  325.47 \nBULK DRUGS, DRUG \nINTERMEDIATES  KGS  305675879  3610.81  328391895  3561.39  322720535  3263.84 \nDYE \nINTERMEDIATES  KGS  137915055  248.15  1036"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |182", "t": "Cancer Centres", "head": "                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 182 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nMethod to Award Ph.D Degree \n \n182. SHRI G. HARI: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has introduced/proposes to introduce any new method to \naward Ph. D degree to students in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has received any suggestion from any organisation in this \nregard; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (d): A Ph.D degree can be awarded by a University in the country in compliance \nwith the regulations notified by the University Grants Commission (UGC) for \nmaintenance of minimum standards for the Ph.D degree.  Accordingly, the UGC has \nnotified the UGC(Minimum S tandards and Procedure for award of Phil/Ph.D Degree) \nRegulations, 2009.  \nThe UGC has approved that women candidates and persons with disability \n(more than 40% disability) may be allowed a relaxation of two years for Ph .D in the \nmaximum duration  of the course. The UGC has introduced a provision in the \nregulations allowing  transfer of research data,  in case of relocation of an M.Phil/Ph.D \nwoman scholar due to marriage or otherwise, to the relocated institution and also \napproved amendments in its regulations  for allowing women candidates maternity \nleave/child-care leave once in the entire duration of M.Phil./Ph.D for up to 240 days.  \nThe Commission has further approved amendments to its regulations for \nimplementing the report of the Committee, under the chairpersonship of Prof.Arun \nNigavekar, former Chairman, U GC to examine the issues related to attracting and \nretaining talented and quality manpower in the teaching profession. The Committee \nhas reported that it ascertained the views of various stakeholder s through r egional \nconsultative meetings and the responses from them to its questionnaire uploaded on \n\nthe Ministry and the UGC website.  The Commission has approved that c andidates \nwho have been awarded / registered for Ph.D. degree before the implementati on of \nthe University Grants Commission (Minimum Standards and Procedure for Award \nof   Ph. D Degree) Regulations 2009 shall be exempted from the requirement of \nthe minimum eligibility condition of NET/SLET/SET for recruitment and appointment of \nAssistant P rofessor or equivalent positions in Universities/ Colleges / Institutions \nsubject to the fulfillment of the following conditions:  \ni) Ph.D. degree of the candidate awarded in regular mode only \nii) Evaluation of the Ph.D. thesis by at least two external examiners \niii) Candidate published two research papers out of which at least one in a \nrefereed journal from out of his/her Ph.D. work \niv) The candidate presented two papers in seminars/conferences from out of \nhis/her Ph.D. work \nv) Open Ph.D. Viva voce of the candidate conducted. \ni) to (v) as above are to be certified by the Vice Chancellor/Pro- Vice Chancellor / \nDean (Academic Affairs/University Instructions/Studies). \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |183", "t": "Women Pilots", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 183 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nMeeting of Education Ministers \n \n183. SHRIMATI K. MARAGATHAM: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether six zonal meetings were held by the Government and the respective education \nMinisters from the States participated therein; and  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) and (b): Yes, Madam.  Six Zonal Meetings were held by the Minister of Human Resource \nDevelopment in Eastern, Central, North- Eastern, Western, Southern and Northern Zones covering \nall States and UTs in September -October 2015 which was attended by Education Ministers and \nofficials of the respective States/UTs. States have submitted their outcome doc uments with \nsuggestions.  Details of the meetings held are as follows: \n \n \nDate Venue States Covered \n11 Sept. 2015  Kolkata Eastern Region \nBihar, Jharkhand, Odisha and West Bengal \n14 Sept. 2015 Lucknow Central Region \nChhattisgarh, Uttarakhand, Uttar Pradesh and Madhya \nPradesh \n18 Sept. 2015 Agartala North East Region \nAssam, Arunachal Pradesh, Manipur, Tripura, Mizoram, \nMeghalaya, Nagaland and Sikkim \n20 Oct. 2015 Bangalore Southern Region \nAndaman & Nicobar Islands, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, \nKarnataka, Kerala, Lakshadweep, Tamil Nadu and \nPuducherry \n29 Oct.2015 \n \nGoa Western Region \nGoa, Gujarat, Maharashtra, Daman & Diu and Dadra & Nagar \nHaveli \n31 Oct. 2015 Gurgaon Northern Region \nHaryana, Himachal Pradesh, Jammu & Kashmir, Punjab, \nRajasthan, Delhi and Chandigarh \n \n********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |186", "t": "Healthcare", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 186\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nHEALTHCARE\n†*186. SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY:\nSHRI UDAY PRATAP SINGH:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government is aware that households with low and average income\nare unable to avail expensive treatment in private hospitals, if so, the details thereof\nalong with the health insurance penetration amongst them;\n(b) whether any low cost insurance cover scheme is proposed for the benefit of poor\nand Below Poverty Line (BPL) families, if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government has formulated/proposes to formul ate any policy to\nprovide accessible, affordable and quality health services in urban and rural areas to\nsuch vulnerable group, if so, the details thereof;\n(d) the steps taken by the Government in this regard along with the funds allocated\nand utilised by States/UTs during each of the last three financial years; and\n(e) whether the Government has sought/proposes to seek assistance from the World\nHealth Organisation, World Bank and other International Agencies to improve\nhealthcare in rural and non -metro cities, if so, the details thereof and the outcome\nthereon?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 186* FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\n(a) The Government is aware that poor people are unable to afford expensive\ntreatment in private hospitals. The Out of Pocket Expenditure on health is very\nhigh and has been estimated at  62% for 2014. Some of the key find ings of 71st\nRound survey on Social Consumption on Health of National Sample Survey\nOrganization (NSSO) is at Annexure- I.\nAs per NSSO, 11% persons in lowest two quintiles in rural areas and 10.5%\npersons in lowest two quintiles in urban areas are covered under some insurance\nschemes. As per information provided by the Insurance Regulatory and\nDevelopment Authority of India (IRDAI), the number of lives covered under\nhealth insurance policies during financial 2014 -15 was 28.80 crore including\ngovernment sponsored schemes like RSBY.\n(b) The Central Government plans to start a new health protection scheme as\nannounced in the Budget 2016 -17 which will provide a cover upto Rs. 1 lakh\nwith additional top up of Rs. 30,000 per senior citizen in those families. The\nproposed scheme envisages coverage of poor and vulnerable families on family\nfloater basis with no capping on family size. Detailed modalities are being\nworked out.\n(c) The Government has formulated a draft National Health Policy, 2015 , which\nenvisages attainment of the highest possible level of good health and wellbeing\nwith priority to the needs of the most vulnerable, the marginalized or socially\nexcluded and the poor.  Under the National Health Mission (NHM) , the Central\nGovernment supplements the eff orts of the State/UT governments to provide\nquality, accessible, equitable and affordable healthcare to all its citizens\nparticularly the poor and vulnerable population in both rural and urban areas.\n(d) The funds released and utilized by States/UTs under NHM during FY 2013-14\nto 2015 -16 is at Annexure -II. Further, details of funds released to State\nGovernments under RSBY for FY 2013-14 to 2015-16 is at Annexure-III.\n(e) Currently, there is no such proposal by the Ministry of Health and Family\nWelfare.\n\nAnnexure-I\n(i) 2.3% of ailing persons in rural and 2% in urban areas did not seek medical\nadvice due to financial constraint.\n(ii)In rural areas, 71.1% of household in top quintile as against 44.8% of\nhouseholds in the lowest two quintiles are using private hospitals for hospitalized\ncare.  In urban areas, 81.3% of household in top quintile as against 54.25 % of\nhouseholds in the lowest two quintiles are using private hospitals for hospitalized\ncare. The average treatment cost for the lowest two quintiles in rural and urban\nareas is Rs. 10711 and Rs.12866 respectively. The average medical expenditure\nin the top quintile is Rs. 21293 in rural areas and Rs. 42675 in urban areas.\n(iii) The average medical expenditure on hospitalization is Rs 25850 in private\nfacilities as compared to Rs 6120 in public facilities. The average total medical\nexpenditure per childbirth shows that expenditure in public facilities was just\nRs 1587 as against Rs 14778 in private facilities in rural areas and Rs 2117 in\npublic facilities as against Rs 20328 in private facilities in urban areas.\n\nAnnexure-II\nStatement Showing State-wise Release and Expenditure under NHM for the period from F.Y.2013-14 to\n2015-16\nRs. in crore\nSl.\nNo. States 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16\nCentral\nRelease Exp Central\nRelease Exp Central\nRelease Exp\n1\nAndaman & Nicobar\nIslands 29.06 29.12 23.36 24.33 37.26 7.46\n2 Andhra Pradesh 878.73 1044.81 519.73 902.91 642.89 538.39\n3 Arunach"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |187", "t": "Mental Health Institutes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 187\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nMENTAL HEALTH INSTITUTES\n*187. SHRIMATI RITA TARAI:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether cases of mental disorder are on the rise in the country in the recent past,\nif so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether any study/survey has been undertaken to find out the causes of sudden\nrise in mental and neurological disorders, if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government has any plan/proposes to formulate any plan for the\nupgradation of Mental Health Institutes into super specialist institute of mental\nhealth and neurosciences at par with the National Institute of Mental Health and\nNeurosciences (NIMHANS);\n(d) if so, the details thereof, State/UT wise including Odisha; and\n(e) the follow-up action taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 187* FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\nIn 2005, the National Commission on Macroeconomics and Health reported\nthat 10-20 million (1-2% of population) suffered from severe mental disorders such\nas schizophrenia and bipolar disorder and nearly 50 million (5% of population) from\ncommon mental disorders such as depression and anxiet y, yielding an overall\nestimate of 6.5 per cent of the population.\nThe Government has commissioned a National Mental Health Survey through\nthe National Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences (NIMHANS), Bangalore\nwith, inter-alia, the following objectives:\n• estimate the prevalence and burden of Mental, Neurological and substance\nuse  disorders in a representative population of India,\n• identify the health care seeking and services utilization patterns, and\n• assess mental health care resources and facilit ies in the surveyed states for\nplanning, and strengthening of resources and services.\nThe survey was initiated on 1st June, 2015 and about 27000 interviews were\ncompleted at the field level as on 5th April, 2016.\nDuring the 12th Five Year Plan period, Government has approved a scheme for\nupgradation of two Central Government mental health institutes namely Lokopriya\nGopinath Bordoloi Regional Institute of Mental Health (LGBRIMH), Tezpur,\nAssam and Central Institute of Psychiatry, Ranchi to provide neurological and\nneuro-surgical facilities on the pattern of National Institute of Mental Health and\nNeurosciences (NIMHANS), Bangalore. The Government does not have any scheme\nfor upgradation of State Government mental health institutes into super specialist\ninstitute of mental health and neurosciences at par with NIMHANS, Bangalore.\nHowever, Government has provided financial assistance for establishment of\nCentres of Excellence in the field of mental health at 15 Institutes belonging to the\nState Governments/UT Administrations including Odisha. Information relating to\nthe funds released for establishment of Centres of Excellence is annexed.\n\nANNEXURE\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 187 FOR\nANSWER ON 06.05.2016 REGARDING “MENTAL HEALTH INSTITUTES”\nDetails of funds released for establishment of Centres of Excellence in the field of mental\nhealth\nS.No. Mental Hospital/ Institute Amount Released\n1. Institute of Mental Health & Hospital, Agra, Uttar Pradesh Rs. 28,81,00,000/-\n2. Hospital for Mental Health, Ahmedabad, Gujarat Rs. 24,79,00,000/-\n3. State Mental H ealth Institute, Pandit Bhagwat Dayal Sharma\nUniversity of Health Sciences, Rohtak, Haryana\nRs. 26,36,38,788/-\n4. Institute of Psychiatry- Kolkata, West Bengal Rs. 24,90,00,000/-\n5. Institute of Mental Health, Hyderabad, Andhra Pradesh Rs. 5,28,00,000/-\n6. Psychiatric Diseases Hospital, Government Medical College,\nSrinagar, Jammu & Kashmir\nRs. 28,84,00,000/-\n7. Department of Psychiatry, Govt. Medical College, Chandigarh Rs. 18,59,00,000/-\n8. Mental Health Institute, SCB Medical College, Cuttack Rs. 27,78,00,000/-\n9 Institute of Mental Health and Neuro -Sciences, Kozhikode,\nKerala\nRs. 28,84,00,000/-\n10. Institute of Human Behaviour and Allied Sciences, Shahdra,\nDelhi\nRs. 5,28,00,000/-\n11. Maharashtra Institute of Mental Health, Pune Rs. 30,00,00,000/-\n12. Dharwad Institute of Mental Health and Neuro Sciences,\nDharwad, Karnataka\nRs. 5,00,00,000/-\n13. Institute of Psychiatry and Human Behaviour, Bambolim, Goa Rs. 4,76,75,000/-\n14. Gwalior Mansik Arogyashala, Gwalior, Madhya Pradesh Rs. 4,75,75,,000/-\n15. Institute of Mental Health, Amritsar, Punjab Rs. 4,75,76,000/-\nTotal Rs. 256,24,64,788/-"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |188", "t": "Misleading Advertisements", "head": "adolescent minds and promote immoral cultural ethos . The Press should refuse to accept such \nadvertisements. \n \nxiv) Classified advertisements of health and physic al fitness services using undignified languages, \nindicative of covert soliciting, are violative of l aw as well as ethics. The newspaper should adopt a \nmechanism for vetting such an advertisement to ensu re that the soliciting advertisements are not \ncarried. \n \nxv) Advertisements of contraceptive and supply of b rand item attaching to the advertisement is not \nvery ethical, given the social milieu and the tradi tional values held dear in our country. A newspaper  \nhas a sacred duty to educate people about precautio nary measures to avoid AIDS and exhibit greater \nfar sight in accepting advertisement even though issued by social welfare organisation. \n \n(xvi) Employment News which is trusted as a purveyor of authentic news on government jobs should \nbe more careful in accepting advertisements of only bonafide private bodies. \n \n(xvii) While accepting advertisements of educationa l institutes newspapers may ensure that such \nadvertisements carry the mandatory statement that t he concerned institutes are recognized under the \nrelevant enactments of law.  \n \nxviii) Advertisements play extremely vital role in shaping the values and concerns of the present day \nsociety and as more and more lenient view is taken of what is not the norm, the speedier may be \nacceptability of such matters in ‘public perception ’ but at what cost is the essential point for \nconsideration. It should be borne in mind that in t he race to be globally relevant we do not leave \nbehind the values that have earned India the unique  place it enjoys globally on moral and ethical \nplane. \n \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-IV  \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e ) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 188 FOR 06.05.2016  \n \n \nComplaint cases relating to allegedly Misleading Advertisements (Excluding cases disposed for \nnot being perused by the Complainant)  \n \n2013-2014  \n \nS.No. Complainant Respondent Subject Action Taken/S tatus \n1. Sh. Deepak Chhabria , \nChairman, Employment \nPromotion Council of \nIndian Personnel, 105, Sai \nPrasad Commercial \nComplex, Khar, Mumbai \n– 400052 \nEmployment \n& NRI Times \nPublication of \nunauthorized overseas \nrecruitment \npublication \nClosed on 12 .0 7.13 \nwith reiteration of the \nprevious adjudication \n/ guidelines of the \nrespondent. \n2. Shri Arvind Kumar, \nUnder Secretary, M/o \nOverseas Indian Affairs \nEmigration Section, \nAkbar Bhavan, \nChanakyapuri, New Delhi \n \nRashtra \nDeepika \nIssuing \nadvertisements for \noverseas employment \nby unregistered \nrecruiting agent in \nviolation of the \nEmigration Act, 1983 \nClosed with \nreiteration of the \nprevious adjudication \n/ guidelines of the \nrespondent.  \n \n2014-2015  \n \nS. No  Complaint Respondent Subject Action Taken/Status \n \n----NIL----- \n \n \n \n2015-2016 \n \n \nS. No  Complaint Respondent Subject Action Taken/Status \n \n----NIL----- \n \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |189", "t": "Over Valuation of Coal Imports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 189\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016\n \n \nJOB PORTAL FOR MSME\n \n \n189.\n \n \nSHRI PRALHAD JOSHI:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nthe details of number of persons registered as job seeker, and as employers \nwith the job portal, ‘Employment Exchange for Industries’, launched by the \nGovernment; \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nwhether the \ndifferent jobs available have been listed on the website;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the number of persons actually applied for a job \nthrough the portal, if not, the reasons therefor;\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nwhether the portal lists jobs in the manufacturing as well as the se\nrvices sector, \nif not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(\ne\n)\n \nwhether the Government plans to tie up with other private job portals and if \nso, the details thereof?\n \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \n \nMICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ \nSINGH)\n \n \n(a) :\n \n    \n3,45,024 job seekers and 2933 employers have registered on the portal \nnamely “Employment Exchange for Industries” as on 18\nth\n \nApril 2016 \n \n \n(b)& (c):\n \n     \nPresently, the main objective of the portal is to facilitate matchmaking \nbetween job \nseekers and employers for their mutual benefits. Very few employers have \nnotified their vacancies on the portal at present.\n \n \n(d):\n \n       \nMainly the employers belonging to manufacturing sector are using the \ninformation available on the portal.  However, the\n \nemployers from service sector can \nalso use the portal.\n \n \n(e): \n \n      \nWith the launch of National Career Service (NCS) by Ministry of Labour \n& Employment, there is a need for convergence of job portal for MSME (Employment \nExchange for Industries) with the N\national Career Service.\n \n \n \n********\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |19", "t": "Euro VI Standard Norms", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. †19 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nNon-NET Fellowships \n \n†19. SHRI NANA PATOLE:  \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) the details of the announcement made by the University Grants Commission (UGC) \nabout discontinuance of non- National Eligibility Test (NET) fellowships for M.Phil. and \nPh.D. students;  \n \n(b) the details of the steps taken by the Government to tackle the anger amongst \nstudents belonging to various universities and the countrywide agitation held by them \nagainst the said decision; \n \n(c) the details of the action taken against the Delhi University Teachers 'Association and \nother Teachers' Associations who came out in support of the said agitation; \n \n(d) whether the Government has received any demand from any student organisation \nfor raising the amount of scholarships; and \n \n (e) if so, whether the said demand is also likely to be considered in conjunction with the   \nNET issue and if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b): The non- National Eligibility Test (NET) fellowships for M.Phil. and PhD. have \nnot been discontinued. The University Grants Commission (UGC) had, in its 510\nth \nmeeting held on 7 th October, 2015, considered the report of its Expert Committee for \nframing guidelines and also for considering enhancement of non-National Eligibility Test \n\n(NET) fellowships and resolved to discontinue the scheme. The Central Government \ndirected the UGC on 28 th October, 2015 not to implement the Commission’s decision \ndiscontinuing the Non -NET fellowships and to continue all existing and continuing \nfellowships, NET as well as Non- NET. It was decided that the existing awards under \nNon-NET fellowships will not be reduced rather expanded and that guidelines for \nselection, coverage, award and administration of Non- NET fellowships be developed to \nenable an enhancement in these fellowships. \n The Government appointed a Committee on 28 th October, 2015, under the \nchairpersonship of Prof. Gautam Barua, former Director, Indian Institute of Technology \n(IIT), Guwahati to go into the issues related to the research fellowships provided by the \nUGC, covering both NET fellowships and Non- NET fellowships. The details of the \ncommittee are available at http://mhrd.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/ \nReviewUGCFellowships.pdf . \n(c): No such action has been taken by the Government against Teachers’ Association. \n(d) & (e): The  Terms of Reference of the Committee under the chairpersonship of Prof. \nGautam Barua include recommending guidelines for the selection, coverage, award, \nand administration of the non-NET fellowships. The report of the Committee is awaited.  \n**** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |190", "t": "National Infrastructure Investment Fund", "head": " \n \n \n \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Economic Affairs \n  \n  \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *190 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6th MAY 2016  \n \nNATIONAL INFRASTRUCTURE INVESTMENT FUND \n \n*190. SHRI DHARMENDRA YADAV: SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE \n \n(a) the initial corpus and the current level of the National Infrastructure Investment Fund \n(NIIF) set up to reduce the financial gap for infrastructure projects in the country;  \n \n(b) the details of investment made during the last three years, sector and year-wise; \n \n(c) whether a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) between India and the United Arab \nEmirates (UAE) was signed to mobilise long term investment in NIIF, if so, the details \nthereof; and \n \n(d) the details of the terms of conditions, principles and other criteria laid down jointly by \nboth the countries to take forward the cooperation as envisaged in the MoU? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF FINANCE \n(SHRI ARUN JAITLEY) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n**** \n  \n\n \n \n \n \nStatement referred to in the reply to LOK SABHA Starred Question no * 190(a to d ) \nraised by Shri Dharmendra Yadav  and Shri Shrirang Appa Barne, Member of \nParliament due reply for 6th May, 2016  regarding “ National Infrastructure \nInvestment Fund”.  \n \n(a) & (b) The initial authorized corpus of National Investment and Infrastructure Fund \n(NIIF) would be Rs 40,000 crore, which may be raised from time to time.  NIIF is yet to be \ncapitalized and has not made any investment till now. \n(c) & (d) Yes Madam.  \n \nIndia and the United Arab Emirates (UAE) signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) \non 11 th February, 2016 to mobilise long term investment into the NIIF. The MoU is \ndesigned to establish a transparent and high -level framework and collaboration platform \nunder which both Countries intend to explore ways to facilitate and expand the \nparticipation of UAE’s investment institutions in appropriate infrastructure projects and \ninstitutions in India including NIIF, in a manner consistent with the established \ninvestment criteria and standards in a mutually agreed manner. \n \nA joint working group comprising of the concerned representatives of both Parties would \ntake forward cooperation under this MoU and discuss and agree to the terms, principles \nand criteria jointly. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |191", "t": "Respiratory Diseases", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 191\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nRESPIRATORY DISEASES\n†*191. SHRI SUKHBIR SINGH JAUNAPURIA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether respiratory ailments are on the rise in the country, if so, the details\nthereof and the reasons therefor, State/ UT-wise;\n(b) the number of cases of respiratory ailments reported during the las t three years,\nState/UT-wise;\n(c) whether the Government has conducted any study/survey to assess the\nrespiratory health status of people, if so, the details thereof and the outcome thereon;\nand\n(d) the preventive measures taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 191* FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\n(a) to (d) There is a slight increase in the cases of the Acute Respiratory Infection\n(ARI) as reported by the Central Bureau of Health Intelligence (CBHI). The details\nof the cases reported due to ARI during 2013, 2014 and 2015 are placed at\nAnnexure.\nNo Nationwide survey has been conducted to assess the respiratory health status of\nthe people. As per a survey conducted by Vallabbhai Patel Chest Institute, Delhi in\n2006, which covered 5900 adults belonging to urban, rural and slum population in\nDelhi , 11.69% were found to be suffering from Rhinits and 11.03% from Asthma.\nThe measures being taken to prevent respiratory ailments include the following:\n1. Government has taken various measures to control environ mental pollution\nincluding tightening of vehicular and industrial norms, promotion of cleaner\ntechnologies, strengthening of network of air quality monitoring stations,\npromoting public awareness etc.\n2. Under the Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme, th e Districts and\nStates have been strengthened by providing manpower, training of identified\nRapid Response Team (RRT) members for outbreak investigations,\nstrengthening of laboratories for detection of epidemic prone diseases\nincluding acute respiratory infections.\n3. Under Reproductive and Child Health Programme, prevention and treatment\nof acute respiratory infection including pneumonia is being addressed on\npriority basis.\n4. For prevention and control of Tuberculosis, Revised National Tuberculosis\nControl Pro gramme (RNTCP) is being implemented under National Health\nMission (NHM).\n\nAnnexure\nState/UT-wise Cases due to Acute Respiratory Infection (ARI) reported during the years 2013–2015\nS.No. State /U.T 2013 2014\n(Provisional)\n2015\n(Provisional)\nCases Cases Cases\n1 Andhra Pradesh 3110536 2413509 2096497\n2 Arunachal Pradesh 46643 17887 10910\n3 Assam 145047 68334 20456\n4 Bihar 1758655 1488744 1184411\n5 Chhattisgarh 287771 363570 375805\n6 Goa 59590 67720 60998\n7 Gujarat 1041042 1249351 1369389\n8 Haryana 1003454 1123370 1164780\n9 Himachal Pradesh 1631779 1523572 1591505\n10 Jammu & Kashmir 789714 746222 762025\n11 Jharkhand 238206 224576 201591\n12 Karnataka 1577354 2213159 2118979\n13 Kerala 6233391 6042825 5879901\n14 Madhya Pradesh 1276515 1529711 1702426\n15 Maharashtra 896949 1146185 1537325\n16 Manipur 31468 33470 37477\n17 Meghalaya 329019 375220 169854\n18 Mizoram 33807 28790 33169\n19 Nagaland 37664 38312 22529\n20 Odisha 1599506 2125240 2127921\n21 Punjab 692618 638908 612122\n22 Rajasthan 2515972 3038583 2822582\n23 Sikkim 93726 83893 53220\n24 Tamil Nadu 1944280 1904592 1962629\n25 Telangana NA NA 1137894\n26 Tripura 158988 154227 145522\n27 Uttarakhand 185034 211385 253292\n28 Uttar Pradesh 1720714 1627509 1772916\n29 West Bengal 2514606 2831623 2991474\n30 A & N Islands 58307 61716 44465\n31 Chandigarh 100541 109081 127479\n32 D & N Haveli 96346 102487 61639\n33 Daman & Diu 48603 60571 56483\n34 Delhi 390170 369406 307597\n35 Lakshadweep 43944 49807 37297\n36 Puducherry 731148 772188 795892\nTotal 33423107 34835743 35650451\nSource: Monthly Health Condition Reports from Directorate of Health Services of States/UTs\nNote:\n1. NA  stands for Not Available\n2. 2014: Andhra Pradesh Excludes data of 10 districts of Telangana from July 2014 Onwards."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |192", "t": "Digitalisation in the Banks", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT  \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 192 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25TH APRIL, 2016 \n \n \nNumber of Fake Schools \n \n \n†192. SHRIMATI RANJANBEN BHATT:  \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n (a) whether it has come to the notice of the Government that number of schools in \nvarious parts of the country are functioning on papers only and if so, the details thereof, \nState/UT-wise;  \n \n (b) whether the Government has taken steps against such schools and if so, the details \nthereof; and  \n \n (c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a):  Education being a concurrent subject, majority of schools and the State Examination \nBoards come under the purview of respective State Government/UT Administration. No data \nis maintained at Central level with regard to fake schools. \n \n(b) & (c): Do not arise.  \n \n   \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |193", "t": "Women Directors in Listed Companies", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 193 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nTeaching of Sanskrit in Schools \n \n†193. SHRI A.T. NANA PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has formulated any scheme to teach Sanskrit at primary and \nmiddle school levels in various parts of the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether it is a fact that many national and international organizations have mentioned \nthat Sanskrit language helps mental and cultural development of children; \n \n(d) if so, whether the Government has taken any steps in this regard; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b) As per Three Language Formula, Sanskrit is taught as a third language (Modern \nIndian Language) across the country from class VI onwards.  In some states viz. Uttar Pradesh, \nUttarakhand and Odisha, Sanskrit is taught in primary stage in classes II/ III/ IV. \n \n(c) to (e) National Policy on Education (NPE) – 1986 (as modified in 1992) states that \nconsidering the special importance of Sanskrit to the growth and development of Indian \nlanguages and its unique contribution to the cultural unity of the country, facilities for it s \nteaching at the school and university stages should be offered on a more liberal scale. National \nCurriculum Framework (NCF) – 2005 states that Sanskrit is rich in its inflectional grammatical \nstructure and aesthetic value, and can illuminate our lives as  many languages keep borrowing \nwords from it. It further states that Sanskrit may also be studied as a Modern Indian Language \n(MIL).  \n  \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |194", "t": "AYUSH Industry Clusters", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA\nAND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 194\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nAYUSH INDUSTRY CLUSTERS\n*194. SHRI R. GOPALAKRISHNAN:\nSHRI RAGHAV LAKHANPAL:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of the AYUSH Industry Clusters (AIC) set up in various parts of the\ncountry along with the funds allocated for each of these clusters, State-wise;\n(b) whether the Government has formulated any plan for setting up of more such\nclusters across the country, if so, the details thereof, State-wise;\n(c) whether any requests from States for setting up of such clusters in the country are\npending with the Government, if so, the details thereof, State-wise; and\n(d) the response of the Government thereon?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND\nHOMOEOPATHY(AYUSH)\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO 194 FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\n(a): So far, the Government has sanctioned 10 AYUSH Industry Clusters in the\ncountry. The details of funds allocated to each AYUSH Cluster is at Annexure.\n(b): Government is implementing a Central Sector Scheme for AYUSH Clusters\nin the 12th Plan period (2012-17) for setting up more such clusters in the country.\nUnder this Scheme, Government provides financial assistance upto 60% of the\nproject cost subject to a maximum of Rs.15 crores to the Special Purpose Vehicle\n(SPV) formed by at least 15 enterprises located in an existing cluster, that is, a group\nof AYUSH enterprises located in an area covering a radius of 100 Km . The projects\nare awarded based on the pro posal received not state -wise. However, preference is\ngiven to states where such SPVs do not exist.\n(c) & (d): No proposal from any of the State/UTs is pending with this Ministry.\n*****\n\nAnnexure\nS.\nNo\nName of the\nState Name of SPVs with Address\nTotal\nProject\nCost\nTotal Grant-\nin-aid\nSanctioned\nTotal Grant-\nin-aid\nReleased\n1 Karnataka\nAyurpark Health Care Limited, 168,\nChelakere Kalyan Nagar, Bangalore,\nKarnataka\n1736.00 1000.00\n991.82\n2 Kerala\nCARE Keralam, Kinfra Small Industries Park,\nNalukettu Road, Kinfra Park P.O., Thrissur,\nKerala\n1692.00 1000.00\n968.81\n3 Punjab\nHerbal Health Research Consortium Pvt. Ltd.\n277, East Mohan Nagar, Amritsar, Punjab 1679.00 1000.00\n985.00\n4 Rajasthan\nM/s Ayushraj Enterprises Pvt. Ltd;  35,\nSushilpura South, Ajmer Road Bridge, Shyam\nNagar 2nd Lane, Sodala, Jaipur, Rajasthan\n1620.00 970.00\n952.34\n5\nMaharashtra\nKonkan Ayur Pharma Pvt. Ltd.Gat No. 144,\nA/p.: Dhamani, Tal.: Sangameshwar, Dist. :\nRatnagiri, Maharashtra\n1479.00 888.00\n862.60\n6\nMaharashtra Ayurved Center Pvt. Ltd.,\nAradhana, 55, Amchi Colony, Pashan - NDA\nRoad, Bawdhan khurd, Pune, Maharashtra\n1582.00 949.00\n770.24\n7 Uttrakhand\nSanskar Ayush Medicare Pvt Ltd; Arya\nNagar, Jawalapur, Haridwar, Uttrakhand 1785.00 1000.00\n600.00\n8 Tamilnadu\nTraditional Ayurveda Cluster of Tamilnadu,\n48, Grand West Trunk Road, Kancheepuram\nDist., Sriperumbudur, Tamilnadu\n1659.00 965.00\n499.00\n9 Orissa\nRushikulya Ayurvedic Cluster pvt Ltd,\nAjodhya Nagar, 2nd lane, Berhampur,\nGanjam, Orissa\n999.00 599.00\n120.00\n10 Andhra\nPradesh\nLepakshi Ayush Park Private Limited, 5 -72\nPVC Colony, Bellary Road, Anantapur,\nAndhra Pradesh\n1801.00 1000.00\n200.00\nTOTAL 16032.00 9371.00 6949.81"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |195", "t": "Mission Indradhanush", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 195\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nMISSION INDRADHANUSH\n*195. SHRI AJAY MISRA TENI:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) the salient features of the immunization programme, Mission Indradhanush;\n(b) whether the Government has received any proposals from States to include some\nmore vaccines under the mission, if so, the details the reof and the action taken\nthereon;\n(c) whether the Government has assessed the achievements made under the said\nprogramme, if so, the details and the outcome thereof; and\n(d) whether the Government proposes to run the programme on regular basis and\nalso integrate it with the vaccination programme for pregnant women for\nsafeguarding mother and child from diseases, if so, the details thereof and the steps\ntaken in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 195* FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\n(a): The Universal Immunization Programme, despite being operational since 1985 ,\nhas documented only 1% increase of immunization coverage per year in the last four\nyears (61% in CES 2009 to 65% in RSOC 2013 -14). Mission Indradhanush (MI)\nwas launched as a special drive to vaccinate all unvaccinate d and partially\nvaccinated children in high burden districts and rapidly improve immunization\ncoverage, with the objective to increase full immunization coverage to 90% and\nsustain the same by 2020.\n(b): No, the Government has not received any proposal fr om States to include some\nmore vaccines under this mission.\n(c): Yes, the Government has assessed the achievements made under Mission\nIndradhanush. Against an estimated target of 75,85,731 children, as on 30th April\n2016, 38,70,447 children were fully vac cinated in two Phases of Mission\nIndradhanush. In addition, 38,07,948 pregnant women were vaccinated against\ntetanus. This has resulted in at least 5% increase in full immunization coverage.\n(d): The Government plans to run Mission Indradhanush in the high priority districts\nto cover left out children and sustain the immunization coverage by 2020. The\nprogramme for vaccination of pregnant women with tetanus toxoid is already\nintegrated as evident from the details at (c) above."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |196", "t": "IT Paying Senior Citizens and Women", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 196 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nSetting up of Central University in AP \n \n196. SHRI KESINENI NANI: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any proposal to set up a new Central University in the State of \n Andhra Pradesh as envisaged in the Andhra Pradesh Re-organisation Act, 2014;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether any survey has been conducted for selection of site for establishing the University \n and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(d)  whether the Government has made any financial allocation for the same during the current \n financial year and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e)  the time by which it is likely to be implemented? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n (a) to (e): Yes, Madam. In pursuance of Andhra Pradesh Re -organisation Act, 2014, the Central \nGovernment has decided to es tablish a Central University in the State of Andhra Pradesh. A Site \nhas already been finalised for this purpose in Ananthapur District of the State.  Adequate funds \nwould be released by the Universit y Grants Commission in accordance with the requirement and \nphasing of establishment and construction activities.  No definite time period can  be given as \nestablishment of a new Central University depends on enactment of the relevant Bill. \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |197", "t": "Fund Allocation for Health Projects", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO.\n197\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n25.04\n.2016\n \nVAISAKHA\n \n5, \n1938 (SAKA)\n \n \nPRESERVATION OF CULTURE \n \n \nWill the \nMinister  of \nCULTURE be pleased \nto state:\n \n \n                                                        \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE, CULTURE\n \nAND \nTOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n \nAND  MINISTER OF STATE, CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n \n \n197\n.  \n \nSHRI RAVNEET SINGH: \n \nKUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE\n:\n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government has any special policy/scheme to preserve the tangible and \nintangible cultural heritage of the country;\n \n(b)\n \nif so, the main features of the policy and mechanism set up by the Government for \npreservation of tangible \nand intangible cultural heritage;\n \n \n(c)\n \nthe action taken by the Government in this regar\nd along with the measures taken\nfor \nvarious States including Punjab and the outcome thereof; and\n \n \n(d)\n \nthe funds allocated and utilised by the Government during the last\n \nfive years, State/UT\n-\nwise including Punjab in this regard?\n \n(a\n \nto\n \n(c)\n \nYes Madam. The Ministry of culture is responsible for preservation, development and \npromotion of both tangible and intangible heritage of India, which it implements through \nits schemes and Organisations located in various States of the country. \n \nWith r\negard to tangible heritage, the Ministry ensures preservation and  conservation of \ncentrally protected monuments of national importance through Archaeological Survey of \nIndia in terms of provisions contained in the Ancient Monuments & Archaeological Sites \nand Remains Act, 1958 and amendments thereunder.  Similarly, the Ministry promotes \nthe museum movement in the country through various Museums under its administrative \ncontrol and also through grant\n-\nin\n-\naids under Museum Grant Scheme. \n \nRegarding intangible c\nultural heritage, the Ministry extends financial support to \nindividuals, groups of individuals and cultural organizations engaged in performing visual \nand literary arts directly as well as through Organizations under its control. Also, a \nscheme named “Sche\nme for Safeguarding Intangible Cultural Heritage and Diverse \nCultural Traditions of India” was launched in 2013\n-\n14, which covers all recognised \ndomains of ICH such as oral traditions and expressions, including language as a vehicle \nof the intangible cultur\nal heritage, Performing Arts, Social Practices, rituals and festive \nevents, Knowledge and practices concerning nature and universe, traditional \ncraftsmanship etc. \n \n \n(d)\n \nThe Government does not allocate funds State and UT\n-\nwise. Funds are provided to the \nOrganizations under the Ministry of Culture located in various parts of the country in the \n form of grant - in - aid for fulfilling the mandate of the Ministry.  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |199", "t": "Task Force for National AYUSH Mission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA\nAND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 199\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nTASK FORCE FOR NATIONAL AYUSH MISSION\n*199. SHRI DUSHYANT CHAUTALA:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) the initiatives taken by the Government under the National AYUSH Mission\n(NAM) across the country;\n(b) whether the Government had constitu ted a task force under NAM to suggest\nways for making AYUSH accessible to the rural India, if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the task force has submitted its report and urged the Government to\npromote Ayurveda and other Indian systems of medicine an d make healthcare\naccessible to rural population, if so, the details thereof; and\n(d) whether the Government has contemplated any plan of action to ensure\naccessibility to the rural population of AYUSH medicines, if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND\nHOMOEOPATHY(AYUSH)\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO 199 FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\n(a) The Central Government launched National AYUSH Mission (NAM) on\n29.09.2014 which envisages better access to AYUSH services through upgradation\nof AYUSH Hospitals and Dispensaries, setting up of upto 50 bedded integrated\nAYUSH Hospitals, mainstreaming of AYUSH through co -location of AYUSH\nfacilities at Primary Health Centers (PHCs), Community Health Centers (CHCs) and\nDistrict Hospitals (DHs) and ensures availability of AYUSH drugs and trained\nmanpower. It also aims at improvement in quality of AYUSH education through\nenhancement in number of upgraded educational institutions, sustained availability\nof quality of raw material and improved availability of quality Ayurveda, Siddha and\nUnani & Homoeopathy (ASU &H) drugs through strengthening of Pharmacies and\nDrug Testing Laboratories in the States. Accordingly, Government of India provided\nfinancial assistance to State/UT Governments during 2014 -15 and 2015 -16 as per\ntheir requirement reflected in the Sta te Annual Action Plans (SAAPs). The detailed\nstatus of grant-in-aid released, State/UT-wise is furnished at ANNEXURE.\n(b) & (c) Under National AYUSH Mission (NAM), no task force has been\nconstituted to suggest ways for making AYUSH accessible to the rural India.\n(d) National AYUSH Mission envisages provision of AYUSH services with a\nuniversal access including rural population through upgradation of AYUSH\nhospitals and dispensaries, co -location of AYUSH facilities at Primary Health\nCentres (PHCs), Communi ty Health Centres (CHCs) and District Hospitals (DHs).\nThere is also provision for supply of AYUSH medicines for co -located AYUSH\nfacilities and exclusive AYUSH dispensaries and hospitals for benefitting the rural\npopulation.  Further, NAM also incorporate s the component s of Public Health\nOutreach activity, AYUSH Gram and School Health Programme through AYUSH\nmainly for the rural population. Public Health Outreach activity is focused on\n\nincreasing awareness about AYUSH’s strength in solving community health\nproblems resulting from nutritional deficiencies, epidemics and vector -borne\ndiseases, Maternal and Child Health Care etc. In AYUSH Gram, one village per\nblock is selected for adoption of method and practice of AYUSH way of life and\ninterventions of healt h care. AYUSH based lifestyles are promoted through\nbehavioral change communication, training of village health workers towards\nidentification and use of local medicinal herbs and provision of AYUSH health\nservices. In School Health Programme through AYUSH , t he main focus is to\naddress the health needs of school going c hildren by providing AYUSH services\nincluding Yoga and counselling.\n\nANNEXURE\nStatus of funds released under National AYUSH Mission (NAM) for the year\n2014-15, State/UT-wise\nRs. In lakhs\nSl.\nNo.\nName of The\nState\nAllocation of Central\nShare during 2014-\n15\nCentral Share of\napproved during\n2014-15\nInitial installment of\nGrant-in-aid released\nduring 2014-15\n1) Andhra Pradesh 859.854 859.856 309.925\n2)\nArunachal\nPradesh 171.662 171.248 101.135\n3) Assam 1068.941 1068.357 668.979\n4) Bihar 2755.399 - -\n5) Chhattisgarh 872.845 830.67 281.413\n6) Gujarat 956.665 909.886 332.393\n7) Haryana 640.56 612.807 213.589\n8)\nHimachal\nPradesh 273.885 - -\n9)\nJammu &\nKashmir 666.851 618.46 226.268\n10) Jharkhand 981.409 - -\n11) Karnataka 1043.578 1041.592 359.116\n12) Kerala 716.184 712.548 254.670\n13)\nMadhya\nPradesh 2070.764 1948.918 644.938\n14) Meghalaya 247.131 228.047 134.647\n15) Manipur 426.061 373.218 226.813\n16) Mizoram 228.263 190.125 116.270\n17) Maharashtra 1555.779 1480.604 534.670\n18) Nagaland 388.945 191.728 115.613\n19) Odisha 1322.567 1322.57 471.723\n20) Punjab 604.063 585.289 316.000\n21) Rajasthan 1862.295 1768.789 638.065\n22"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |2", "t": "Social Security for Labourers", "head": "����\n�\n�\n������������������� �\n������������������������������������� �\n�\n��� ������ �\n���������������������� �� �\n������������������ �� �������� ������ �\n�\n����������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n����������������������� ��\n�����������\n���������������������������� �� ���������������������������\n����� ���� ��������� ��� ���������� ���� �������� ���� ��� �������� ���\n�������\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ���� �������� ���������������� �������� ��������� ������ ������� ���� ����� ������\n�����������\n�\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ��������� ���� ���� ���� ���� ������� ��� ������ ����������\n����������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������ �\n������������������������������ �� �����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������� ��� ������ ������ ��� ���� ��������� ��� ���������� �� ��������\n������������������������������ �\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������\n�����\n����������� �\n���������������\n���������������������\n����� �\n������������\n����� �\n���� ���� ������ ������ ��������\n���� ���� ������ ������ ��������\n���� ����� ������ ������ ��������\n����������������������������\n����� ����������������������\n����� ����������������������\n�\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������\n� � � � � � �������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |200", "t": "AYUSH Products/Medicines", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA\nAND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 200\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 06TH MAY, 2016\nAYUSH PRODUCTS/MEDICINES\n*200. SHRI C. MAHENDRAN:\nSHRI P. NAGARAJAN:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY, UNANI,\nSIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has taken various steps to promote and popularise\nAYUSH system of medicines internationally, if so, the details thereof;\n(b) the details of countries that have shown interest in AYUSH systems of medicine\nand the success achieved in promoting and popularising AYUSH systems of\nmedicine internationally;\n(c) whether any quality regulations/ standards have been laid down f or use of heavy\nmetals in Ayurveda and other AYUSH products/medicines as those are detrimental\nto human health; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND\nHOMOEOPATHY(AYUSH)\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 200 FOR 06TH MAY, 2016\n(a) & (b):  In line with its mandate for global promotion and propagation of the\nAYUSH systems of medicine, the Ministry of AYUSH has been organizing/\nparticipating in International exhibitions/ conferences/ workshops/ seminars/ road\nshows/ trade fairs, etc. to have wider reach for international propagation of AYUSH.\nIncentives are also being provided (a) to AYUSH drug manufacturers, entrepreneurs,\nAYUSH institutions, etc. for participating in International exhibitions/ conferences/\nworkshops/ seminars/ road shows/ trade fairs, etc. for generating awareness amongst\nthe participating public about the Indian Medicine and; (b) to AYUSH drug\nmanufacturers for registration of AYUSH products with regulatory agencies of\ndifferent countries to enhance export of the prod ucts. To meet the worldwide\nincreasing demand of Yoga experts, the Ministry of AYUSH has launched a scheme\nfor Voluntary Certification of Yoga Professionals on 22nd June, 2015 in\ncollaboration with Quality Council of India (QCI).  The Scheme aims at promot ing\nauthentic Yoga as a preventive and health promotive drugless therapy and involves\ncertifying the competence level of the professionals to help their deployment within\nand outside the country.\nAs a result of concerted efforts Ministry of AYUSH has ach ieved great\nsuccess by marking presence of AYUSH systems of medicine across the globe, as is\nevident from the following:\n(i)   Memorandums of Understanding (MoUs) for Country to Country cooperation in\nfield of Traditional Medicine have been signed with Nep al, Bangladesh, Hungary,\nTrinidad & Tobago, Malaysia, China, Mauritius, Mongolia and Turkmenistan.\n(ii)  MoUs for undertaking collaborative research have been signed with Royal\nLondon Hospital for Integrated Medicine, UK and National Centre for Natural\nProduct Research (NCNPR) University of Mississippi, USA and College of\nHomeopaths of Ontario, Canada; and Letters of Intent (LoIs) with Mexico and\nUniversity of Strasbourg, France.\n(iii)  The Ministry has also set up Academic Chairs in Hungary, Trinidad & T obago\n(Ayurveda Chair) and South Africa (Unani Chair).  Besides, MoUs have also been\n\nsigned with Universities in Russia, Indonesia, Slovenia, Thailand and Armenia for\nsetting up of AYUSH Academic Chairs.\n(iv) AYUSH Information Cells for dissemination of a uthentic information about\nAYUSH Systems of Medicine have been set up at Malaysia, Trinidad & Tobago,\nMexico, Cuba, Hungary, Russia, Indonesia (2 Cells), Mauritius, Slovenia, Sweden,\nChina, Dubai, Croatia, Kyrgyzstan, Argentina and Israel.\n(v) Recently, t he Union Cabinet has approved the signing of Agreement with the\nWorld Health Organisation (WHO) for long term collaboration under which, the\nWHO would help in improving international acceptability and promotion of AYUSH\nsystems.\n(vi)  An Indo-US workshop on Traditional Systems with special focus on cancer was\norganized in New Delhi on 3-4 March, 2016. A US team comprising of experts from\nNational Cancer Institute (NCI) took part in the two-day exhaustive deliberations.\n(c) & (d) : Drugs & Cosmetics Act, 1 940 and Rules thereunder have regulatory\nprovisions for Ayurvedic, Siddha and Unani (ASU) medicines, under which it is\nmandatory for the manufacturers to comply with the quality standards of ingredients\nof minerals and metallic origin as prescribed in the pharmacopoeia  and with Good\nManufacturing Practices (GMP) for manufacturing of herbo -mineral-metallic\ncompounds of Ayurveda, Siddha and Unani medicines as contained in part ‘D’  of\nSchedule ‘T’ of the Drugs & Cosmetics Rules, 1945.\nAlso, permissible lim its of heavy metals in ASU medicines manufactured\nfrom plant materials are prescribed in the pharmacopoeia.\nIt is also pertinent to state that heavy metals are used in the manufacturing"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |202", "t": "New Kendriya/Navodaya Vidyalayas", "head": "1 \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 202 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.05.2016 \n \nNEW KENDRIYA/NAVODAYA VIDYALAYAS \n*202. SHRI BISHNU PADA RAY:  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be \npleased to state:  \n(a)  whether norms have  been laid down for s etting up of new Kendriya \nVidyalayas/ Navodaya Vidyalayas and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b)  whether the Govern ment has received proposals to establish \nKendriya and Navodaya Vidyalayas at various places, including South \nAndaman district in Andaman and Nicobar Islands and if so, the details \nthereof; and  \n(c)  the action taken/proposed to be  taken by the Government in this \nregard? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n****** \n \n\n2 \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 202 RAISED BY SHRI BISHNU PADA RAY, HON’BLE \nMEMBER OF PARLIAMENT TO BE ANSWERED ON 9 TH MAY, 2016 REGARDING \nNEW KENDRIYA / NAVODAYA VIDYALAYAS. \n \n(a) : The Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) are opened primarily to cater to the educational \nneeds of the children of transferable Central Government employees including defence \nand para–military personnel by providing a common programme of education. Opening of \nKVs is continuous process. Proposals for opening of new KVs are considered only if \nsponsored by Ministries or Departments of t he Government of India / State Governments \n/ Union Territories Administrations and Organization of employees belonging to the \neligible categories thereby committing resources for setting up a new KV as well as on \nthe availability of necessary sanction of the Government.    \nAs per the prescribed norms for opening of new KVs under Civil / Defence sector, \nthe concerned sponsoring authority is liable to provide suitable and sufficient rent free \ntemporary accommodation for setting up of the newly sanctioned KV to make the school \nfunctional till the construction of permanent building by the Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan \n(KVS) on the land to be provided by the sponsoring agency, free of cost. The identified \nand demarcated land is also required to be transferred by the State Government / District \nAdministration to KVS.  \nThe Navodaya Vidyalaya Scheme provides for opening of one Navodaya \nVidyalaya in each district of the country. Opening of new JNVs is a continuous process \nwhich depends on the willingness of the conc erned State Government to make available \nrequisite suitable land free of cost for construction of school buildings and required \ntemporary accommodation (free of rent) to start the Vidyalaya. Actual sanction and \nopening of new JNV depends on the availability of funds and approval by the Competent \nAuthority. \n \n(b)  Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan has received 163 proposals for establishment of \nnew KVs at various places in the country. The State/UT-wise details are annexed. KVS  \nand NVS have not received any specific proposals recently from the UT administration of \nA&N Islands for establishment of new KV or JNV in South Andaman district though a \nrequest was received by this Ministry in September, 2015 for sanction of 2 additional KVs \nand 2 additional JNVs in Andaman & Nicobar Islands. \n \n(c)  The proposals for opening of new KVs are considered only after their feasibility is \nestablished, and subject to availability of resources and sanction of competent authority. \nSimilarly, JNVs are opened in districts not having the same after land and temporary \naccommodation are arranged by the State Government/ UT Administration and subject to \navailability of resources and sanction of competent authority.  \n***** \n\n3 \n \nANNEXURE \n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. \n202 RAISED BY SHRI BISHNU PADA RAY, HON’BL E MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT TO BE ANSWERED \nON 09th MAY, 2016 REGARDING NEW KENDRIYA/NAVODAYA VIDYALAYAS \n \n \nLIST OF PROPOSALS RECEIVED FROM THE STATE GOVTs./MHA/DEFENCE/ RAILWAYS/ FOR \nOPENING OF NEW KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS UNDER CIVIL/DEFENCE SECTOR (As on 04-5-2016) \n \nS.N. Name of State S. No. Locati on of proposed  Kendriya Vidyalaya  \n1.  Assam 1 Chirang \n2.  2 Ranga Para, Distt. Sonitpur \n3.  3 North Lakhimpur, Distt. North Lakhimpur \n4.  4 PTC Dergaon, Distt. Golaghat \n5.  Andhra Pradesh 1 Kalichedu, Distt. Nellore \n6.  2 Srikalahasti, Distt. Chittoor \n7.  3 West Gudur, Sri Potti, Sriramulu, Distt. Nellore \n8.  4 Gudipala, Distt. Chittoor \n9.  5 Jangalapalle, Yadamari Mandal, Distt. Chittoor \n10.  6 Naidupet, Distt. Nellore \n11.  7 Kandukur Town, Distt. Prakasam \n12.  8 Sattenapalli, Distt. Guntur \n13.  9 No. 2 Ongole, Distt. Prakasam \n14.   10 Rajampallim Yerragondapalem, Distt. Prakasam \n15.  Bihar 1 East Central Railway, Narkatiyaganj \n16.  Chandigarh (UT) 1 GC,CRPF, Halloma jra Chandigarh\n17.  Chhattisgarh 1 Tilkeja, Distt.Korba "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |203", "t": "Open and Distance Learning Institutions", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 203  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 9TH MAY, 2016 \nOpen and Distance Learning Institutions \n*203. SHRI GODSE HEMANT TUKARAM:  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government is promoting Open and Distance Education in the country, if so, the \ndetails thereof;  \n(b) the major streams/disciplines of distance education; \n(c) the names of conventional/Deemed Univer sities having Open and Distance Learning \nInstitutions in the country, State/ UT-wise;  \n(d) whether the Programmes/Courses run by these Un iversities have the consent/ approval of the \nDistance Education Council and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) if not, the action taken by the Government against the defaulting universities? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n3rd POSITION \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PA RTS (A), (B), (C), (D) & (E) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 203 FOR 9 TH MAY, 2016 ASKED BY SHRI \nGODSE HEMANT TUKARAM REGARDING OPEN AND DISTANCE LEARNING \nINSTITUTIONS \n(a): Yes, Madam. At present there is one Central University i.e. Indira Gandhi National Open \nUniversity (IGNOU) and fourteen (14) public f unded State Open Universities (SOUs) apart from \nthe Distance Education Institutions (DEIs) of the Conventional Universities and Deemed \nUniversities offering courses/programmes in the field of Open & Distance Learning (ODL) in the \ncountry. The details are given in Annexure-I. The Central Government provides some Plan \nFunds towards development of course material and quality assurance, student support services at \nthe Head Quarter and Regional Centres, staff trai ning and development, technical support, library \nfacilities and its augmentation, creation of e-content etc. to State Open Universities (SOUs) \nthrough the University Grants Commission (UGC) which is the regulatory authority for \nmonitoring and maintenance of standards in the Open and Distance Education in the country.  \n(b): The major streams/disciplines of ODL  programmes/courses offered by various \nUniversities/Deemed Universities as approved by the UGC in the field of ODL education are \nHumanities, Social Sciences and General Sciences etc.   \n(c): As per the information furnished by UGC, the State-wise names of the Conventional/Deemed \nUniversities which are approved by the UGC to offer specific programmes through Open and \nDistance Learning mode during the academic year 2015-16 is given at Annexure-I. Further, UGC \nwhile approving the proposals of Conventional/Deemed Universities for running ODL \nprogrammes/courses maintains its territorial jurisdiction policy. \n(d): Yes, Madam. The universities as refere d above can offer only those programmes/courses \nthrough ODL mode which are specifically mentioned in the approval letter addressed to the \nparticular university by UGC from time to time. \n(e): With a view to maintain standards and quality in running ODL programmes/courses by \nvarious Conventional/Deemed Universities, UGC as a regulator examines all complaints received \nby it, if any, and based on the gravity and seri ousness of the complaint(s), issues warning(s), \nshow-cause notice(s) and even withdrawal of the ODL programmes/courses of such universities \nfor not adhering to the UGC guidelines.  The UGC also issues public notices etc. for the use of \ngeneral public to make them aware about the issues of territorial jurisdiction and validity of \nrecognition of the ODL programmes/courses. \n\n******* \n \nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE IN REPLY TO PART ‘A’ AND ‘C’ OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 203 FOR ANSWER ON 09.05.2016 ASKED BY SHRI GODSE HEMANT \nTUKARAM REGARDING OPEN AND DISTANCE LEARNING INSTITUTIONS \nLIST OF UNIVERSITIES / INSTITUTIONS WHICH WERE APPROVED BY UGC TO OFFER \nPROGRAMMES THROUGH OPEN AND DISTANCE LEARNING MODE DURING THE \nACADEMIC YEAR 2015-16  As on 04.05.2016 \nS.No State Name of University/ Institution Type of \nUniversity/Institute \n1 Andhra Pradesh Andhra University State University \n2 Andhra Pradesh Dr. Bhim Rao Ambedkar Open University State University \n3 Andhra Pradesh Dravidian University State University \n4 Andhra Pradesh Gandhi Institute of Technology and \nManagement (GITAM) \nDeemed University \n5 Andhra Pradesh Sri Venkateswara University State University \n6 Andhra Pradesh University of Hyderabad Central University \n7 Andhra Pradesh Rashtriya Sanskrit Vidyapeetha Tirupati Deemed to be University \n8 Arunachal Pradesh Rajiv Gandhi University Central University \n9 Assam Assam Don Bosco University Private University \n10 Assam Tezpur University Central University \n11 Assam Assam Down Town University Private University \n12 Assam Dibrugarh University State University \n13 Assam Krishana Kanta Handiqui State Open University State University \n14 Assam Ga"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |205", "t": "National Mineral Inventory", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. \n205\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n25\nth\n \nAPRIL\n, 2016\n \n \n“\nAPPOINTMENT OF MUSEUM DIRECTORS\n”\n \n \n205\n. \n \nSHRI MUTHAMSETTI SRINIVASA RAO(AVANTHI):\n \n \nWill the Minister of \nCULTURE \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a)\n \nwhether \nthere are vacant posts of Directors in many museums in the country;\n \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof, State/UT\n-\nwise and museum\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(c)\n \nthe reasons for such vacancies; and\n \n \n(d)\n \nthe time by which these vacancies are likely to be filled up?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (\nIC) CULTURE & TOURISM \n \nAND \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b) Insofar as museums which are directly under the administrative control of  \nMinistry of Culture are concerned, the details of vacancies of Directors are given \nbelow\n: \n \n \nName of \nState/UTs\n \nName of Organization/Museums\n \nNumber of \nvacant post\n \nDelhi\n \nNational Museum, New Delhi\n \n1\n \nMaharashtra\n \nNational Gallery of Modern Art, Mumbai\n \n1\n \nKarnataka\n \nNational Gallery of Modern Art, \nBengaluru\n \n1\n \nWest Bengal\n \nIndian Museum, Kolkata\n \n1\n \nTelangana\n \nSalarjung Museum, Hyderabad\n \n1\n \n \n(c)\n \nThe posts are laying vacant mainly because of non\n-\navailability of suitable \ncandidates and litigation. \n \n \n(d)\n \nThe selection process in conjunctuion with UPSC and DoPT is already \nunderway for the existing vacancies. Gene\nration of vacancies and filling up \nthe same is a continuous process\n.\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |206", "t": "Prices of Petroleum Products", "head": "भारत सरकार�\nवाणिज्य और उद्योग मंत्रालय�\nऔद्योगगक नीतत और संवर्धन ववभाग�\n���������������\n�������������������������� ���������������������������\n�\n����������������� ������������������������������������������������������ �� ���������\n�\n��������������������������������\n�\n����������������������������� ���������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������\n�\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�क) क्या�सरकार�ने�हाल�ही�में�मुम्बई�में��मेक�इन�इंडिया�वीक��मनाया�है���\n�ख) यदि�हां��तो�तत्संबंर्ी�ब्यौरा�क्या�है�और�इसके�लक्ष्य�और�उद्देश्यों�का�ब्यौरा�क्या�है�और�इन�\nपर�क्या�प्रततक्रिया�प्राप्त�हुई�है���\n�ग) क्या�सरकार�ने�उक्त�कायधिम�के�िौरान�ववभभन्न�भागीिारों/वविेशी�कम्पतनयों�के�साथ�कोई�\nसमझौता�ज्ञापन�पर�हस्ताक्षर�क्रकए�हैं���\n�घ) यदि�हां��तो�तत्संबंर्ी�ब्यौरा�क्या�है�और�इनसे�आकवषधत�तनवेश/आकवषधत�क्रकए�जाने�वाले�तनवेश�\nका�क्षेत्र-वार�ब्यौरा�क्या�है��और��\n�ङ) सरकार�द्वारा�िेश�में�ऐसे�तनवेशों�की�बार्ाओं�को�िूर�करने�के�भलए�क्या�किम�उठाए�गए�हैं�\nऔर�सरकार�को�इस�संबंर्�में�क्रकस�सीमा�तक�सफलता�प्राप्त�हुई�है���\n������ �������\n������������ ������������������������������ �������������������� �����������������������\n�����������������������������������������������\n�\n(��) जी� हां� 13-18 फरवरी� 2016 के िौरान मुंबई में मेक इन इंडिया वीक मनाया गया था। �\n�\n(��) इसका ब् यौरा इस प्रकार है -�\n \n������ ���������������������� �������\n भारत की ववतनमाधि क्षमताओं के प्रिशधन के भलए अवसर � तनवेश के अवसर सृजजत \nकरना और भारतीय ववतनमाधि क्षेत्र में भावी तनवेश के संबंर् में अभभसरि एवं तनिधय के \nभलए वैजश्वक सीईओ � ववचारक समूहों� नीतत तनमाधताओं � कूटनीततज्ञों तथा राजनीततक \nनेताओं के भलए एक प् लेटफ्मध प्रिान करना।�\n\n�����������������������\n मेक इन इंडिया वीक कायधिम में लगभग 9�00�000 ववजजटर आए थे।�\n 150 कायधिम आयोजजत क्रकए गए जजसमें लगभग 1250 प्रवक् ताओं ने भाग भलया। �\n 102 िेशों के प्रतततनगर्यों ने मेक इन इंडिया वीक में भाग भलया।�\n इसमें भारतीय तथा वैजश्वक कंपतनयों के 100 से अगर्क सीईओ ने भाग भलया। �\n मेक इन इंडिया वीक के मौके पर 8000 से अगर्क बी2बी� बी2जी तथा जी2जी बैठकें \nआयोजजत की गईं। �\n 20 िेशों के मंत्रालयी राजनीततक भशष् टमंिल ने मेक इन इंडिया वीक में भाग भलया। �\n�\n����������������������� संबंगर्त राज् यों/मंत्रालयों से ब् यौरें एकरितत्रत क्रकए जा रहे हैं। �\n�\n��������� सरकार िेश में तनवेश लाने के भलए अनेक क्षेत्रों में वविेशी प्रत् यक्ष तनवेश खोलने � \nएफिीआई से संबंगर्त सुर्ार एवं उिारीकरि करने तथा िेश में व् यवसाय करने की \nआसानी में सुर्ार करने जैसे ववभभन् न उपाय कर रही है । हाल ही में की गई  पहलें \nअनुबंर् में िी गई हैं।   \n�\n�����������\n\n�������������\n�\n������������������������������������������������ �������������������������� �� �������������������������������������������������������������� ������������ ���������������� �� �������������������� �� ��\n������ �����������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������\n�\n1. ववतनदिधष् ट रेल अवसंरचना पिरयोजनाओं में स् वत  अनुमोिन मागध के  तहत 100�  एफिीआई की अनुमतत िी \nगई है।�\n2. फ े मा (भारत से बाहर के तनवासी व्यजक्तयों द्वारा प्रततभूतत का अंतरि अथवा जारीकरि) ववतनयम की \nअनुसूची 4 के तहत एनआरआई� पीआईओ तथा ओसीआई द्वारा गैर-प्रत् यावतधनीय आर्ार पर क्रकए गए तनवेश� \nतनवासी भारतीयों द्वारा क्रकए गए तनवेश के समतुल्य घरेलू तनवेश माने जाएंगे। �\n3. कोई कंपनी, ट्रस्ट तथा भागीिारी फमध � जो भारत से बाहर तनगभमत है और जजसका स्वाभमत्व तथा तनयंत्रि \nअतनवासी भारतीय का हो� के भलए भी अतनवासी भारतीयों हेतु उपलब्र् ववशेष छूट उपलब् र् करायी गई है।�\n4. गचक्रकत् सा उपकरिों क े  ववतनमाधि क े  भलए स् वत  अनुमोिन मागध के तहत 100�  एफिीआई की अनुमतत िी \nगई है।�\n5. बीमा क्षेत्र संबंर्ी एफिीआई नी  तत की स् वत  अनुमोिन मागध के तहत 26�  तक वविेशी तनवेश क्रकए जाने \nसदहत 26�� से 49�  तक वविेशी तनवेश की क्षेत्रगत सीमा बढोत् तरी क े  भलए समीक्षा की गई है। पेंशन क्षेत्र \nसंबर्ी नीतत में समान पिरवतधन क्रकए गए हैं। �\n6. एफिीआई नीतत को सरल बनाने तथा ववभभन् न क्षेत्रों संबंर्ी शततों  एवं अनुमोिन अपेक्षाओं को लागू करने के  \nसंबंर् में स् पष् टता लाने क े  उद्देश् य से एक संयुक् त सीमा क े  तहत वविेशी तनवेशों क े  ववभभन् न प्रकारों को \nप्रततमोच् च क्रकया गया है।�\n7. व् हाइट लेबल एटीएम प्रचालनों में स् वत  अनुमोिन मागध के मा्‍ यम से 100�  तक एफिीआई की अनुमतत िी \nगई है।�\n8. तनमाधि ववकास क्षेत्र संबंर्ी एफिीआई नीतत में क्रकए गए सुर्ारों में शाभमल हैं �\nक) तनमाधि ववकास पिरयोजनाओं में 20�000 वगधमीटर क े  फ्लोर एिरया क े  एिरया संबंर्ी प्रतत बंर् तथा \nव् यवसाय के शुूआआत के 6 माह की अवगर् क े  भीतर क्रकए जाने वाले 5 भमभलयन अमरीकी िालर क े  \nन् यूनतम पूंजीकरि संबंर्ी शततों  को हटाना।�\nख) तीन वषतों  की पिरपक् वता अवगर् क े  बाि वविेशी तनवेश तनगधम एवं प्रत् यावतधन की अब अनुमतत िी गई \nहै। तनवेश क े  प्रत् यावतधन क े  रितबना एक अतनवासी से िूसरे अतनवासी को स् टेक का हस् तांतरि भी न तो \nक्रकसी पिरपक् वता अवगर् और न ही क्रकसी सरकारी अनुमोिन के अ्‍ यर्ीन है।�\nग) यदि पिरयोजना अथवा मुख् य अवसंरचना पिरपक् वता अवगर् से पहले पूरी हो जाती है� तो क्रकसी भी \nसमय तनगधम की अनुमतत। �\nघ)  टाऊनभशपों � म्ल   स/श्वपंग काम् पलेक् सों तथा व् यवसाय कें्ों के प्रचालन एवं प्रबंर्न के भलए पूिध \nपिरयोज"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |207", "t": "Education Reforms", "head": " \n1 \n \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 207 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.05.2016 \n \nEducation Reforms \n \n†*207. SHRI LAXMI NARAYAN YADAV:  \n  \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURC E DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government is satisfied with the quality and standard of education and \nteaching in the Universities, if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether the University Grants Commissi on (UGC) is implementing any scheme for \nimproving the standard of education/teaching in  Universities/Colleges in the country, if \nso, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has reviewed the working of such schemes, if so, the \noutcome thereof; and  \n(d) the steps taken by the UGC in the di rection of education reforms along with the \nimpact of these steps on education reforms? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n**** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n2 \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO  PARTS (a) to (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.207 FOR 09.05.2016 ASKED BY  SHRI LAXMI NARAYAN \nYADAV, HON’BLE MEMBER OF P ARLIAMENT, REGARDING EDUCATION \nREFORMS. \n \n(a) & (b):   Higher education reforms for enhancing quality along with equity and access \nis a continuous process. Educat ion, being in the concurrent list, is the responsibility of \nboth the Central as well as the State Governments.  The Government initiates education \nreforms after consultations with the stak eholders, including St ate Governments and \nacademics. \n The University Grants Commission (UGC) is implementing several schemes for \nimproving the standard of education/ teaching in universities and colleges in the country.  \nThe UGC undertakes maintenance  of standards in educat ion and teachi ng and quality \nassurance in Universities, Deemed to be Universities and Colleges through the following \nmechanisms, namely: framing regulations and schemes and; disbursing grants to the \neligible institutions. The UGC has notified several regulations wit h the objective of \nsustenance and improvement in the quality of  higher education and for undertaking \nacademic reforms. The regulat ions are available at http://www.ugc.ac.in/page/UGC-\nRegulations.aspx. In order to encourage research and development in the country, UGC \nhas laid out schemes, awards, fellowships, chairs and programmes under which \nfinancial assistance is provided to institutions of higher education as well as faculty \nmembers working therein to undertake quality research co vering areas of knowledge \nacross disciplines including revival & prom otion of indigenous languages. These \nschemes are at http://www.ugc.ac.in/page/XII-Plan-Guidelines.aspx\n .  \nIn addition to above, UGC has launched guidelines for Choice-based credit \nSystem (CBCS) for enhancing student mobility and providing a wider choice in subjects \nand for facilitation of quality education th rough innovation and improvements in \ncurriculum, teaching-learning process, examination and evaluation systems. \n \n(c): The UGC has informed that its schemes  are reviewed and moni tored with the help \nof reported progress, presentations to its expert committees, etc.  \n  The Central Government has reviewed the schemes of the UG C with the help of \nExpert Committees. The Department of Higher Education has also reviewed the impact \n\n \n3 \n \nand outcomes of the UGC schemes. As a resu lt several initiatives have been taken, \nnamely: (i) policy direction to the UGC to  amend the UGC(Minimum qualifications for \nappointment of teachers and other academic staff in universities and colleges and other \nmeasures for the maintenance of standards in  higher education) Regulations, 2010 to \nimplement modified Academic  Performance Indicator ( API) scheme and prescribe \nminimum qualifications for appoi ntment of teachers; (ii) po licy direction to the UGC to \nnot to discontinue the non-Natio nal Eligibility Test(NET) fello wships; (iii) policy direction \nto the UGC to establish an Inter Universi ty Centre on Teacher education in Banaras \nHindu University to strengthen the University system in teacher education; (iv) notifying \nof UGC (Furnishing of Information by Univer sities) Rules, 2015 for making it mandatory \nfor Universities to comply with new a ccounting standards and upload information on the \nAll India Survey on Higher Education; (v) fr aming of UGC (Prevention, prohibition and \nredressal of sexual harassment of wo men employees and st udents in higher \neducational institutions) Regulations, 2015  for ensuring a safe environment for both \nwomen employees and students in campuses and excursions  from higher educational \ninstitutions; (vi) ensuring UGC fund release to Universities and Colleges through Public \nFinancial Management System from 1 st April, 2016 onwards; (v ii) Strengthening of \ngrievance redressal mechanism in UGC a nd launch of Student grievanc"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |21", "t": "Drought in States", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA  \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 21 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.4.2016  \n \nNumber of Central Universities \n \n21. SHRI M.RAJA MOHAN REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of Human Resource Development be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total number of Central universities in the country at present, location- wise \nand name-wise;  \n \n(b) the present strength of teaching staff in each university;  \n \n(c) whether there is any shortage of teaching staff in these universities; and  \n \n(d) if so, the steps taken by the Government to fill up the posts? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) : At present, there are 41 Central Universities under the administrative control of this \nMinistry and their details are at annexure – I .  \n \n(b): The total number of sanctioned teaching posts in various Central Universities is \n16,600 (2371 Professor, 4708 Associate Professor, 9521 Assistant Professor) . T he \ndetails are given at annexure II.   \n \n(c) &  (d) : Occurring of vacancies and filling them  up is a continuous process. M HRD \nand UGC continuously monitor it with Universities. However, the onus of filling u p the \nteaching posts lies on Central Universities which are aut onomous bodies created under  \nActs of Parliament.  \n \nThis issue has been discussed in the Conference of Vice- Chancellors of the Central \nUniversities held on 4th – 5\nth February, 2015 and Visitor’ s Conference on 4 th -6th \nNovember, 2015 which were chaired by the Hon’ble President. In the Vice Chancellors’ \nConference and Visitor’s Conference, the Vice Chancellors were exhorted to fill up the \nvacant position of teachers in a time bound manner. Further , it was also discussed in a \nmeeting with Vice Chancellors of Central Universities on 18\nth February, 2016.  With the \nappointment of regular Vice Chancellors and providing of Visitor’s nominees to all Central \nUniversities for Selection Committees for teachers, the process of filling up of vacant \nteaching posts has gathered momentum.  \n \n******** \n \n\nAnnexure-I  \nS.No. Name of Central University \n1 Rajiv Gandhi University, Rono Hills, P.O. Doimukh, Itanagar, Arunachal Pradesh – 791 112. \n2 Assam University, PO: Assam University, Silchar - 788 011. \n3 Tezpur University, Napaam, Sonitpur, Assam - 784 028. \n4 University of Hyderabad, Hyderabad, Andhra Pradesh – 500 046. \n5 Maulana Azad National Urdu University, Gachibowli, Hyderabad, Andhra Pradesh – 500 032. \n6 English and Foreign Languages University, O.U. Campus, Hyderabad, Andhra Pradesh – 500 007. \n7 Jamia Millia Islamia, Jamia Nagar, New Delhi – 110 025. \n8 University of Delhi, Delhi – 110 007. \n9 JawaharLal Nehru University, New Mehrauli Road, New Delhi – 110 067. \n10 Indira Gandhi National Open University, New Delhi. \n11 The Indira Gandhi National Tribal University, Amarkantak, Madhya Pradesh – 484 886. \n12 Dr. Harisingh Gour Vishwavidyalaya, Sagar, Madhya Pradesh - 470 003. \n13 Mahatma Gandhi Antarrashtriya Hindi Vishwavidyalaya, Post Box No. 16, Panchtila, Umri Village, \nArvi Road, Wardha, Mumbai – 442 001. \n14 Mizoram University, Post Box No. 190, Aizwal, Mizoram – 796 009. \n15 North Eastern Hill University, NEHU Campus, Shillong, Meghalaya – 793 022. \n16 Manipur University, Imphal, Manipur – 795 003. \n17 Nagaland University, Headquarter Lumami, Zunheboto, Nagaland – 798 627. \n18 Pondicherry University, R. Venkataraman Nagar, Kalapet, Puducherry, Pondicherry – 605 014. \n19 Sikkim University, 6th Mile, Samdur, P.O. Tadong, Gangtok, Sikkim-737 102. \n20 Tripura University, Suryamaninagar, Agartala, Tripura – 799 130. \n21 Aligarh Muslim University, Aligarh, U.P. - 202 002. \n22 Babasaheb Bhimrao Ambedkar University, Vidya Vihar, Rae Bareily Road, Lucknow, U.P. – 226 \n025. \n23 Banaras Hindu University, Varanasi, U.P. - 221 005. \n24 University of Allahabad, Allahabad, U.P. - 211 002. \n25 Visva Bharati, Shantiniketan, West Bengal – 731 235. \n26 Hemwati Nandan Bahuguna Garhwal University, Srinagar, Garhwal – 246 174. \n27 Central University of Tamil Nadu, C/O. Collectorate Annexe, Tiruvarur, Tamil Nadu - 610 001. \n28 Central University of Rajasthan, NH-8, Bandar Sindri, Dist-Ajmer – 305 801, Rajasthan. \n29 Central University of Punjab, City Campus, Mansa Road, Bathinda – 151 001, Punjab. \n30 Central University of Odisha, Central Silk Board Building, Landiguda, Koraput – 764 020. \n31 Central University of Kerala, BKM Towers, Nayanmar Moola, Vidyanagar P.O., Kasaragod – 671 \n123. \n32 Central University of Karnataka, Kadaganchi, Aland Road, Aland Taluk, Gulbarga – 585 311, \nKarnataka. \n33 Central University of Jharkhand, Ratu-Lohardaga Road, Brambe, Ranchi – 835 205, Jharkhand. \n34 Central University of Kashmir, Transit Campus : Sonwar, Near GB Pant Hospital, Srinagar – 190 \n005 (J&K). \n35 Central University of Jammu, 8/8 Trikuta Nagar, Jammu – 180 012 (J&K) \n36 Central University of Himachal Pradesh, PO Box No. 21, Dharamash"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |210", "t": "Tribal Carnival", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n (MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*210 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.05.2016 \n \nTRIBAL CARNIVAL \n*210. SHRI MANOJ TIWARI:  \n Will the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)   whetheradequate steps have been taken to preserve, protect and promote ethnic culture of \nthe tribals, if so, the details, thereof; \n(b)  the details of various Centrally Sponsored schemes under implementation for the purpose;  \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to organize National Tribal Carnival in the near future; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof along with the aims and objectives of organizing such carnivals; and \n(e) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government for showcasing and promotion of \nvarious facets of tribal culture on a large scale in the country? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI JUAL ORAM) \n \n(a) to (e) A Statementis laid on the Table of the House \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (e) of the LokSabha  Starred Question No. \n*210 asked by SHRI MANOJ TIWARI to be answered on 09.05.2016  \n(a) &  (b) :Under Central Sector  scheme of “ Grant-in Aid to Tribal  Research Institutes \n(TRIs)”, Ministry of Tribal Affairs  extends financial assistance to TRIs for protection and \npromotion of traditional culture and languages of Scheduled Tribes . Functions of  TRIs, inter -\nalia, include organizing tribal festivals  for promotion of tribal culture. Ministry has also been \norganizing tribal festivals viz. ‘Prakriti’ and ‘Vanaj’ in past years, to showcase the tribal culture. \nTribal guests also take partin Republic Day celebrations so as topromote tribal culture and spread \nawareness about the same. \n“Development of Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups ” (PVTGs) scheme aims  at \nplanning socio-economic development in a comprehensive manner while retaining culture and \nheritage of PVTGs.Further, Tribal Cooperative Marketing Development Federation of India Ltd. \n(TRIFED) organizes exhibitions like ‘Aadichitra’ and ‘Aadishilp’where tribals showcase their \nrich tribal heritage of art and crafts . TRIFED also participates in  OCTAVE – a dance festival of \nNorth Eastern Region.  \n \n(c) & (d):Yes, Madam. Tribal Carnival aims to show-case tribal ways of life including their \nculture, tradition and customs. U nderlying idea is to preserve and promote traditional culture \nthrough display of various facets of tribal life relating to culture, tradition, customs and skills. \nThis also aims to instill a sense of pride amongst tribals for their rich cultur e heritage.  Tribal \nartists and artisans get national level stage for exhibiting their talent. Thisalso facilitatesvisitorsto \nmake themselves aware about unique and diverse traditional culture of  tribal population in real \nperspective.  \n \n(e):Besides, Ministry of Culture has  also been releasing funds under Tribal Sub -Plan to various \ninstitutions of Central Government for protection and promotion of traditional culture and \nlanguages of Scheduled Tribes. The intervention includes activities of Zonal Cultu re Centres, \nAnthropological Survey of India (Kolkata), Indi ra Gandhi RashtriyaManavSangrahalaya \n(Bhopal), Indira Gandhi National Centre for the Arts, Central Institute of Buddhist Studies (Leh), \nLalit Kala Akademi etc. The State level Tribal Festival s are also specifically supported  for \nshowcasing and promotion of various facets of tribal culture. \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |213", "t": "Employment Generation Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.213 \nANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nHIMALAYAN TOURIST CIRCUIT \n \n213. SHRI AJAY TAMTA:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to form a Himal ayan Tourist \ncircuit for development of tourism in Himalayan States in the \ncountry;  \n(b)  if so, the locations proposed to be connected under this circuit, \nState-wise;  \n(c)  whether the Jageshwar Dham situated at Almora and the Mahakali \ntemple situated at Gangolihat in Pithoragarh in Uttarakhand are also \nproposed to be developed as world class tourist destinations;  \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to give priority to connect the \nsaid religious places with the tourist circuits in India to bring them at \nworld-level; and  \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a) to (e): The Ministry of Tourism has launched the Swadesh Darshan \nScheme in 2014-15 with a vision to develop theme based t ourist circuits in \nthe country on the principles of high tourist value, competitiveness and \nsustainability in an integrated manner by synergizing efforts to focus on \nneeds and concerns of all stakeholders to enrich tourist experience and \nenhance employment opportunities.  \n \nHimalayan Circuit is one of the circuits identified for development \nunder this scheme.  Himalayan Circuit includes the States of Jammu and \nKashmir, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand and North Eastern Regions.  \n \nMinistry of Tourism provides Ce ntral Financial Assistance for \nprojects identified in consultation with the State Governments/UTs for \ndevelopment of tourist infrastructure under this scheme. The projects are \nsanctioned subject to availability of funds, liquidation of pending \nutilization certificates, submission of suitable Detailed Project Report \n(DPR) and adherence to the scheme guidelines. \n********* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |214", "t": "Hike in H1B Visa Fee", "head": " GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 214 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nWomen in Higher Education \n214. SHRIMATI VANAROJA R.: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has any  proposal to give additional time to women and \ndifferently abled persons to complete their M.Phil and Ph.D programme; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether it is true that many women are getting enrolled in higher education but  not \nmany are seen in faculty of colleges and universities; \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the remedial measures being adopted by the \nGovernment in this regard; and \n(e) whether the female students would be given maternity leave of 240 days which would \nbe excluded from their total time of  studies and if so, the details thereof and the action \ntaken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a): Yes, Madam.  \n(b):  The University Grants Commission (UGC) has approved that women candidates  \nand persons with disability (more than 40% disability) may be allowed a relaxation of one \nyear for M.Phil. and two years for Ph.D in the maximum duration of these courses.  \n(c) to (e): The All India Survey on Higher Education (AISHE) provisional report for the \nyear 2014-15 has estimated t he total number of teachers to be 14,18,389 out of which \n39% are women.  This report has estimated that during 2014- 15 women students \nconstituted 39% of the total enrolment in Ph.D programme in higher education institutions \nin the country.  \nA Ph.D degree is one of the minimum qualifications for recruitment to the post of \nAssistant Professor in Universities and Colleges. The UGC has initiated several \nmeasures to promote enrolment of women in Ph .D courses . The measures include \namendments in the Ph.D Regulations by relaxing the maximum duration for completion \nof Ph .D or MPhil.  by women candidates for  a further two years  and one year, \nrespectively; allowing transfer of research data in case of relocation of an M.Phil/Ph.D \nwoman scholar due to marr iage or otherwise; and allowing women candidates maternity \n\nleave/child-care leave once in the entire duration of M.Phil./Ph.D for up to 240 days.  \nFurther, t he UGC is implementing p ost-doctoral fellowship for women c andidates (un-\nemployed); Swami Vivekananda single girl c hild scholarship for research in s ocial \nsciences  and PG Indira Gandhi Scholarship for single girl child for postgraduate courses \nto promote the enrolment of women at post- doctoral, doctoral and its feeder levels.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |215", "t": "Target of Crude Oil/Gas Production", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 215 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nRanking of Universities \n \n215. SHRI RAOSAHEB DANVE PATIL: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is true that Indian Universities/Colleges have been successful in \nfinding place in the top twenty list of leading small universities;  \n(b) if so,  the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is taking any steps to develop other small \nuniversities/colleges to make them competitive an international level; and \n(d) if so, the details and the outcome thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n(a) & (b):  The Times Higher Education rankings has brought out the World Small \nUniversity Rankings in 2016 naming therein Indian Institute of Technology, \nGuwahati (ranked 14) and Savitribai Phule Pune University (ranked 18) in the top \n20.  \n(c) & (d): With a view to improving the competitiveness of the higher educational \ninstitutions, the National Institutional Ranking Framework (NIRF)  has been \nlaunched on September 29, 2015. The NIRF rankings are based on clearly verifiable \nobjective criteria covering five broad para meters. The NIRF is expected to \nbenchmark good performing institutions and would help others to understand \ntheir areas of strength and weakness and to take corrective action as necessary. \nThe first India Rankings 2016 have been announced on 04.04.2016 and are \navailable at https://www.nirfindia.org/Home . \n \n********* \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |216", "t": "Adarsh Smarak Yojana", "head": "1\n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.216\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 9.5.2016\n \n \nVAISAKHA 19, 1938 (SAKA)\n \n \n \nADARSH SMARAK YOJANA \n \n \n \n*216.\n \nSHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN: \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether t\nhe Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) proposes to implement a \nproject ‘Adarsh Smarak’; \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof including the number of monuments identified by the \nASI to implement the said project, State/UT\n-\nwise; \n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government is \nplanning to upgrade all the monuments managed \nby  ASI to the standards of the Adarsh Smarak in a phased manner; and \n \n(d)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the time by which these monuments are likely to \nbe upgraded?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE, CULTURE AND\n \nTOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) AND \nMINISTER OF STATE, CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n \n(a)to(d) \n \nA statement is laid on the table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n2\n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERR\nED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d\n) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 216 FOR 9\n.5.2016\n \n \n \n(a)\n \n‘Adarsh Smarak’ scheme has already been implemented.\n \n \n(b)\n \nThe details of 25 centrally protected monuments identified and included in \nthe list of ‘Adarsh Smarak’ in the country (State/UT\n-\nwise) is at Annexure. \n \n \n(c)&(d)\n \nNo, Madam.  Besides, b\nasic tourist amenities, Adarsh Monuments are \nprovided with additional facilities like Wi\n-\nFi, cafeteria, interpretation centre, \nbrail signage, modern toilets etc. However, these identified facilities would be \nmonument specific on the basis of actual require\nment and feasibility on case \nto case basis. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n3\n \n \n \n \n \nANNEXURE\n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 216 FOR 9.5.2016\n \n \nDETAILS OF PROTECTED MONUMENTS IDENTIFIED AND INCLUDED IN THE LIST \nOF ADARSH SMARA\nK IN VARIOUS STATES OF THE COUNTRY \n \n \nSL.NO.\n \nNAME OF MONUMENT\n \n \nSTATE\n \n1\n \nRang Ghar, Sibsagar\n \nAssam\n \n2\n \nVaishali\n-\n \nKolhua\n \nBihar\n \n3\n \nHumayun’s Tomb\n \n \nDelhi\n \n4\n \nQutb Complex\n \n5\n \nRed Fort\n \n6\n \nRani\n-\nki\n-\nVav, Patan\n \nGujarat\n \n7\n \nRock\n-\ncut Temples, Masroor\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n8\n \nLeh Palace\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n9\n \nMartand Temple\n \n10\n \nHampi, Bellary\n \nKarnataka\n \n11\n \nPattadakal Group of Monuments \n \n12\n \nElephanta Caves, Mumbai \n \nMaharashtra\n \n13\n \nDaultabad Fort\n \n14\n \nMandu \n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n15\n \nKhajuraho\n \n16\n \nKonark Temple\n \nOdisha\n \n17\n \nKumbhalgarh F\nort, Kelwada\n \nRajasthan\n \n18\n \nShore Temple Mahabalipuram\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n19\n \nBrihadeshwar Temple, Thanjavur\n \n20\n \nTaj Mahal, Agra\n \n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n21\n \nGroup of Monuments at Fatehpur Sikri, Agra\n \n22\n \nSravasti\n \n23\n \nSarnath, Varanasi\n \n24\n \nJageshwar Group of Temples\n \nUtta\nrakhand\n \n25\n \nHazardwari Palace, Murshidabad\n \nWest Bengal\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |217", "t": "Safety Measures on GAIL Pipelines", "head": "������������������� �\n��������������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n�\n�������������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n�\n�������������������������� �\n�\n������������������������ �\n���������������������������