��������� �� �������� ���� ������������� ����\n������������������������������������������ �\n��������� �� ���� �� ������� � ������ ��� ������������ � � ������� ���������\n����������� ����� ��� ����������� �� ���������� ������� ������� �������� ����\n�������������������������������� �\n�\n������ �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |22", "t": "Violence in J&K", "head": " GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 22 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nE-GOVERNANCE AWARD TO EPFO \n \n22. SHRI K. ASHOK KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Employees Provident Fund Organisation (EPFO) has \nbagged the national award on e-Governance for the year 2015-16 for \nits Universal Account; and \n(b)if so, the details thereof? \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a) & (b):     Yes, Madam. Employees’ Provident Fund Organisation has \nbeen given Gold Award under category “Cat -III – Innovative use of \nTechnology in e -Governance” in the National Award on e -Governance \n2015-2016 for its Universal Account Number (UAN) Programme.  The \naward was presented during the 19 th National Conference on               \ne-Governance held at Nagpur, Maharashtra on 21st -22nd January 2016. \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |220", "t": "Acquisition of Assets Abroad by ONGC/OVL", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 220 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON  25.04.2016  \n \nCut in Scholarships \n  \n220.  SHRI HARISH MEENA:    \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether it is a fact that Government has made a drastic cut in amount given to States \nfor pre-matric, post-matric and merit -cum-means scholarships to Students belonging to \nSCs/STs/OBCs/Minorities; \n(b) if so,  the details thereof along with the reasons therefor; and  \n(c) the details of grants sought by State Governments and sanctioned grants by c entre to \nState in past two years ?  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n(a) to (c) : The Ministries of Social Justice and Empowerment,  Tribal Affairs and Mino rity \nAffairs are administering Pre- matric, Post -matric and Merit -cum-Means scholarship \nschemes for students belonging to SCs/STs/OBCs/Minorities.  The Department of School \nEducation and Literacy is administering two scholarship schemes namely National Means -\ncum-Merit Scholarship Scheme and National Scheme of Incentive to Girls for Secondary \nEducation for students belonging to economically weaker sections of the society. The \nMinistry/ Department-wise details are as follows:  \nDepartment of Social Justice & Empowerment :  \nThe Central Assistance under the Centrally Sponsored Post-Matric Scholarship Scheme for \nSC students during last 2 years has  been released as per the no tional allocation made to \nthe States each year . Central Assistance released under this scheme wa s Rs. 196337 .63 \nlakhs during 2014-15 and Rs. 221388.00 lakhs during 2015-16.  \nThe Central Assistance of Pre- matric Scholarship for SC students studying in classes IX & \nX during the last two year s has been released after taking into consideration the unspent \nfunds available with the States /UTs. Central Assistance has also been released on DBT \nmode during the last 2 years based on the details furnished by the States/UTs. Central  \n\n \nAssistance released under this scheme wa s Rs. 51403.34 lakhs during 2014-15  and Rs. \n52470.31 lakhs during 2015-16.  \nMinistry of Tribal Affairs \nFor Post-Matric Scholarships, Rs. 72100.82 lakhs and Rs. 85714.54 lakhs had been \nreleased against the demand by States amounting  to Rs. 177482.14 lakhs and Rs. \n194202.43 lakhs in 2014-15 and 2015-16 respectively.  \nFor Pre -Matric Scholarships, Rs. 20070.87 lakhs and Rs. 22868.95 lakhs had been \nreleased against the demand by States amounting to Rs. 36447.61 lakhs and Rs.45288.61 \nlakhs in 2014-15 and 2015-16 respectively.  \nMinistry of Minority Affairs \nGovernment has not made any cut in amount for scholarship under Pre- Matric, Post-Matric \nand Merit -Cum-Means scholarship schemes o f this Ministry. Th e funds for s cholarship \nunder Pre-Matric for the year 2014- 15 was given to all State Governments for release of \nscholarship fund amount to the eligible students.  A total fund of Rs.1129.27 Crore was \nreleased to State Government s during 2014 -15 and the fun d for Pre -Matric Scholarship \nScheme is being released directly to the students bank accounts through DBT  w. e. f. \n2015-16, whereas Post -Matric and Merit -Cum-Means scholarship are being distributed \nthrough DBT w.e.f  2014-15. \nDepartment of School Education and Literacy   \nUnder Centrally Sponsored National Means -cum-Merit Scholarship Scheme, Rs. 65.20 \ncrore and Rs. 127.13  crore had been sanctioned for scholarships against the requirement \nof funds  amounting to Rs. 159.82  crore and Rs. 109.38 crore in 2014- 15 and 2015- 16 \nrespectively. The funds released in 2015- 16 also included release of funds for pending \nscholarships received in 2014-15. The scholarships are sanctioned as per the availability of \nthe budget. The scholarship amount is transferred directly into the students bank accounts \nand scholarship funds are not released to the State Governments. \nUnder Centrally Sponsored National Scheme of Incentive to Girls for Secondary Education, \nRs. 94.23 crore and Rs. 153.54 crore h ad been sanctioned as  incentive amount for girl \nstudents against the requirement of funds amounting to Rs. 169.19 crore and Rs. 288.29 \ncrore in 2014- 15 and 2015- 16 respectively . The scholarships are sanctioned as per  the \navailability of the budget. The incentive amount is transferred directly into the students bank \naccounts and funds are not released to the State Governments. \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |221", "t": "Livestock Insurance Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†221 \nANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nSPECIAL TOURIST SECURITY FORCE \n \n†221. SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Governm ent is adopting any special measures for \nthe safety of Historical sites and idols in temples at tourist \nplaces;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to use technical \napplications and constitute a special tourist place sec urity \nforce for the protection of such tourist places;  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e)  the action taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a) to (e): The primary r esponsibility, including the safety of \nHistorical sites and idols, is in the domain of the respective \nState/UT Government.  However, the Government of India provides \nCentral Financial Assistance to the States and UTs for the purpose.  \nFurther, a committee of Ministry of Culture has recommended a \nSpecial Force along with the help of tools and electronic gadgets for \nprotection of Monuments and Art Installations as part of a \ncomprehensive security policy. \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |222", "t": "Rise in Prices of Essential Commodities", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *222 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 10.05.2016 \n \nRISE IN PRICES OF ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES \n \n*222.  SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI:  \nSHRI B.S. YEDIYURAPPA:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether prices of essential commodities including those monitored by Price Monitoring \nCell continued to rise over a period of time despite various steps taken by the \nGovernment, if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor;  \n(b)  whether the Government has received all necessary information/logistics on price trends \nduring the last three years, if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon;  \n(c)  the steps taken by the Union Government to check hoarding and ensure compliance with \nvarious rules related to supply of essential commodities and price control by the States, \nif so, the details and the outcome thereof; and  \n(d) whether any assessment has b een made about impact of water shortage on prices and if \nso, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=ky; ea=h  \n¼Jh jke foykl ikloku½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a) to (d) :    A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*  222 FOR 10.05.2016 REGARDING RISE IN PRICES OF ESSENTIAL \nCOMMODITIES. \n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ \n \n(a) :  Prices of most of the essential food items monitored by Price Monitoring Cell (PMC) has \nremained relatively stable or declined over the year. The food items which showed an increase \nin prices include pulses (especially tur, urad and chana), potato and suga r. Increase in prices of \nfood items is mainly on account of shortfall in production owing to adverse weather conditions, \nincreased transportation costs, supply chain constraints like lack of storage facilities and \nartificial shortage created by hoarding and black marketing. The details are at Annnexure I. \n \n(b)  :  Yes sir. The requisite information is given at Annexure II & III.  \n \n(c) : States have been advised to take effective action against hoarders of essential \ncommodities under the Essential Commoditie s Act, 1955 and the Prevention of Black -\nMarketing and Maintenance of Supplies of Essential Commodities Act 1980. States/UTs have \nbeen conducting raids and seized pulses are being disposed, as per the provisions under the EC \nAct, 1955. The Action Taken Report is given at Annexure IV. \n \n(d) : Water shortage adversely affects the sown area and productivity of crops. The resultant \nshortfall in production in turn affects the quantum of market arrivals and thereby prices. \nHowever, no official assessment of direct impact of water shortage on prices has been made. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE-I \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.* 222 FOR 10.05.2016 REGARDING RISE IN PRICES OF ESSENTIAL \nCOMMODITIES. \n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \nALL INDIA AVERAGE RETAIL PRICES (Rs./Kg) \n \nCommodities Prices as on \n(5/5/2016)* \nPrices as on \n(5/5/2015) \nPercent \nchange \nRice 26.92 27.29 -1.36 \nWheat 23.3 22.06 5.62 \nAtta (Wheat) 24.49 24.34 0.62 \nGram Dal 72.33 53.5 35.2 \nTur/Arhar Dal 141.38 87.92 60.81 \nUrad Dal 149.39 86.37 72.97 \nMoong Dal 101.34 100.97 0.37 \nMasoor Dal 83.07 76.33 8.83 \nSugar 38.93 31.85 22.23 \nMilk @ 40.25 38.93 3.39 \nGroundnut Oil (Packed) 128.28 120.9 6.1 \nMustard Oil (Packed) 108.95 99.6 9.39 \nVanaspati (Packed) 73.22 75.55 -3.08 \nSoya Oil (Packed) 82.09 85.32 -3.79 \nSunflower Oil (Packed) 95.14 94.48 0.7 \nPalm Oil (Packed) 69.32 67.58 2.57 \nGur  40.93 40.29 1.59 \nTea Loose 197.67 205.05 -3.6 \nSalt Pack (Iodised) 14.8 14.65 1.02 \nPotato 18.52 13.48 37.39 \nOnion 16.33 22.02 -25.84 \nTomato 22.8 25.08 -9.09 \n \nNR -> Not Reported      * -->    (Iodised) @ -->    (Rs./Ltr.)      *latest available \nSource:-  State civil supplies Department \n***** \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE-II \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. \n*222 FOR 10.5.2016 REGARDING RISE IN PRICES OF ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES. \n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ \nRETAIL AND WHOLESALE PRICES OF ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES FOR LAST THREE \nYEARS. \n \nSl.No. Commodities ALL INDIA AVERAGE RETAIL PRICES \n(Rs./Kg)  \nALL INDIA AVERAGE WHOLESALE \nPRICES (Rs./Qtl)  \n2016-17 2015-16 2014-15 2016-17 2015-16 2014-15 \nas on (5/5/2016)* as on \n(5/5/2015) \nas on \n(5/5/2014) \nas on \n("}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |223", "t": "Fire Safety", "head": " \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.223 \nFOR ANSWER ON 25/04/2016 \n \nSTEEL CONSUMPTION \n \n223. SHRI VENKATESH BABU T.G.: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total quantity of steel produced  and imported for domestic consumption in  \nthe country during each of the last three years; \n \n(b) whether the Government is aware  of the import, sale and storage of cheaper  \nquality of steel not in conformity with  Quality Control Order (QCO), if so, the  details \nthereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government has ta ken any steps to cease the poor quality steel  \nimports and initiated any action against those indulging in such malpractices; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF         (SHRI VISHNU DEO SAI) \nSTEEL AND MINES \n \n(a) Total production & import of steel during the last 3 years is as follows:- \n \nYear Total Finished Steel (million tones) \nProduction of Sale Import \n2013-14 87.67 5.45 \n2014-15 92.16 9.32 \n2015-16* 90.39 11.71 \nSource: JPC: * prov. \n \n(b) After the issue of the quality control orders, import of seconds & defective \nproducts by some unscrupulous importers were reported in respect of some \ncategories of steel products.  However, Government does not maintain data on \nimport, sale and storage of such sub-standard steel products standards-wise. \n \n(c)&(d): Governm ent has notified the following Steel and Steel P roducts ( Quality \nControl) Orders inter-alia to ensure availability of quality steel to end-users. \n \n(i) Steel and Steel Products (Quality Control) Order, 2012. \n(ii) Steel and Steel Products (Quality Control) Second Order, 2012. \n(iii) Steel and Steel Products (Quality Control) Order, 2015. \n \n Further, Government has issued orders to allow import of steel products \nstrictly as per the aforesaid Steel Quality Control Order inter -alia prohibiting import of \nsub-standard steel products not conforming to the relevant Indian Standards. \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |224", "t": "Border Management", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*224 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 10TH MAY, 2016/VAISAKHA 20, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nBORDER MANAGEMENT \n \n*224.  SHRI NINONG ERING: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether adequate measures have  been taken by the Government to \nsecure effective management of Indian borders  particularly in Arunachal \nPradesh and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government has set up/proposes to set up border haats \nalong the Indo-Myanmar and Indo-Bangladesh borders and if so, the details \nthereof, location/State-wise; \n \n(c) whether the border haats are likely  to help in maintaining peace and \ntranquility in the border regions and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) the concrete steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to \ncurb insurgency and trafficking problems as well as to maintain peace and \nsecurity in the border regions including Indo-Myanmar border region? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n****** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n-2- \n \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.*224 \nFOR 10.05.2016. \n \n \n(a) & (b): The Government has adopted a multi-pronged strategy to secure \neffective management of India n borders, curb insurgency as well as to \nmaintain peace and security in the border regions which inter -alia \nincludes, area domination, round the clock surveillance & patrolling on the \nborders, establishment of Border Out Posts (BOPs), construction of border \nroads, upgradation of intelligence set up and close interaction with local \npopulace.   Wherever required and possible on respective borders, fence \nand flood light works are taken up.  Force multip Pliers and High -Tech \nsurveillance equipment to enhance the surveillance of border have also \nbeen introduced.   Further, deployment of technological solutions on Pilot \nbasis has been approved.    Adequate measures are taken to curb human \ntrafficking like issuing advisories to State Gov ernments,  sensitizing law \nenforcement agencies and creating awareness among border population. \n \n(c) & (d):    Four border haats, two in Meghalaya and two in Tripura are \nfunctional at present along Indo-Bangladesh border.   Government of  \n \n…..3/ \n\n \n-3- \nL.S.S.Q.NO.*224 FOR 10.05.2016 \n \nIndia and Bangladesh have agreed to set up six more border haats -two in \nTripura and four in Meghalaya.   There are no border haats at Indo -\nMyanmar border.   The establishment of border haats is expected to \npromote the well being of the people dwelling in areas across the borders \nof the two countries, which in turn will maintain peace and tranquility in \nthe border regions.   \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|STARRED   |225", "t": "Promotion of Official Language", "head": "������������������� �\n�������������������