Oman 4.71 13.20 51.00 68.91 \n50 Panama 0.19 2.54 1.17 3.90 \n51 Philippines 1.41 90.17 7.48 99.07 \n52 Poland 45.89 4.88 0.12 50.89 \n53 Portugal 2.17 4.45 4.81 11.43 \n54 Qatar 1.80 0.50 0.80 3.09 \n55 Romania 1.66 0.75 0.55 2.96 \n56 Russia "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |178", "t": "Projects by Coordinating with Industries", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. †178 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nProjects by Coordinating with Industries \n \n†178. SHRI RAJAN VICHARE: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the University Grants Commission (UGC) has advised all the universities to \nmake projects by coordinating with industries and take their help for improving the \ncurriculum;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the UGC has made any guidelines for the same; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor and the corrective steps \ntaken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (d): Yes, Madam. The University Grants Commission ( UGC) has informed that it \nhas framed the guidelines for the scheme of setting up of University -Industry Inter -\nLinkage (UIL) Centers in universities. The establishment of UIL Centres will help in \nidentifying the expertise available in the university which can be of use for the industries \nin the locality/region by way of consultancy services, evaluation of R esearch & \nDevelopment activities of the industr ies, etc.; taking the help of the expertise of the \nindustries to improve/redesign the curriculum periodically in tune with the requirements \nof the industries;  creating skilled manpower for industry requirements at various levels;  \nconducting Management Development Programmes (MDPs), Entrepreneurship \nDevelopment Programmes (EDPs), etc.;  initiating schemes for student/teacher training, \njoint research;  setting up Science & Technology Entrepreneurs’ Parks (STEPs), \nTechnology Business Incubators (TBIs); etc. \nThe guidelines of the scheme are available at \nhttp://www.ugc.ac.in/pdfnews/ \n0971667_English.pdf . \n  \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |179", "t": "Fee Charged by Institutions", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 179 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nFee Charged by Institutions \n \n†179. SHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has not framed any guidelines/rules with regard \nto the fees being charged by the private technical educational institutes in \nthe country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the action ta ken by the Government to check the exploitation of students \nwith regard to fees in such private technical educational institutes? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b) : The All India Council for Technical Education (AICTE) had constituted a \nCommittee under the Chairmanship of Justice Srikrishna (former Judge of Hon’ble \nSupreme Court) on 07.05.2014 to recommend the f ee to be charged by the private \ntechnical educational institutes in the country. The Committee submitted its report \non 07.04.2015. \nThe AICTE Council approved the recommendations of the \nCommittee in its Meeting held on 11.12.2015 and letters were sent to all State \nGovts. for implementation of the report vide AICTE letter No.3 -16/PC / Fee \nCom./2014 dated 11.02.2016. The Committee recommended the maximum tuition \nand development fee to be charged by the institutions. \n(c):  As per the Approval Process Handbook of  AICTE , any institution reported to \nbe charging capitation fee, other than the tuition and d evelopment fees is liable for \npunitive action  which includes withdrawal of approval. \n \n********* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |18", "t": "Girls Hostels in Uttar Pradesh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \n  LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 18 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nGirls Hostels in Uttar Pradesh \n \n \n†18. DR. SWAMI SAKSHIJI MAHARAJ : \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n (a) the details of  updated status of the amended proposal with regard to the technical change in \nthe construction of girls hostels in Uttar Pradesh; \n(b) whether the Government has released its share of funds for the said proposal; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n \n(a) Government of India had approved construction of  191 girls hostels till 2015-16 in the \nState of Uttar Pradesh.  As construction of 87 girls hostels  could not be started, the \nState Government has submitted a proposal for surrender of these 87 girls hostels  and \nfor fresh approval at cur rent State Schedule of Rates(SSo R) in its Annual Work Plan   \n& Budget proposal for the year 2016-17.  \n \n(b)&(c)  The Government of India has released Rs.  86.39 crore as first installment for \nconstruction of girls hostels in the years 2012-13 and 2013-14.   \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |180", "t": "Trade with Developing Nations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.180 (H) \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25th APRIL, 2016 \n \nTrade with Developing Nations \n \n180 (H).  SHRI ALOK SANJAR: \n \n      Will the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY   (वािणÏय एवं उɮयोग मंत्री )  \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the total quantity and value of products imported into and exported from the country \nduring the last three years, item‐wise and country‐wise; \n(b) the total decline  in trade  with the  developing nations during the said  period  and  the \nnames of the countries with which India is likely to achieve its trade targets;  \n(c) whether  the  Government  has  conducted  any  study  to  explore  the  possibilities  to \nincrease export to these countries; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n        वािणÏ य एवं उɮयोग राÏ य मंत्री )Įीमती िनमर्ला सीतारमण (( èवतंत्र प्रभार ) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n (SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN)  \n \n(a) The commodity-wise and country-wise data on quantum and value o f exports and \nimports of the country for the last three years  i s  available in the CDs of DGCI&S \npublication ‘Monthly Statistics of  Foreign Trade of India’ Vol.  1 (Exports) and Vol.II \n(Imports) for March 2014, March 2015 and January 2016 (latest a vailable CD). These \nCDs are also regularly sent to Parliament Library by DGCI&S, Ko lkata for reference.  \nThe data in this regard for certain key commodities are as under:  \n \n(A) Exports   \n(VALUE in US $ Million) \nCommodity \n   2013‐14  2014‐15 \n2015‐16(Apr‐Feb) \n(Provisional) \nUnit  QTY  VAL  QTY  VAL  QTY  VAL \nTEA  KGS  249907559  798.79  215407703  681.79  227271538  668.44 \nCOFFEE  KGS  253902132  798.83  221396663  814.02  219164997  685.97 \nRICE ‐BASMOTI  TON  3754102  4864.89  3698927  4516.28  3718893  3221.54 \nRICE(OTHER THAN \nBASMOTI)  TON  7148472  2925.16  8278233  3336.84  5715937  2081.72 \nWHEAT  TON  5572025  1569.08  2924053  828.75  575947  140.54 \nOTHER CEREALS  TON  4637536  1204.16  3514351  869.11  871197  231.18 \n\nPULSES  TON  345553  290.28  222139  199.86  217838  213.60 \nTOBACCO \nUNMANUFACTURED  KGS  237106036  789.07  219572233  680.01  190424631  587.59 \nSPICES  KGS  896565395  2497.30  926020950  2430.35  731327027  2249.06 \nCASHEW  TON 