6  2.28  2.66  \nMCF:Mangalore 1.18  1.41  1.10  \nTCL:Haldia 2.41  0.83  0.57  \nGSFC:Si"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |309", "t": "Plastic Waste", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 309  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.04.2016 \n \nPlastic Waste \n \n309.  SHRI. RAHUL SHEWALE:  \n SHRI DHARMENDRA YADAV:  \n DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE : \n SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FO RESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  the estimated quantum of  plastic bags manufactured in the country annually;  \n(b) the quantum of plastic waste generated as a re sult thereof annually during each of the last \n three years;  \n(c)  whether the Government has recently notifie d more stringent norms for the use of plastic \n and plastic carry ba gs and if so, the details thereof;  \n(d)  the names of States where plastic bags are banned and still being used; and  \n(e)  the steps taken/proposed to  be taken by the Government to tackle the environmental \n damage caused by plastic waste?  \n  \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDE PENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVI RONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) & (b) The quantum of plastic bags manufactured in the countr y has not been estimated.   \nCentral Pollution Control Board (CPCB) has estimated the quantum  of plastic waste generation \nin the country as5.6 metric tons per annum.  \n \n(c)  Ministry of Environment,  Forest and Climate Change has comprehensively revised the \nPlastic Waste Mana gement Ruleson 18\nth March, 2016 in supersessi on of the Plastic Waste \n(Management and Handling) Rules, 2011. The salient  features of the revi sed Rules in clude the \nexpansion of the applicability area to include the entire country; theminimum permissible \nthickness of plastic carry ba gs increased from 40 to  50 mi crons; minimum thickness of 50 \nmicron imposed for  plastic shee ts;introduction of Extended Pr oducers Responsibility (EPR) for \nthe brands packaging thei r products in plastics; use of waste plastic in co-generation of energy \nand road construction; plastic waste minimizati on; source segregation&  recycling involving \nwaste pickers,  recyclers & waste processors etc. \n\n(d) The use of plastic carry bags has been comp letely banned in 14 States / Union Territories \nnamely; Chandigarh, Chhattisga rh, Delhi, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, Jammu& Kashmir, \nKarnataka, Andaman & Nicoba r Islands, Lakshadweep, Nagala nd, Rajasthan, Sikkim, Tripura \nand Uttar Pradesh. Use of plasti c carry bags has been partiall y banned in some pilgrimage \ncentres, tourist and historical places located in the States of Andhra Pradesh, Arunachal Pradesh, \nAssam, Goa, Gujarat, Karnataka, Odisha, Tamil Nadu, West Bengal, Uttar Pradesh and \nUttarakhand.  \n \n(e) The provisions of Plastic Waste Manageme nt Rules, 2016 aim at  environmentally sound \nmanagement of plastic waste.   \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |31", "t": "Petrol Pumps having CNG Facility", "head": "LOK SABHA  \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 31  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.4.2016 \nPETROL PUMPS HAVING CNG FACILITY  \n \n31.  SHRI MANOJ TIWARI: \n      \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkd`frd xSl ea=h \n \n(a) whether all the petrol pumps in Delhi have CNG refilling facility and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) the total percentage of petrol pumps in Delhi having CNG refilling facility; and \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to make CNG refilling facility available at all petrol pumps \nacross Delhi and if so, the details thereof and action taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nपेट्रोलियम और प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्रािय में राज् य मंत्ररी  स्‍  िंत्र प्राार  \n(श्ररी  धमेन्‍द र प्रधा      \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nPETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) & (b): No, Madam. All the petrol pumps in Delhi do not have Compressed Natural Gas \n(CNG) refueling facilities. A s on 31.3.2016, 134 petrol pumps in Delhi are having CNG \nfacilities.  This makes about 34% of the petrol pumps of Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) \nwhich are having CNG facilities in Delhi.    \n \n(c): CNG refilling facility is being proposed in technically feasible petrol pumps. In order to \nassess the technical feasibility, around 200 petrol pumps of OMCs in Delhi have been surveyed \nby Indraprastha Gas Limited, the company supplying CNG in Delhi.  Out of these, 59 petrol \npumps have been identified to be feasible for CNG facilities. CNG facilities have already been \nset up at 42 such petrol pumps in Delhi.     \n \n***** \n \n \n\n b) : Yes, Madam. There has been a gap between the demand and supply of pulses in the \ncountry during the last year and the current year as the domestic demand for pulses is higher than \nthe domestic production, as indicated below:- \n                                                                                            (in million tonnes) \nYear 2014-15 2015-16 \nProduction 17.15 17.33 \nImports  4.58   5.79 \nExports  0.22   0.26 \nTotal Availability  21.51 22.86 \nDemand  22.68   23.66 \nGap/Shortage -1.17 -0.80 \n \nSources:-  Niti Ayog, D/o Commerce (DOC) and D/o Agriculture, Cooperation & \nFarmers Welfare  (DAC&FW) \n                               \n            The shortfall in domestic production has been due to adverse weather conditions and \nthe rise in demand on account of rise in population, rise in per capita income and change in \nfood habits among others. B lack-marketing/hoarding has also put pre ssure on prices of \npulses. The details of State -wise corrective steps taken against black -marketing/hoarding in \nthe country during the said period are at Annexure-I.  \n                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \n(c) : The details are at Annexure-II. \n \n(d) : Yes, Madam. In order to check the rise in pri ces of pulses as well as protect the \ninterest of farmers, Government has been implementing Price Support Scheme (PSS). The \nMinimum Support Price (MSP) of pulses has also been increased to encourage farmers to \ngrow more pulses. Further, a buffer stock of pu lses has been built with support from Price \nStabilisation Fund (PSF) both through domestic procurement directly from farmers as well as \nimports. All these initiatives will increase availability of pulses, thereby stabilising their \nprices.  \n***** \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.* 31 FOR 26.04.2016 REGARDING SHORTAGE OF PULSES. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \nA)     ACTION TAKEN UNDER THE ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES ACT, 1955 DURING 2015 (Relating to offences under EC Act- for other than violation of stock \ncontrol orders/ for violation of stock control orders) \n \nSl. No. STATES/UTs No. of Raids \nConducted \nNo. of Persons Value of goods Confiscated \n(Rs. In Lakhs) Arrested Prosecuted Convicted \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 \n1 ANDHRA   PRADESH 1300 0 23 23   1056.51 \n2 ARUNACHAL  PRADESH 3 1 - - 0.01 \n3 ASSAM NR NR NR NR NR \n4 BIHAR 299 NIL NIL NIL NR \n5 CHHATTISGARH 673 0 0 0 42.28 \n6 DELHI 24 2 NIL NIL NIL \n7 GOA 96 NIL NIL NIL NIL \n8 GUJARAT 9098 25 16 - 112.31 \n9 HARYANA 3229 123 138 - 120.38 \n10 HIMACHAL PRADESH 39314 - - - 31.69 \n11 JAMMU & KASHMIR NR NR NR NR NR \n12 JHARKHAND 140 NR NR NR NR \n13 KARNATAKA 4529 199 0 6 16.71 \n14 KERALA 8465 26 12 1 64.00 \n15 MADHYA PRADESH 38648 - 111 6 92.46 \n16 MAHARASHTRA 6302 777 277 2 4669.66 \n17 MANIPUR NIL NIL NIL NIL NIL \n18 MEGHALAYA 41 NIL NIL NIL NIL \n19 MIZORAM 141 - - - - \n20 NAGALAND NIL NIL NIL NIL NIL \n21 ODISHA 2291 NIL NIL NIL 0.02 \n22 PUNJAB 10443 - - - - \n23 RAJASTHAN 840 NR NR NR NIL \n24 SIKKIM 71 - - - NIL \n25 TAMILNADU "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |310", "t": "PPP in Sports", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.310 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26-04-2016 \nPPP in Sports \n310. SHRI P. KUMAR: \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether  the Government has prepared any policy for fostering Public - Private \nPartnership (PPP) in the field of sports and in the preparation of sports infrastructure in \nthe country; \n(b) the details of the steps taken by the Government to attract rural youths and providing \nthem adequate sports facilities for training and sustenance; and \n(c) whether the Government has explored the possibilities of allowing sponsorship of \nplayers and sports grounds, etc., by the PSUs and private business houses particularly \nin rural areas and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n(a) to (c): No specific policy for Public-Private Partnership (PPP) in the field of sports has \nbeen prepared. However, the Government has taken following steps for encouraging the \nprivate sector in promotion and development of sports in the country:  \n(i)  setting up National Sports Development Fund,  \n(ii)  including sports in the list of permissible activities for deployment of Corporate \nSocial Responsibility (CSR) funds, and \n(iii)  instituting Rashtriya Khel Protsahan Puruskar  for recognizing contributions of \ncorporate entities.  \n National Sports Development Fund was set up by the Central Government in 1998, \nunder Charitable Endowments Act 1890, with a view to mobilizing resources from the \nGovernment as well as non-governmental sources, including the private/corporate sector \nand non-resident Indians, for promotion of sports and games in the country.  \nThe Ministry of Youth Affairs & Sports instituted Rashtriya Khel Protsahan Puruskar \nin 2009 with the objective of encouraging and promoting involvement of corporates, \nvoluntary organizations, sports control boards etc., in the promotion and development of \nsports in the country.  \n ‘Training to promote rural sports, nationally recognized sports, Paralympic sports \nand Olympic sports’ has been included in Schedule VII of the Companies Act, 2013, which \nwould allow companies to spend on development of sports from the funds earmarked for \nCorporate Social Responsibility (CSR)  - related activities. Inclusion of sports in this \nSchedule is expected to give a boost to the promotion and development of sports in the \ncountry and the much needed funds from companies, both in public and private sector, \nare expected to flow into the sports sector. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |311", "t": "Inclusion in SC List", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE & EMPOWERMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †311\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.04.2016\n†311. INCLUSION IN SC LIST\nSHRI MUKESH RAJPUT:\nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT  be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has received any proposal from Uttar Pradesh to include Nishad -\nLodhi community and its sub -caste Kewat, Manjhi, Mallah, Watham, Kumhar, Dheemar,\nLodhi, Lodha etc., in the List of Scheduled Castes;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the present status of the said proposals; and\n(c) the time by which the above proposal is likely to be implemented by the Government?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI VIJAY SAMPLA)\n(a) to (c): Government of Uttar Pradesh vide their letter dated 15.02.2013 had recommended\ninclusion of Kahar, Kashyap, Kewat, Mallah, Nishad, Dheever, Bind, Dheemar (not\nDheemaar), Batham (notWatham), Turha, Godiya, Manjhi, Machhua, Bhar, Rajbhar, Kumhar\nand Prajapati communities in the list of Scheduled Castes (SCs). The proposal was examined,\nas per approved Modalities, in consultation with the Registrar General of India (RGI). As the\nRGI had not supported the proposal even on a second reference, the sam e has been rejected,\nas per approved Modalities, with the approval of competent authority. There is no\nrecommendation from Government of Uttar Pradesh for inclusion of Lodhi, Lodh a\ncommunities in the list of SCs\n****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |312", "t": "Revival of HOCL", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF CHEMICALS & PETROCHEMICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 385 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.04.2016 \n \nPlug and Play System in Chemical Industry \n \n385:   SHRIMATI KOTHAPALLI GEETHA. \n \n Will the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:- \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to implement ‘plug and play’ system by \ngiving all required sanctions to set up chemical industries so as to encourage \ngrowth of this industry in the country; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the steps being taken in this regard and if not, \nthe time by which such a system will be implemented in the country? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n \n(a)  to (b)  The concept of ‘plug and play’ model is visualized as a part of creation \nof Infrastructure facilities under Draft ‘National Chemical Policy’  for the growth and \ndevelopment of chemical industry. The Draft ‘National Chemical Policy’ is still under \nexamination by the concerned Ministries/ Departments. ‘Plug and Play’ system will \nalso require concurrence by the concerned state government.  \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |313", "t": "Setting up of Old Age Homes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE & EMPOWERMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †313\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.04.2016\n†313. SETTING UP OF OLD AGE HOMES\nSHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN:\nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to set up an Old Age Home in every district in\nthe country;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the steps being taken in this regard?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT\n(SHRI VIJAY SAMPLA)\n(a) to (c): Section 19 of the Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens\nAct(MWPSC Act), 2007 mandates that State Governments may establish and maintain such\nnumber of Old Age Homes at accessible places, as it may deem necessary, in a phased\nmanner, beginning with at least one in each district to accommodate in such homes a\nminimum of one hundred fifty senior citizens who are indigent. This Ministry has been\nproviding financial assistance to Non Governmental/ Voluntary Organizations, Panchayati\nRaj Institutional etc. for running and maintenance of Old Age Home for indigent senior\ncitizens, under the Central Sector Scheme of Integrated Programme of Older Persons(IPOP).\n****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |314", "t": "Damage to Stocks", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 314  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nDAMAGE TO STOCKS \n \n314.  SHRI PINAKI MISRA: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken note of all those FCI godowns \nwhere the damage of food stock has been reported repeatedly, if so, the \ndetails thereof and the reasons for these losses along with the list  of such \ngodowns where the loss/damage to  food stock has been reported  \nrepeatedly during the last three years, State-wise; \n \n(b) whether any manager/staff of said godowns has been held responsible \nfor the losses; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the action taken against such officers?\n \n \n \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n \n \n(a) (b) & (c) Yes Madam. The quantities of damaged foodgrains are small. \nWherever, any negligence is observed on the part of any officer/official, \nappropriate disciplinary action is taken against them. Region- wise, \ngodown wise details of damaged foodgrain reported repeatedly durin g \nlast three years alongwith the reasons and action taken against the staff \nis at the annex. \n \n**** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnex  \nANNEX REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (c) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO 314 TO BE ANSWERED ON 26th APRIL, 2016 IN LOK SABHA. \n \nRegion-wise, godown wise details of damaged foodgrain reported repeatedly during last three years alongwith the reasons and action taken against the staff \nRegion FCI Depot Commodity  \n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nManagers/staff responsible and action taken \nQty.  Reason for \ndamage Qty Reason for \ndamage Qty \nReason \nfor \ndamage \nBihar FSD Saharsha \nWheat 82.400 \nRail head \ndamage \ndue to Rain \n3.165 Quality \ncomplaint 21.437 Rain For railhead damage action was taken \nagainst Handling and Transport  contractor \nFor damage due to rain/water leakage \nwhich affected the bottom layers bags no \nofficial was found responsible. For damage \non account of quality complaint, as per FCI's \nQuality Complaint instructions in vogue, \nclaim of Rs. 61 ,338/- have been preferred \nwith Consignor depot and claims settled  \nWheat 38.420 \nCover and \nPlinth \n(CAP) \ndamage \ndue to rain \n----   ----   \nWheat 38.100 Water \nleakage ----   ----   \nRice ---- - ----   20.596 Rain \nKarnataka FSD Mysore Rice 7.898 Transit \nDamage 1.088 Transit \ndamage 2.628 transit \ndamage \nFor damage occurred during rail transit, \nresponsibility cannot be fixed on any official, \nClaims has been lodged with Railways. \nAssam \nFSD \nChinnamara, \nJorhat \nRice 8.850 Transit \nDamage 35.417 Storage \nDamage 21.334 Storage \nDamage \nDisciplinary action against three Manager \n(QC) and one Depot In charge  under major \npenalty have been initiated for storage \ndamage. For damages occurred during rail \ntransit, responsibility cannot be fixed on any \nofficial.Claims have been lodged with  \nRailways. \nFSD New \nGuwahati Rice 37.151 \nRain, \nTransit \ndamage, \nstorage \ndamage \n38.845 \nStorage \nDamage, \nTransit \ndamage \n6.422 Transit \ndamage \nDuring 2013-14 and 2014 -15, storage \ndamage occurred due to water \nlogging/flood, being natural calamities no \nresponsibility could be fixed on any official. \nFor rail transit, damage occurred during las t \nthree years.  Claims have been lodged with \nRailways. \nNagaland FSD,Dimapur \nWheat 2.820 \nStorage \ndamage \n---- \nTransit & \nStorage \ndamage \n---- \nTransit & \nStorage \ndamage \nDue to seepage and longer storage, bottom \nlayer got damage for which no action is \nwarranted. For rail transit, damage occurred \nduring las t three years . Claims have been \nlodged with Railways. Rice 10.920 23.000 10.6 \n• All quantities are in Metric tons \n**** \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |315", "t": "Cotton Production", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 315 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 26TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nCOTTON PRODUCTION \n \n315.    SHRI C.R. PATIL:  \n           SHRIMATI RAKSHATAI KHADSE:  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   कृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  whether the acreage and production of cotton has increased in the country, if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the funds allocated for promotion of cotton cultivation and production \nin the country during the last three years and the current year, State-wise;  \n \n(b)  whether the Government has provided any technological, financial, institutional \nassistance to the Central Institute for Cotton Research, Nagpur and All India Coordinated \nCotton Improvement Project, Coimbatore to conduct basic strategic applied researc h and \nestablish Centre for Cotton Excellence in Cotton growing States during the said period, if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the details regarding Centres for Cotton Excellence established/likely \nto be established in the country and justification for selection of the locations; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has fixed any area limit for providing compensation for the loss \nof cotton crop on account of natural calamities in the country, if so, the details thereof and the \ncorrective steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to safeguard the cotton farmers \nin the country;  \n \n(d)  the efforts of the Union Government to introduce good cotton growing practices in the \ncountry along with the vision for increasing cotton production in the country; and  \n \n(e)  the details of technological, financial and institutional help provided to the States by the \nUnion Government for increasing cotton production in the country? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nकृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्राऱय में राज्य मंत्री     (SHRI  MOHANBHAI  KUNDARIYA) \n(a):  The area & production of cotton in the country from 2013 -14 to 2015 -16 is as \nunder:-  \nYear  Area  (Lakh ha)  Production (Lakh tones) \n2013-14  119.60 359.02 \n2014-15  128.19 348.05 \n2015-16*  118.59 306.92     \n * 2nd  Advance  estimates  \nContinue….2/-  \n\n \n-2- \nDepartment of Agriculture Cooperation and Farmers Welfare promotes cotton \ncultivation under National Food Security Mission in 15 major cotton growing States viz., \nAndhra Pradesh, Assam, Gujarat, Haryana, Karnataka, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, \nOdisha, Punjab, Rajasthan, Tamil Nadu, Telangana, Tripura, Uttar Pradesh & West Bengal \nfrom 2014-15 onwards.  The State-wise allocation of funds for cotton from 2014-15 to 2016-\n17 is at Annexure-I. \n \n(b):    Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) has given technical & financial \nassistance to Central Institute for Cotton Research (CICR), All India Coordinated Cotton \nImprovement Project (AICCIP) on Cotton and Technology Mission on Cotton (TMC) during \n2013-14, 2014-15 & 2015-16 as under:- \n \n(Rs. In lakhs) \nSchemes 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nCICR 198.23 219.57 504.51 \nAICCIP 1209.70 1150.00 1519.99 \nTMC 376.84 379.98 260.00 \n \n     There is no proposal from ICAR -CICR/AICCIP to establish “Centre of Excellence” for \ncotton. \n \n(c):     Government provides input subsidy to the farmers who suffer crop loss due to \nnatural calamities from State Disaster Response Fund (SDRF) and Natural D isaster \nResponse Fund (NDRF).  Under SDRF/NDRF, input subsidy is provided in cases where \ncrop loss is 33% or more.  As per norms, assistance is given @ Rs. 6800/ - per hectare in \nthe rainfed areas restricted to sown area and Rs. 13500/ - per hectare in assur ed irrigated \nareas subject to minimum assistance not less than Rs. 1000 and restricted to sown areas \nfor the farmers having land holding less than 2 ha.  Above assistance is also given to the \nfarmers having land holding more than 2 ha subject to a ceiling 2 ha per farmer.  \n \n(d) & (e): In order to transfer latest technologies & best practices to cotton growers, \nState Department of Agriculture, Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR), State \nAgriculture Universities (SAUs), Krishi Vigyan Kendras  (KVKs)  conduct  Front Line \nDemonstration (FLD) on Integrated Crop Management (ICM), Desi Cotton, Extra Long \nStaple Cotton, High Density Planting System, Intercropping, Insecticide Resistant \nManagement for increasing cotton production under National Food Security  Mission \n(NFSM).  Besides, the State support cotton cultivation under Rashtriya Krishi Vigyan \nYojana (RKVY). \n     \n       ICAR-Central Institute for Cotton Research has a national network called E -KAPAS of \naround 2 lakh cotton farmers across the country to whom weekly voice mail advisories are \nsent.  Good cotton practices are also broadcast to farmers at weekly intervals all through \nthe season through the CICR website. \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE-I  \nFund allocation of "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |316", "t": "Deployment along Indo-Pak Border", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.316 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 26TH APRIL, 2016/ VAISAKHA 6, 1938 (SAKA)  \n \nDEPLOYMENT ALONG INDO-PAK BORDER  \n \n316.  SHRIMATI KAVITHA KALVAKUNTLA: \nSHRI RAHUL KASWAN: \nDR. K. GOPAL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is true that the Border Security Force has submitted a \ndetailed report to the Government stating that there is no evidence of \ninfiltration along the International Border for the attack  on Pathankot \nairbase recently; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to deploy more forces and \nstrengthen security at riverine patches at the Indo -Pak border to meet \nthe shortage/lapses in border security; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n  \n(a) & (b): The Border Security Force (BSF) has informed that \nnothing has so far come to the notice to conclude any route adopted \nby the terrorists through the Area of Responsibility (AOR). However, \nthe National Investigation Agency (NIA), which is investigating the \nterror attack on Pathankot Airbase, has informed that the terrorists \nhad infiltrated from Pakistan side crossing the border.  \n--2/- \n \n\n \n \n-2- \nLS.US.Q.NO.316 FOR 26.4.2016 \n \nThe exact mode and route taken by them are still under investigation.   \n \n  (c) & (d):  The following steps have been taken to strengthen \nsecurity at the Indo -Pakistan border to meet the shortage/lapses in \nborder security: \n \ni. In order to strengthen border domination, one more Battalion \nhas been inducted in Gurdaspur sector on Punjab Border. \nii. One pilot project for deploying technological solutions in 2 \npatches of 5-6 kilometers of riverine gaps and sensitive areas \nof Jammu Frontier has been sanctioned. \niii. Introduction of Force Multipliers and Hi -tech surveillance \nequipment’s to reduce stress level of  troops and to enhance \nthe surveillance of border. \niv. Upgradation of Intelligence Network and coordination. \nv. Conduct of special operations along the Border and in -depth \nareas. \nvi. Review of vulnerability mapping of BOPs along Indo Pak \nBorder from time to time. \n \n--3/- \n\n \n \n-3- \nLS.US.Q.NO.316 FOR 26.4.2016 \n \nvii. Approval of four Pilot Projects of approx 30 -40 kilometers \neach in different terrains and sensitive riverine gaps in \nJammu, Punjab, Gujarat and West Bengal States. \nviii. Forty five more laser / IR Intrusion detection system are being \nfabricated and being installed in Punjab (eight have already \nbeen fabricated and installed). \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |317", "t": "Production of Quality Seeds", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 317 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 26TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nPRODUCTION OF QUALITY SEEDS \n \n317.    SHRI RAGHAV LAKHANPAL:  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   कृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken note of the fact that seed is a basic input for \nattaining higher crop yields and sustained growth in agricultural production, if so, the steps \ntaken by the Government during the last one year to improve production and dist ribution of \nassured quality seeds to small and marginal farmers at controlled rates; and  \n \n(b)  the steps taken by the Government to ensure that seed industry is not only producing \nadequate quantity of quality seeds but also achieving varietal diversity to  suit various agro -\nclimatic zones of the country? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nकृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्राऱय में राज्य मंत्री     (DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam. Government has made adequate arrangements to improve \nproduction and distribution of certified/quality seeds to farmers including small and \nmarginal farmers at reasonable rates through various crop development                 \nprogrammes /schemes .  The details are at Annexure. During the year 2015 -16 a total \nquantity of 343.52 lakh quintals of certified/quality seeds were produced and made \navailable against the requirement of 337.09 lakh quintals. \n    \n India follows a well established seed generat ion system of production of seeds \nfrom Breeder Seeds to foundation and then certified seeds. To produce adequate \nquantity of certified/quality seeds of suitable varieties for various agro climatic zones of \nthe country, 86213.25 quintals of Breeder Seed was  produced by the Indian Council of \nAgricultural Research Institutes and State Agricultural Universities in 2014 -15. Based on \nindents received both from the public sector (including State Governments) and private \nindustry, 68517.74 quintals was allocated to  all the seed producers in 2015 -16 for \nproduction of adequate quantity of certified/quality seeds. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \n \nDetails of Assistance/Subsidy for seed production and distribution provided under \nvarious existing Schemes/Programmes implemented by States \n \nSl. \nNo. \nScheme/Compon\nent \nCrop Scale of Assistance \n1. National Food \nSecurity \nMission(NFSM) \n \n \n \n \nRice a) Rs.5000/- per quintal or 50% of seeds cost whichever \nis less for certified hybrid rice seed distribution. \nb) Rs.10/- per kg. or  50% of the cost, whichever is less \nfor certified high yielding varieties certified  seed \ndistribution for less than 10 years old varieties. \nWheat Rs.10/- per kg. or 50% of the cost whichever is less for \ncertified high yielding varieties seed distribution for less \nthan 10 years old varieties. \nPulses Rs.2500/- per quintal or 50% of cost whichever is less for \ncertified seeds distribution for less than 10 years old \nvarieties. \nCoarse cereals- \nHYVs seed \n \nHybrid seed \nHYVs Rs.1500/- per quintal or 50% of cost whichever is \nless for HYVs certified seeds distribution for less than 10 \nyears old varieties. \nRs. 5000/- per quintal or 50% of cost of seeds whichever \nis less for   Hybrid coarse cereal certified seeds \ndistribution for less than 10 years old varieties. \nCommercial \nCrops-Jute \nbased \ncropping \nsystem  \n \nSeed Village \nProgramme \nand Production \nof seed in Govt. \nFarms \na) Seed production  \nb) Production of breeder seed @Rs.20000/qtl. \nc) Production of certified Seeds 50% of the cost limited to \nRs.120/- per kg for foundation seed production. \nd) Rs.50/- per kg for certified seed production. \ne) Rs.5500/qtl. for certified seed produced by State \nDepartment of Agriculture, State Agricultural \nUniversities, KVKs, SSCs, NSC and SSCA.  \nCommercial \nCrop – \nSugarcane \nAssistance for breeder seed production \n@Rs.40000/ha.(Rs.34000 for inputs and Rs.6000 for \ncontingency) to  SAUs, ICAR and Sugarcane Research \n“Institute (SRI)  \nProduction of tissue culture raised plantlets/ seedlings \n@Rs.3.5 per seedling. \n2 Bringing Green \nRevolution in \nEastern India \n(BGREI) \nRice and \nWheat \nDistribution of seeds  \na) Rs.5000/- per quintal or 50% of seeds cost whichever \nis less for certified hybrid rice seed distribution. \nb) Rs.10/- per kg. or  50% of the cost, whichever is less \nfor certified high yielding varieties certified  seed \ndistribution for less than 10 years old varieties. \n  Rice and \nWheat \nProduction of seeds \n         The assistance for production of seeds  @ Rs. \n1,000 per qtl. for  HYVs of paddy and wheat and Rs. \n5,000 per qtl.for  paddy hybrid for less than 10 years old \nvarieties from the year 2015-16.  Out of this, 75% amount \nof subsidy is meant for farmers and 25 % to seed \nproducing agencies for meeting expenditure including \nce"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |318", "t": "Bamboo Summit", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 318 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 26TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nBAMBOO SUMMIT \n \n318.     SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA:  \n            SHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO:  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   कृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether a three day Global Bamboo Summit was held in Indore recently and if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the aims and objectives of the summit;  \n \n(b) the details of the issues discussed and opinions exchanged about the challenges faced \nby different stakeholders during the Summit; \n \n(c)  the details of funds allocated and released to the various States during each of the \nlast three years and the current year, State/UT-wise; and  \n \n(d)  the steps taken/being taken by the Government for increasing productivity and \noptimum utilization of existing resources for scientific management of bamboo sector? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nकृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्राऱय में राज्य मंत्री     (SHRI  MOHANBHAI  KUNDARIYA) \n \n(a) & (b): Government of India did not organize the Global Bamboo Summit but it \nwas organized by Government of Madhya Pradesh and Madhya Pradesh State Bamboo \nMission in the month  of April, 2016 at Indore with the objectives to deliberate on overall \ndevelopment of Bamboo sector like Transfer of Technology for substituting wood with \nbamboo, to streamline policy and legal framework for felling and transit of bamboo and to \nutilize the  capacity of b amboo as the change agent for p overty alleviation and climate \nchange etc. \n \n(c): The details of funds allocation and release to various States for the last three \nyears is given at Annexure-I.  There is no provision of budget for the current year. \nHowever, allocation of Rs.75.00 crore has been made in current year budget for National \nProject on Agroforestry which will also take care of Bamboo as part of Agroforestry. \n \n(d):  To promote the sector, Government has adopted an end to end approach for this \nsector and that includes incentivizing production of quality planting material, increasing \nthe bamboo areas inside and outside forest through new plantation/rejuvenation of \ndegraded bamboo areas, incentivizing value addition, creation of marketing infrastructure \nand capacity building in all the above areas. \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n****************** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |319", "t": "Computerisation of PDS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 319 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nCOMPUTERISATION OF PDS \n319.  SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA: \nSHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the present status of computerisation of the Public Distribution \nSystem and linkage of ration cards with Aadhaar Cards State-wise; \n \n(b)  the number of bogus cards identified and eliminated after digitisation; \n \n(c)  whether the implementation of the said project has been delayed in \nsome States; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(e)  the steps taken to encourage States  to complete the process at the \nearliest; and \n \n(f)  the response of the States thereto? \n \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n(a): Under 12th Five Year Plan (2012- 17), the Government has approved a \nplan scheme on ‘End- to-end Computerization of TPDS Operations’ which \ncomprises of activities namely, digitization of ration cards/beneficiary and \nother databases, computerization of supply -chain management, setting up \nof transparency portals and grievance redressal mechanisms etc. The \npresent status of implementation of various activities of the project and \nseeding of Aadhaar in beneficiaries’ data base (State-w\nise) is annexed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \n \n(b): As reported by States/UTs, total 65,99,129 bogus/ineligible ration \ncards have been deleted during the last three years.  \n \n(c) to (f): Initially the implementation of the project was uneven across the \ncountry due to delay in finalization of action  plans, late submission of  \nproposals for financial assistance and practical problems being faced \nduring implementation of the project by States/UTs. Due to constant \nmonitoring of the project through meetings, Video Conferences, visits, \nletters and advisories with States/UTs to review their progress/problems \nfaced etc. There have been significant achievements in implementation of \nthe project as indicated in the annexure. \n*** \n \n\n \nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( a) OF THE UN STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 319 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 26.04.2016 IN THE LOK SABHA \nStatus of End-to-End Computerization of TPDS Operations \nSl. States/UTs Digitization    of \nRation Cards \nAadhaar Seeding \nin Ration Cards \nOnline \nAllocation \nSupply-chain \nManagement \nTransparency \nPortal \nOnline \nGrievance \nRedressal \nToll Free \nHelpline \nNumbers \n1 Andaman & Nicobar  100% 100% Partial - Yes Yes Yes \n2 Andhra Pradesh  100% 100% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 100% 0.71% - - Yes - Yes \n4 Assam 100% 0% Implemented - Yes Yes Yes \n5 Bihar 100% 0.06% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes \n6 Chandigarh 100% 100% NA NA Yes Yes Yes \n7 Chhattisgarh 100% 100% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes \n8 Dadra & Nagar \nHaveli 100% 70% - - Yes - Yes \n9 Daman & Diu 100% 91% Implemented - Yes - Yes \n10 Delhi 100% 100% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes \n11 Goa 100% 96% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes \n12 Gujarat 100% 71% Implemented Implemented Yes Yes Yes \n13 Haryana 100% 86% Implemented - Yes Yes Yes \n14 Himachal Pradesh 100% 95% - - Yes Yes Yes \n15 Jammu and Kashmir 100% 59% Partial - Yes - Yes \n16 Jharkhand 100% 73% Implemented - Yes Yes Yes \n17 Karnataka 100% 47% Implemented Implemented Yes - Yes \n18 Kerala 100% "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |32", "t": "Concessions to Startups regarding Labour Laws", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *32 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26-04-2016 \n \ne-Waste \n \n \n*32.  SHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FO RESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a) whether India has become a destination for dumping of e-waste generated in \ndeveloped countries; \n(b) if so, the quantum of e-waste imported into the country during each of the last three \nyears and the current year, country-wise; \n(c) whether India has adequate facilities for treatment of the imported e-waste, if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, the manner in which the e-waste is treated; \n(d) whether any study has been conducted to ascertain the harmful effects of e- waste on \nenvironment, if so, the details and findings thereof; and \n(e) the steps taken by the Government to ensure better management of  e-waste in the \ncountry? \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST \nAND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) to (e):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n****** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement referred to in reply to Parts (a) to  (e) of the Lok Sabha Starred     Question \nNo 32 for answer on 26 th April, 2016 regarding “e-Waste” raised by SHRI PRATHAP \nSIMHA: \n \n(a) No Madam. \n \n(b) Does not arise in view of answer at (a) above. \n \n(c) The e-waste (Management and Handling) Rules, 2011 and e-waste \n(Management) Rules, 2016 provide for a system of granting authorization and \nregistration of environmentally sound dismantling and recycling facilities of e- \nwaste. Presently one hundred and forty eight (148) dismantling and recycling \nfacilities are authorized. The cumulative capacity of these authorized \ndismantlers and recyclers is 455059 Tonne per annum. CPCB in the year 2005 \nestimated 8 Lakh MT of e-waste generation in the country. \n \n(d) Ministry has not carried out any study to ascertain the harmful effects of e-\nwaste on environment and health. However, e-waste contains hazardous \nsubstances such as Lead, Cadmium, Mercury, Chromium VI, Polybrominated \nbiphenyls and Polybrominated diphenyl ethers in their components, which \nmay cause risk to health and environment if not managed in environmentally \nsound manner. \n \n(e) The Ministry has comprehensively revised e-waste Rules, 2011 and notified E-\nWaste (Management) Rules in March, 2016 for effective and environmentally \nsound management of e-waste. \n \nThe salient features of e-waste (Management) Rules, 2016 are as follows:  \n \n(i) Restructured Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) which has now \nbeen elaborated in terms of implementation procedure.  \n(ii) The procedure simplification has been introduced, wherein EPR \nAuthorization for Producers is now being made CPCB‘s responsibility \nto ensure pan India implementation. \n(iii) Simplification in registration/ authorization for dismantling and \nrecycling through one system i.e. Authorization instead of both \nregistration and authorisation is now introduced. \n(iv) Authorization is not required for Collection centers which shall now be \nProducers responsibility with collection mechanism approach.  \n(v) The target based approach with introduction of more flexibility for \nimplementation of Extended Producer Responsibly (EPR) has been \nintroduced and phase wise target has been fixed for ease of compliance \nof the EPR by the Producers.  \n(vi) Applicability of the Rules is now being extended to components, \nconsumables and spare parts of EEE which makes the product \noperational.  \n(vii) Compact Fluorescent Lamp (CFL) and other mercury containing lamp \nare brought under the purview of rules.  \n(viii) Stakeholders to be covered under the rules are being expanded to \nmanufacturer, dealer, refurbisher and e-retailer to address leakage of e-\nwaste to informal sector at any stage of the chain.  \n\n(ix) Option has been given for setting up of Producers Responsibility \nOrganization (PRO), e-waste exchange, Deposit Refund Scheme as \nadditional channel for implementation of EPR by Producers to ensure \nefficient channelization of e-waste.  \n(x) Bulk Consumer is being redefined in terms of turnover and the number \nof employees and they need to file annual returns now.  \n(xi) The roles of the State Government has been also introduced in the \nRules in order to ensure safety, health and skill development of the \nworkers involved in the dismantling and recycling operations.  \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |320", "t": "Ban on Diesel Vehicles", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES \nDEPARTMENT OF HEAVY INDUSTRY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.320 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.04.2016 \n \nBan on Diesel Vehicles \n \n320. SHRI RAOSAHEB DANVE PATIL: \n \n \n Will the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether a ban has been imposed on sale of diesel cars in Delhi and NCR to check rising \npollution and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether the auto manufacturers/ dealers have sought time to clear stocks;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the Government thereto;  \n(d) whether the Government has assessed the impact of the said ban on investment in the country; \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the corrective steps proposed in this regard; and \n(f) whether the Government proposes to extend the said ban to other cities facing air pollution and \nif so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC \nENTERPRISES (SHRI  G.M. SIDDESHWARA) \n \n(a): No Madam.  There is no ban on sale of diesel cars in Delhi and NCR. However, Hon’ble \nSupreme Court vide its order dated 16 -12-2015 passed in the case of M C Mehta v/s Union of \nIndia has directed that the registration of SUVs and private cars of the capacity of 2000 CC and \nusing above diesel fuel shall stand banned in the NCR up to 31 st March 2016. The same was again \nextended on 31st march 2016 by Hon’ble Supreme Court a nnouncing to take up the matter on 30 th \nApril 2016.   Implementation of the said order is to be carried out by the State Governments/Union \nTerritories Administrations. \n \n(b) & (c):  Department of Heavy Industry, Ministry of Heavy Industries & Public Enterprises \nhave not received any such proposal from the auto manufacturers/ dealers. \n \n(d) & (e):  No Madam, It is too soon to understand its impact on the said ban on investment in \nthe country. \n \n(f):  No Madam.  \n \n***************  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |321", "t": "Revival of Urea Plants by PSUs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF FERTILIZERS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.321 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.04.2016 \n \n \nRevival of Urea Plants by PSUs \n \n321.  SHRI NAGENDRA KUMAR PRADHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to assign revival work of closed urea plants of \nHindustan Fertiliser Corporation and Fertiliser Corporation of India to profit \nmaking and financially sound PSUs like ONGC, NTPC CIL etc;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether any assessment has been done to find out the expenditure likely to be \nincurred on various such urea plants; \n(d) if so, the details thereof, plant-wise; and \n(e) the time by which the PSUs are likely to start revival of closed urea plants? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \n \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) & (b):   In view of poor response from bidders for Request for Qualifications (RFQ) \nfor Gorakhpur and Sindri Units of Fertiliser Corporation of India (FCIL) and keeping in \nview current market scenario, it is under consideration to revive Sindri and Gorakhpur \nunits of FCIL and Barauni Unit of Hindustan Fertilizer Corporation Limited (HFCL) \nthrough Joint Ventures of profit making and financially strong Public Sector \nUndertakings (PSUs) of Ministries of Power, Coal and Petroleum & Natural Gas \nrespectively.  \n \n(c) & (d) :    No  specific assessment of likely expenditure for setting up Urea plants at \nthese locations has been made.  However, the likely cost of setting up an Ammonia-\nUrea plant of 1.27 million MT capacity  is approximately Rs. 6000/- crore. \n \n(e):  Revival process will start after formation of Joint Ventures and on receipt of all \nnecessary approvals.  \n \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |322", "t": "Review of Environmental Clearance", "head": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.322 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.04.2016 \n \n \nReview of Environmental Clearance \n  \n \n322.  SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FO RESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether Government proposes to review the environmental clearances required for various \nprojects; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to extend the standard for public utilisation of land; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST \nAND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a)  No, Madam. \n \n(b) Does not arise. \n \n(c)   No, Madam. \n \n(d) Does not arise. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |323", "t": "Work undertaken by Krishi Vigyan Kendras", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 323 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26/04/2016 \n \n \nWORK UNDERTAKEN BY KRISHI VIGYAN KENDRAS \n \n \n323. SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) which are functional in \nWardha and Amravati region of Vidarbha, Maharashtra; and \n \n(b) the details of the work undertaken by the said Krishi Vigyan Kendras during \nthe last three years and the current year? \n  \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f”k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h \n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n \n(a) & (b): There is one KVK functioning in Wardha region of Vidarbha, \nMaharashtra under State Agricultural University (Dr. Panjabrao Deshmukh Krishi \nVidyapeeth (PDKV), Akola) since 1979 and two KVKs are functioning in Amravati \nregion, of Ma harashtra under NGOs, one in Durgapur (Shram Sadhana) and the \nother in Ghatked (Shramsafalya Foundation) since 1995.  The details of work \nundertaken by these Krishi Vigyan Kendras during the last three years and the \ncurrent year are given in Annexure-I.  \n \n  \n******** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-I \n[Part (a) & (b) to Lok Sabha USQ No.323 for 26-04-2016] \n \nThe details of work under taken by the KVKs in Wardha and Amravati region during the last three \nyears and current year. \n \nSl \nNo \nName of \nKVKs \nActivities Achievements \n2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \n1. Amravati \n(Durgapur) \nOn- farm trials  and demonstrations (Number) 376 368 296 160 \nFarmers and extension personnel trained \n(Number) \n3948 4643 3418 3240 \nProduction of Seed (in qtls) 33.55 58.91 65.18 70.16 \nProduction of planting material(in number) 6207 4674 2795 5213 \nLive-stock strains and fingerlings(in number) 44 314 4907 11 \n2. Amravati \n(Ghatked) \nOn- farm trials  and demonstrations (Number) 347 450 469 356 \nFarmers and extension personnel trained \n(Number) \n4411 3753 3648 6976 \nProduction of Seed (in qtls) 15.75 4.60 19.18 27.88 \nProduction of planting material(in number) 61569 146333 103282 7478 \nLive-stock strains and fingerlings(in number) 90 100 103 37 \n3. Wardha On- farm trials  and demonstrations (Number) 296 247 186 197 \nFarmers and extension personnel trained \n(Number) \n4175 3202 3202 2905 \nProduction of Seed (in qtls) 51 45 45 19.5 \nProduction of planting material(in number) 80000 90000 90000 18000 \nLive-stock strains and fingerlings(in number) 399 46 0 22 \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |324", "t": "Protection of Airports", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.324 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 26TH APRIL, 2016/ VAISAKHA 6, 1938 (SAKA)  \n \nPROTECTION OF AIRPORTS \n \n324.  SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY: \nSHRI DILIPKUMAR MANSUKHLAL GANDHI: \nSHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \n \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Central Industrial Security Force (CISF) has been \nmandated to provide security to all civilian airports in the country; \n \n(b) if so, whether all such airports are protected by the CISF; \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor and the details and number of airports \nwhere CISF security is mandated for but not provided along with the \ncorrective measures taken/proposed to be taken by the Government \nin this regard; \n \n(d) whether other a gencies that are patrolling the civilian airports \ninstead of the CISF are trained appropriately in order to replace the \nCISF in their duties and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(e) the total funds paid to the CISF on an annual basis by public and \nprivate airport operators for providing security services during each \nof the last three years and the current year, State-wise; and \n \n(f) the unpaid dues to the CISF for providing security services to the \nairports, airport -wise along with the steps taken to recover all  \npending funds? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARIBHAI PARATHIBHAI CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (c) : Out of 98 operational airports in the country, the \nCentral   Industrial   Security   Force (CISF)   is deployed  at  59  \n--2/- \n \n\n \n-2- \nLS.US.Q.NO.324 FOR 26.04.2016 \n \nairports.  The remaining airports are guarded by other security \nforces, including the Central Reserve Police Force (CRPF) and \nthe State Police. \n \nFor reasons of exigency and / or logistics, some of the airports \nare guarded  by forces other than CISF.  Induction of CISF is \nsubject to joint survey to fix the strength required,   \nMemorandum of Understanding between CISF and the Airport \nOperators, payment of security deposits etc.  In some of the \nairports, the procedures prior t o induction of CISF for guarding  \nairports have not been undertaken by Ministry of Civil Aviation, \nas per their requirement. \n   \n(d) : The Bureau of Civil Aviation Security (BCAS) appropriately \ntrains and evaluates the suitability of personnel to perform \naviation security functions. \n \n(e) : The total funds paid to the CISF on an annual basis by \npublic and private airport operators of 59 airports for providing \nsecurity services during each of the last three years, is as \nunder : \n \n2013-14 (April-March) \nRs.983.30 crore \n2014-15 (April-March) \nRs.1042.46 crore \n2015-16 (April-March) \nRs.1012.71 crore \n \nThe state-wise details are enclosed at Annexure-‘A’ \n  \n(f): The total unpaid dues to the CISF for providing security \nservices as on date is 545.18 Crore approx. \n--3/- \n \n\n \n-3- \nLS.US.Q.NO.324 FOR 26.04.2016 \n \nThe airport-wise details are enclosed at Annexure-‘B’. \n \nCISF has been vigorously pursuing the matter of unpaid dues, \nwith the authorities concerned at various levels including \nAirport Directors, Regional Executive Directors of the Airport \nAuthority of India (AAI), Chairman, AAI, other Airport Operators \nand the Ministry of Civil Aviation. \n***** \n  \n\n \n \n-4- \nANNEXURE – A \nLS.US.Q.NO.324 FOR 26.04.2016 \nLOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.324 FOR ANSWER ON 26.04.2016 \nSl  \nNo \nName of  Airport TOTAL FUNDS PAID TO THE CISF ON AN ANNUAL BASIS BY \nPUBLIC AND PRIVATE AIRPORT OPERATORS FOR \nPROVIDING SECURITY SERVICES DURING EACH OF THE \nLAST THREE YEARS STATE-WISE. \n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nANDHRA PRADESH \n1 TIRUPATI AIRPORT 38049038 50258405 57639706 \n2 VIZAG AIRPORT 68876450 72463313 83595897 \nANDAMAN & NICOBAR ISLANDS \n3 PORT BLAIR AIRPORT 30683467 33764472 38126158 \nASSAM \n4 DIBRUGARH AIRPORT 57028574 61832634 66285736 \n5 GUWAHATI AIRPORT 120990437 138462351 14104502 \n6 JORHAT AIRPORT 32797924 34422049 37237073 \n7 LILABARI AIRPORT 18656918 19357569 22695481 \n8 SILCHAR AIRPORT 28916587 29814241 32040850 \n9 TEZPUR AIRPORT 14846078 26009003 19701927 \nBIHAR \n10. GAYA AIRPORT 58238984 70627951 67718757 \n11. PATNA AIRPORT 88998736 102433976 138963689 \nCHHATTISGARH \n12. RAIPUR AIRPORT 122439377 132663579 148146733 \nDELHI \n13. IGI AIRPORT 2126860240 1242389775 30308369 \nDAMAN AND DIU \n14. DIU AIRPORT 28876698 28869988 29701688 \nGOA \n15. GOA AIRPORT 128940309 173195687 188242623 \n\nGUJARAT \n16. AHMEDABAD AIRPORT 279263616 332218618 316221168 \n17. BHAVNAGAR AIRPORT 33780842 40048164 40154418 \n18. BHUJ AIRPORT 30074267 31746711 31762984 \n19. PORBANDAR AIRPORT 28246167 30302228 33950490 \n20. RAJKOT AIRPORT 50032746 55949234 73910662 \n21. VADODARA AIRPORT 76827095 82839153 92204794 \nHIMACHAL PRADESH \n22 BHUNTER AIRPORT 23183065 28731129 31766550 \n23 SHIMLA AIRPORT 20516483 20915720 28803493 \nJHARKHAND \n24 RANCHI AIRPORT 61815420 121064502 125771266 \nKARNATAKA \n  \n\n \n \n-5- \n25 BANGLARORE AIROPRT 578985700 834796625 780937800 \n26 MANGL"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |325", "t": "Promotion of Bio-Fertilizers", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 325 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 26TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nPROMOTION OF BIO-FERTILIZERS \n \n325.    SHRI SHARAD TRIPATHI:  \n           SHRIMATI RITI PATHAK:  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   कृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total quantum of bio-fertilizers required and actually produced in the country;  \n \n(b)  the details of the steps being taken by the Government to increase the production and \nutilization of bio-fertilizers in various States of the country;  \n \n(c)  the details of the bio -fertilizers/ organic fertilizers produced and distributed during the \nlast three years and the current year, State-wise; and  \n \n(d)  whether several organisations are involved in research and development of organic \nfertilizers/bio-fertilizers in the country, if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nकृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्राऱय में राज्य मंत्री     (SHRI  MOHANBHAI  KUNDARIYA) \n \n(a) to (c): The total requirement of various bio -fertilizers that are required for \nseed/root treatment and soil is estimated to be about 0.426 million ton, based on net \ncultivated area.  Government is promoting bio -fertilizers through   various schemes of \nNational Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA)/ Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana \n(PKVY), Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana (RKVY) and National Mission on Oilseeds and Oil \nPalm (NMOOP), National Food Security Mission (NFSM) and Indian Council of \nAgricultural Sciences (ICAR). The pattern of assistance is given in Annexure I.   The \ndetailed state wise production of organic fertilizer in the country is at Annexure II. \n \n(d):  The Indian Council of Agriculture Research (ICAR) under Network project on \nOrganic Farming is undertaking research to develop location specific organic farming \npackage of practices for crops and cropping systems. Presently, the project is being \nimplemented in 20 centres located in state covering 16 States. Organic farming package \nof practices for 18 crops/cropping systems have been developed. Besides, under ICAR’s \nNetwork project on Soil Biodiversity -Biofertiliser has developed improved and efficient \nstrains of biofertiliser specific to different crops and soil types. Liquid Biofertiliser \ntechnology with higher shelf life has also been developed. The Council has developed \ntechnologies to prepare various types of organic manures such as phospho compost, \nvermi compost, municipal solid waste compost, bio -enriched compost etc. from various \norganic wastes. The ICAR also imparts training, organizes Front Line Demonstrations \n(FLDs) to educate farmers on all these aspects. \n \n\nAnnexure I \n \nA. National Mission of Sustainable  Agriculture (NMSA) -Organic & INM \nComponent of Soil Health Management (SHM): \n \n1. Setting up of State of art liquid/ carrier based Biofertilizer/ Biopesticide units, \n100% Assistance to State Govt/Govt. Agencies upto a maximum limit of \nRs.160.00 lakh /unit and 25% of cost limited to Rs.40 lakh/unit for individuals/ \nprivate agencies through NABARD as capital investment of 200 TPA production \ncapacity. \n \n2.  Setting up of Bio-fertilizer and Organic fertilizer testing Quality Control Laboratory \n(BOQCL) or Strengthening of existing Laboratory under FCO, assistance  up to \nmaximum limit of Rs. 85 lakh for new laboratory and up to a maximum limit of \nRs. 45 lakh for strengthening of existing infrastructure to State Government \nLaboratory under Agriculture or Horticulture Department. \n \n3. Promotion of Organic Inputs on farmer’s field (Manure, Vermi-compost, Bio-\nFertilizers Liquid / solid, Waste compost, Herbal extracts  etc.), 50 % of cost \nsubject to a limit of Rs. 5000/- per ha and Rs. 10,000 per beneficiary. Propose to \ncover 1 million ha area. \n \nB. Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY): It is the first comprehensive \nscheme launched by the Central Government as a centrally sponsored \nprogramme (CSP). The scheme is implemented by the State Governments on a \ncluster basis of 20 hectare each. The farmer within the cluster is given financial \nassistance upto a maximum of 1 ha and the limit of assistance is Rs.50,000 per \nha during the conversion period of 3 years. The target is to promote 10,000 \nclusters covering 5 lakh acres over the period of 3 years, 2015 -16 to 2017 -18. \nFurther, following assistan ce is given for procuring liquid bio -fertilizer and bio -\npesticides: \n \n1. Liquid Bio -fertilizer consortia (Nitrogen fixing/ Phosphate Solubilizing/ potassium \nmobilizing bio-fertilizer) @ Rs.500/acre x 50 of Rs.25000 per cluster in first year. \n \n2. Liquid Biopestici des (Trichoderma viridae, Pseudomonas, fluorescens, \nMatarhizium, Beaviourie bassiana, Pacelomyces, verticillium) 2 Rs.500/ acre x 50 \nof Rs.25000 per cluster in second year. \nContinu"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |327", "t": "Representation of Tribals in CAPF", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.327 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 26TH APRIL, 2016/ VAISAKHA 6, 1938 (SAKA)  \n \nREPRESENTATION OF TRIBALS IN CAPF \n \n327. SHRI B. SRIRAMULU: \n \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the representation of tribals  have decreased in Central \nArmed Police Forces (CAPF) such as CRPF, BSF and CISF; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to increase the representation \nof tribals in CAPF? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (b): There is no decrease in representation of Schedule Tribes in \nBSF, CISF, ITBP & Assam Rifles. There is a minor decrease in CRPF \nand SSB. The Force wise representation of tribals as on 01.01.2015 \nand 01.01.2016 is enclosed as per Annexure-I.     \n(c): The fol lowing steps have been taken to increase the \nrepresentation of tribals in CAPFs:- \ni)     Imparting of finishing training and interview skills. \nii)   Conduct of separate recruitment drives for allied officers. \niii)   Wide publicity to special recruitment drives by forwarding copies   \nto National Commission for SC/ST and State Commissions. \n***** \n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE – I \nLS.US.Q.NO.327 FOR 26.04.2016 \n \nREPRESENTATION OF TRIBALS IN CAPFs \n \nForce Total \nnumber of \nemployees \nas on \n01.01.2015 \nRepresentation \nof ST as on \n01.01.2015 \nTotal \nnumber of \nemployees \nas on \n01.01.2016 \nRepresentation \nof ST as on \n01.01.2016 \nST %age ST %age \nCRPF 285295 27663 9.69 295417 27421 9.28 \nBSF 245779 25604 10.41 248811 26476 10.64 \nCISF 125687 12417 9.87 127008 12849 10.11 \nITBP 79367 7281 9.17 81772 7972 9.74 \nARs 65037 10750 16.52 64785 11014 17.00 \nSSB 77740 9801 12.60 77972 9757 12.51 \nTotal 878905 93516 10.64 895765 95489 10.66 \n \n------ "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |328", "t": "Export of Foodgrains", "head": "ORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 328 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nEXPORT OF FOODGRAINS \n328.  SHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the quantity and value of foodgrains  exported and/or imported during \neach of the last three years and the current year  along with the names of \nexporting/importing countries of the said foodgrains; \n \n(b)  the names and quantum of the foodgrains imported on the prices \nhigher than their export price along with the extent  of losses suffered by \nthe Government as a result thereof; and \n \n(c)  the reasons for undertaking such loss making imports? \n \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a): The quantity and value of wheat exported on Government Account \nduring each of the last three years and current year are given as under:- \n \nYear Quantity (in \nlakh metric \ntons) \nValue realized (in \nIndian Rupees in \ncrores) \n2012-13 28.99 4,937.01 \n2013-14 25.71 4,489.87 \n2014-15 3.27 552.35 \n2015-16 Nil Nil. \n \nThe list of countries to which the shipment has been made is given in \nthe statement at Annexure. \n \n  There has however, no export of rice and no import of wheat and rice \non Government Account during last three years and current year.  \n \n(b) & (c): Does not arise. \n \n**** \n\n \n \nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF THE UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.328 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 26.04.2016 IN THE LOK SABHA \n \nList of countries to which shipment of wheat was made in 2012-13 to 2014-15 \n \n(Fig. in MTs) \nSl. \nNo. \nCountry Total Quantity Exported \n1 Bangladesh 13,36,934 \n2 South Korea 12,48,147 \n3 Ethiopia 8,53,932 \n4 UAE/Oman 6,97,365 \n5 Yemen 4,73,566 \n6 Indonesia 4,32,568 \n7 Thailand 2,71,767 \n8 Saudi Arabia 1,33,545 \n9 Vietnam 79,500 \n10 Philippines 73,650 \n11 Sri Lanka 5,350 \n12 Sudan 48,000 \n13 Qatar 44,000 \n14 Tanzania 27,000 \n15 South Africa 13,750 \n16 Malaysia 10,500 \n Total 57,97,724 \n \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |329", "t": "Setting up of Krishi Vigyan Kendras", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 329 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26/04/2016 \n \n \nSETTING UP OF KRISHI VIGYAN KENDRAS \n \n \n329. SHRIMATI P.K. SREEMATHI TEACHER: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has got any proposal to start new Krishi Vigyan \nKendras (KVKs); \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Unio n Government has received any proposal from the State \nGovernment of Kerala to start KVKs in Kannur district; and \n \n(d) if so, the present status of the proposal? \n  \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f”k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h \n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam. There are 67 proposals received from different states to \nstart new Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) The State/ district -wise details of \nproposals received to start the new KVKs is given in Annexure.  \n \n(c) & (d): No, Madam. There is already one KVK functioning in Kannur district \nunder the administrative control of Kerala Agriculture University located at \nPanniyur, Post Office -Kanhirangad, Taliparamba, Pin Code - 670142. As per the \nEFC for the 12 th Plan there is no provision for establishment of additional KVK in \nKannur district. \n******** \n \n\n2 \n \n \nAnnexure \n[Part (a) & (b) to Lok Sabha USQ No.329 for 26-04-2016] \n \nState/ district-wise details of proposals received to start the new KVKs \nName of  Zone S.No. State Name of the KVK \nDistrict \nZone-I, Ludhiana 1 Punjab Fazilka \n 2. Pathankot \n 3. Haryana Mewat \n 4. Panchkula \n 5. Jammu & \nKashmir \nRamban \n 6. Samba \n 7. Udhampur \n 8. Kishtwar \n 9. Kargil \n 10. Himachal Pradesh Lahul & Spiti \nZone-II, Kolkata 11 West Bengal Murshidabad \nZone-III, Umiam, \nBarapani \n12 Nagaland Paren \n 13 Kiphire \n 14 Assam Dima Hasao \n 15 Meghalaya South Garo Hills \n 16 East Garo Hills \n 17 Tripura West Tripura \n 18 Gomati \n 19 Unakoti \n 20 Sepahijala \n 21 Arunachal \nPradesh \nDibang valley \n 22 Kurung kumey \n 23 Longding \nZone-IV, Kanpur 24 Uttar Pradesh Gorakhpur \nZone-\nV,Hyderabad \n25 Andhra Pradesh YSR Kadapa \n 26 Sri Potti Sriramullu -\nNellore,  \n 27 Visakhapatnam \n 28 Telangana Adilabad \n 29 Khammam \n 30 Medak \n 31 Maharashtra Sangli \n 32 Kolhapur \n 33 Nagpur \n 34 Thane \n 35 Jalna \nZone-VI,Jodhpur 36 Rajasthan Sriganganagar \n 37 Udaipur \n 38 Jalore  \n 39 Pali  \n 40 Bhilwara \n 41 Sikar  \n\n3 \n \n 42 Gujarat Vadodara  \n 43 Bhavnagar \n 44 GandhiNagar \n 45 Jamnagar \n 46 Junagadh \n 47 Surendranagar \n 48 Morbi \n 49 Aravali \n 50 Chote Udeypur \nZone-VII, \nJabalpur \n51 Chhatisgarh Mungeli \n 52 Balod \n 53 Surajpur \n 54 Kondagaon \n 55 Bemetara \n 56 Sukma \n 57 Raipur \n 58 Durg \n 59 Madhya Pradesh Agar Malwa \n 60 Alirajpur \n 61 Singrauli \n 62 Sagar \n 63 Dhar \nZone-\nVIII,Bangalore \n64 Karnataka Yadagir \n 65 Vijayapur (erstwhile \nBijapur) \n 66 Tamil Nadu Thiruppur \n \n \n67 Villluppuram \n \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |33", "t": "Socially Disadvantaged Students", "head": " \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *33 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.04.2016 \n \nEMPOWERMENT OF RURAL CONSUMERS \n \n*33. SHRI R. PARTHIPAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the present consumer movement has largely been confined to the urban centres  \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether the expanding rural markets have now necessitated shift in focus to the rural \nconsumers, if so, the details thereof and the steps taken thereon; and  \n(c) whether the Government has put any mechanism in place to  protect the rural consumers \nby educating them about their rights and empowering them to make decisions based on \ninformation about the goods and services and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=ky; ea=h  \n¼Jh jke foykl ikloku½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a) to (c) :    A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART S (a) TO (c ) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *33 FOR 26.04.2016 REGARDING EMPOWERMENT OF \nRURAL CONSUMERS. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n \n(a) to (c):  The present consumer movement in the country benefi ts both the rural and the \nurban consumers. L iberalization and privatization of the economy has resulted in our markets \nbeing integrated with global supply chains. This combined with information explosion and \naggressive marketing has given rise to a rapidly growing rural demand for consumer goods.  \n \nThe enactment of the Consumer Protection Act in 1986 has set in motion a veritable consumer \nmovement in the entire country. 657 Consumer Fora have been established at the district level \nacross the country for the protection and promotion of consumer rights. A wide network of more \nthan 1250 voluntary consumer organisations across the length and breadth of the country has \nspread the consumer movement in the rural areas.  \n \n For strengthening the movement in rural are as, the Government has been encouraging rural \nconsumer centric projects, supporting the State Governments to carry forward the movement to \nthe rural consumers, setting up of consumer clubs in schools and colleges, carrying out publicity \ncampaigns on consum er education and awareness through electronic and print media.  \nDoordarshan   has a significant geographic reach and a diverse viewership cutting across the \nurban-rural population segments and the reach of   All India Radio is almost 99% of the \ncountry’s population and provides an effective platform for reaching out to rural consumers. \nPrint advertisements are published in the National and Regional Newspapers in vernacular \nlanguages is significantly aimed at rural consumers. Besides, State Governments and U nion \nTerritory Administrations are funded to carry out campaigns in rural and remote areas in local \nlanguages. The Department also conducts publicity campaigns in rural melas. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |330", "t": "National Bulk Drug Development Authority", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 330 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 26th April, 2016 \n \nNational Bulk Drug Development \nAuthority \n \n330. DR. SANJAY JAISWAL: \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has given its approval for setting up of a \nNational Bulk Drugs Development Authority, if so, the details \nthereof along with the progress on the same; and  \n \n(b) the details of other measures that the Government is planning to \ninitiate for making India self-reliant in terms of production of Active \nPharmaceutical Ingredients (APIs)? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND \nFERTILIZERS (SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a):  No, Madam. \n(b): The Government is now examining the viability of supporting the \nproposal for providing assistance for common minimum facilities for 03 \nGreenfield Bulk Drugs/ API Parks to the extent of Rs. 200 cr each. Besides \nthis, the D epartment in association with Ministry of Environment, Forests & \nClimate Change and Ministry of Commerce & Industry are sorting out the \nissue relating to the difficulties being faced by the Bulk Drug Manufacturers \nrelating to Environmental issues.  \nxxxxxx \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |331", "t": "Processed Foods", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 331 \nANSWERED ON 26TH APRIL, 2016 \nPROCESSED FOODS \n331.  SHRI GANESH SINGH: \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken any steps to establish good coordination between farmers \nand food processing industries in the country, if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the Government proposes to provide financial assistance to sensitize  entrepreneurs for \nsetting up of new food processing units in diversified agriculture sector; \n(c)   if so, the details thereof; \n(d)   the details of agricultural  products used as raw material in food processing industries during \neach of the last three years; and \n(e)  the details of production, consumption and export of processed and packaged food items during \nthe said period? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI ) has been implementing Mega Food Park  \nScheme (MFPS) since 11 th Five Years Plan to create modern infrastructure facilities for the food \nprocessing along the value chain from the farm to the market with a cluster based approach  based on a \nhub and spokes model. It includes creation of infrastructure for primary processing and storage near  the \nfarm at Primary Processing Centres (PPCs) and Collection Centres (CCs) and common facilities and \nenabling infrastructure at Central Processi ng Centre (CPC). The scheme also aims to provide a \nmechanism to bring together farmers, processors and retailers and link agriculture production to the \nmarket so as to ensure maximization of value addition, minimization of wastages and improving \nfarmers’ income.  \nThe scheme of Integrated Cold Chain, Value Addition and Preservation Infrastructure also promotes \nbackward linkages with farmers through Minimal Processing Centres.   \n(b) & (c):   To create awareness and to sensitize entrepreneurs for setting up of new food processing \nunits in diversified food processing  sectors, Ministry is implementing a  Scheme for Promotional \nActivities, under which  financial assistance is provided for organizing seminars/ workshops/ fair/ \nexhibitions in various parts of the country. The Ministry also participates in All India level fairs/ \nexhibitions for dissemination of information regarding food processing industries, familiarizing the  \nentrepreneurs with modern techniques of production and preservation.   \n(d) &  (e): The data of agricultural produce used as raw material in food processing, production and \nconsumption of processed and packaged food items are not maintained in the Ministry. Th e details of \nexport of food products are given in Annexure.  \n***** \n\n \n \nAnnexure \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (d) & (e) OF  THE LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.331 FOR  ANSWERED ON 26 TH APRIL, 2016 \nREGARDING EXPORT OF PROCESSED FOODS  \n  \n  India Export of Agri Products \n  \nCountry Name/Region Name :- All \n  \nQty In MT; Value in Rs. Lacs & US$ Mill   \nSl. No. Product Name \n2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 \nQty Rs. Lacs US$ Mill Qty Rs. Lacs US$ Mill Qty Rs. Lacs US$ Mill \n1 Marine Products 965099.42 1884119.52 3464.08 1000818.64 3062727.68 5016.63 1073269.70 3368545.26 5510.02 \n2 Buffalo Meat 1076103.00 1740898.73 3201.14 1365643.00 2645781.59 4350.38 1475540.00 2928258.19 4781.18 \n3 Spices 932591.86 1517674.51 2786.12 896565.40 1514635.87 2497.30 923271.28 1484235.66 2429.48 \n4 Sugar 2793778.00 857632.24 1574.62 2477640.