 -  -  1 -  4 1800 \n12 Karnataka 1900  - -  -  -   - 25 1925 \n13 Kerala 358 130 -  -  -   - 12 500 \n14 \nMadhya \nPradesh 4476 6 -  -  -  -  93 4575 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |631", "t": "NMDFC Projects", "head": "                                   GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.631 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nNMDFC PROJECTS \n \n631.  SHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE: \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the National Minorities Development and Finance Corporation (NMDFC) has \nreceived any projects from various State Governments during each of the last three \nyears and the current year;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State-wise;  \n(c) the details of projects/proposals sanctioned by the Government, State-wise;  \n(d) the time by which pending projects are proposed to be sanctioned, project -wise; \nand  \n(e) the reasons for delay in this regard and the steps taken by the Government for \ntheir early disposal, project-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \n     MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n (SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) to (d): The schemes of National Minorities Development and Finance Corporation \n(NMDFC) are implemented through State Channelising  Agencies (SCAs) nominated by \nrespective State Governments. As per the lending policy of NMDFC, the SCAs are \nrequired to furnish their proposals in the form of Annual Action Plans (AAP) to the \nNMDFC at the beginning of each financial year.  Consequent upon  the furnishing of the \nAAPs by the various SCAs, the NMDFC allocates fu nds to the SCAs based on their \nrequirements but subject to their past performance in implementing NMDFC Schemes, \nproper utilisation of f unds disbursed earlier by NMDFC, maintaining sati sfactory \nrepayments to the NMDFC , availability of sufficient Government guarantee and equity \ncontribution of the States’ share by the respective State Governments .The state-wise \ndetails of funds disbursed and beneficiaries covered during last three years i s at \nAnnexure. The allocation for the current financial year i.e. 2016 -17 are under \nfinalization.  There is no pendency of proposals at this stage. \n \n(e): Does not arise in view of the above. \n  \n\n \nAmount No. of \nbeneficiaries Amount No. of \nbeneficiaries Amount No. of \nbeneficiaries Amount No. of \nbeneficiaries Amount No. of \nbeneficiaries Amount No. of \nbeneficiaries Amount No. of \nbeneficiaries Amount No. of \nbeneficiaries Amount No. of \nbeneficiaries\n1 CHANDIGARH 0.20 21 0.20 21 0.20 21\n2 CHHATISGARH 1.50 158 1.50 158 1.50 667 1.50 667 3.00 825\n3 GUJARAT 2.00 133 2.00 133 2.00 133\n4 HARYANA 0.20 21 8.60 574 8.80 595 1.50 667 0.80 356 1.00 400 3.30 1,423 12.10 2,018\n5 HIMACHAL PRADESH 3.50 368 5.50 580 3.75 250 12.75 1,198 12.75 1,198\n6 JAMMU & KASHMIR 15.00 1,579 20.00 2,106 37.94 2,530 72.94 6,215 0.90 360 0.90 360 73.84 6,575\n7 KARNATAKA 18.50 1,947 20.00 2,105 10.00 667 48.50 4,719 48.50 4,719\n8 KERALA 48.00 5,052 54.50 5,737 111.60 7,439 214.10 18,228 25.00 11,110 38.00 16,887 30.00 12,000 93.00 39,997 307.10 58,225\n9 MAHARASHTRA 10.00 1,053 10.00 1,053 10.00 1,053\n10 MIZORAM 2.00 211 2.00 133 4.00 344 4.00 344\n11 NAGALAND 4.00 422 5.00 527 9.00 949 0.96 427 3.50 1,556 4.46 1,983 13.46 2,932\n12 PUDUCHERRY 1.50 158 1.00 105 3.25 217 5.75 480 1.50 667 1.00 444 1.75 700 4.25 1,811 10.00 2,291\n13 PUNJAB 7.00 738 11.00 1,159 4.50 300 22.50 2,197 22.50 2,197\n14 RAJASTHAN 40.00 4,211 19.50 2,053 20.00 1,333 79.50 7,597 0.50 222 0.50 222 80.00 7,819\n15 TAMILNADU 6.00 632 7.00 737 5.00 333 18.00 1,702 14.00 6,222 31.50 14,000 5.00 2,000 50.50 22,222 68.50 23,924\n16 TRIPURA 9.00 948 12.00 1,263 15.00 999 36.00 3,210 36.00 3,210\n17 UTTRAKHAND 1.00 67 1.00 67 1.00 67\n18 WEST BENGAL 50.00 5,263 75.00 7,895 85.00 5,668 210.00 18,826 80.00 35,555 110.00 48,889 125.00 50,000 315.00 1,34,444 525.00 1,53,270\n202.50 21,318 244.40 25,731 309.64 20,643 756.54 67,692 122.96 54,648 186.80 83,021 163.65 65,460 473.41 2,03,129 1,229.95 2,70,821\nG R A N D \nT O T A L\nSTATESr.No.\nGrand Total\nAnnexure\nNATIONAL MINORITIES DEVELOPMENT & FINANCE CORPORATION\nAnnexure  referred to in part (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.631, for answer on 27.4.2016 regarding \"NMDFC Projects\" asked by Shri Ashok Mahadeorao Nete\nAmount Rs. In Crore\n2013-2014 2014-2015 2015-2016 Micro Credit\nT O T A L \nMicro CreditTerm Loan\n2013-2014 2014-2015 2015-2016 Term Loan\nT O T A L \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |632", "t": "Modernisation of Railway Infrastructure", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO. 632 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \n \nMODERNISATION OF RAILWAY INFRASTRUCTURE \n \n632.        SHRI K. PARASURAMAN: \n  ADV. NARENDRA KESHAV SAWAIKAR: \n \n      \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   whether the Government has entered into agreements with foreign \ncountries for modernisation of railway infrastructure; \n \n(b)   if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)   whether the Railways has raised/proposes to raise some funds fr om \ntax free bonds with a view to mobilis e funds for infrastructure development \nof railways; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n \n \n \n \n    (SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n \n \n(a) & (b):  Ministry of Railways have en tered into Agreements/Memorandum \nof Understandings with various foreign countries viz. Spain, France, \nGermany, Czech Republic, China, South Korea, Canada, Kazakhstan, Slovak \nRepublic, United Kingdom, Japan, Russia and Sweden for technical \ncooperation in ra il sector, which inter -alia includes modernization of rail \ninfrastructure as a cooperation area. \n \n(c) & (d): During the Financial Year 2015 -16, Indian Railways Finance \nCorporation Ltd. (IRFC) raised  ` 9,500 crore through issue of Tax -free bonds \nto finance acquisition of rolling stock. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |633", "t": "Kalpakkam Nuclear Power Station", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nDEPARTMENT OF ATOMIC ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.633  \n TO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nKALPAKKAM NUCLEAR POWER STATION \n \n633. SHRI C.MAHENDRAN: \n    \n  \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the third unit of Kalpakkam Nuclear Pow er Plant has been completed and \nfilling of fuel is proposed to be started very soon; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether after completion, the third unit is lik ely to produce 500 MW electricity and it \nhas been decided to allot only 250 MW to the State of Tamil Nadu; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons the refor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVANCES & PENSIONS AND \nPRIME MINISTER’S OFFICE (DR.JITENDRA SINGH):     \n \n \n(a) & (b)  Yes, Sir. Construction of 500 MWe Protot ype Fast Breeder Reactor (PFBR) at   \nKalpakkam, Tamil Nadu is completed and commissionin g is in advanced stage. \nFuel loading in this reactor is proposed to be take n up after obtaining clearance \nfrom Atomic Energy Regulatory Board (AERB), prior to first approach to criticality. \n \n(c) & (d)  Yes, Sir, Prototype Fast Breeder Reactor  will generate 500 MWe. Allocation of \nPower from PFBR, made by Ministry of Power is as follows.  \n    Tamil Nadu – 151.8 MWe \n Andhra Pradesh – 146.9 MWe \nKarnataka – 84.4 MWe \n Kerala – 35.7 MWe \n Pondicherry – 6.2 MWe \n Unallocated – 75.0 MWe  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |634", "t": "Prefabricated Houses for Homeless", "head": "                \n \n  GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 634 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON APRIL 27, 2016 \n \nPREFABRICATED HOUSES FOR HOMELESS \n \n \nNo. 634       SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AN D URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether there is any scheme to provide prefabricated houses \nfor the homeless in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  the cost of each such prefabricated house; and  \n(c)  the number of such houses provided to the homeless in the \nState of Gujarat? \n                                                       ANSWER \n THE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n      [SHRI M. VENKAIAH NAIDU] \n (a): There is no separate scheme to provide prefabricated houses \nfor homeless in the country. Howeve r, under Pradhan Mantri Awas \nYojana -Housing for All (Urban) mission, a Technology Sub-mission \nhas been set up to facilitate a doption of modern,  innovative and \ngreen technologies and building ma terials for faster and quality \nconstruction of houses.  \n \n(b): The cost of pref abricated construction would vary depending \nupon the place of constructio n, type of prefab system, \nspecification used for the building and the scale of construction.  \n \n(c): No proposal to provide pref abricated houses to the homeless \nhas been received from the State of Gujarat.  \n \n******\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |635", "t": "Funds under TDP", "head": " \n1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY \nDEPARTMENT OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.635 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27/04/2016 \n \nFUNDS UNDER TDP \n \n635. SHRI ASADUDDIN OWAISI: \n \nWill the Minister of SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY òãðìððÐð ¡ðøÜ ÑßðøÌðð÷ò±ð¨îó Ùðüëðó \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the funds allocated, sanctioned and utilised under Technology Development \nProgramme (TDP) including facilitating centers/cells during the last three years and the \ncurrent year, year-wise and head-wise; \n(b) whether keeping in view the competition in patents from different countries, the \nGovernment proposes to allocate more funds to patents facilitating cells; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof and further steps taken/being taken by the Government \nto allocate more funds to such cells? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY \nAND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES \n(SHRI Y. S. CHOWDARY) \nòãðìððÐð ¡ðøÜ ÑßðøÌðð÷ò±ð¨îó ÙðüëððâðÚð Ùð÷ü Üð¸Úð Ùðüëðó +ÉÉè® ÑðöÆãðó òãðìððÐð ÙðüëððâðÚð Ùð÷ü Üð¸Úð Ùðüëðó \n (gÉÉÒ ´ÉÉ<Ç. AºÉ. SÉÉèvÉ®ÉÒ) \n (a) The funds allocated, sanctioned and utilized under Technology Development \nProgramme (TDP) during the last three years and Current year are given below: \n \n               Budget Heads 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \nFunds allocated (Rs. In Crores) \n1. Office Expenses - 0.08 0.20 0.20 \n2. Grant-in-Aid \ngeneral \n51.50 60.46 88.16  119.76 \n3. Contributions 0.16 0.18 0.20 0.20 \n4. Grant for creation \nof \nCapital Assets \n56.34 58.88 61.60 79.84 \n5. NECTAR - 11.67 2.00 - \n TOTAL 108.00 131.27 152.16 200.00 \n \n \n \n \n\n \n2 \n \nFunds sanctioned/utilized (Rs. In Crores) \n1. Office Expenses - 0.07 0.09  \n2. Grant-in-Aid \ngeneral \n50.87 60.30 75.77  \n3. Contributions 0.12 0.13 0.12  \n4. Grant for creation \nof \nCapital Assets \n55.35 58.43 52.02  \n5. NECTAR - 11.67 2.00  \n TOTAL 106.34 130.59 130.00  \n \nThe funds allocated, sanctioned and utilized under Patent Facilitation Programme (PFC) \nduring the last three years and Current year are given below: \n \n 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \nFunds allocated  \n(Rs. In Crores) \n 2.50  2.50   3.50  Funds are not \nyet allocated  \nFunds \nsanctioned/utilized \n(Rs. In Crores) \n2.16 1.62 3.40  \n \n(b) Department of Science and Technology (DST) has been implementing Patent \nFacilitation Programme through Patent Facilitation Cell (PFC) established in 1995 at \nTechnology Information Forecasting and Assessment Council (TIFAC), an autonomous \ninstitute of DST. In addition, 26 Patent Information Centers (PICs) have been established \nin various states for creating awareness and extending assistance on protecting \nIntellectual Property Rights (IPR) including patent, copyright, geographical indication \netc. for academi c sector. These PICs are also creating Intellectual Property Cells in \nUniversities (IPCU) in their respective states. As of now, 71 IPCU’s have been created in \ndifferent universities of the states. Government has not proposed to allocate more funds \nto Patent Facilitation Cell. \n \n(c) Does not arise.  \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |636", "t": "Accrual Accounting System", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.636 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nACCRUAL ACCOUNTING SYSTEM \n \n636.    SHRI SHASHI THAROOR: \n \n          Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has successfully completed the pilot \nstudy for the introduction of the accrual accounting system in \nthe Ajmer Division in the North Western Railway Zone (NWRZ); \n (b)  if so, the details thereof including the proposed time period for \nextending the comprehensive accrual accounting plan to all \nRailway Zones, including the Southern Railway Zone (SRZ); \n(c)  whether the Government has undertaken employee training \nprogrammes to equip employees of the Railways with the skill \nrequired for accounting under the accrual system; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n (SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n(a) & (b) The pilot study for introduction of accrual accounting on \nAjmer Division of North Western Railway is nearing completion with its \n\nrelated reports and manuals. The time period for extending the plan to \nall Railway Zones including the Southern railway zone ha s not yet \nbeen finalized. \n(c) Yes, Madam. Training on various aspects of accrual accounting is \nbeing imparted to railway officials and staff at Centralized Training \nAcademy for Railway Accounts (C -TARA), Secunderabad under a \nstructured training module. \n(d) Each month Supervisors and Officers from all over Indian Railways \nare being regularly trained in accrual accounting. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |637", "t": "Technical Snag in Delhi Metro", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 637 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON APRIL 27, 2016 \n \nTECHNICAL SNAG IN DELHI METRO \n \nNo. 637 SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \n   \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Delhi Metro has been suffering technical snags \nregularly and if so, the number of times technical snags have \nbeen reported during the last year and the current year, so far; \n \n(b) whether any assessment has been made in regard to the nature \nof technical problems causing halts/delays; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the remedial measures taken in this \nregard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT) \n[SHRI M. VENKAIAH NAIDU] \n \n \n(a): Delhi Metro Rail Corporation Ltd. (DMRC) has informed that \nduring the calendar year 2016, only two incidents of major technical \nfailure have occurred whereas during the calendar year 2015 there \nwere nine such cases. \n(b)&(c): DMRC has informed that each failure is analysed and \nremedial action taken either through special drives or regular \npreventive maintenance of assets to avoid recurrence of such \nincidents. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |638", "t": "Legal Emigration", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.638 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nLEGAL EMIGRATION \n \n†638.   SHRIMATI KAMLA DEVI PAATLE: \n \nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether there is huge demand of domestic helps and house nurses abroad; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether any steps have been taken by the Government to make systematic and legal \nemigration for them; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; \n(e)  whether the G overnment has conducted any study to know the working conditions of \ndomestic helps, nurses and other Indian workers working in foreign countries;  \n(f)  if so, the details thereof; and \n(g)  if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a) & (b) The Government regulates the emigration of Emigration Check R equired (ECR) \ncategory passport holders going for employment to the 18 ECR countries, namely \nAfghanistan, Bahrain, Indonesia, Iraq, Jordan, Kuwait, Lebanon, Libya, Malaysia, Oman, \nQatar, Saudi Arabia, Sudan, South Sudan, Syria, Thailand, UAE and Yemen. It is evident from \ndata on workers granted emigration clearance over the past three years, (given in Annexure) \nthat there is a demand for Indian workers abroad. This data includes details of domestic \nmaids and house nurses who would have gone abroad after taking emigration clearance.  \n \n(c) & (d) The Emigration Act, 1983, regulates the terms and conditions of overseas \nemployment and seeks to protect and safeguard the interests of Indian workers going \noverseas for employment on contractual basis. \n \nSome of the specific measures for the protectio n of female domestic household \nworkers in the above category include a minimum age restriction of 30 years for eligibility to \nmigrate and mandatory pre -attestation of the employment contract by the Indian Mission.  \nThe employer is also required to provide a prepaid mobile phone and make a deposit of a \nsecurity amount of US$2500 with the Indian Mission. \n        \nThe Government has signed MOUs/Agreements on labour with major labour receiving \ncountries, namely, UAE, Qatar, Oman, Bahrain, Saudi Arabia, Jordan an d Malaysia, to lay \ndown the framework for bilateral cooperation for the protection and welfare of workers.   \n \n(e) to (g) The erstwhile Ministry of Overseas Indian Affairs has partly funded the Centre \nfor Development Studies, Kerala for publishing the following papers concerning overseas \nmigrants: \n \n(i)  Transnational Masculinity: Indian Nurses’ Husbands in Ireland. \n(ii)  Nurse emigration from Kerala: “Brain circulation” or “Trap”. \n \nThese papers were included in a book relating to social costs of migrati on. \n*****  \n\n \n \n \nAnnexure \n \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of Countries Emigration Clearances given \n \n01.04.2013 -\n31.03. 2014 \n01.04.2014 -\n31.03. 2015 \n01.04.2015 -\n31.03. 2016 \n \n1  Afghanistan                  200 76 64 \n2  Bahrain                      15073 16314 13644 \n3  Indonesia                    34 19 4 \n4  Iraq                         7379 1171 - \n5  Jordan                       1638 2113 2090 \n6  Kuwait                       76474 78396 67414 \n7  Labanon                      249 393 304 \n8  Libya                        415 0 - \n9  Malaysia                     22014 25548 17906 \n10  Oman                         57337 57458 86165 \n11  Qatar                        79631 74839 49638 \n12  Sudan                       217 194 8 \n13  Syria                        5 0 - \n14 Saudi Arabia  339857 340001 265170 \n15  Thailand                     27 50 2 \n16  United Arab \nEmirates                          220392 229504 201192 \n17  Yemen                        5 3 - \n Total 820947 826079 703601 \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |639", "t": "Regular Surveys", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 639 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 27th APRIL, 2016 \n \nREGULAR SURVEYS \n \n639. DR. K. GOPAL: \n \nWill the Minister of STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME \nIMPLEMENTATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to move a more regular \nsurvey, conducted annually, with some reports coming out \non a quarterly basis; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the design of the new annual employment survey \nhas been finalised on the basis of a peer and stakeholders \nreview of the pilot survey's outcomes; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR MINISTRY OF \nSTATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION AND MINISTER OF \nSTATE FOR MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS [GENERAL (Dr.) V.K. \nSINGH (Retd.)] \n \n(a):  Yes, Madam.   \n \n(b): At present, widely used comprehensive data on labour market \n(i.e., employment and unemployment statistics) are available only from \nEmployment and Unemployment Surveys conducted by NSSO once in \nfive years.  On the recommendation of the National Statistical \nCommission (NSC) , a proposal to carry out Employment and \nUnemployment S urvey at more frequent intervals , namely,  Periodic \n\nLabour Force Survey (PLFS), has been included in 12 th Five Year Plan \n(2012-17) of the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementat ion \nto measure changes in the labour market on quarterly/annual basis.  \n \nThe main objective of the Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS) is \nto measure quarterly changes of various indicators of the labour \nmarket in urban areas as well as to generate the annu al estimates of \ndifferent labour force indicators both in rural and urban areas. The \nestimates will be brought out at State/UT and all India level. The \nAnnual estimates of the parameters from the PLFS (both rural and \nurban areas) will broadly cover (i) Lab our Force Participation Rate \n(LFPR), Worker Population Ratio (WPR) & Unemployment Rate  (UR), (ii) \nDistribution of workers by industry and occupation, and (iii) Average \nearnings of workers. The quarterly estimates of changes for urban \nareas will inter -alia cover Labour Force Participation Rate (LFPR), \nWorker Population Ratio (WPR) and Unemployment Rate (UR). \n \n(c) & (d) : Yes, Madam. Government has constituted a Standing \nCommittee on Labour Force Statistics (SCLFS) under the Chairmanship \nof Dr. S. P. Mukherjee with eminent experts/ stakeholders and \nrepresentatives from major user Ministries/Departments of the \nGovernment for the purpose of coordinating and integrating labour \nforce surveys in the country and to recommend sampling design for \nsurveys on labour force statistics, including PLFS, by taking into \naccount related international and national standards and other \naspects.  The Committee has finalized the sampling design for PLFS \nand it has been submitted for the approval of the National Statistical \nCommission.  \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |64", "t": "New Science Institutes", "head": " \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.64  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nPRODUCTION OF GARMENTS \n \n*64.  SHRI AJAY NISHAD: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES  वस्त्र मंरी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether any target has been fixed  for production of \ntextile/garments in the country; \n \n(b)  if so, the details of target fixed and achievements made during each \nof the last three years and the current year, State/UTwise; \n \n(c)  whether the textile workers in the  country are facing problems due \nto recession in the sector, if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d)   the remedial steps taken or  proposed to be taken by the \nGovernment to protect the interest of textile workers? \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज् य मंरी (स्त्वतंर प्रभार) (श्री संतोष कुमार गंगवार) \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF TEXTILES \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *64 FOR ANSWER ON 28.04.2016 \nREGARDING ‘PRODUCTION OF GARMENTS’ BY SHRI \nAJAY NISHAD:. \n \n(a) & (b):   Production of textiles and garments is predominantly in the \nprivate sector.  Therefore, the Government does not prescribe specific \nproduction targets for the textile and garmenting industry. \n   \n(c) & (d):   Increasing production of textiles garments through various \npolicy interventions is among the foremost objectives of the Government. \nThe production of major textile items during the last three years and the \ncurrent year is given in the table below:- \n \nProduction and Export of textile/garment items \nItem 2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 \n2015-16 \n(Apr-\nFeb) \nSpun Yarn  \n(Million Kg) 4868 5309 5488 5189 \nMan made fibre \n(Million Kg) 1263 1307 1344 1232 \nMan made filament yarn \n(Million Kg) 1371 1293 1248 1069 \nFabrics (including Khadi, \nwool & silk) (Million Sq. \nMeter) \n61949 62624 64333 59396 \nExports (Million US $) \nincluding Garments 34929 40852 41606 36086 \n \nThe details of State -wise production of spun yarn, man -made fibre \nand filament yarn are given in Annexure-I & II respectively.   \n \nThe government has not received any report of textile workers \nfacing problems due to recession in the sector. The data available on \nproduction and export shows an increasing trend during the last three \nyears and current year.  \n  \nIn order to strengthen the textile industry in the country and to \nprotect the interests of textile workers, Government has launched various \npolicy initiatives and schemes, such as Technology Upgradation Fund \nScheme (TUFS), Schemes for the development of the Powerloom Sector, \nSchemes for Technical textiles, Scheme for Integrated Textile Parks \n(SITP), Integrated Skill Development Scheme.  These are intended to \nstrengthen the Indian textile sector and generate employment \nopportunities for textile workers. \n \n  \n\n \nAnnexure -I \nSTATE-WISE PRODUCTION OF SPUN YARN ('000 kg.) \nState/ UT 2012-13 2013-14  2014-15  \n2015-16 \n(Apr-Feb) \n(prov.) \nSTATES         \nAndhra Pradesh 377209 443539 476100 404626 \nGoa 132 132 132 121 \nGujarat 224057 223576 225631 201996 \nHaryana 182408 206973 234994 232640 \nHimachal Pradesh 133382 144776 148772 140638 \nJammu & Kashmir 46062 47442 50390 46826 \nJharkhand 675 623 643 589 \nKarnataka 37837 37750 34109 28473 \nKerala 32964 31713 34010 34962 \nMadhya Pradesh 353106 401067 432355 417302 \nMaharashtra 402841 424007 414931 388127 \nOrissa 1833 1966 1787 1646 \nPunjab 761117 818424 845014 789085 \nRajasthan 428209 443185 469395 446849 \nTamil Nadu 1624617 1819309 1891255 1770843 \nTelangana N.A. N.A. N.A. 72088 \nUttaranchal 60852 60836 56943 47091 \nUttar Pradesh 63195 68673 66938 66822 \nWest Bengal 27207 27500 24655 20996 \nUNION TERRITORIES         \nDadra Nagar Haveli 99504 95183 68880 63558 \nDaman & Diu 3166 3794 350 3784 \nPondicherry 7238 8994 10356 9962 \nTOTAL 4867612 5309464 5487640 5189026 \n NA : Not available. Data from Telangana from June 2015 \nonwards \n  \n  \n\n \n**** \n      \nAnnexure-II  \nMan-made Fibre / Filament Yarn State-wise Production \n        \n(Mn.Kg.) \nState Name \n2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16                       \n(Apr-Feb) (P) \nFibre Yarn Fibre Yarn Fibre Yarn Fibre Yarn \nAndhra Pradesh   - 2.92 - 2.92 - 2.92 - 2.67 \nDadra Nagar \nHaveli   1.73 101.44 1.73 153.21 1.73 180.35 1.58 169.28 \nDaman & Diu   - 130.86 - 130.86 - 130.86 - 119.96 \nGujarat   597.12 634.88 621.97 593.04 620.47 536.58 583.35 497.84 \nKarnataka   58.73 - 82.48 - 86.52 - 80.89 - \nMadhya Pradesh   151.53 - 155.01 - 153.65 - 115.44 - \nMaharashtra   264.27 448.90 233.79 378.19 268.76 365.23 237.24 259.64 \nOrissa   15.79 - 15.79 - 15.79 - 14.48 - \nPunjab   108.69 12.33 126.27 12.83 122.11 14.11 123.16 13.47 \nUttar Pradesh   65.53 32.35 69.73 15.45 74.84 11.57 76.26 0.04 \nWest Bengal   - 7.19 - 6.92 - 6.14 - 6.05 \nTotal 1,263 1,371 1,307 1,293 1,344 1,248 1,232 1,069 \nP=provisional \n        "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |640", "t": "Shortage of Staff in CIC", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVANCES AND PENSIONS \n(DEPARTMENT OF PERSONNEL & TRAINING) \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 640 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016) \n \nSHORTAGE OF STAFF IN CIC \n \n640. SHRI R. GOPALAKRISHNAN: \n \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Central Information Commission (CIC) is facing acute shortage of staff; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(c)  whether the staff of CIC are being appointed through deputation of personnel from \nother Ministries/Departments/ Organizations; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(e)  the steps taken/being taken to fill up the vacancies in CIC? \n \nANSWER \n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions and Minister \nof State in the Prime Minister’s Office. (DR. JITENDRA SINGH) \n(a) & (b):   No, Madam. Out of total 160 sanctioned posts in various categories in the Central \nInformation Commission (CIC), 38 posts had been filled up by regular officers and staff \nthrough deputation and direct recruitment. Balance posts have been filled -up on outsourced \nbasis and through contractual appointments till such time regular recruitments are made.  \n(c) to (e):  Filling-up of vacancies in various categories of posts in CIC is an ongoing process. \nAs per the Recruitment Rules (RRs) of the CIC, certain percentage of the sanctioned strength \nof various categories of posts is filled up on deputation b asis from other \nMinistries/Departments/Organisations. Advertisements are issued from time to time by CIC \nto fill up the posts from deputation quota, as per the procedure. \nFurther, appointments through direct recruitment to certain categories of posts are \ndone by placing indent with Staff Selection Commission (SSC).   \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |641", "t": "Implementation of DFCS and SFTO", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO. 641 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \n \nIMPLEMNTATION OF DFCS AND SFTO \n \n641.        SHRI P.R. SUNDARAM:       \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   whether the R ailways unveiled a policy to implement  the Dedicated \nFreight Corridor Scheme (DFCS) and Special Freight Train Operator  (SFTO)  \nscheme;   \n \n(b)   if so, the details thereof and the performance details under the DFCS \nand SFTO since the  inception of the scheme; \n \n(c)   the details of contai ner train operators given licenc e to operate under \nthe DFCS and SFTO schemes and their performance in container traffic; \n \n(d) whether the Government’s innovative scheme Automobile Freight Train \nOperator (AFTO) scheme to incre ase I ndian Railway’s  market share in \ntransportation of automobile have attracted adequate investments from \nprivate players; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and  the investments made by automobile \ncompanies under this scheme since introduction? \n \nANSWER \n \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n \n \n \n \n    (SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n \n \n(a) to (c):  Presently two Dedicated Freight Corridors (DFC), one on the  \nWestern route (Jawaharlal Nehru Port to Dadri) and another on the Eastern \nroute (Ludhiana to Dankuni), have been taken up for implementation. In Rail \nBudget 2016 -17, the following three freight corridors: - (i) East West \nCorridor (Kolkata-Mumbai), (ii) North -South Corridor(Delhi-Chennai) and (iii) \nEast Coast Corridor ( Khargpur-Vijaywada) have been proposed. \n…2.. \n\n-2- \n \n    To increase rail share of the non -traditional commodities like \nmolasses, fly ash, edible oil, caustic soda, chemical, petrochemicals, \nalumina & bulk cement, Special Freight Train Operator (SFTO) Scheme was \nlaunched in the year 2010 and amended in  the years 2013 and 2014. \n        M/s. Fourcee Infrastructure Equipment Private Limited and M/s. Jindal \nSteel and Power Limited have been registered under SFTO Scheme. Three \nrakes have been inducted by M/s. Jindal Steel Power Limited. \n       Container Train Operators cannot operate under SFTO Scheme. \nHowever, 17 container train operators including CONCOR have been \nlicensed for container operation and they can operate on the DFCs as well. \n     Container rail traffic has grown at an average rate of 12 .7% per annum \nfrom 2007-2008 to 2014-2015. \n \n(d) & (e): Two firms viz. M/s Maruti Suzuki India Ltd. and M/s. APL Logistics \nVascor Automotive Pvt. Ltd. have registered under the Automobile Freight \nTrain Operator (AFTO) Scheme and have been given approval for  \nprocurement of six rakes  each of Automobile carrier wagons (BCACBM)  \ninvolving an investment of  ` 80 crores approximately. So far, three rakes \nprocured by M/s Maruti Suzuki India Ltd. and five rakes by M/s. APL Logistics \nVascor Automotive Pvt. Ltd. have  been put up to use on Indian Railways \nsystem.   \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |642", "t": "Rail Line Connectivity", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 642                                                                                    \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nRAIL LINE CONNECTIVITY \n    \n642.   SHRI NISHIKANT DUBEY: \n      Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has any proposal for rail line connectivity to \nPirpainti, Deoghar, Hansdiha, Godda, Vikramshila, Katoria, Pakur, Chitra \nand Basukinath;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI  MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a)  to  (c):    A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( c) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 642 BY SHRI NISHIKANT DUBEY TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 27.04.2016 REGARDING RAIL LINE \nCONNECTIVITY. \n \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam. Details of new lines projects taken up by Railway \nwith a view to connecting Pirpainti, Deoghar, Hansdiha, Godda, \nVikramshila, Katuria, Pakur, Chitra and Basukinath are as under:-  \nS.No. Name of Project Year Anticipated Cost \n(In crores of `) \n1 Deoghar-Sultanganj including B anka-\nBarahat and Banka -Bhitiah Road \n(149.5 km) \n2000-01 1315 \n2 Sultanganj-Katuria via Asarganj, \nTarapur & Belhar) (75 km) \n2007-08 939 \n3 Hansdiha-Godda (30 km) 2011-12 267 \n4 Pirpainti-Jasidih (97 km) 2013-14 916 \n \nIn addition three more new line projects namely (i) Godda -Pakur (80 km), \n(ii) Chitra -Basukinath (37 km) & (iii) Vikramshila -Katareah (Pirpainti -\nNaugachia) (18 km) at anticipated cost of `1723, `860 & `1601 crores \nrespectively have been included in Railway Budget 2016 -17 subject to \nrequisite approvals. \n(c): Does not arise. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |643", "t": "High Resolution Satellite", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n DEPARTMENT OF SPACE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 643 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, APRIL 27, 2016 \n \nHIGH RESOLUTION SATELLITE \n643.  SHRI RAGHAV LAKHANPAL: \n Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether high resolution satellites capable of continuously \nmonitoring major swathes along India's borders have been \nlaunched in the last two years and if so, the details thereof; \nand \n(b)  whether these satellites are transmitting continuous and \nuninterrupted live pictures and if so, the details thereof ? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PG & \nPENSIONS AND IN THE PRIME MINISTER’S OFFICE \n(DR. JITENDRA SINGH): \n(a)  No Madam. \n(b) Does not arise.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |644", "t": "Funds under Maulana Azad Sehat Scheme", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 644 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nFUNDS UNDER MAULANA AZAD SEHAT SCHEME \n644. SHRI BADRUDDIN AJMAL: \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of funds allocated/utilised under “Maulana Azad Sehat Scheme” during each \nof the last three years and the current year; \n(b) the number of students provided with “Sehat Card” along with the number of doctors and \nnurses deployed for the scheme during  the above period. State -wise and Institution-wise; \nand \n(c) the steps taken by the Government for effective implementation of the scheme? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI)  \n(a) to(c) The Maulana Azad Sehat Scheme has been merged with the Nai Manzil Scheme . As \nsuch, no fund has been allocated and no „Sehat Card‟ issued. \n                  \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |645", "t": "Thirty Metre Telescope", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n DEPARTMENT OF SPACE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 645 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, APRIL 27, 2016 \n \nTHIRTY METRE TELESCOPE \n645.  SHRI JAYADEV GALLA: \n Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether Thirty Metre  Telescope International Observatory \nBoard has shortlisted Ladakh for setting up world's largest \ntelescope; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  the total cost of the project and the share of India; and \n(d)  the manner in which Thirty Metre  Telescope may assist to \nexplore new vistas in the universe? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PG & \nPENSIONS AND IN THE PRIME MINISTER’S OFFICE \n(DR. JITENDRA SINGH): \n(a)&(b) The Thirty Meter Telescope (TMT) project is the joint \nresponsibility of the Department of Science & Technology \n(DST) and the Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) from \nIndia. As per the information provided by Indian Institute of \nAstrophysics (IIA) under DST, the TMT is an international \nproject being funded by scientific organisations of Canada, \nChina, India, Japan and USA. The Evaluation process for an \nappropriate site includes scientific suitability (water vapour \nin the atmosphere, atmospheric turbulence and number of \ncloud-free nights in a year), infrastructure and logistics for \n\nsetting-up of such a large international scientific project.  \nWhile Mauna Kea, Hawaii is the preferred choice for the \nTMT project, given the large investments that have already \nbeen m ade and committed, the project is also looking at \nalternate sites both in the northern and southern \nhemispheres. Hanle, Ladakh is one of the sites being \nevaluated for hosting the telescope.  Hanle being the \nprotected area in the state of J&K, the project r equires \nclearances from State and Central agencies such as \nenvironmental, defence, external affairs and home affairs. \n(c) The total cost of TMT project is about 1.5 billion US dollar s. \nThe Union Cabinet has given its approval for India’s \nparticipation in the Thirty Metre Telescope (TMT) project at \nMauna Kea, Hawaii, USA at a total cost of ` 1299.8 crores \nfrom 2014 -2023. From the Indian side, this will be a joint \nproject of the Department of Science and Technology (DST) \nand the Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) with a DST \nshare of ` 675.25 crores and DAE share of ` 624.55 crores. \n(d) TMT will enable scientists to study fainter objects far away \nfrom us in the Universe, which gives information about early \nstages of evolution of the Universe. Also, it will give us finer \ndetails of not-so-far-away objects like undiscovered planets \nand other object s in the Solar System and planets around \nother stars. TMT being the largest optical and infrared \ntelescope in the northern hemisphere will enable several \ndiscoveries which will surely inspire future generations. \nProject will also provide state -of-the-art h igh end \ntechnologies to the country, which would benefit a number \nof industries and R&D centers in the country. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |646", "t": "ISHUP", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 646 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON APRIL 27, 2016 \n \nISHUP \n \nNo. 646 SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AN D URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the broad features of the Interest Subsidy for Housing the \nUrban Poor (ISHUP) schem e including the economic \nparameters for selection of the beneficiaries; \n \n(b) the number of beneficiaries and the funds s pent since its \ninception, State-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has re ceived any proposals from the \nStates regarding subsidy for providing housing to the poor \nslum dwellers in the urban areas  during each of the last three \nyears and the current year and if  so, the details thereof and \nthe action taken thereon, State-wise; and \n \n(d) the other measures being taken to provide relief to the urban \npoor against rising price of housing in urban areas? \n \n                                             ANSWER \n THE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n[SHRI M. VENKAIAH NAIDU] \n \n(a) to (c): Government of India had introduced the In terest Subsidy \nScheme for Housing and Ur ban Poor (ISHUP) Scheme on \n26.12.2008. Under the Scheme, an interest subsidy of 5 % \nper annum for whole duration of the loan (15-20 years) was \nprovided on loans up to Rs.1.00 lakh extended to  \nCont…p/2.. \n\n-2- \n \nEconomically Weaker Secti on (EWS)/Lower Income Group \n(LIG) beneficiaries by the Primary Lending Institutions \n(PLIs). Maximum loan amount was Rs.1.00 lakh for EWS \nand Rs.1.60 lakh for LIG. The scheme was implemented \nthrough PLIs viz. Banks and Housing Finance Companies. \nNational Housing Bank ( NHB) and Housing & Urban \nDevelopment Corporation Lt d. (HUDCO) were designated \nas the Central Nodal Agencies (CNA) for implementation \nthe scheme. \n \n                 The number of beneficiaries and funds released since its   \ninception, State-wise, is Aannexed. \n \n(d):      Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana – Credit Linked Interest   \nSubsidy: \n \n Ministry of Housing & Pove rty Alleviation (MoHUPA) has \nlaunched the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (Urban) mission \nw.e.f. 25.06.2015.  Unde r the Credit Linked Subsidy \nScheme (CLSS), component of  PMAY (Urban) beneficiaries \nof Economically Weaker Sect ion (EWS) and Low Income \nGroup (LIG) seeking housing loans from Banks, Housing \nFinance Companies and other s uch identified institutions \nwould be eligible for an interest subsidy at the rate of \n6.5% for a maximum tenure of  15 years. The credit linked \nsubsidy will be available only for loan amounts upto ₹6 \nlakh.  The carpet area of h ouses being constructed should \nbe upto 30 square metres and 60 square meters for EWS \nand LIG, respectively, in order to avail of the credit linked \nsubsidy. \n \n \n******* \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE \n \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of State No. of \nBeneficiaries \nAmount of \nSubsidy in Rs. \n1 Andhra Pradesh 9213  68188362 \n2 Assam 6  178373 \n3 Chattisgarh  932  10698238 \n4 Rajasthan 956  32341349 \n5 Karnataka 3491  42597331 \n6 Kerala 1873  48035437 \n7 Madhya Pradesh 44  1383514 \n8 Maharashtra 643  6017117 \n9 Tamil Nadu 1269  19896517 \n10 Uttar Pradesh 8  146895 \n   TOTAL : 18435  229483133 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |647", "t": "Socio-Economic Development of Minority Women/Children", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.647 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nSocio-Economic Development of Minority Women/Children.  \n \n647. SHRI NAGAR RODMAL : \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has implemented any schemes for the Socio-\nEconomic Development of Women  and Children belonging to the minority \ncommunities; \n(b) if so, the State -wise details thereof during each of the last three years  and  \nthe current year; \n(c )the State-wise number of women and children benefited by these schemes   \nduring the current five year plan, scheme-wise; \n(d) whether the Government proposes to launch any new scheme in this \nregard; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) The following schemes of Ministry of Minority Affairs are implemented for \nthe Socio Economic Development of Women and children belonging to \nMinority Communities. \n1. Nai Roshni: This scheme is being implemented from the year 2012 \nwith the objective to empower and instill confidence among minority \nwomen including women from other communities (not exceeding 25%) \nby providin g knowledge, tools and techniques for interacting with \nBanks and other institutions. \n2. Maulana Azad Scholarship for Meritorious Girls  students: This \nscheme recognizes, promotes and assists meritorious girls’ students \nbelonging to economically weaker section of minorities. Scholarships \n\nare given for payment of school/college fee, purchase of syllabus \nbooks, purchase of stationery/equipment required for the course & \npayment of Boarding/Lodging charges. \n3. Integrated Child Development Services(ICDS ) under 15PP : \nUnder the scheme, 2.41 lacs  Aaganwadi centers have been \noperationalized in the Minority Concentration Areas. The benefits are \ngiven to the children in the age group of 1 -6 years and for pregnant \nwomen and lactating mothers.  \n4. Besides, 555 Kasturba Gand hi Balika Vidyalayas (KGBV) have been \noperationalized in areas with substantial minority population for \nenrollment of minority girls. \n5. Women’s hostels are also being constructed in the areas with \nsubstantial minority population for accommodation of minority girls. \n \nIn rest of the schemes, like Pre -Matric, Post Matric, Merit –cum Means \nScholarship, Maulana Azad National Fellowship, Free Coaching, Nay i \nUdaan (Fin ancial support to Minority candidates clearing Prelims \nExaminations conducted by UPSC, SSC, and State Public Service \nCommission), NayaSavera – Free Coaching and Allied Scheme, Padho \nPradesh – scheme of Interest Subsidy on Educational Loans for Overseas \nStudies for the Students belong ing to the Minority Communities, 30% is \nreserved for Women/girls. \n \n(b) &(c )  The State -wise details of the scheme Na i Roshni, Maulana  Azad \nScholarship for Meritorious Girls students and ICDS under 15PP \nduring the last three year s and the current year  and the State -wise no. of \nwomen and children benefited by the scheme during the cu rrent five year \nplan  is placed at Annexure I ,II & I II. State-wise figures in respect of \nKasturba Gandhi Balika  Vidyalayas (KGBV) and Women’s hostels are not \navailable. \n \n(d)& (e) There is no proposal at present to launch any new scheme.   \n      *****  \n\nAnnexure to LSPQ No 647 asked by Shri Nagar Rodmal to be answered on 27.04.2016                 Annexure-I Nai Roshani  \nSr. No. States Fund Release \n(in Rs.Crores)\nTrainees \nCovered \nFund Release \n(in Rs.Crores)\nTrainees \nCovered \nFund \nRelease (in \nRs.Crores)\nTrainees \nCovered \nFund Release (in \nRs.Crores)\nTrainees \nCovered \n1 Andhra Pradesh - - 0.47 2650 0.18 475 0.13 725\n2 Arunachal Pradesh - - 0.05 375 - - 0.00\n3 Assam - - 0.64 3650 0.78 3450 1.15 5250\n4 Bihar - - 0.11 750 0.64 4275 0.53 1675\n5 Chattisgarh 0.5 225 0.05 375 0.03 225 0.11 575\n6 Delhi - - 0.2 1125 0.28 1775 0.06\n7 Gujarat 0.25 1125 0.06 375 0.23 1525 0.10 575\n8 Haryana - - 0.04 250 0.09 600 0.04\n9 Himanchal Pradesh - - 0.02 125 0.04 250 0.01\n10 Jammu & Kashmir - - 0.07 500 0.09 350 0.0032\n11 Jharkhand - - 0.19 1100 0.15 725 0.27 1575\n12 Karnataka 0.22 675 0.36 2050 0.23 1250 0.38 1850\n13 Kerala 0.22 350 0.1 625 0.1 700 0.37 2325\n14 Madhya Pradesh 0.6 2500 0.89 4925 1.35 8400 1.36 5450\n15 Maharashtra 0.14 450 0.31 2000 0.31 1950 0.15 550\n16 Manipur 0.55 1300 0.66 3000 0.41 2100 0.35 575\n17 Nagaland - - 0.04 250 0.17 1075 0.00\n18 Odissa 0.18 675 0.22 1375 0.14 575 0.37 1650\n19 Punjab - - 0.25 1250 0.05 350 0.00\n20 Rajasthan 0.58 1775 0.48 2625 0.99 6000 0.55 1725\n21 Sikkim - - - - 0.03 225 0.00\n22 Telangana - - - - 0.03 225 0.03\n23 Tripura - - - - 0.12 850 0.03\n24 Tamil Nadu 0.05 225 0.22 1200 0.09 375 0.15 875\n25 Uttar pradesh 7.09 26225 5.39 25475 6.62 28825 7.81 31225\n26 Uttarakhand 0.55 1425 0.46 1875 0.33 2125 7.81 1175\n27 West Bengal - - 0.56 3200 0.31 1650 0.49 950\nTotal 10.45 36950 11.86 60875 13.78 71075 14.81 58725\nGrand Tot"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |648", "t": "Indians Jailed Abroad", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.648 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nINDIANS JAILED ABROAD \n \n648.  SHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \nSHRI RAHUL KASWAN: \n \n Will the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   the number of Indians currently incarcerated abroad, country -wise \nand the details of those who completed their prison terms and are \ndue to be freed; \n(b)   whether delays in their release has been noted and if so, the details \nthereof and reasons therefor;  \n(c)   whether India is ensuring that the basic rights of Indian prisoners \nabroad are being taken care of and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)   whether the Government is in contact with  foreign governments to \nfind out the exact number of Indian prisoners in foreign jails so as to \nprotect the rights of Indian prisoners and if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a)   Due to the strict provisions of privacy laws,  many countries in \nEurope, United States of America, Canada & Australia do not share \ninformation about Indian nationals who are in their prisons.  However, as \nper the information available, 7213 Indian nationals are lodged in foreign \njails. A list showing the number of Indians lodged in foreign prisons as on \ndate is annexed. \n  \n(b) to (d) As per data available, there are 479 Indian nationals who have \ncompleted their sentences and are waiting for completion of deportation \nformalities. Indian Missions and Post s in those countries have taken up \nwith the respective foreign governments the speeding up of the process, \nincluding issue of final Exit Visas, waiver of penalties imposed on Indian \nworkers, etc. from the concerned authorities and for the early return of \nthe Indian nationals to India. Where required, the Indian Missions and \n\nPosts abroad also provide air tickets for facilitating the return of the \nIndian prisoners who have completed their sentences to India. \n \n Indian Missions/Posts abroad always strive to res pond promptly to \nthe requests/appeals by Indian citizens in distress. As soon as the \ninformation about detention/arrest of an Indian national is received by the \nIndian Mission/Post, it gets in touch with the local Foreign Office and other \nconcerned local a uthorities to get consular access to the \ndetained/arrested Indian national to confirm his or her Indian nationality \nand ensure their welfare. In some countries where pro bono lawyers are \navailable, the Mission arranges such legal assistance to the Indian \nprisoners. Government of India also provides initial legal assistance to \ndistressed Indian nationals in deserving cases. \n \n Steps taken by our Missions include requesting local authorities for \nspeedy trials, seeking remission of sentence, providing advice an d \nguidance in legal and other matters, ensuring fair and humane treatment \nin foreign jails, issue of emergency certificates and repatriation to India of \nthose who are released. In some countries, our Mission also requests the \nlocal government for grant of amnesty to the arrested Indians and if any \nmercy petition for remission of sentences is received from the family \nmembers of the Indian prisoners, the Mission forwards them to the local \nauthorities for consideration. India has signed Treaties for Transfer o f \nSentenced Persons with 35 countries, under which Indian prisoners have \nbeen brought back to India from some of these countries. \n \n*****  \n\nAnnexure \n \n \nIndians Jailed Abroad \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of Country No. of \nprisoners \nNo. of Indian prisoners who have completed \ntheir sentence and are due to be freed \n1 Angola  2   \n2 Armenia  2   \n3 Australia  63   \n4 Austria  5   \n5 Azerbaijan  1   \n6 Bahrain  97 29 \n7 Bangladesh  122 57 \n8 Belgium  24   \n9 Bhutan  61   \n10 Brunei Darussalam  2   \n11 Canada  21   \n12 China  107   \n13 Colombia  5   \n14 Cuba  2   \n15 Cyprus  2   \n16 Denmark  8   \n17 Egypt  5   \n18 Ethiopia 2   \n19 Fiji  1   \n20 France  48   \n21 Germany  6   \n22 Ghana  6   \n23 Guatemala  3   \n24 Indonesia  21   \n25 Iran  26   \n26 Ireland  1   \n27 Israel  11   \n28 Italy  137   \n29 Japan  7   \n30 Jordan  9   \n31 Kenya  3   \n32 Korea 3   \n33 Kuwait  383   \n34 Kyrgyzstan  2   \n35 Libya 1   \n36 Lebanon  8   \n37 Madagascar  3   \n38 Malaysia  356 201 \n39 Maldives  26   \n40 Mauritius  9   \n41 Mexico  2   \n\n42 Myanmar  172   \n43 Nepal  859   \n44 New Zealand  13   \n45 Niger  1   \n46 Nigeria  13   \n47 Oman  187   \n48 Pakistan  230 173 \n49 Panama  3 1 \n50 Philippines  5   \n51 Portugal  4   \n52 Qatar  87   \n53 Republic of Korea   2   \n54 Russian Federation  4   \n55 Saudi Arabia  1697 14 \n56 Senegal  1   \n57 Seychelles  2   \n58 Singapore  115   \n59 Slovak Republic 2   \n60 South Africa  9   \n61 Spain  21   \n62 Sri Lanka  32   \n63 Tanzania  1   \n64 Thailand  83   \n65 United Arab Emirates  1143 4 \n66 United Kingdom  360   \n67 USA  560   \n68 Uzbekistan  1   \n69 Yemen  2   \n70 Zambia  1"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |649", "t": "Premium Tatkal Tickets", "head": "        GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n         MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n        LOK SABHA \n         UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 649 \n         TO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \n           PREMIUM TATKAL TICKETS \n \n \n           649.  SHRI ANTO ANTONY: \n       \n      Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Railways has started Premium Tatkal Ticket facilities \nfor passengers; \n(b) if so, the details thereof including the salient features of Premium \nClass Tickets; \n(c) the revenue generated through Premium Class Tickets so far; \n(d) whether at some point of time the Premium Class Ticket fares are \nhigher than Air fares; \n(e) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n(f) the steps taken by the Government to reduce the fares of Premium \nTatkal Tickets? \n \n      ANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n                                        (SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n(a):  Yes, Madam. \n(b): With effect from 01.10.2014, in some  of the selected trains \nidentified by Zonal Railways which are high in demand, 50% of the \nexisting accommodation under Tatkal Quota has been earmarked as \nPremium Tatkal Quota and is being booked on dynamic pricing. This \nis a distance -slab based fare scheme  where the fare increases by \n20% after each slab of 10% berths are sold subject to maximum three \ntimes of tatkal fare.  \n--2/- \n\n \n-2- \n \n(c): The total revenue generated since inception of Premium Tatkal \nQuota upto March, 2016 is `1195/- Crores approximately.  \n(d) & (e): In view of variable fare structure, the fare may or may not \nbe higher than Air fares. \n(f): At present, there is no such proposal. \n******* \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |65", "t": "Housing Facility for Beedi Workers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 65 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016  \n \nHOUSING FACILITY FOR BEEDI WORKERS \n \n†65. SHRI SADASHIV LOKHANDE: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government has taken any steps to provide housing \nfacility to beedi workers in the country; \n(b)if so, the details thereof; and \n(c)the funds allocated as on date for the housing facility of beedi \nworkers and the number of beedi workers likely to  be benefited \nfrom it? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a):   Yes Madam, Ministry of Labour & Employment has taken a step \nto provide housing facility to beedi workers in the country.  \n(b):  A newly Revised Integrated Housing Scheme (RIHS), 2016 with \neffect from  02.03.2016 for beedi workers is being implemented in the \ncountry through the 17 Welfare Commissioners of the Labour Welfare \nOrganisation of the Ministry. The housing subsidy of Rs. 1 ,50,000/- will \nbe disbursed in three installments in the ratio of 25:60:15.  The 1 st \ninstallment of subsidy Rs. 37,500/ - (25 % of subsidy) in advance after \nadministrative approval of Director General (Labour Welfare), the 2 nd \ninstallment of subsidy Rs. 90,000/ - (60% of subsidy) after construction \nreaches the lintel level, and 3rd installment of subsidy Rs. 22,500/- (15% \nof subsidy) on receipt of report of 100% inspection by the \nEngineers/officers of Labour Welfare Organization. \n(c):  The fund of Rs. 55.12 Crore has been allocated in the Financial \nYear 2016 -17 for housing facility to the beedi workers and 14,000 \nworkers (approx) are likely to be benefited from the fund this year.   \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |650", "t": "Agreement with Bahrain", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n                                                            \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.650 \nTO BE ANSWER ON 27.04.2016 \n \nAGREEMENT WITH BAHRAIN \n \n†650.  SHRI KANWAR SINGH TANWAR:  \n \nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n      \n(a)  whether the government has entered into an agreement with \nBahrain to tackle the menace of international terrorism and \nsmuggling of narcotic drugs;   \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the time by when the said  \nagreement will come into effect; and \n(c)  whether the intelligence inputs are also likely to be shared \nunder the said agreement, and if so, the details thereof? \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a)  Yes. \n \n(b) An agreement between the Government of the Republic of \nIndia and the Government of the Kingdom of Bahrain on cooperation \nin combatting international terrorism, trans-national organized crime \nand trafficking in illicit drugs, narcotic an d psychotropic substances \nand precursor chemicals was signed on 2 nd December, 2015 at New \nDelhi.  The agreement is in the process of ratification.  The \nagreement shall come into force from the date of exchange of the \ninstruments of ratification.   \n \n(c)  The agreement provides for exchange of information between \nthe competent authorities of the two countries relating to \ninternational terrorism , transnational organized crime and illicit \ndrug trafficking, etc.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |651", "t": "Space Act", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n DEPARTMENT OF SPACE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 651 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, APRIL 27, 2016 \n \nSPACE ACT \n651.  DR. P. VENUGOPAL: \n Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government is considering to come  out with a \nSpace Act soon; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  the objective of the said Space Act; and \n(d)  whether any suggestion has been sought from all concerned \nin this regard and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PG & \nPENSIONS AND IN THE PRIME MINISTER’S OFFICE \n(DR. JITENDRA SINGH): \n(a)&(b) The Department of Space is currently engaged in \npreparation of National Space Act for India for supporting \nthe overall growth of space activities, with en hanced level \nof private sector participation. After consultations with \ninternal experts and experts on space law, a draft version of \nthe Act has been prepared, which has been taken up for \nconsultations.   \n \n \n\n(c)  The objective of this Act is to support the overall growth of \nspace activities in India, with enhanced levels of private \nsector participation  in space activities and services , \nthrough appropriate regulations.  \n(d) Necessary consultations would be made as per the \nprevailing Parliamentary Procedures. Currently, approval \nprocess for pre-legislative consultations on the Draft Space \nAct is being pursued. This would be followed by inter -\nministerial consultations through Draft Cabinet Note.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |652", "t": "Visa to Indian Professionals", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.652 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nVISA TO INDIAN PROFESSIONALS \n \n652.      DR. K. KAMARAJ: \n      \n Will the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the US and European countries had made many changes in their \npolicy towards granting visa to scientists/professionals;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether the Government has asked the US and European countries to relax \nvisa regime for Indian Scientists and IT professionals; and \n(d) if so, the response received by the Government from the US and European \nauthorities in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS  \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a) & (b) While most of the European countries have not made many changes/any \nmajor change in their policy towards granting visa to scientists/professionals, the \nUK and the US have announced a few changes in the recent past. In March 2016, the \nUK Government announce d its decision to accept a majority of the \nrecommendations of the Migration Advisory Committee (MAC) on changes to Tier -2 \nvisa route for those undertaking skilled work in the UK. The key changes to the Tier -\n2 visa route, proposed to be implemented, relate to increase in salary thresholds, \nintroduction of Immigration Skill Charge (ISC) from April 2017 and Immigration \nHealth Surcharge (IHS). With regard to the US, on December 18, 2015, the US \nCongress passed the Omnibus Spending Bill which included doubling o f the L -1 and \nH-1B visa fees for 50: 50 companies. With the legislation in place, these companies \nwould need to pay $4,500 extra for each L -1 visa and $4000 extra for each H-1B visa. \nThe increase in visa fee would be for a period of 10 years and would also  apply to \nvisa extensions. These funds would be used by the US to fund treatment of 9/11 \nterrorist attack victims and biometric tracking system.  \n \n(c) & (d) The Government raises these issues on a regular basis at various \nbilateral fora. These issues are a lso taken up and discussed with the countries \nunder the framework of General Agreement on Trade in Services (GATs). In line with \nthis approach, the issue has been raised with the UK government. With regard to the \nUS, the Government of India has raised its concerns strongly with the US \ninterlocutors at various levels over the last few months. We have conveyed to the US \nGovernment that this provision is discriminatory as it basically targets Indian IT \ncompanies. Prime Minister had raised this issue with the U S President Barack \nObama during their talks over telephone on December 16, 2015. We continue to \nreiterate our serious concerns to the US.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |653", "t": "Stoppage of Trains at Dahod", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 653 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nSTOPPAGE OF TRAINS AT DAHOD \n \n†653. SHRI JASVANTSINH SUMANBHAI BHABHOR: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether in view of the persons of Ratlam and Badodara \nemployed in railway workshop in Dahod district and located at a \ndistance of   115 km. and 146 km. respectively from Dahod, \nproposals for providing  stoppage of Okha -Gorakhpur, Surat -\nMujaffarpur, Bandra -Haridwar, Ajmer -Ernakulam, Gandhidham -\nHowrah, Bandra -Kanpur, Valsad -Haridwar and Bhuj -Shalimar train \nservices passing through Dahod is likely to be provided at this \nstation in Gujarat; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(d) the details of the action taken by the Government thereon and \nthe outcome thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a) to (d)  No, Madam.  Dahod station is presently being served by 33 \npair of Mail/Express trains and 07 pair of Passenger trains which are \nconsidered adequate for the present level of traffic.   \nThere is no proposal to provide stoppage of additional trains at \nDahod as the stoppage is  neither operationally feasible nor \ncommercially justified, at present. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |654", "t": "Visa Provision between India and China", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.654 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nVISA PROVISION BETWEEN INDIA AND CHINA \n \n654.      SHRI V. ELUMALAI: \n      \n Will the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether India has included China in the list of countries which \nhave been extended the facilities of electronics tourist visa on \narrival and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b)  whether China has pressed for lifting restrictions on \nconference and research vis a as well and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(c)  whether China is yet to respond India’s demand to offer a \nsimilar arrangement for its citizens and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(d)  whether China has assured that they are working on a \nmechanism that would ease visa restrictions for Indians; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a) Yes, electronic tourist visa (e -TV) facility has been extended \nto the nationals of China w.e.f. the 30th of July, 2015.  \n \n(b) During official meetings and discussions, the Chinese officials \nhave brought up the issue of delays in obtaining Indian Conference  \nand Research visas from time to time. \n \n(c) to (e) Our Embassy in China has not come across any difficulty \nfaced by Indian nationals in obtaining Chinese visas for \nConference/Research purposes. All visa related matters are \ndiscussed through the mechanism o f “Consular Dialogue” between \nthe two countries on a regular basis. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |655", "t": "Gauge Conversion", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 655 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nGAUGE CONVERSION \n \n†655. SHRI RAHUL KASWAN: \n \n       Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:    \n \n(a) whether the work  related to gauge conversion on Sadulpur-\nHanumangarh section of North Western Railway has been \ncompleted and stations have been constructed in two big villages \nviz., Pahadsar and Shriramgarh of this area;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to construct under bridge or \nfoot over bridge to help villagers to reach the railway station and \nthe platform; and   \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a) to (d): Yes, Madam. Gauge conversion work of Sadulpur -\nHanumangarh section has been completed. The stations at Paharsar \nand Shriramgarh have been constructed. Approach road to \nShriramgarh has already been provided by construction of \nunderpass whereas at  Paharsar, construction of an underpass has \nbeen taken up by the state government. As both the stations are  \nhalt stations having only rail level platforms, foot over bridges have \nnot been provided. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |656", "t": "Delay in Doubling Work", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 656 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nDELAY IN DOUBLING WORK \n \n†656.     SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL: \n                     \n     Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:   \n \n(a) the re asons for delay in doub ling work of around 247 kilomet er \nbroad gauge line between Palanpur to Patan;  \n \n(b) whether the Government has received any complaints with regard \nto damage caused to ‘Rani ki Vav’ located in Patan of Gujarat due \nto operation of trains in the past and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Archaeological Survey of India has registered its \nobjection on the rail line passing through ‘Rani ki Vav’ and if so, the \ndetails thereof and the reaction of the Government thereto;   \n \n(d) whether the Government is formulating any scheme in this regard \nand if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(e) whether the Government has fixed any time limit to complete the \nsaid railway project and if so, the details thereof?  \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n*****    \n \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 656 BY SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 27.0 4.2016 REGARDING DELAY IN \nDOUBLING WORK \n \n(a): Palanpur and Patan are connected by 105 km long broad gauge \nnetwork via Mahesana. No work of doubling on this section has been \nsanctioned and hence, question of delay for completion does not arise.  \n \n(b) : No, Madam. \n \n(c) to (e) : Patan–Bhildi new line project includes renovation of old \nrailway line from Patan to Khalipur (10 km) and 40 km new \nconstruction from Khalipur to Bhildi. The track linking has been \nexecuted in Patan -Khalipur section except for work near ‘Ran i ki Vav’ \nwhich was objected by Archeological Survey of India (ASI). Railway \nhad appro ached National Monument Authority (NMA) for grant of No \nObjection Certificate (NOC) in this re gard, which was denied by them.  \nConsequently, railway suggested an  alternative alignment for which  \n“No Objection Certificate (NOC)” was issued by Archaeological Survey \nof India, New Delhi on 06.05.2015 . Railway accordingly planned and \nstarted execution of project including the process of land acquisition \non the alternative alignme nt. However, Superintending Archeologist, \nVadodara has approached National Monument Authority  for review of \nthe No Objection Certificate (NOC) granted by them.  \n \nAs a result, n o time line has been fixed for completion of Patan -\nBhildi new line project.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |657", "t": "Computerisation of Railway Stations", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.657 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \n \nCOMPUTERISATION OF RAILWAY STATIONS \n†657. DR. VIRENDRA KUMAR: \nWill the Minister of Railways be pleased to state : \n \n(a) the details of railway stations earmarked for \ncomputerisation during the current year, zone wise; and   \n  \n(b)   the details of budgetary allocation made for the purpose \nduring the above period? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n \n(a)  Computerisation projects are implemented centrally and \nare available throughout the country.  Computerised \nPassenger Reservation System has been provided at 3323 \nlocations. Computerised Unreserved ticketing System has \nbeen provided at 5860 locations.     \n(b)  ₹ 336.88 crores have been allocated for computerisation \nduring the financial year 2016-17. \n...... \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |658", "t": "SRESHTA", "head": "        \n         GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n         MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 658 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \nSRESTHA \n \n658.  DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \n         SHRI ANANDRAO ADSUL: \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Research Design and Standards Organisation (RDSO) \nhas not been able to do in -house research work on various \ntechnologies required for upgradation in signalling and \ntelecommunication system, track designing, improvement in rolling \nstock and many such areas and if so, the reasons therefor; \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to create a special unit, \nSpecial Railway Establishment for Strategic Technology and Holistic \nAdvancement (SRESTHA) for conducting in -house research in Railways \nand if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the details of expenditure, manpower both scientific and \nadministrative and infrastructure likely to be required for the \nfunctioning of the new research wing of the Railways; and \n \n(d) the manner in which functioning of the new wing will be different \nfrom the functioning of RDSO? \n                            \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a): Research, Designs and Standards Organisation (RDSO) has been \ncarrying out in -house research o n technologies related to various \nfacets of Railway working.  \n….2/- \n\n \n                                               \n                                             -2- \n \n(b) to (d):  Yes, Madam. Setting up of Special Railway Establishment \nfor Strategic Technology & Holistic Advancement (SRESTHA), to drive \nlong-term research on Railways has been announced as part of the \nbudget speech of Minister for Railways for 2016 -17. Implementation of \nthe Budget item has been taken on hand including concretisation of the \norganisational structure, manpower requirement and manner of \nfunctioning. \n                               \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |659", "t": "Linking Skill Development and Entrepreneurship", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 659\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016\n \n \nLINKING SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND\n \nENTREPRENEURSHIP\n \n \n659.  SHRI S.P. MUDDAHANUME GOWDA:\n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL \nDEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) whether the Government has any scheme for linking skill development and \nentrepreneurship in the country; and\n \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof also indicating the funds allocated in the current finan\ncial \nyear State\n-\nwise including Karnataka?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF\n \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP\n \n(SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY)\n \n \n(\na) & (b) \n \n \nAt present there is no particular scheme for linking skill development \nand entrepreneurship. However, it has been decided to introduce entrepreneurship \norientation modules in all skill development programmes being implemented by the \nMinistry of Skill deve\nlopment and Entrepreneurship such as Pradhan Mantri Kaushal \nVikas Yojna (PMKVY) and in Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs). There is no \nprovision for separate allocation of funds state\n-\nwise for the purpose.\n \n \n***\n****\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |66", "t": "District Mineral Foundation", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.66\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28TH APRIL, 2016\nDEVELOPMENT OF SHIPPING SECTOR\n*66. SHRI AJAY MISRA TENI:\nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to expand/develop the infrastructure in\nshipping sector in the country;\n(b) if so, the details and the present status thereof along with the details of schemes\nproposed/under implementation;\n(c) the locations identified/proposed to be developed under the said schemes along\nwith the financial implications involved therein;\n(d) the extent to which employment opportunities are likely to be generated as a\nresult thereof; and\n(e) whether some foreign countries are also extending their support for the purpose\nand if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF SHIPPING\n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI)\n(a)to(e):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.66 TO BE ANSWERED ON 28TH APRIL, 2016 RAISED\nBY SHRI AJAY MISRA TENI, MP REGARDING “DEVELOPMENT OF\nSHIPPING SECTOR”.\n-----\n(a)to(c):Yes, Madam.   At present 37 projects under Public Private Partnership (PPP) and\n32 non PPP projects involving an investment of Rs.44,585.22 Crores and capacity addition\nof 463.60 Million Tonnes Per Annum (MTPA) have been taken up in twelve Major Ports.\nThe details of these projects are annexed.\n(d) These projects when completed will lead to creation of significant employment\nopportunities.  The extent of new employment opportunity created will depend on the\nnature of the project and level of mechanisation.\n(e) Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) upto 100% under automatic route is allowed in\nthe Port sector.  A number of reputed foreign companies have been participating in the bids\nfor projects in ports, particularly on PPP basis for container terminals and capital dredging\nprojects.\n\nAnnexure -I\nPPP PROJECTS UNDER IMPLEMENTATION\nSl.\nNo\nProject Port Cost\n(Rs. in Crores)\nCap\n(MTPA)\n1 Development of Barge jetty at Bharathi Dock Chennai Port Trust 27.29 1.35\n2 Development of standalone container handling\nfacility with a quay length of 330 m North of NSICT\nTerminal\nJLN Port Trust 600.00 9.60\n3 Development of Container Terminals of 2000 Mtrs\nLength at JNPT (4th Container terminal)\nJLN Port Trust 7915.00 60.00\n4 Special Economic Zone. JLN Port Trust 4000.00 6.00\n5 Upgradation of the existing Non -TNEB Coal\nTerminal developed by M/s. Chettinad International\nCoal Terminal Pvt. Ltd\nKamrajar Port Ltd.\n(Ennore)\n351.08 8.00\n6 Development of LNG Terminal by IOCL Kamrajar Port Ltd.\n(Ennore)\n4512.00 5.00\n7 Development of Container Terminal Kamrajar Port Ltd.\n(Ennore)\n1270.00 16.80\n8 Development of Multi-Cargo Berth Kamrajar Port Ltd.\n(Ennore) 151.00 2.00\n9 Development of Oil Jetty to handle liquid cargo ship\nbunkering Terminal\nKandla Port Trust 233.50 3.39\n10 Development of SPM in OOT Kandla Port Trust 448.00 25.00\n11 Construction of Oil Jetty No. 7 on BOT basis for\nliquid cargo.\nKandla Port Trust 72.00 2.00\n12 Development, operation & maintenance of\nContainer Terminal (Berth 11&12) on BOT basis\nKandla Port Trust 263.00 7.20\n13 Development of Haldia Dock II (North) Kolkata Port Trust 821.40 11.70\n14 Floating Storage & Regasification Unit (FSRU) Kolkata Port Trust 3500.00 4.00\n15 Construction of Offshore Container Berths and\nDevelopment of  terminal on BOT basis at Mumbai\nHarbour\nMumbai Port Trust 2098.56 9.60\n16 Facilities for handling & storage of Bulk Cement\nand Bagging Plant at Petroleum Godown Plot at\nSewree\nMumbai Port Trust 95.00 1.25\n17 Bunkering Terminal Mumbai Port Trust 50.00 2.00\n18 Construction of Deep Draft Coal Berth at Paradip Paradip Port Trust 479.01 10.00\n19 Development of Clean Multi -cargo Berth in\nSouthern Dock\nParadip PortTrust 387.31 5.00\n20 Development of Deep Draft Iron Ore berth. Paradip Port Trust 740.19 10.00\n21 Mechanisation of EQ1 to EQ3 berths Paradip Port Trust 1633.44 30.00\n22 Development of EQ – 1A in East Docks Visakhapatnam Port\nTrust\n313.39 7.36\n\n23 Installation of Mechanised handling facilities for\nfertilizers at EQ 7 in the Inner Harbour\nVisakhapatnam Port\nTrust\n217.58 3.33\n24\nInstallation of Mechanised Iron Ore handling\nfacilities at WQ -1 in the northern arm of Inner\nharbour of VPT for handling Dry bulk cargo and\nModernisation of  Ore Handling Complex\nVisakhapatnam Port\nTrust\n940.00 23.70\n25 Container Terminal expansion Visakhapatnam Port\nTrust\n633.11 7.56\n26 Multi Modal Logistic Hub Visakhapatnam Port\nTrust\n400.00 0.00\n27 Establishment of Container Freight Station through\nexisting BOT operator by VCTPL.\nVisakhapatnam Port\nTrust\n100.00 0.90\n28 Construction of Coal Berth at NBW for NLC –\nTNEB\nVOC Port Trust,\nTuticorin\n49.50 6.30\n29 Construction of North Cargo Berth-II VOC Port Trust,\nTuticorin\n332.16 7.15\n30 Construction of Shallow draft berth for handling\ncement\nVOC Port Trust,\nTuticorin\n84.08 2.67\n31 Development of NCB-IV for handling thermal  coal\n& Copper concentrate\nVOC Port Tru"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |660", "t": "Religious Discrimination of Indians in US", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.660 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nRELIGIOUS DISCRIMINATION OF INDIANS IN US \n \n660.  DR. BHOLA SINGH: \n  \nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of instances of Indians being killed/humiliated in the name of religion  \nor race in USA during the last three years; \n(b) whether hate messages were seen scribbled on the walls of Hindu temples and \ngurudwaras in the US in the recent past and if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the details of steps taken by the Government to stop such incidents in future and \nensure safety and security of Indians abroad?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS  \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a) There are no  clear indications to suggest racial or religious attacks generally \ndirected against Indians in the United States. In the last three years, one incident of an \nIndian national being attacked in the name of religion ha s been brought to the notice of \nthis Ministry. On December 26, 2015, a 68 -year old Indian national, who was wearing a \nturban, was attacked in Fresno, California. The police had arrested the culprits.  There \nhave been some other incidents involving Indian Americans.  \n \n(b) Three incidents of vandal ism of/attack o n Hindu Temples  have come to the \nnotice of this Ministry. The details are as follows: \n \n(i) On February 14, 2015, graffiti was painted on the outer wall of a Hindu Temple \nand Cultural Centr e in Bothell, near Seattle in Washington State . The p olice filed a \nreport in this regard.  \n \n(ii) On February 27, 2015,  the Sanatan Dharma Hindu Temple  located in Kent, near \nSeattle in Washington State was damaged.  Graffiti was also sprayed. Local police was \ninvestigating the matter. No arrests have been made so far.  \n \n(iii) In April 2015, a small tool shed attached to North Texas Hindu Mandir, a temple \nnear Dallas, Texas, was broken into .  The temple walls wer e desecrated with graffiti.  \nFollowing the incident, Dallas Police increased patrolling in the area. \n \n(c)  Government of India is sensitive to such incidents. Missions and Posts remain \nvigilant and closely monitor any untoward incidents. These issues are regularly raised \nduring meetings with the US Government officials. Government brings to the attention \nof US federal and local authorities any incidents seen as ‘hate crimes’ or racial attacks. \nThe representatives of the Indian-American community and members of India Caucus in \nthe US Congress and Senate have been impressed upon to take up such cases of \nserious assault for further investigation. The safety and security of Indians abroad is \namong the top diplomatic priorities for the Government of India. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |661", "t": "New Catering Policy", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 661 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nNEW CATERING POLICY \n \n†661. SHRI AJAY NISHAD:  \n          DR. KIRIT P. SOLANKI:            \n          Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n                    \n(a)  the salient features of new catering policy in the Railways;  \n(b) whether this policy has adequately addressed various complaints \nreceived regarding the deteriorating quality of food being served in various \ntrains;  \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d)  the steps taken to improve the quality of food in Railways? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n \n(a): The Catering Policy 2010, presently in force for management of catering \nservices over Indian Railways was issued in July 2010.  The salient features \nof this Policy include (i) Shifting management and monitoring of all catering \nservices (except Food Plaza, Food  Courts and Fast Food Units) from Indian \nRailways Catering & Tourism Corporation (IRCTC) to Zonal Railways.          \n(ii) IRCTC made responsible for managing the catering services at Food \nPlazas, Food Courts and Fast Food Units. (iii) Zonal Railways to hav e an \neffective and transparent contract awarding, management and monitoring \nsystem as per provisions of Standard Bid Document. (iv) Zonal Railways are \nresponsible for strengthening supervision and monitoring of catering \nservices for maintaining quality and  hygiene of the food served to \n\npassengers and have been empowered to take corrective actions including \nimposition of fines on licensees to mitigate passengers complaints.  \n         However, in a paradigm shift, as announced in Rail Budget  2016 -17, it \nhas been decided to give back the catering services to IRCTC in a phased \nmanner with unbundling catering services by creating a distinction primarily \nbetween food preparation and food distribution.  It has also been decided \nthat there will be no complete handing over of catering service by IRCTC to \nprivate licensees. Zonal Railways will have powers of supervision and \nmonitoring to ensure quality food is served to passengers. \n(b) and (c): Improvement of catering services is an on -going process.  In its \nendeavour to provide quality and hygienic food to the passengers, Railways \nhave developed and operationalized an institutionalized mechanism for \nmonitoring of quality and hygiene of catering services through regular \ninspections at various levels. Th e passenger satisfaction levels are also \nregularly monitored through direct feedback and other means to address \ncatering complaints.   \n(d):  The steps taken to improve the quality of food in Railways inter -alia \ninclude: (i) Introduction of station based e -catering at all A1 and A category \nstations for widening the range of options available to passengers for \nordering food of their choice. (ii) Introduction of precooked food (‘ready to \neat’ meals) in the range of options available to passengers. (iii) Operat ion of \ncentralized Catering Service Monitoring Cell (CSMC)                                    \n(toll free number 1800 -111-321) for prompt redressal of passenger \ngrievances relating to the catering activities and real time assistance to \ntravelling public. (iv) Imposition of penalties in case of deficiencies detected \nin services. (v) Operation of all India Helpline (No.138) for rail -users to lodge \ncomplaints/suggestions regarding food and catering services (vi) A Twitter \nhandle with the address @IRCATERING has  also been made operational to \ncater to the complaints/suggestions with regard to catering services. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |662", "t": "Recruitment of Scientists", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY \nDEPARTMENT OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.662 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27/04/2016 \n \nRECRUITMENT OF SCIENTISTS \n \n662. SHRI B. SRIRAMULU: \n \nWill the Minister of SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY òãðìððÐð ¡ðøÜ ÑßðøÌðð÷ò±ð¨îó Ùðüëðó \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the recruitment of scientists to various groups has declined over the last \nfew years; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor, group/category-wise; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to hold special recruitment drive, especially for \nScheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes, for recruitment of scientists to address the said \nshortfall; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the other steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY \nAND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES \n(SHRI Y. S. CHOWDARY) \n \nòãðìððÐð ¡ðøÜ ÑßðøÌðð÷ò±ð¨îó ÙðüëððâðÚð Ùð÷ü Üð¸Úð Ùðüëðó +ÉÉè® ÑðöÆãðó òãðìððÐð ÙðüëððâðÚð Ùð÷ü Üð¸Úð Ùðüëðó \n (gÉÉÒ ´ÉÉ<Ç. AºÉ. SÉÉèvÉ®ÉÒ) \n \n(a) to (e):    The information is being collected and will be laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |663", "t": "Vacancies of RPF", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.663 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nVACANCIES OF RPF \n \n663. SHRI C.S. PUTTA RAJU: \n        SHRI CH. MALLA REDDY: \n \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether  a large number of safety related posts/posts of Railways \nProtection Force (RPF) are lying vacant in the Railways which is adversely \naffecting the safety of passengers in trains; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof, zone-wise; and \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to fill up these posts? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI  MANOJ SINHA) \n \n \n(a) to (c) Railway Protection Force (RPF ) is mandated to provide security to \nrailway property, passengers and passenger area. There are no safety related \nposts in RPF.  Occurrence of vacancies due to retirement  etc. and  filling up \nof the same  is an ongoing process. About 16,000 Constables wer e recruited \nin RPF in the year 2015, as a result of which  the vacancies in RPF have \nreduced substantially. To ensure security of passengers, about 2300 \nimportant Mail/Express trains are escorted by the RPF, on an average, daily. \nThis is in addition to esc orting of about 2200 such trains by the Government \nRailway Police (GRP) personnel daily. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |664", "t": "Multi-Functional Complexes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 664 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nMULTI FUNCTIONAL COMPLEXES \n \n664. SHRIMATI RITA TARAI: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether the Railways have announced to establish 8 Multi -\nFunctional Complexes (MFCs) in the State of Odisha; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the progress made so far in this regard; and \n(d)     the details of timeline for commissioning of these MFCs? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n(a) to (d) :  Nine stations have been identified for provision of Multi \nFunctional Complexes (MFCs) in the State of Odisha.  These are \nBalasore, Banspani, Berhampur, Bhubaneswar, Cutta ck, Jharsuguda, \nRayagada, Sambalpur and Talcher.  Out of these, MFC building has \nbeen completed at Banspani.  Valuation of MFC site at Sambalpur has \nbeen done.  Further, finalisation of alternate sites has been \nundertaken at Balasore, Berhampur, Jharsuguda and Rayagada.   \n          Process of development of MFCs involves clearances from local \nbodies, construction of building and their leasing/licensing largely \n\nthrough private sector participation which is done after conducting \nfeasibility studies, bidding process, etc. It is, therefore, not possible to \ngive any fixed time frame for development of MFCs and making them \noperational. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |665", "t": "Increase in number of General Coaches", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n               MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \n       UNSTARRED  QUESTION  NO. 665 \n                TO  BE  ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nINCREASE IN NUMBER OF GENERAL COACHES \n \n†665.    SHRIMATI RITI PATHAK:  \n  \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)      the details of the number of passengers travelling in the general \ncoaches, third AC and other categories including the ratio of revenue \nearned from these categories; \n \n(b)    whether more passengers travel in general coaches of most of \nthe trains than any other category of coaches and the Railways earn \nsame revenue from these coaches as from sleeper and third AC \ncoaches and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)     whether the Government proposes to provide more facilities to \nthe passengers travelling in general coaches and also to increase the \nnumber of general coaches; and \n \n(d)    if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n \n(a) to (d)  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n****** \n \nSTATEMENT REFERED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.665 ASKED BY SHRIMATI RITI PATHAK TO \n\nBE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 27.04.2016 REGARDING INCREASE \nIN NUMBER OF GENERAL COACHES. \n \n(a) & (b)  The number of passengers travelling in the general  \n \ncoaches, third AC and other categories/classes and the ratio of their  \n \nrespective revenue earned during 2014-15 is as follows:- \n \nClass No. of \nPassengers        \n(in millions) \n% of Total \nRevenue \nGeneral \nclass \n7342 38.1 \nIII AC 78 18.3 \nOther \nclasses \n804 43.6 \n \n As per details given above, in most of the trains more passengers  \n \ntravel in the General Compartment than in any other compartment and   \n \nRailway’s average  classwise income from a General Compartment is \nlesser than that from a 3 rd AC Compartment and that from a \ncompartment of other categories.  \n \n( c) & (d)  As part of the continual improvement in passenger amenities  \n \nin trains, following actions have been taken by Indian Railways : \n \n(i) Provision of cushioned seats/berths in General Second Class non  \n \nAir Conditioned coaches. \n \n(ii) Besides making provision of dustbins, it has also been decided to  \n \nprovide stainless steel mugs with chain arrangement in non Air  \n \nConditioned coaches including General Second Class coaches. \n \n           ….2/- \n \n\n-2- \n \n \n \nDuring the period 2015 -16, total 1022 coaches have been \nattached in existing trains on permanent basis, out of which 402 \ncoaches are General Class coaches, which is about 40% of the total \naugmentations done during the period.  \n \nFurther, Indian Railways, in the Railway Budget 2016 -17, have \nproposed to introduce the Antyodaya Express, a long distance, fully \nunreserved, superfast train service, and also add two to four Deen \nDayalu coaches  (General Class coaches) in some long distance trains \nfor unreserved travel to enhance the Railways ’ carrying capacity of \nunreserved passengers. \n \n \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |666", "t": "Task Force for Post Office Network", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nDEPARTMENT OF POSTS       \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.666 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27th APRIL, 2016 \n \nTASK FORCE FOR POST OFFICE NETWORK \n \n666.  SHRI DUSHYANT CHAUTALA: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has finalised consultations on the report submitted by \nthe task force regarding leveraging the Post Office network and whether \nrecommendations include i nternet based access to postal life insurance and rural \npostal life insurance; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has also fixed any time frame for the implementation \nof those recommendations; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and the present status regarding implementations of \nthese recommendations? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a) & (b)   Consultation with various stakeholders including service unions etc. is \nunderway.  The task force on leveraging the Post Office network in its report (para \n11.6.1. of Chapter 11) has recommended to ‘make insurance selling and servicing IT \ndriven to optimize the input -output ratio and enhance customer satisfaction and a lso \nenrich the work life of human resources’.  Internet access to Postal Life Insurance and \nRural Postal Life Insurance has been provided under Core Insurance Solution (CIS) \nthat has commenced from February, 2014, which is in line with this recommendation. \n \n(c) & (d)   The task force has made a total of 181 recommendations, out of which \n149 recommendations have already been implemented or are under implementation.  \nSubsequent to the implementation of the IT Modernization Project, that is changing \nthe operational procedures in the Department and based on the inputs received from \nvarious stakeholders including service unions etc., the remaining recommendations of \nthe task force shall be considered.   Use of technology has been given a thrust in the \nlast two yea rs.  As on date Core Insurance Solution (CIS) has been rolled out in \n25406 Post Offices.  Besides this, Core Banking Solution (CBS) has been rolled out in \n21267 Post Offices and 908 ATMs have been installed. \n \n********************** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |667", "t": "Post Offices in Rented Buildings", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nDEPARTMENT OF POSTS       \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.667 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27th APRIL, 2016 \n \nPOST OFFICES IN RENTED BUILDINGS \n \n†667. DR. BANSHILAL MAHATO: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of post offices  functioning in rented buildings in the  country at \npresent, State-wise and the reasons therefor; \n \n(b)  whether the Government has taken  any steps to provide o fficial buildings to  \nsuch post-offices in the country; and \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof, State -wise including Chhattisgarh and if not, the  \nreasons therefor? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a) The number of post offices functioning from rented buildings is 19668. The \nState-wise detail is enclosed as Annexure A.    Due to limited number of vacant land \navailable with the Department of Posts and as construction of post office is a Plan \nactivity with limited financial resources, many post offices have to operate from rented \nbuildings.  \n \n(b) & (c) Yes, Madam. The State - wise detail of post office buildings approved for \nconstruction during XII Five Year Plan, including that for the state of Chattisgarh, i s \nenclosed as Annexure B. \n \nContd…2/- \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n \n \n-2- \nAnnexure ‘A’ \n \nNUMBER OF POST OFFICES FUNCTIONING IN  RENTED BUILDINGS \nName of State No. of Rented buildings \nAndhra Pradesh  1352 \nTelangana 643 \nAssam 443 \nBihar  803 \nChattisgarh 280 \nDelhi 222 \nGujarat ( including Daman  & Diu & Dadra & \nNagar Haveli) 1041 \nHaryana 356 \nHimachal Pradesh 371 \nJammu & Kashmir 202 \nJharkhand 339 \nKarnataka 1272 \nKerala (including Lakshadweep) 1216 \nMadhya Pradesh 778 \nMaharashtra 1681 \nGoa 80 \nManipur 45 \nMeghalaya 35 \nArunachal Pradesh 12 \nMizoram 23 \nNagaland 26 \nTripura 49 \nOdisha 956 \nPunjab (including Chandigarh UT) 528 \nRajasthan 923 \nTamil Nadu (including Puducherry ) 2228 \nUttar Pradesh 2032 \nUttarakhand 298 \nWest Bengal (including Andaman & Nicobar \nIslands) 1417 \nSikkim 17 \nTOTAL 19668 \n \nContd…3/- \n\n \n \n-3 – \nAnnexure ‘B’ \n \nNUMBER OF APPROVED PROJECTS FOR CONSTRUCTION OF POST OFFICE \nBUILDINGS DURING XII FIVE YEAR PLAN \nState \nNumber of Post Office building \nprojects  \nAndhra Pradesh  5 \nTelangana 2 \nAssam 1 \nBihar  3 \nChattisgarh 3 \nDelhi 5 \nGujarat ( including Daman  & Diu & Dadra & \nNagar Haveli) 2 \nHaryana 2 \nHimachal Pradesh 1 \nJammu & Kashmir 2 \nJharkhand 2 \nKarnataka 1 \nKerala (including Lakshadweep ) 9 \nMadhya Pradesh 4 \nMaharashtra 5 \nGoa 0 \nManipur 1 \nMeghalaya 0 \nArunachal Pradesh 1 \nMizoram 0 \nNagaland 0 \nTripura 1 \nOdisha 3 \nPunjab (including Chandigarh UT) 4 \nRajasthan 3 \nTamil Nadu( including Puducherry) 5 \nUttar Pradesh 5 \nUttarakhand 0 \nWest Bengal(including Andaman & Nicobar \nIslands) 3 \nSikkim 1 \nTOTAL 74 \n \n**************** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |668", "t": "Landline Telephones", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS        \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.668 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27th APRIL, 2016 \n \nLANDLINE TELEPHONES \n \n†668.  SHRI ANANDRAO ADSUL: \nSHRI NAGENDRA KUMAR PRADHAN: \nSHRI VINAYAK BHAURAO RAUT: \nDR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \nSHRI RAKESH SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether landline telephone base  of public sector companies have been facing \nchallenges from their private counterparts; \n \n(b)  if so, the details of consumer base  of Government and private sector  \ncompanies, company -wise along with the  details of customers of BSNL and MTNL  \nwho have surrendered their landline telephone or Wi -Fi service during the last  three \nyears; \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes  to introduce any new services, schemes \nand cheap tariff plans to attract landline telephone consumers in the country; \n \n(d)  if so, the details in this regard; and \n \n(e)  the time by which such new schemes,  services will be available to the \nconsumers? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a)&(b) Details of landline consumer base of Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited \n(BSNL) and Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Limited (MTNL) vis -à-vis Private Sector \nCompanies as on 31.12.2015 are as follows: \nName of Service Provider Landline customer base \n(As on  31.12.2015) \nBSNL 1,51,33,945 \nMTNL 35,17,794 \nBharti Airtel Ltd. 35,97,462 \nQuadrant Televenture 2,49,964 \nSistema Shaym Teleservices Ltd. 57,932 \nTata Teleservices Ltd. (including \nTTML) \n17,03,364 \nReliance Communications Ltd. 11,64,544 \nVodafone 93,480 \nCont….2/- \n\n \n \n-2- \n Details of customers of BSNL and MTNL who have surrendered their landline \ntelephone connections including Wi -Fi services during the last three years are as \nfollows: \n \nYear No of customers who surrendered landline \nconnections  \n BSNL MTNL \n2013 35,48,964 1,29,799 \n2014 30,75,584 1,37,666 \n2015 29,43,773 1,17,958 \n (Note : the above figures includes disconnections due to non-payment) \n \n(c) to (e)  BSNL and MTNL are offering competitive and affordable tariff plans to \nfulfill the needs of their customers belonging to various strata of the society.  BSNL \nand  MTNL introduce/revise their tariff plans as per market trend and commercial \nviability.   \nFurther, BSNL and MTNL have taken various steps to make their landline \nservices more customer friendly and attractive. Some of them are as follows: \n BSNL \n \n Setup of wireline call centres for the customers of landline and broadband at \nDehradun, Ajmer and Bangalore. Traffic from all  circles has been migrated \nto these call centres.  \n Launched various customer centric initiatives like Night free calling from \nlandline from 9 PM to 7 AM.  \n Increased minimum speed of broadband to 2 Mbps  \n \nMTNL \n \n Set up of wireline call centers for the customers of landline, mobile & \nbroadband at Delhi and Mumbai.  \n Launched Night free calling from landline from 10 PM to 7 AM. \n Increased minimum speed of broadband to 2 Mbps  \n \n***************** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |669", "t": "Increase in MPLAD Fund", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 669 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 27TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nINCREASE IN MPLAD FUND \n \n669.   SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS: \n     \nWill the Minister of STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME \nIMPLEMENTATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has received any representation \nto increase annual allocation of Members of Parliament \nLocal Area Development (MPLAD) fund; and  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the action taken by the \nGovernment thereon? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR MINISTRY OF \nSTATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION AND MINISTER \nOF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS [GENERAL (Dr.) \nV.K. SINGH (Retd.)] \n \n(a):  Yes, Madam. \n(b): The Lok Sabha Committee on MPLADS (Members of Parliament \nLocal Area Development Scheme) in its sitting held on 02.07.2013 \nhad recommended to increase the annual allocation per Member of \nParliament from rupees 5 crore to rupees 10 crore.  \nHon’ble Deputy Speaker Lok Sabha (16 th Lok Sabha)  and Chairman, \nLok Sabha Committee on MPLADS vide letter dated 25.02.2015 had \nrequested for increase of annual allocation of MPLADS funds per \nMember of Parliament to Rs. 25 crore.  \nThe matter was referred to Ministry of Finance. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |670", "t": "Buddhist Entrepreneurs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.670 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nBUDDHIST ENTREPRENEURS \n \n670. DR. UDIT RAJ : \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether the Government has conducted any study to ascertain the State -wise \nnumber of Buddhist entrepreneurs in the country ; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) if not, whether the Government proposes to conduct any such study considering \ntheir minority status; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n \n(a) & (b) No, Madam. \n \n(c) & (d) No, Madam \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |671", "t": "Misleading Job Ads on Websites", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(DEPARTMENT OF ELECTRONICS &INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  671 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 27.04.2016 \n \n \nMISLEADING JOB ADS ON WEBSITES \n \n671     SHRI PRABHAKAR  REDDY  KOTHA:  \n  \nWill the Minister of Communications & Information Technology be pleased to state: - \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that misleading employment notifications are \nfrequently coming up in some websites about vacancies in Government departments and \norganisations, and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government has any technique to track and verify the veracity of the \nadvertisements and the website from where these misleading advertisements are put in the net, \nand if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the steps and measures being taken by the Go vernment to book the website \nadministrators and save the public from such maligning website administrators? \n  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY   \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a), (b) and (c):  As per the information reported to the Indian Computer Emergency Response \nTeam (CERT-In), a total no. of 12 and 2 cyber security incidents involving misleading job ads \nfor fake recruitment pretending to be from legitimate organizations in Government and Pu blic \nsector were reported during the year  2015 and 2016 (till March) respectively. Such incidents are \nreported by affected organizations, users and Law Enforcement agencies. In order to resolve \nsuch incidents, CERT -In examines the details and coordinates response actions with concerned \nhosting and service providers for disabling the fake information and obtaining relevant details for \nfacilitating further investigation by the Law Enforcement agencies. Further, the affected \norganisations are advised to create awareness among users regarding such fraudulent activities.  \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |672", "t": "Cyber Crimes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(DEPARTMENT OF ELECTRONICS &INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  672 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 27.04.2016 \n \nCYBER  CRIMES \n \n672      SHRI  DUSHYANT  SINGH:   DR.  K.  KAMARAJ:  SHRI G.  HARI: \n  \nWill the Minister of Communications & Information Technology be pleased to state: - \n \n(a) the details of the major and minor Cyber Attacks which have been attempted on the Government \nwebsites/databases during the last year; \n(b) whether there has been a loss/theft/adulteration of significant data through these attacks; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d) the steps taken by the Government to deal with this grave cyber security issue? \n  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY   \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n(a):   As per the information reported to and tracked by Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT -In), a \ntotal number of   164 Government websites were hacked during the year 2015.  \n \n(b) and (c):  The Government websites host information for public dissemination. No sensitive information is hosted on \nGovernment websites. As per the guidelines of the Government, the Computer systems with sensitive information are \nisolated from Internet. \n \n(d):     Government has taken the following steps to enhance cyber security and to protect the websites: \n \ni. The Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT -In) regularly tracks the hacking of websites and alerts \nthe website owners concerned to take actions to secure the websites to prevent recurrence.   \nii. CERT-In issues alerts and advisories regarding latest cyber threats and countermeasures on regular basis. CERT-In \nhas published guidelines for securing the websites, which are available on its website (www.cert-in.org.in). CERT-\nIn also conducts regular training programmes to make the system administrators aware about secure hosting of the \nwebsites.  \niii. In order to protect the websites of Government departments a layered security approach in the form of practices, \nprocedures and technologies is put in place. National Informatics Centre (NIC) that hosts the Government websites \nhas deployed state -of-the-art security solutions including firewall, intrusion prevention systems and anti -virus \nsolution. \niv. All major websites are being monitored regularly to detect malicious activities. A 24x7 security monitoring centre \nis operational in NIC for responding to security incidents. The security events generated from various security \nsolutions on NIC Network (NICNET) are monitored round the clock for taking remedial measures. \nv. All Central Government Ministries / Departments and State / Union Territory Governments have been advised to \nconduct security auditing of entire Information Technology infrastructure. All the new government websites and \napplications are to be audited with respect to cyber securi ty prior to their hosting. The auditing of the websites and \napplications is to be conducted on a regular basis after hosting also. Indian Computer Emergency Response Team \n(CERT-In) provides necessary expertise to audit IT infrastructure of critical and other ICT sectors. \n\nvi. Government has formulated Crisis Management Plan for countering cyber attacks and cyber terrorism for \nimplementation by all Ministries/ Departments of Central Government, State Governments and their organizations \nand critical sectors. \nvii. Cyber security mock drills are being conducted regularly to enable assessment of cyber security posture and \npreparedness of organizations in Government and critical sectors.  \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |673", "t": "Digitising Land Records of Railways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 673 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nDIGITISING LAND RECORDS OF RAILWAYS  \n      \n +673.  SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \n   SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \n   SHRI DHARMENDRA YADAV: \n   DR. HEENA VIJAYKUMAR GAVIT: \n   SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \n   SHRI ADHALRAO PATIL SHIVAJIRAO: \n   SHRIMATI RAMA DEVI: \n   SHRI RAHUL SHEWALE: \n    \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Indian Railways has developed web based  \napplication called Land Management Module integrated with \nTrack Management system to digitize the land records;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the total area of railway land that  \nhas been encroached till date;  \n \n(c) whether the Indian Railways has evolved a system that would \nprovide the status of railway land whether encroached or not \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d) the time by which the system will be evolved and put in place;    \nand  \n \n(e)  the other steps taken by the Government to put an end to illegal  \nencroachment of railway land in the country? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n(a) & (b):  A web based application called Land Management Module \nintegrated with Track Management System (TMS) of Indian Railways \n\nhas been developed for maintaining data i.e. details of \nacquisition/area/usages and land plans of Land Bank of Indian \nRailways. At  present about 879.48 hectare Railway land is \nencroached.           \n(c) & (d):  A work costing 36.24 crore has been sanctioned for \nmapping of all fixed Railway assets on Geographical Information \nSystem (GIS) of Bhuvan  Satellite Imagery of Indian Space Research \nOrganisation (ISRO) integrated with Track Management System \n(TMS) of Indian Railways.  This system is designed to provide the \nstatus of Railway land whether encroached or not and thus would \nbe helpful in tackling  the menace of encroachment. Pilot of GIS \nmapping of Indian Railways network and land parcels has already \nbeen started on Delhi Division of Northern Railway.   \n(e): Railway carry out regular surveys of encroachment and takes \naction for their removal.  If t he encroachments are of temporary \nnature (soft encroachment) in the shape of jhuggies, jhopries and \nsquatters, the same are got removed in consultation and with the \nassistance of Railway Protection Force and local civil authorities.  \nFor old encroachments, where party is not amenable to persuasion, \naction is taken under Public Premises (Eviction of Unauthorized \nOccupants) Act, 1971 (PPE Act, 1971). During the last three years \napproximately 89 hectares of encroached land has been retrieved.   \nRegular measure s are taken to protect Railway land from \nencroachment which include, licensing of land to Railway \nemployees for Grow More Food (GMF) Scheme etc., provision of \nboundary wall, tree plantation at vulnerable locations.   \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |674", "t": "Gauge Conversion Project", "head": "-2- \n--2-- \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 674 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nGAUGE CONVERSION PROJECT \n \n674.   SHRIMATI SANTOSH AHLAWAT: \n        SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARSWATI: \n \n        Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:    \n \n(a) whether the Railway has taken any steps for timely completion \nof Churu-Jaipur via Sikar gauge conversion project;  \n \n(b) if so, the details therof and the fund earmarked for the same; and  \n \n(c) the time by which the said project of gauge conversion is likely \nto be completed?  \n \n \nANSWER  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a) to (c): Jaipur -Ringus-Churu &  Sikar-Loharu gauge conversion \nproject (320kms) has been taken up with latest anticipated cost      \n`915 crore. Sikar -Loharu (122kms) section has been completed & \ncommissioned in the year 2015 -16. On Jaipur -Sikar-Churu section \n(198 kms), earthwork, bridgew ork, ballest supply and track linking \nworks have been taken up. 41 kms track linking has been \ncompleted on Sikar-Churu. \n\n-2- \n--2-- \n An expenditure of `454 cr. has been incurred on the project till \n31.03.2016. An outlay of `100 cr. has been earmarked for the project \nduring 2016-17. \n Completion of the project depends on many factors including \navailability of funds, site conditions etc. By providing adequate \nfunds for this project in 2016 -17, the project has been fast tracked \nfor its early completion                       \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |675", "t": "National Urban Sanitation Policy", "head": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 675 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON APRIL 27, 2016 \n \nNATIONAL URBAN SANITATION POLICY \n \nNo. 675  DR. RAMESH P OKHRIYAL \"NISHANK\": \nSHRI VIJAY KUMAR HANSDAK: \nSHRI OM BIRLA: \nSHRI BHARAT SINGH: \nDR. SWAMI SAKSHIJI MAHARAJ: \n \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the steps taken by the Government to achieve the goals set under \nNational Urban Sanitation Policy (NUSP) for waste management; \n \n(b) whether the Government has addressed the concer ns of lack of \nfunds as well as shortage of technical staff for efficient \nimplementation of waste management and if so, the details thereof;and \n \n(c) the details of the Memoran da of Understanding (MoU) signed \nbetween BSNL and Urban Development Ministry over tracking of solid \nwaste management and the action plan for its implementation? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI M.VENKAIAH NAIDU) \n \n(a): To achieve the g oals set under National Urban Sanitation Policy, \nthe Ministry has laun ched Swachh Bharat Mission and Atal Mission for \nRejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT) to provide assistance \nfor construction of indivi dual/community/public to ilets, assitance for  \nsolid waste management and assitanc e for setting up of sewerage and \nseptic system.  \n(b): Under Swachh Bharat  Mission, Government of India provides \nRs.4000/- as incentive to an indivi dual for constructi on of household \n\n-2- \n \ntoilet and 40% as a grant for construction of community toilet by Urban \nLocal Body and 20% of project cos t for solid waste management.  \nUnder AMRUT scheme, Government of  India provides 35% of project \ncost for setting up of sewerage and s eptic system in Million Plus cities \nand 50% of project cost for other  cities. For both the Missions, \nGovernment of India is provid ing assitance to set up project \nimplementaton unit to take care of shortage of technical staff. \n (c): The Ministry of Urban Deve lopment has signed a Memorandum of \nUnderstanding (MoU) with BSNL on 21 st March, 2016 for helping cities \nto implement a vehicle tracking and monitoring system for monitoring \nsolid waste management.  \n                     ****** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |676", "t": "Extra Budgetary Resources", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.676 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nEXTRA BUDGETARY RESOURCES    \n \n  676.   SHRI K.C. VENUGOPAL:  \n                               \n   Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has received any extra budgetary \nresources for the infrastructural and other development projects \nin the Railways; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof, zone-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has made any policies and \nregulations reg arding profit sharing, interest rate in extra \nbudgetary funding from other entrepreneurs and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n \n(d)     if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n (SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n(a) to (d):      A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.       \n \n***** \n  \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY T O PARTS (a) TO (d) OF  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 676 BY SHRI K.C. VENUGOPAL TO \nBE ANSWERED IN LOK  SABHA ON 27.04.2016 REGARDING \nEXTRA BUDGETARY RESOURCES.  \n   \n(a) Yes, Madam. \n(b)  Zonal Railway -wise number of projects for which Extra \nBudgetary Resources  (Institutional Finance) (EBR-IF) have been \nreceived in financial year 2015 -16 (Revised Estimates) are as \nunder: \n \n…………./- \nZonal Railway Doubling Electri-\nfication   \nGauge Conversion \nand Traffic Facilities \nCentral 5     \nEastern 8     \nNorthern 10     2 \nNorth Eastern 2     \nNortheast Frontier 4     \nSouthern 13     \nSouth Central 8     4 \nSouth Eastern 18     \nWestern 5     1 \nEast Central 6     \nEast Coast 9     \nNorth Central  4     1 \nNorth Western 10     \nSouth East Central 9    2   \nSouth Western 6     \nWest Central 8   1 \nCentral Orgn. for \nRailway Electrifi-\ncation \n \n75   \n Total 125  75  11 \n\n-2- \n(c) EBR-IF funds drawn from Life Insurance Corporation of India \nby Indian Railway Finance Corporation (IRFC) for financing \nRailway projects carry interest rate linked to 10 year benchmark  \nyield of Government Security (G. Sec) plus a spread of 30 basis \npoints. \n          The policy related to engagement with stake holders for \nimplementation of various types of rail connectivity and capacity \naugmentation projects is contained in the following models: \ni.  Non-Government Private Line Model. \nii.     Joint Venture (JV) Model. \niii. Build, Operate and Transfer (BOT) Model. \niv.   Capacity augmentation with funding provided by customers. \nv.       Capacity augmentation through annuity model.   \n \n(d) Does not arise. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |677", "t": "Assistance to RWB and ROBCFDC", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 677 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nAssistance to RWB and ROBCFDC \n677. DR. MANOJ RAJORIA:  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n (a) the  State-wise details of the assistance provided/proposed  to be provided to \nRajasthan Wak f  Board (RWB), Rajasthan Other Backward Classes Finance \nDevelopment Corporation (ROBCFDC) and to other institutions in Rajasthan and \nother States during each of the last three years and the current year;  \n(b) whether  the said institutions have sent any proposals for approval during the  \nsaid period and if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the Government  \nthereto; and \n(c) the number of approved and pending proposal s and the reasons for their  \n pendency along with the time by which pending proposals are likely to be  \napproved? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) to (c) The State -wise details of funds released under the scheme pertaining to \nComputerisation of   records of State  Waqf  Boards and funds released to the \nNAWADCO since inception of the scheme for Strengthening of State Waqf Boards \nincluding Rajasthan Waqf  Board  for further disbursement to State/UT Waqf Boards \nare at Annexure-II. Under the above mentioned two Plan schemes, Ministry does not \ninvite any proposal from State/UT Waqf Boards. The assistance is released to \nState/UT Waqf Boards as per the provisions made  in various components of the \nscheme. \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nThe Statewise details of releases to Other Backward Classes Finance \nDevelopment Corporation s in various State s is at Annexure -III. The Rajasthan \nOther Backward Classes Finance & Development Cooperative Corporation Ltd. had \nsubmitted the Annual Action Plan (AAP) for last three years as tabulated below:- \n                                                                        (Rs.in lakh) \nYear Notional \nAllocation \nAAP \nreceived \nfrom SCA \nAAP \napproved \nNo. of \nbenefi- \nciaries \nAmount \nDisb. \nBenef. \n2015-\n16 \n1000.00 885.20 885.00 3613.00 0.00 0.00 \n2014-\n15 \n2000.00 1641.70 1642.00 5895.00 0.00 0.00 \n2013-\n14 \n1850.00 1641.70 1642.00 5895.00 0.00 0.00 \n \nNo funds were released to ROBCFDC due to: – \n i) non-availability of Government Guarantee. \n ii)non-submission of utilization certificate for an amount of Rs.5.42 Cr. \n iii)non-repayment of outstanding loans of Rs.10.55 Cr.  \nNo proposal has been received during current financial year (2016 -17) from \nROBCFDCC.  \nThere is no pending proposal from Rajasthan Other Backward Classes  \nFinance & Development Cooperative Corporation Ltd. as on date. \nThe disbursement made to Rajasthan Minorities Finance and Development \nCorporation (RMFDCC)  by National Minorities Development and Finance \nCorporation (NMDFC) during the last three years and current year is at Annexure -\nIV. \nDuring the current financial year 2016 -17, RMFDCC has requested NMDFC \nto release Rs. 5.00 crore. However, the funds would be released to RMFDCC by \nNMDFC on submission of Utilization Certificate for the balance amount of Rs. 10.30 \nCrore out of Rs. 20.00 crore disbursed during the financial year 2015 -16.   Necessary \ncommunication has since been issued to RMFDCC.      \n \n        \n  \n\n \n \nA table showing the funds disbursed and the beneficiaries assisted by NMDFC \nduring the last 3 years i.e. 2013-14, 2014-15 and 2015-16 is at Annexure-V.  \nUnder the Free Coaching and Allied Scheme, assistance is provided to only \nempanelled organizations/inst itutions. The grants -in-aid are provided to only those \norganizations/institutions which have completed required documentation. The details \nof assistance given under the Scheme during the last three years are at Annexure VI. \n \n \n                                                  **** \n  \n\nAnnexure I \nAnnexure I referred to in reply to Parts (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 677 for \nanswer on 27. 04.2016 regarding Assistance to RWB and ROBCFDC asked by Dr. \nManoj  Rajoria. \n \nFunds released to State Wakf Boards under the scheme pertaining to \nComputerisation of State Wakf Boards. \n(Rs. in lakh) \nS.No. Name  2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \n1.  Central Waqf Council 257.80 300.00 - - \n2.  Himachal Pradesh State Waqf \nBoard \n6.46 - - - \n3.  Karnataka State Waqf Board 6.62 - - - \n4.  Madhya Pradesh State Waqf \nBoard \n9.75 - - - \n5.  Manipur State Waqf Board 7.20 - - - \n6.  West Bengal State Waqf Board 10.06 - - - \n \n  \n\nAnnexure II \nAnnexure II referred to in reply to Parts (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 677 for \nanswer on 27. 04.2016 regarding Assistance to RWB and ROBCFDC asked by Dr. \nManoj  Rajoria. \nFunds released to NAWADCO since inception of the scheme for strengthening \nof State Waqf Boards for further disbursement to State/UT Wakf Boards. \n       (Rs. in lakh) \n \n \n \n \n \n \nS.No.   Name 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \n1 Waqf Board of Andman Nicobar Islands  - 6.00 5."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |678", "t": "NERUDP", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 678 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON APRIL 27, 2016 \n \nNERUDP \n \nNo. 678.  SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU \n \nWill the Minister Of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state :- \n \n(a) the details of the North Eastern Region Urban Development \nProgramme (NERUDP) and the amount spent during the last three \nyears under NERUDP, State-wise; \n \n(b) the details of the projects exe cuted for water supply, sewage \nand sanitation, and  solid wast e management under NERUDP, \nState-wise; \n \n(c) whether some of the projects  are incomplete and if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the ti me since when th ese projects are \nlying incomplete and reasons therefor project-wise; and  \n \n(d) the details of the projects t hat have been sanctioned for the \ncurrent and coming years? \n \n\n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN \nTHE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI BABUL SUPRIYO) \n \n(a): The Ministry of Urban Developm ent (MoUD) is implementing \nthe North Eastern Region Urban Development Programme \n(NERUDP) with the financial a ssistance from Asian Development \nBank (ADB)  covering capital cities of 5 North Eastern States viz. \nAgartala (Tripura), Aizawl (Miz oram), Gangtok (Sikkim), Kohima \n(Nagaland),  and  Shill ong (Meghalaya) for wa ter supply, sewage \nand sanitation, and  solid waste management. \n \nAmount spent during last three year s under NERUDP State-wise is \nas under: \n     R s  C r o r e  \n State 2013-14\n 2014-15 2015-16 \nNagaland 11.16  33.23 14.90 \nMizoram 11.56  61.75 47.74 \nMeghalaya 2.77  7.56 4.20 \nSikkim 12.73  34.21 13.75 \nTripura 9.51  55.75 13.95 \nTotal  (States)   47.74 192.49 94.54 \nMoUD 1.04  1.20 1.20 \nTotal 48.77  193.69 95.74 \n \n(b): The list of projects which hav e been executed and completed \nis at Annex – I \n \n(c): The list of projects which are under  execution is at Annex – II \n \n(d): The list of projects which have been sanctioned for the \ncurrent and coming years  is at Annex – III \n\n \n \nAnnex - I \nList of the projects which have been executed and completed \nSl.No. State City projects \n1 Mizoram Aizawl \nWater Supply : Replacement of \n7 water reservoirs,  installation \nof 2 chlorinators and household \nwater meters \n2 Sikkim Gangtok  \nWater Supply :  \nWater distribution netwrok in \nBurtuk and Chandmari and \nallied works \n3 Tripura Agartala  Water Supply : Rehabilitation of  \n22 tubewells \n4 Nagaland Kohima \nWater Supply : \nReplacement of 18 water \nreservoirs and refurbishment of \nwater treatment plant.     \nSolid Waste Management : \nDevelopment of land-fill site \nincluding composting plant. \n \n\nAnnex – II  \nList of projects which are under  execution \nSl. \nNo. State City Projects under execution \n1 Mizoram Aizawl \nWater Supply : \nConstruction of 3 water reservoirs,  \nwater distribution network in 8 wards \nand allied works  \nSolid Waste Management : \nProcurement of household bins for \nwaste segregation at source \nSewage and sanitation:  \nConstruction of sewage treatment \nplant, sewage network, and 10 \ncommunity toilet blocks. \n2 Sikkim Gangtok  \nWater Supply : \nImprovement of existing water supply \nsytem in remaining areas and allied \nworks \nSolid Waste Management : \nConstruction of land-fill site, compost \nplant, and collection and \ntransportation vehicles \n3 Tripura Agartala  \nWater Supply : \nConstruction of 7 ground water \ntreatment plants, 14 overhead tanks, \nwater distribution system for South \nZone, and allied works. \n4 Nagaland Kohima  \nWater Supply : \nRehabilitation of water distribution \nnetwork and allied works \nSolid Waste Management : \nWaste collection vehicles, equipment, \nand household bins for waste \nsegregation at source \nSewage & Sanitation : \nSeptage management unit \n5 Meghalaya Shillong  \nSolid Waste Management : \nProcurement of collection and \ntransportation vehicles for SMB area, \nequipment, and household bins for \nwaste segregation at source \n\n \nAnnex – III  \nList of projects which have been  sanctioned for the current and \ncoming years \nSl.No. State City Projects sanctioned \n1 Tripura Agartala  \nWater Supply :  \nWater distribution network for Central \nZone, construction of 14 tubewells \nSolid Waste Management,  \nProcurement of collection and \ntransportation vehicles and \nequipment  \nSewage & Sanitation : \nSeptage treatment plant and \nequipment \n2 Mizoram Aizawl \n Water Supply: \nAugmentation of bulk water including \nwater treatment plant, distribution \nnetwork in 33 wards including \nreservoirs, and allied works \nSolid Waste Management : \nDevelopment of land-fill site, \ncollection and transportation \nvehicles, composting plants, and \nother equipment  \nSewage and sanitation : \nBio-digestor unit including waste \ntreatment plant, and other equipment\n \n3 Meghalaya Shillong \n Solid Waste Management \nDevelopment of land fill site at Martin \nExtension (Phase-II), replacement of \nold compost plant and allied works, \nProcurement of collection and \ntransportation vehicles for outside \nSMB area \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |679", "t": "Rolling out of Telecom Services", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS        \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.679 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27th APRIL, 2016 \n \nROLLING OUT OF TELECOM SERVICES \n \n679. SHRI B. VINOD KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Telecom Service Providers (TSP) have to obtain permission  from \nvarious Government agencies to roll out their service; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether there is a proposal for  uniform rules and single window clearance  \nsystem in telecom to remove bottlenecks and delay; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and the action taken in this regard so far? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a) & (b) In order to roll out the telecommunications services in different licensed \nService Areas, the concerned service providers are required  to obtain permissions f rom \nvarious Government agencies at Union as well as State level. This may include permissions \nfor Right of Way and installation of towers by local administrative bodies, permission for \noperating wireless equipments by the Department of Telecommunications, permission for \nimports by the Indian Customs, Department of Revenue. \n \n(c) & (d) Yes, there is a proposal to simplify the process of granting Right of Way \npermissions and provide these through a single window clearance system in a \ntransparent and time bound  manner. The Department of Telecommunications has \ndrafted and circulated  ‘Indian Telegraph Right of Way Rules, 2016’ and has sought \nviews  from to stakeholders including State Governments, concerned Departments of \nthe Union and administrative bodies such as National Highway Authority of India and \nRailway Board by 25th April, 2016 to take a considered view in this regard. \n \n \n************** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |680", "t": "Closing of Energy Security Division", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.680 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nCLOSING OF ENERGY SECURITY DIVISION \n \n680. SHRI PREM DAS RAI: \n \n Will the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is fact that the Energy Security Division of the Ministry \nof External Affairs has been shut down, \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether the energy sector is recognized as an important and \ngrowing concern for the nation and if not, the r easons therefor; \nand \n(d) the details of the mechanism by which the Ministry of External \nAffairs proposes to handle the work previously overseen by the \nES division? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a) & (b) No, the Energy Security (ES) Division of Ministry of External \nAffairs (MEA) has not been shut down. The ES Division has only been \nmerged with Investment & Technology Promotion (ITP) Division of MEA \nand the Division is renamed as ITP & ES Division. The merger has been \nundertaken based on functional requirements. \n \n(c) & (d) Yes, the energy sector is recognized as an important and \ngrowing concern for the nation. The re -designated ITP & ES Division \nnow serves as the nodal point in MEA for ener gy security -related \nmatters. The Division maintains close coordination with relevant line \nMinistries related to energy including NITI Aayog, Petroleum & Natural \nGas, Coal, Power & New and Renewable Energy etc., and supports their \ninternational engagement t hrough appropriate diplomatic \ninterventions. The Division supports efforts of Indian entities in \nacquiring energy assets overseas and in building strategic partnerships \nwith foreign companies. The Division deals with internal energy related \norganizations and forums and acts as an interface with leading energy \nsecurity think-tanks and experts on energy-related issues. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |681", "t": "Rail Ticket Frauds", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO.681 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \n \nRAIL TICKET FRAUDS \n \n \n681. SHRI K. ASHOK KUMAR : \n \n    \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether to make the railway ticketing system more \ntransparent, the Centre for Railway Information S ystems has \ninitiated printing of encrypted QR code on unreserved tickets and \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether it has been found that a large number of passengers \nwere travelling on the carbon copy of tickets and if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(a) whether with the  encrypted QR code on the tickets, the \nticket checking staff can scan the bar code and can validate the \ninformation provided on the ticket to prevent any fraudulent \nactivity; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a) Yes , Madam.  Pilot project for printing of encrypted QR code \nhas been launched on 01 -03-2016 at nominated counters at New \nDelhi, Delhi and Hazrat Nizamuddin railway stations. \n….2/- \n\n-2- \n \n(b) No , Madam.  However, one case of passenger travelling on \nduplicate tickets issued by using carbon has been detected during \nchecks.  Further, carbon copy of Excess Fare Ticket (EFT) is also \ngiven to the pas sengers by on board ticket checking staff \nwhenever required, which also becomes authority to travel. \n(c) Yes, Madam.  The ticket checking staff can scan the encrypted \nQR code and validate the information provided on the ticket to \nprevent any fraudulent activity. \n(d) The encrypted QR code can be scanned by using smartphones \nand also with other Handheld devices.  The scanned encrypted QR \ncode can be decrypted and the information printed on the ticket \ncan be validated. \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |682", "t": "Rapid Rail", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 682 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \nRAPID RAIL \n682.  SHRI KESINENI NANI: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the steps being taken by the Government to start ‘Rapid Rail’ \nconnectivity between Hyderabad and New Capital of Andhra Pradesh as \nmandated by the Section 13 of Andhra Pradesh State Reorganisation \nAct; and \n \n(b) the details of the projects being undertaken to expand the reach of \nRailways to the unconnected and backward regions of Andhra Pradesh? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a) The new capital of Andhra Pradesh is in proximity to the cities of \nVijayawada and Guntur which are well connected by Railway network. \nAt present, 35 pairs of Mail/Express trains run between \nHyderabad/Secunderabad and Vijayawada; and 14 pairs, between \nHyderabad/Secunderabad and Guntur. \n(b) A 309 km long Nadikude – Srikalahasti new line project has been \nundertaken jointly by Ministry of Ra ilways and Government of Andhra \nPradesh at an approx. cost of ₹2440 crore on cost sharing basis, to \nprovide connectivity to hinterland of districts of Guntur, Nellore and \nPrakasam, besides providing alternative to the coastal link between \nVijayawada and Chennai. \nContd.- \n \n\n \n \n-2- \n \nIn addition, the 3rd line proj ect has been undertaken between \nVijayawada and Kazipet to improve connectivity to the regions between \nthese two cities.              \nIn all, 32 major projects consisting of 17 new lines and 15 doubling, \nfalling fully/partly in the State of Andhra Pradesh, have been taken up. A \nRailway project invariably acts as a catalyst of growth in unconnected \nand backward regions. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |683", "t": "Shortfall in Revenue", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.683 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nSHORTFALL IN REVENUE \n \n683. SHRI SANJAY DHOTRE:  \n SHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB: \n SHRI MALLIKARJUN KHARGE: \n  \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether there is a shortfall in revenue earned vis -à-vis its target by \nthe Railways from passengers and freight during each of the last \nthree years and the current year and if so, the details thereof, zone -\nwise; \n \n(b) whether Railways has incurred huge losses on account of under \nutilisation of Railway Cargo Services in the country  during each of \nthe last three years and the current year and if so, the details \nthereof, zone-wise and the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) the losses incurred /profit earned by the Railways during the said \nperiod; \n  \n(d) the reasons  for the fall of market share of the Railways in the \nfreight market; and \n \n(e) whether the Government proposes to improve the share of Indian \nRailways in the freight market and make it more attractive for \nbusiness and if so, the details thereof and if not , the reasons \ntherefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n(a)  to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n\n***** \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( e) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 683 BY SHRI  SANJAY DHOTRE, SHRI \nBHARTRUHARI AND SHRI MALLIKARJUN KHARGE  TO BE ANSWERED \nIN LOK SABHA ON 27.04.2016 REGARDING SHORTFALL IN REVENUE \n \n(a): The details of shortfall in revenue earned vis-à-vis its target by the \nRailways during last three years zone -wise are appended in \nAppendix-I & II. Current financial year 2016-17 has just begun w.e.f. \n01.04.2016 and details are unavailable. \n (b): During the last three years, Railways earnings ha s increased year \non year as indicated in Appendix -II. Under utilization of freight \nservices is reflected in the empty running of trains. In 2012 -13, \noverall empty running ratio was 34.70% which slightly deteriorated \nin 2013 -14 wh en this ratio was 34.90% and the ratio improved in \n2014-15 at 34.80%. Railway zone-wise details of Empty Return Ratio \nfor 2012-13 to 2014-15 is appended in Appendix-III. \n (c): The surplus generated  by Railways during the last three years is \nas under: \n(` in crore) \nYear Amount \n2013-14 3740.40 \n2014-15 7664.94 \n2015-16 (approx) 10869.30 \n  \n(d): The market share of Indian Railways in freight market  has been  \nimpacted due to a number of factors includ ing hike in freight rates \nand growth and competition from alternative modes of \ntransportation. \n(e): The G overnment is committed to improve the share of Indian \nRailways in the freight market and this is reflected in the Budget \n\nannouncement made during the curr ent year , which contemplates \nreview of tariff policy to evolve a competitive rate structure vis a \nvis other modes, permit multi -point loading/unloading and apply \ndifferentiated tariffs to increase utilization of alternate routes. The \npossibility of signing  long term tariff contracts with key  freight \ncustomers using pre -determined price escalation principles will be \nexplored w hich would provide predictabili ty of revenues  to Indian \nRailways and of costs to the customers. It has also been announced \nthat the freight basket of Indian Railways will be expanded and \ncontainer sector will be opened to all traffic (barring Coal & \nspecified Mineral Ores). All existing terminals/goods sheds will be \ngiven access to container traffic. \nThe Government has initiated various steps to increase the share of \nRail in transportation market. Some of these are as under: \n(i) All covered wagons have been permitted for booking of traffic \nto two-point/multi-point combination and mini rake. \n(ii) Distance restriction applicable for mini rake has been relaxed \nfrom 400 to 600 kms. \n(iii) In order to provide economical and reliable transportation \nsolution to short lead traffic, a revised scheme under Merry -\nGo-Round (MGR) system has been introduced. \n(iv) Port Cong estion Charge leviable @ 10% on all traffic \noriginating from Port has been discontinued from 13.04.2016 \nup to 31.03.2017. \n(v) Automatic Freight Rebate Scheme for traffic loaded in empty \nflow direction has been extended. \n(vi) In addition, the reforms initiated by th e government in sectors \nsuch as Coal, Power, Steel and other industries is likely to \n\ngive a push to activity in these industries resulting in \nincreased originating traffic tonnage. \n(vii) Further, in order to increase the market share of Railways in \nfreight transportation and to improve the efficiency of Freight \nTraffic specially for non -conventional traffic in high capacity \nand special purpose wagons, the following schemes have \nbeen introduced by Indian Railways for procurement of rakes \nfor transportation of frei ght traffic by inviting private \ninvestment through Public Private Partnership (PPP): \n(a) Liberalized Wagon Investment Scheme - S"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |684", "t": "Average Speed of Trains", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n               MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED  QUESTION  NO. 684 \nTO  BE  ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nAVERAGE SPEED OF TRAINS \n \n684.     Shri G. Hari:  \n             Shri Kunwar Pushpendra Singh Chandel:  \n                      Shri Mallikarjun Kharge:  \n  \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:     \n  \n   \n(a) the details of the average speed of all passenger and freight \ntrains in the country at present; \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to increase the average \nspeed of passenger and freight trains; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government has a time-bound mechanism to \nachieve the improvement or increase in speed of the trains \nand if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) the details of various schemes related to high speed trains? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n*** \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.684 BY SHRI G. HARI, SHRI KUNWAR \nPUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL AND SHRI MALLIKARJUN KHARGE  \nTO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 27.04.2016 REGARDING \nAVERAGE SPEED OF TRAINS  \n \n(a) Average speed of all passenger and freight trains in the \ncountry during 2014-15, is given as under : \n \nS.No Type of trains Average speed ( Km/hour) \nBroad Gauge \n1 Mail/Express 50.8 \n2 EMU 45.3 \n3 Ordinary Passenger trains    \n( including mixed) \n36.1 \n4 Freight ( All traction ) 24.2 \n \n(b) & (c ) Yes, Madam.  Recently, a specific Directorate i.e. \nMobility Directorate has been set up of which one of the \nprimary objectives is to initiate measures for increasing the \nspeed of trains.  Besides, in Railway Budget 2016 -17, a new \ncategory of train service for the reserved passengers ‘Tejas’ \nhas been announced with the speed of 130 KMPH and above.  \n(d )& (e) “Mission Raftar” declared in the Railway Budget 2016 -\n17 entails doubling of average speed of freight trains and \nincreasing the average speed of superfast Mail/Express trains \nby 25 KMPH in the next five years.  \n          ----2/- \n\n-2- \n      Nine corridors for raising the speed of passenger trains \nto 160/200 KMPH have been identified. The details of these \ncorridors are (i) Delhi -Agra (ii) Delhi - Chandigarh (iii) Delhi -\nKanpur (iv) Nagpur -Bilaspur (v) Chennai -Bengaluru-Mysore (vi) \nMumbai-Goa (vii) Mumbai-Ahmedabad (viii) Chennai-Hyderabad, \nand  (ix) Nagpur-Secunderabad. \n  Further, under the Quadrilateral network, 6 more \ncorridors have been identified for feasibility studies for high \nspeed rail connectivity to the four major metros and growth \ncenters of the coun try.  These are Delhi -Mumbai, Mumbai -\nChennai, Chennai -Kolkata, Kolkata -Delhi, Delhi -Chennai and \nMumbai-Kolkata.  \nHowever, due to the high cost of the high speed rail \nprojects, at this stage, only Mumbai -Ahmedabad high speed \nrail project has been approved i n December, 2015, for \nimplementation.   \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |685", "t": "Third Party Auditor", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 685 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \nTHIRD PARTY AUDITOR \n \n685. SHRIMATI VANAROJA R.: \n \n          \n        Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n                    \n(a)  whether the Railways is considering to appoint a third party auditor \nto look into the maintenance of catering standards and quality in \ntrains; \n(b)   if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the quality and standards of catering in trains have \nreached to a very low level recently; and  \n(d)  if so, the steps taken by the Railways other than appointing a third \nparty auditor to improve the catering services in trains? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a) and (b):   Railways have decided to conduct Third Party Audit of \ncatering services at periodic intervals by independent and reputed \nauditing agencies accredited by  NABCB (National Accreditation Board \nfor Certification Bodies).  As per the guidelines issued in this regard,  \n16 Railway Zones have been divided into four groups with Northern, \nEastern, Western and Southern Railways as the nodal Zonal Railway for \neach group.  The nodal zonal railways have been entrusted to empanel \nthese accredited auditing agencies for the third party audit for their \nrespective group of railways.  \n\n(c) and (d): Improvement of catering services is an on-going process.  In \nits endeavour to provide quality and hygienic food to the passengers, \nRailways have developed and operationalized an institutionalized \nmechanism for monitoring of quality and hygiene of catering services \nthrough regular inspections at various levels. The passenger \nsatisfaction levels are also regularly monitored through direct feedback \nand other means to address catering complaints.  The steps taken to \nimprove the quality of food in Railways inter -alia include: (i) \nIntroduction of station based  e-catering at all A1 and A category \nstations for widening the range of options available to passengers for \nordering food of their choice. (ii) Introduction of precooked food (‘ready \nto eat’ meals) (iii) Operation of centralized Catering Service Monitoring  \nCell (CSMC) (toll free number 1800 -111-321) for prompt redressal of \npassenger grievances relating to the catering activities and real time \nassistance to travelling public. (iv) Imposition of penalties in case of \ndeficiencies detected in services. (v) Oper ation of all India Helpline \n(No.138) for rail -users to lodge complaints/suggestions regarding food \nand catering services (vi) A Twitter handle with the address \n@IRCATERING has also been made operational to cater to the \ncomplaints/suggestions with regard to catering services. \n \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |686", "t": "Wireless on Local Loop", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS        \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.686 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27th APRIL, 2016 \n \nWIRELESS ON LOCAL LOOP \n \n†686. SHRI GANESH SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes  to increase capacity of Wireless in Local  \nLoop (WLL) and mobile towers to improve quality of service; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof, State-wise including Madhya Pradesh; \n \n(c)  whether there is a long waiting list with Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited for \nWLL connections in Madhya Pradesh and other States; and \n \n(d)  if so, the action taken by the Government to clear the waiting list? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n \n(a)&(b)  With regard to Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Limited (MTNL) and \nBharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL), the details are as follows: \n \nMTNL \n \nMTNL has closed its CDMA (Code Division Multiple Access ) based mobile and \nfixed Wireless in Local Loop (WLL) services w.e.f. 29.2.2016.   \n \nBSNL \n \nBSNL has reported that it has no  expansion plan in respect of CDMA based \nWLL services in its Telecom Circles including Madhya Pradesh. \n \n(c)&(d)  BSNL has reported that there is no waiting list for WLL (Wireless in \nLocal-Loop) connections as on 31.3.2016 in its Telecom Circles including M adhya \nPradesh. \n \n \n \n************** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |687", "t": "Indian Railways Freight Operations Information System", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO.687 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016 \n \n \nINDIAN RAILWAYS FREIGHT OPERATIONS INFORMATION SYSTEM \n \n \n†687. SHRIMATI SAKUNTALA LAGURI: \n          SHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN: \n \n       \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has implemented ‘Indian Railways \nFreight Operations Information System’; \n \n(b)   if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has received any complaints during the \nlast three years on this account;  \n \n(d)   if so, the details thereof, zone-wise; and \n \n(e)  the number of persons found guilty in the matter along with the \naction taken by the Government against said persons? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a) Yes, Madam. \n \n(b)   Freight Operations Information Systems (FOIS) has been \nimplemented across Indian Railways network and caters to all freight \nbookings and invoicing  and  captures  running  position of  all  freight  \n \n….2/- \n\n \n-2- \n \ntrains.  About 99% of invoices are generated through this system.  \nFOIS has two core modules viz., Rake Management System for \nmonitoring of Rake movement and Terminal Management System for \ncommercial activities related to freight business. \n \n(c) to (e)       Technical issues regarding day to day working of the \nsystem are reported from  locations where FOIS terminals have been \ninstalled.  These are resolved on daily basis by the technical team of \nCentre for Railway Information System (CRIS). \n \nNo other complaints regarding the system have been received. \n \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |688", "t": "Funds for Houses", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 688 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON APRIL 27, 2016 \n \nFUNDS FOR HOUSES \n \nNo. 688 SHRIMATI NEELAM SONKER: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AN D URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \nbe pleased to state: \n(a)  the number of States i ncluding Uttar Pradesh where the \ncentrally sponsored Integrated City Development Planning(ICDP) \nhas been implemented; \n(b) whether the families living below the pove rty lines in urban \nareas are sanctioned the grants-in-a id of Rs. 1 lakh 81 thousand \nunder this scheme and whether  the house constructed with the \nsaid amount is sufficient for a family to live in; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to increase the said amount \nto Rs. 4 lakh in view of the rise in the construction cost;  and \n(d) if so, the time by which it is likely to be done? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING & URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n(SHRI M. VENKAIH NAIDU) \n  \n(a) to (d): It has been reported that  City Development Plans (CDP) \nhave been prepared for 27 States  and 3 UTs including Uttar \nPradesh. The responsibility of the implementation of CDP, however,  \nlies with the State Governments. \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |689", "t": "Performance Grant", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT  LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 689 TO BE ANSWERED ON APRIL 27, 2016  PERFORMANCE GRANT   No. 689                 SHRIMATI KAVITHA KALVAKUNTLA:   Will the Minister of URBAN  DEVELOPMENT be pleased to \nstate:- \n (a) the details of eligibility  criteria/conditions for providing \nperformance grant to various States; \n (b) the  details  of  States  eligible  for performance  grants  \nduring  the  year 2014-15; and \n (c) the amount  of  funds sanctioned to such States in the \nfinancial year? \nANSWER THE MINISTER OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT (SHRI M. VENKAIAH NAIDU) (a):  Eligibility criteria fo r performance grant for Urban Local \nBodies, stipulated by 13 th and 14 th Finance Commissions are \ngiven in Annexure-I . \n  (b) & (c): Details are given in Annexure-II. *****   ANNEXURE-I Annexure referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred \nQuestion No.689 for 27.4.2016 regarding Performance Grant.   \n   DETAILS OF ELIGIBILITY CRITERIA/CONDITIONS FOR PROVIDING \nPERFORMANCE GRANT STIPULATED BY THE 13TH FINANCE \nCOMMISSSION \n 1.  Finance Account & Budget \ni) The State Government must put in place a \nii) Certification that accounting systems as per \nNational Munici pal Accounts Manual have been \n2.  Audit of all tiers ULBs \ni) The Comptroller &Auditor General must be given \nTechnical Guidance & Support  over the audit of all the \nlocal bodies in a state at every tier/category and \nAnnual Technical Inspecti on Report as well as the \nA l R t f th  Di t  f L l F d A dit t  The above conditions for release of Performance Grant  \nmust be met by the end of a fiscal year (31 March) for \na State to be eligible to  draw down its performance \ngrant for the succeeding fi scal year. Compliance with \nconditions also needs to be maintained during the \naward period for continued e ligibility for performance \ngrants. Non-compliance with any one condition at any \ntime during the award pe riod will render a State \nineligible for the performance grant until it again \nbecomes compliant with all conditions.   \n Apart from the above nine con ditions,   States covered \nunder Part IXA of the Consti tution, where elections to \nUrban Local have not been held, will not be eligible for \nPerformance Grant for the period during which there \nare no elected Urban Loc al Bodies as per the \nprovisions of articles  243Q and 243R of the \nConstitution. \n The award period of 13\nth Finance Commission \nPerformance Grant  was  from 2011-12 to 2014-15 \nDETAILS OF ELIGIBILITY CRITERIA/CONDITIONS FOR PROVIDING \nPERFORMANCE GRANT FOR URBAN LOCAL BODIES (ULB) \nSTIPULATED BY THE 14th   FINANCE COMMISSSION \n\tThe 14 th Finance Commission has re commended that detailed \nprocedure and the operat ional criteria, i ncluding quantum of \nincentives to be given, for disbur sal of performance grants to duly \nconstituted ULBs will be decided by the State Governments \nconcerned, subject to the eligibility conditions indicated below:-   \n(i) The ULB will have to submit audite d accounts that relate to \nyear not earlier than two years preceding the year in which \nthe Municipality seeks to claim the performance grant, \n(ii)  The ULB will have to show an increase in its own revenues \nover the preceding year as  reflected in the audited \naccounts. The improvement in revenues will be determined \non the basis of these audit ed accounts and on no other \nbasis. For computing the increase in own revenues in a \nparticular year, the proceeds from octroi and entry tax must \nbe excluded, and \n(iii) The ULB must measure and publish the Service Level Bench \nMarks relating to basic urban services each year for the \nperiod of the award and make it publically available. The \nService Level Bench Mark of the Ministry of Urban \nDevelopment may be used for this purpose.  \n The performance grants recommended by the 14\nth Finance \nCommission for ULBs are released from the year 2016-17 by the \nMinistry of Finance. \n******* ANNEXURE-II Annexure referred to in reply to  parts (b) & (c) of Lok Sabha \nUnstarred Question No.689 for 27.4.2016 regarding Performance \nGrant. \nDETAILS OF STATES ELIGIBLE FOR PERFORMANCE GRANTS \nRECOMMENDED BY THE 13TH FINANCE COMMISION DURING THE \nYEAR 2014-15 AND THE AMOUNT OF FUNDS RELEASED \n \n ********           "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |69", "t": "Construction of ESI Staff Quarters", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 69 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF ESI STAFF QUARTERS \n \n69. SHRIMATI P.K. SREEMATHI TEACHER: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government has any proposal for \nconstruction/renovation of ESI staff quarters across the country; \n(b)if so, the details thereof State/UT-wise; \n(c)whether the Government has received any proposal for repair of \nstaff quarters across th e country including staff quarters attached \nto ESI Hospital Thottada, Kannur district of Kerala State; and \n(d)if so, the details thereof and the present status of the said \nproposal? \n \n ANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a) & (b):  No, Madam. Though, there is no specific proposal but the \nEmployees’ State Insurance  (ESI) staff quarters  related works are \nprovisionally carried out with the approval of State Executive \nCommittee and on Case to Case basis, depending on the necessity.   \n \n(c) & (d):  Yes, Madam. Two nos. of estimates for Civil and Electrical \nworks have been received for Specia l Repair Works of Staff Quarters \nattached to ESI Hospital, Thottada, Kannur district, Kerala: \n \ni) Special Repair Works (Electrical) –    Rs. 22,73,696/- \nii) Special Repair Works (Civil) –             Rs. 21,00,000/- \n \n   For undertaking above and such other  works, the ESIC is \ncontemplating to enhance the powers of sanction of local officials for \nspeedy implementation. \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |690", "t": "Budgetary Allocation for Skill Development", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 690\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.04.2016\n \n \nBUDGETARY ALLOCATION FOR SKILL\n \nDEVELOPMENT\n \n \n690. KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE:\n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) whether the budgetary allocation for 2016\n-\n17 for skill development is sufficient to \nmeet its aspirations in skill development; \n \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(c) if \nnot, the steps the Government has taken/proposes to take to persuade the \nFinance Ministry for more allocation of plan outlay to fulfil its objectives; and \n \n \n(d) the other steps taken by the Government in this regard?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT\n \nCHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF\n \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP\n \n(SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY)\n \n \n(a) to (d) \n       \n \nThe Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship has been \nallocated Rs. 1700 crore under Plan and Rs. 104.28 crore under Non Plan in the \nB\nudget 2016\n-\n17. It is estimated that by 2020, 10.46 crore new entrants will be joining \nexisting workforce in the country who need to be skilled to meet industry \nrequirement. In addition 29.82 crore of existing workforce need to be reskilled or \nupskilled. Gi\nven the huge task of skilling, additional funds for skill development are \nneeded to meet the targets. The budget proposals of this Ministry have already been \nsubmitted to Ministry of Finance. For generation of additional funds for skilling,\n \nthe \nMinistry of\n \nSkill Development & Entrepreneurship has reached out to PSUs/ \nCompanies under various Ministries for CSR Funds for Skill Development and \nsigned several tripartite Memorandum of Understandings for skill training projects to \nbe implemented by National Skill\n \nDevelopment Corporation (NSDC) and funded by \nPSUs. In addition,\n \nStrategic partnerships have been undertaken between Ministry of \nSkill Development and Entrepreneurship and other Ministries/Departments in the \nCentral Government, to collaborate on scaling up\n \nskill training activities in specific \nsectors and leveraging existing infrastructure.\n \n \n***********\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |691", "t": "Revision in Royalty Rates", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 691 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nRevision in Royalty Rates \n \n691.  SHRIMATI KAMLA DEVI PAATLE:  \n \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state:  \n(a)  whether the Government has revised the rates of royalty of minerals except \ncoal, if so, the mineral-wise details thereof; \n(b) whether the basic price of coal is fixed by the Coal India Limited, if so, the \ndetails thereof;  \n(c)  whether the separate basic prices are fixed for the power Sector and non-power \nsector, if so, the details thereof and reasons therefor;  \n(d) whether the Government is contemplating to fix only one basic price and \nincrease the royalty rate to 30 per cent, if so, the details thereof; \n(e) whether any proposal has been received from the Government of Chhattisgarh \nin this regard; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof and the action taken by the Government thereon? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL, POWER   AND NEW & \nRENEWABLE ENERGY \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) The Ministry of Mines has revised the rates of royalty for minerals other than coal, \nlignite and sand for stowing, with effect from 01st September 2014, which have been \npublished in the Gazette of India vide Notification No. 630 (E) dated 01.09.2014. A \ncopy of the said Gazette notification is enclosed as annexure. \n \n(b) & (c)  After complete decontrol of coal prices w.e.f. 01.01.2000, Coal India Limited   \n(CIL) fixes  the basic prices of raw coking and non-coking coal produced by CIL and \nits subsidiary companies. The coal companies fix the coal price based on input costs, \ninflation index, market trends etc  \n \nIn the price revision effective from 27.02.2011, for the first time the concept of dual \npricing was adopted by CIL i.e. one price for consumers in the Power utilities ( \nincluding independent power producers), Fertlizers and Defence (Regulated Sector) \nand another for consumers other than regulated sectors.  The p rices for  the non-\nregulated sector (except for higher grade) are kept higher by around 35% than that for \nregulated sector. Since the prices of end products of coal consuming industries in the \nnon-regulated sector are market driven, the price of coal for non -regulated sector are \nfixed at a level closer to the market price of coal. \n \n\n(d) Fixing the price of coal is not in the domain of Government. The royalty rate revision \nis in Governments’ domain, which is being considered by study group. \n \n(e) MOC has received comments from Govt. of Chhattisgarh to increase the rate of \nroyalty from existing 14 % to 30 % in response  to the questionnaire circulated to all \nstakeholders by the Study Group constituted to consider the revision of rate of royalty. \n \n(f) Study Group on revision of rates of royalty on Coal and Lignite  has not yet made any \nfinal report in this regard. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |692", "t": "Real Time Data for Flood Forecast", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 692 \nANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nREAL TIME DATA FOR FLOOD FORECAST \n \n692.  SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE  \n \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether climate change, frequent extreme weather events and an increasingly unpredictable monsoon \nis impacting millions each year in the country;  \n(b) if so, whether there is any proposal to set up a centralised system for providing real-time data for flood \nforecasts, water resources assessment and drought management;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) the time by which a final decision is likely to be taken by the Government in this regard; and  \n(e) the extent to which the project is likely to help in the development of real time flood forecasting? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR  WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION \n(PROF. SANWAR LAL JAT) \n \n(a) The frequent extreme weather events and erratic monsoon pattern observed in the recent past have \naffected huge population in concerned areas. \n(b) to (e) The Central Water Commission under this Ministry maintains a network of 878 stations for \ncollection of flood information and issues flood forecasts to 176 stations located on major rivers and their \ntributaries in the country. In December, 2015, the Ministry has approved the scheme of “Flood \nForecasting” at a cost of Rs. 281 crore for implementation in XII Plan. The scheme envisages \nmodernisation of its existing network and provision of an additional 100 flood forecasting stations for \nreal-time flood forecasting.  \nBesides, the Ministry has taken up National Hydrology Project (NHP) & setting up of National Water \nInformatics Centre (NWIC) at New Delhi. The NHP envisages setting up of a system for timely and \nreliable water resources data acquisition, storage, collation and management. It will help in gathering \nhydro-metrological data for analysis on a real time basis.  \nThe National Water Informatics Centre (NWIC) shall provide a ‘Single Window’ source of updated data \non water resources & allied themes includi ng real time data as per availability; and provide value added \nproducts and services to all stake holders for its management and sustainable development for the benefit \nof all communities across the nation. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |693", "t": "Supply of Water to Drought hit Areas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 693 \nANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nSUPPLY OF WATER TO DROUGHT HIT AREAS \n \n693.      SHRI KIRTI AZAD \n \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is taking any steps to ensure that the drought hit areas in the country are \nsupplied adequate water;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(c) whether there are enough resources available to ensure adequate water supply to these areas;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n(e) the future plan of the Government to ensure adequate water supply in these areas? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA \nREJUVENATION  \n \n(PROF. SANWAR LAL JAT) \n \n(a) to (d) The State Government is primarily responsible for taking necessary relief measures in the wake \nof natural calamities.  Government of India supplements the efforts of State Governments with financial \nassistance and logistic support. For undertaking rel ief measures, funds are available with the State \nGovernments in the form of State Disaster Response Fund (SDRF).  Additional financial assistance, over \nand above SDRF, is considered from National Disaster Response Fund (NDRF) for natural calamities of \nsevere nature and is approved on the basis of Memorandum received from State Governments in \naccordance with established procedure.   \nAn amount of Rs.10275.48 crores have been released for the year 2015 -16 to State Governments. Further, \nthe Central Government has released Rs.2551.875 crores as first instalment of Central share in SDRF to \nthe 10 drought affected States for 2016-17.   \n \n(e) Several measures for meeting water crisis through augmenting, conserving and using water resources \nmore efficiently are undertaken by the State Governments.  This M inistry provides technical and financial \nassistance to the State Governments in this regard through various schemes and programmes viz. \nAccelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme, Scheme for Repair, Renovation  & Restoration of Water-bodies \netc. \nContd.//2//…  \n\n//2// \nCentral Ground Water Board, under this Ministry has prepared a conceptual document ent itled “Master \nPlan for Artificial Recharge to Ground Water in India” during the year 2013 envisaging construction of \n1.11 crore Rainwater Harvesting and Artificial Recharge structures in the country to harness 85 BCM \n(Billion Cubic Meters) of water. The au gmented ground water resources will enhance the availability of \nwater for drinking, domestic, industrial and irrigation purposes. The Master Plan has been circulated to all \nState Governments for implementation.  \n \nWater conservation and water harvesting structures to augment ground water constitute a special focus area \nfor MGNREGA works and about 2/3 rd of the expenditure is directly related to construction of such \nstructures. \n \nCentral Government has launched the National Water Mission with the objective of c onservation of \nwater, minimizing wastage and ensuring its more equitable distribution both across and within States \nthrough integrated water resources development and management.   \nThe National Water Policy, 2012 has been formulated which has made several recommendations for \nconservation, development and improved management of water resources in the country. \nJal Kranti Abhiyan (2015-16 to 2017- 18) has been launched in order to consolidate water conservation \nand management in the country through a holistic a nd integrated approach involving all stakeholders, \nmaking it a mass movement. \nRecycle and reuse of water, after treatment to specified standards as well as rainwater harvesting and \nartificial recharge are being incentivized through various initiatives, programmes/ schemes of the \nGovernment. \nImproved water use efficiency in different sectors such as in irrigation (through micro- irrigation, e.g., \ndrip, sprinkler etc.), industry and household s is being encouraged through various initiatives, \nprogrammes/ schemes of the Government. \nThis Ministry has also formulated a National Perspective Plan (NPP) envisaging inter -basin transfer of \nwater.  The implementation of NPP would give benefits of approximately 35 million hectare of additional \nirrigation potential and 34000 mega watts (MW) hydro generation apart from the incidental benefits of \nflood moderation, navigation, drinking and industrial water supply, fisheries, salinity and pollution \ncontrol etc. \n*****\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |694", "t": "Setting up of Solar Parks", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 694\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016\n \n \nSETTING UP OF SOLAR PARKS\n \n694\n.\n \nSHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA:\n \n \nSHRI NANA PATOLE:\n \n \nSHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of New and Renewable Energy be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nthe details of solar parks set up and proposed to be set up in the country including \nWestern Rajasthan and Pali district and Maharashtra, State/UT\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(b)\n \nwhether the Union Governme\nnt has reviewed the progress of solar park projects in the \ncountry;\n \n \n(c)\n \nif so, the details and present status\n \nthereof;\n \n \n(d)\n \nwhether the Union Government has issued instructions/guidelines to the State \nGovernments for the speedy implementation of these \nprojects and achieve the target within the \nstipulated timeline; and\n \n \n(e)\n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR POWER, COAL & NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \n \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL)\n \n \n(a):\n \n32 Solar Parks of 19,400 MW aggreg\nate capacity have been sanctioned in 20 States \ncovering Rajasthan and Maharashtra. State\n-\nwise details are given at Annexure\n-\nI.\n \n(b)&(c):\n \nThe progress of Solar Parks is reviewed from time to time. The present status is \ngiven at Annexure\n-\nI.\n \n(d)&(e):\n \nDetailed \nguidelines have been provided at the stage of sanction of solar \nprojects. Further, instructions are issued from time to time so that the projects are executed \nwithin the stipulated timelines.\n \n******\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |695", "t": "Technical Consultants", "head": "भारत सरकार \nसड़क पररवहन और राजमार्ग मंत्रालय \nलोक सभा \nललखित प्रश् न सं. 695 \nर्ुरुवार, 28 अप्रैल, 2016/ 08 वैशाि, 1938 (शक) \nतकनीकी परामशगदाता \n \n695. डॉ सुभाष रामराव भामरे: \nक्या सड़क पररवहन और राजमार्ग मंत्री यह बताने की कृपा करेंगे कक: \n(क) क्या पररयोजना के कायाान्वयन के स्टैन्डडा तथा कायाकुशलता बढाने के ललए कुछ तकनीकी परामशादाताओं \n(पररवहन ववशेषज्ञों) की सेवाएं लेने का कोई प्रस्ताव है; \n(ख) यदद हां, तो इन परामशादाताओं की अहाता संबंधी मानदंड, पाररश्रलमक, कायाकाल आदद सदहत तत् संबंधी ब्यौरा \nक्या है; और \n(ग) यदद नह ं, तो इसके क्या कारण हैं? \nउत् तर \nसड़क पररवहन और राजमार्ग मंत्रालय में राज् य मंत्री \n(श्री पोन्. राधाकृष् मन) \n \n(क) से (र्): सड़क पररवहन और राजमागा मंत्रालय 1,00,000/- रू. रालश प्रततमाह के पाररश्रलमक +5,000/- रू. \nप्रततमाह के स् थानीय वाहन सुववधा पर 4 तकनीकी परामशादाता तनयुक् त करना ााहता है   पा त्रता मानदंड \nतनम नानुसार है:- \nक्र. सं. परामशग क्षेत्र शैक्षक्षम और पात्रता मानदंड \n1 सड़क सुरक्षा/प्रवतान पररवहन/आटोमोबाइल/मेक ै तनकल/लसववल/ववधध इत् यादद में \nस् नातक \n2 उत् सजान आटोमोबाइल/मेक ै तनकल/ववधध इत् यादद में स् नातक \n3 भार  मोटर वाहन (3.5 टन से अधधक) बी. टेक/बीई-आटोमोबइल, मेक ै तनकल, \nउत् पादन/औद्योधगक \n4 हल् के मोटर वाहन (3.5 टन से कम) \n \n इसके अलावा, संबंधधत क्षेत्रों में न् य)नतम 20 वषा का अनुभव अपेक्षक्षत है   कें द्र य मोटरयान तनयमावल  \n(सीएमवीआर) मोटरयान अधधतनयम, सड़क सुरक्षा और अनुसमथान के कायाान् वयन में अनुभव प्रा् त क ें द्र य/राज् य \nसरकार अथवा आटोमोबाइल पर क्षण एजेंलसयों अथवा शैक्षणणक संस् थानों के सेवातनवृत् त/कायारत अधधकाररयों को \nप्राथलमकता द  जाएगी    \n तनयुक्क् त की प्रारंलभक अवधध एक  वषा की होगी और तदनन् तर ववस् तार, यदद कोई हो, के  संबंध में तनणाय \nववलशष् ट काया और इसके प)रा होने के समय पर तनभार करते हुए मामला-दर-मामला आधार पर ललया जाएगा    \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |696", "t": "Highway Projects in Rajasthan", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 696 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28TH APRIL 2016 \n \nHIGHWAY PROJECTS IN RAJASTHAN \n \n696.  SHRI RAHUL KASWAN: \n          \n Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of highways projects being undertaken in Rajasthan and the funds allocated for the \npurpose; and \n \n(b) the target fixed for completion of these projects and the mechanism in place to ensure the ir \ntimely completion? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to (b)     61 highway projects amounting to Rs. 22036 cr  are in progress in Rajasthan. The funds for \nthese projects are being allocated as per requirement. Target for completion of these projects  are varying \nbetween May 2016 and April 2018. Progress of these projects is  being closely monitored  by senior \nofficers of Ministry and NHAI to ensure timely completion. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |698", "t": "Assistance to Handloom Weavers", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.698 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nASSISTANCE TO HANDLOOM WEAVERS \n \n698.  SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES  वस्त्र मंरी \nbe pleased to state: \n   \n(a) Whether Government provides any financial assistance/subsidy to handloom \nweavers and artisans through Self -Help Groups/Non-Governmental Organisations \nfor different programmes/schemes; \n \n(b) if so, the details of the plans formulated for this purpose during last three \nyears and current year, State -wise, year-wise and institution -wise along with the \nquantum of financial assistance provided to these institutions/groups; and \n \n(c)  the other steps being take n by the Government to incentivise  and develop \nhandloom and handicraft sectors? \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज  ् य मंरी (स्त्वतंर प्रभार) (श्री संतोष कु मार गंगवार) \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF TEXTILES \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n(a) to(c):  \ni)  Comprehensive Handloom Development Scheme (CHDS)  as a component \nof National Handloom Development Programme (NHDP) was introduced in 2013-\n14. Under the scheme, there was provision for providing financial assistance to the \nNon-Governmental Organisation (NGO) through State Government concerned for \nimplementation of Cluster Development Programme. The guidelines of National \nHandloom Development Programme (NHDP) were amended in June  2015 to \ninclude Block level cluster as one of its components, which has a  provision to \nprovide financial assistance to registered Self-Help Groups/ Non-Governmental \nOrganisations for setting up of Block level cluster.  Details of financial assistance \nreleased to NGOs in the Handloom Sector in the last three year s i.e. 2013-14 to \n2015-16 and current  financial year 201 6-17(till-date) are at annexure- I. Funds \nhave not been released to NGOs during the current financial year (2016-17). \n\nThe Government of India , Ministry of Textiles  through the office of \nDevelopment Commissioner for Handlooms is implementing following schemes \nfor overall development of handloom sector and welfare handloom weavers: \n1. National Handloom Development Programme \n2. Handloom Weavers’ Comprehensive Welfare Scheme \n3. Yarn Supply Scheme \n4. Comprehensive Handloom Cluster Development Scheme \n \nii) In the Handicraft Sector, financial assistance is provided to various \nImplementing agencies such as State Handicrafts Corporation/Apex \nSocieties/Non-Government organizations etc. under various schemes of the \nGovernment for promotion and development of handicrafts in the country \nimplemented during the 12 th Plan period. Recently, schemes have been reviewed \nand rationalized into one umbrella scheme namely “National Handicraft \nDevelopment Programme  [NHDP]” to emphasize integrated approach for \ndevelopment of handicraft clusters in a holistic manner.  The NHDP has following \ncomponents:   \n \nI. A.      Ambedkar Hastshilp Vikas Yojna (AHVY) \n \nI. Dastkar Shashktikaran Yojna  \nII. Design & Technology Upgradation \nIII. Human Resource Development  (HRD)  \nIV. Direct Benefit to Artisans. \nV. Infrastructure and Technology Support. \n \n   B.     Mega Cluster \n \nII. Marketing Support & Services (MSS) \nIII. Research and Development (R&D) \n \nDetails of funds released under various schemes to Non -Government \norganizations are annexed. Funds have not been released to NGOs during the \ncurrent financial year (2016-17). \n\nAnnexure - I \nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a)  to ( c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.698 for \nanswer on 28.04.2016.  \n \nStatement showing amount released to NGOs during 2013-14 to 2016-17 (till date) \n \nS. No. Name of \nState \nName of NGO 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \n1. \nJammu & \nKashmir \nSocial Action for \nSocial Development, \nJammu \n15.22 - - \n2. \nMadhya \nPradesh \nCentre for Research \nand industrial Staff \nPerformance (CRISP), \nBhopal \n8.55 - - \n3. \nUttrakhand Women Development \nOrganization, \nDehradun \n14.691 - - \nShilp Kutumb   40.3925 \n4. \nUttar \nPradesh \nEhsaas Foundation, D-\n6, 6138/8, Vasant Kunj, \nNew Delhi \n17.800 - - \nJal Sangrahan Vikas \nEvam Gramothan \nSmiti, Surbhi Utsav, \nPahadiya, Varanasi \n17.453 - - \nSmriti Sewa Sansthan, \nC-23-E, Park \nMahanagar Extn., \nLucknow \n12.150   \nEhsaas Foundation, D-\n6, 6138/8, Vasant Kunj, \nNew Delhi \n- 30.94 - \n \n  \n\n \n  \n \n \n \n \nAnnexure II \nDetails of Grant-in-Aid Released to the NGO's during the Financial Year 2013-14  to 2015-16 \n   No. of NGOs / Financial Assistance (In Rupees) \nSl. \nNo. \nStates Scheme/ \nProject \nNo. of \nNGOs \n2013-\n14 \nFinancial \nAssistance \n2013-14 \nNo. of \nNGOs \n2014-\n15 \nFinancial \nAssistance \n2014-15 \nNo. of \nNGOs \n2015-\n16 \nFinancial \nAssistance \n2015-16  \n         \n1 Andhra Pradesh AHVY 33 2287723 1 75000 3 375000 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh AHVY 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n3 Assam AHVY 16 1600655 4 410250 28 2100000 \n4 Bihar AHVY 10 817500 0 0 0 0 \n5 Chhattisgarh AHVY 3 225000 2 217628 1 75000 \n6 Daman AHVY 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n7 Delhi AHVY 12 3883556 3 30780000 0 0 \n8 Goa AHVY 0 0 0 0 0 0 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |699", "t": "Power Plant in Andhra Pradesh", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.699 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nPOWER PLANT IN ANDHRA PRADESH \n \n699.  SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Union Government plans to set up new power plants in the \nState of Andhra Pradesh in collaboration with the State Government; and \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER,  \nCOAL AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY \n \n( SHRI  PIYUSH GOYAL ) \n \n(a) & (b) : National Thermal Power Corporation (NTPC) proposes to set up a \n4000 MW Thermal Power Project at Lalam Koduru (erstwhile Pudimadaka) in \nVishakhapatnam Dist. in Andhra Pradesh.  Feasibility Report of the Project \nhas been approved on 28.11.2014.  Further, NTPC is constructing 250 MW \nSolar PV Project at Ananthapur under Green Energy commitment to \nGovernment of India.  In addition, Notice Inviting Tender (NIT) for the another \n750 MW Solar PV Project at Ananthapur was issued on 21.08.2015. \n \n*********** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |7", "t": "Student Exchange Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 7 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25th APRIL, 2016 \n \nStudent Exchange Programme \n \n7. SHRI MULLAPPALLY RAMACHANDRAN:  \n  \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n (a)  whether the Government has any plans for student exchange programme between city and \nvillage schools in the country; and  \n \n (b)  if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a): Yes, Madam. \n \n(b): All States/UTs have been requested in July, 2015 to identify some schools for a student \ninteraction and exchange programme between rural and urban schools so that children get exposure \non varied economic, socio- cultural and academic parameters in rural and urban areas. A concept \npaper on “Partnership among Schools Programme” has been prepared and circulated to all State /UT \nGovernments. The Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan has report ed that 25 Kendriya Vidyalayas have \ntwinned with 25 State Government Schools for this programme. Central Board of Secondary \nEducation has informed that 73 well functioning schools  affiliated to the Board have partnered with \nGovernment run schools in rural areas. \n   "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |702", "t": "Cleaning of Hindon River", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 702 \nANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nCLEANING OF HINDON RIVER \n \n702.        SHRI BAIJAYANT JAY PANDA:  \n \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the excess industrial and municipal discharge in the \nHindon river, if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether any measures have been taken to ensure the river's health during the past three years; \nand  \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF  WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION  \n(SUSHRI UMA BHARTI) \n \n(a) Yes, Madam. The Government is aware of excess industrial and municipal discharge in the \nHindon river. According to a study by the CPCB:- \n \nThe assessment of the water quality of river Hindon  by Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) \nindicates that the river is not meeting the criteria with respect to Dissolved Oxygen, \nConductivity, BOD, Total Coliform and Faecal Coliform. The Dissolved Oxygen as low as 0.4 \nmg/l has been observed downstream Saharanpur. The high level of BOD can be attributed to \ndischarge of industrial effluents from number of electroplating and tannery units. River Hindon \nreceives the municipal as well as industrial effluents from the township of Saharanpur, \nMuzaffarnagar, Shamli, Meerut, Baghpat and Gautam Buddhnagar. \n \n(b) & (c) Under the Centrally sponsored scheme of Yamuna Action Plan (YAP) Phase -I, the \ntowns of Saharanpur, Muzzafarnagar, Ghaziabad and Gautam  Budhnagar located along Hindon, \nwhich is a tributary of river Yamuna, have been covered. Pollution aba tement works pertaining \nto interception, diversion and treatment of sewage, low cost sanitation, improved wood \ncrematoria, afforestation, public participation & awareness, etc. and amounting to Rs 159.35 \ncrore have been implemented in these four towns to reduce the domestic pollution load in river  \n \nContd.//2// \n \n\n//2// \nHindon. A sewage treatment capacity of 269.5 million liters per day (mld) has been created in \nthese towns under YAP Phase-I. \n \nAs per the information received from U.P. Pollution Control Board (UPPCB), out of the 108 \nmajor water polluting industries discharging their waste water directly into river Hindon, 107 \nindustries have installed effluent treatment plants and one unit has been closed down by UPPCB. \nNotices have been issued by UPPCB to 1 di stillery unit, 5 sugar mills and 4 industrial units to \ncomply with various pollution control norms.  \n \nAdditional steps taken by CPCB to control water pollution include: \n• Industrial pollution control measures taken under the provision of Water (Prevention and \nControl of Pollution), Act, 1974; \n• A mutually agreed time targeted programme is implemented under Corporate Responsibility on \nEnvironment Protection (CREP) on various commitments; \n• Special Drives: 17 categories of industries with zero liquid discharge, wherever feasible; \n• Intensive monitoring of industries discharging effluent into rivers and lakes; \n• Promotion of Common Effluent Treatment Plants (CETP) for cluster of Small Scale Industrial \nunits;  \n• Urban centres discharging wastewater in aquatic resources and having no treatment facilities \nhave been identified and recommended for appropriate action to respective authorities; \n• Identified urban centres are being considered under various River Action Plans for interception, \ndiversion and development of treatment facilities; \n• The depletion of flow in the rivers is one of the major factor for water quality degradation. \nSteps are being taken to augment flow in rivers for ecological consideration and the respective \nministries concerned such as Water Resou rces and Power are being persuaded to provide \nadequate flow in the rivers so that rivers can attain its ecological balance including desired water \nquality for various beneficial uses; \n• With respect to industrial effluents, consent management for compliance of standards is being \nenforced by SPCBs/PCCs to improve the water quality of the rivers; \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |703", "t": "District Vigilance and Monitoring Committees", "head": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.703\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016\nDISTRICT VIGILANCE AND MONITORING COMMITTEES\n703. SHRI ALOK SANJAR:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government had reviewed/proposes to review the working/ function of\nDistrict Vigilance and Monitoring Committees involved in monitoring the implementation\nof Rural Development schemes;\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the outcome thereof, State/UT-wise; and\n(c) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to strengthen the functioning of District\nVigilance and Monitoring Committees?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT)\n(a): The guidelines of the Vigilance and Monitoring Committees were revised in 2014 in\nview of the recommendations/observations made by the Parliament Standing Committee after\nexamining the working of Vigilance and Monitoring Committees (VMCs). The guidelines were\nsent to all District authorities for implementation of revised guidelines. A copy of the guidelines\nwas also sent to the Hon’ble MPs of both Lok Sabha & Rajya Sabha and also uploaded in\nMinistry’s website.\n(b): The Ministry advised to all State / UTs for reconstitution of VMCs at district level in all\nthe rural districts nominating Hon’ble elected Members of Parliament as Chairpersons / Co -\nChairpersons, after the formation of XVI Lok Sabha, on 8.8.2014 and circulated to all the States /\nDistricts and also uploaded in Ministry’s website. The Vigilance and Monitoring Committees\n(VMC) are constituted at district levels to function as an important instrument for effective\nmonitoring of implementati on of the programmes of the Ministry of Rural Development with a\ncrucial role for the Members of Parliament and elected representatives of people in State\nLegislatures in reviewing the implementation of the programmes of the Ministry of Rural\nDevelopment. The primary responsibility of District level Vigilance & Monitoring Committee is\nto effectively monitor the implementation of the Schemes/ programmes of the Ministry of Rural\nDevelopment as also cause to ensure that the funds are used for the purpose for w hich they are\nmeant.\n(c): As per the guidelines the district level VMC meeting should be held in every quarter on (i)\nLast Saturday of May; (ii) First Saturday of August; (iii) First Saturday of November; and (iv)\nFirst Saturday of February. The Sta te Governments were reminded from time to time for regular\nmeeting of VMCs and upload the agenda, MOM etc. in the Ministry’s website. Letters from\nSecretary (RD) to Chief Secretaries of all the States/UT Administration issued on 15th May, 2015\n\nand again on 5th November, 2015 to instruct the District Collectors to hold the District level VMC\nmeetings as per the guidelines. Additional Secretary(RD) has also requested Principal\nSecretary(RD) of all the States on 3 rd December, 2015 to instruct the Dist rict Collectors to hold\nthe District level VMC meetings as per the guidelines. Such letters from Adviser (Stat) were\nissued on 28th November, 2014, 17th March, 2015 and 25th April, 2016.\nIt is also emphasized in the Performance Review Commit tee (PRC) meeting held on 18 th\nand 19th September, 2014, 13th and 14th January, 2015 and 8th and 9th July, 2015.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |704", "t": "Encouraging Growth in Power Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.704 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nENCOURAGING GROWTH IN POWER SECTOR \n \n704.  SHRI R. PARTHIPAN: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Indian Power sector has still not been able to induce and sustain the \nrequired capacity addition so as to match the ever-growing power demand of the country \ndespite the encouraging growth trajectory in the energy space over the last few years; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  the key challenges faced by the Government in this regard and the steps proposed \nto be taken in pursuance thereof; \n \n(d)  whether the Government has any specific package for the overall development of \npower sector; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER,  \nCOAL AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY \n \n( SHRI  PIYUSH GOYAL ) \n \n(a) & (b) : During the 12th Plan period, a target of 88,537 MW excluding 30,000 MW of \nRenewal Energy Source, was fixed for capacity addition.  Against this, cumulative \ncapacity addition of 84,990.7 MW has been achieved as on 31.03.2016. \n \n(c) :   Some of the key challenges faced by the Government are delay in land \nacquisition, environment and forest issues, rehabilitation & resettlement issues, natural \ncalamities, law and order problems, local issues, contractual problems, dela y in material \nsupply, geological uncertainties, extreme weather conditions, difficult terrain and poor \naccessibility, funds constraints, force majeure risk, inter – state issues, Right of way \nproblem (ROW) for transmission lines etc. \n \nThe following steps are taken for monitoring ongoing/delayed power \nprojects for their timely completion:  \n \n Central Electricity Authority (CEA) monitors the progress of under construction \npower projects through frequent site visits and interaction with the developers, \nequipment suppliers and other stakeholders to identify issues critical for \ncommissioning of projects and help in resolving them.  \n \n Regular reviews are also undertaken by Ministry of Power, Ministry of Heavy \nIndustries and Cabinet Secretariat to identify the areas o f constraint and \nfacilitate faster resolution of inter- ministerial and other outstanding issues.  \n \n...........2. \n\n- 2 - \n \n A Power Project Monitoring Panel (PPMP) has been set up by the Ministry  of \nPower for monitoring of on -going Thermal and Hydro Generation projects \ntargeted for  commissioning during the 12th P lan and beyond along with the \nassociated transmission system.  \n \n Issues are also raised in PRAGATI, for proactive governance and timely \nimplementation, as and when required. \n \n(d) & (e) :  For development of Power Sector, the following schemes have been launched: \n \n(I)  Deendayal Upadhyaya Gram Jyoti Yojna (DDUGJY) for  \n  \n(i) Separation of agriculture and non -agriculture feeders facilitating judicious \nrestoring of supply to agricultural & non-agriculture consumers in the rural areas; \nand \n \n(ii) Strengthening and augmentation of sub -transmission & distribution infrastructure \nin rural areas, including metering of distribution transformers / feeders / \nconsumers. \n \nThe components at (i) and (ii) of th e above scheme have an estimated outlay of \nRs. 43,033 crore including a budgetary support of Rs. 33,453 crore from Government of \nIndia during the entire implementation period.  In addition, the scheme of Rajiv Gandhi \nGrameen Vidyutikaran Yojana ( RGGVY) has been subsumed in this scheme with an \napproved cost of Rs. 39,275 crore including budgetary support of Rs. 35,447 crore. \n \n(II)  Integrated Power Development Scheme (IPDS) with the following components: \n \n(i) Strengthening of sub -transmission and distribution networks in the urban areas; \nand \n \n(ii) Metering of distribution transformers / feeders / consumers in the urban areas. \n \nThe components at (i) and (ii) above have an estimated outlay of Rs. 32,612 crore \nincluding a budgetary support of Rs. 25,354 crore from Gove rnment of India during the \nentire implementation period.  In addition, the scheme of Restructured Accelerated Power \nDevelopment and Reforms Programme (R-APDRP) has been subsumed in this scheme with \napproved cost of Rs. 44,011 crore including budgetary support of Rs. 22,727 crore. \n \n(III)  National Smart Grid Mission (NSGM) for planning, monitoring and implementation \nof policies & programs related to development of smart grid in India. \n   \nThe total estimated cos t for NSGM activities for 12th P lan is Rs.980 crore \nincluding a budgetary support of Rs.338 crores. Under NSGM, 30% funding will be \nprovided for development of Smart Grid in selected Smart Cities in the coun try along with \ndevelopment of micro grid in the Country.  \n \n \n************ \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |705", "t": "Revival of NTC Mills", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.705 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nREVIVAL OF NTC MILLS \n \n705. SHRI P. Kumar: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES  वस्त्र मंरी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Government plans to revive some of the textile mills of the National \nTextile Corporation in the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof including the funds allocated to each mill; and \n \n(c) the measures being taken to ensure the job security of the workers in these mills, \nfill up the vacancies and also to generate additional employment? \n \n उत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज् य मंरी (स्त्वतंर प्रभार) (श्री संतोष कुमार गंगवार) \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF TEXTILES \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) & (b): National Textile Corporation (NTC) has been implementing the Board for \nIndustrial and Financial Reconstruction  (BIFR) approved revival scheme Modified \nScheme (MS) - 08.  Under the revival scheme MS- 08, NTC has no mandate to revive the \nunits closed under Industrial Dispute Act .  At present, there is no proposal for revival of \nclosed NTC mills.   \n \n(c): The workers in the mills of NTC are protected under the statutes and in the event \nof being declared surplus on account of workload settlement are  readjusted in other mills \nof NTC or given Modified Voluntary Retirement Scheme (MVRS).  Vacancies arising out \nof superannuation , etc. are filled up by the casual workers available in the  mills.  \nGeneration of additional employment is li nked to modernization and expansion of the \nexisting capacity.  \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |706", "t": "Central Road Fund Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \nLOK   SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-706 \nANSWERED ON 28TH April, 2016 \n \nCENTRAL ROAD FUND SCHEME  \n \n706.    DR. KIRIT SOMAIYA: \n \n    Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n    सड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n            be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has received proposals on construction of Road and Bridges under \nthe Central Road Fund Scheme from Government of Maharashtra;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government accorded in principle approval to some of these proposals;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof with total value; and  \n(e) the time by which the final sanction of these proposals of Roads and Bridges would be \nissued? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON.  RADHAKRISHNAN) \n(a)  & (b): Yes, Madam.  The Government has received several proposals for sanction under \nCentral Road Fund Scheme during 2015-16 from Government of Maharashtra.  \n \n(c)   to (e): The Government had accorded In Principal approval for 221 proposals amounting \nto Rs. 4032 crore. Keeping in view the availability of fund s, Government has approved 170 \nworks amounting to Rs. 3111.38 crore during the financial year 2015-16. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |708", "t": "Appellate Tribunal for Foreign Exchange", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE\nDEPARTMENT OF LEGAL AFFAIRS\n****\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.708\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THURSDAY, THE 28TH APRIL, 2016.\nAppellate Tribunal for Foreign Exchange\n708.  SHRI CHANDU LAL SAHU:\nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of appeals received and disposed off by the Appellate Tribunal \nfor Foreign Exchange (ATFE) in the country during each of the last three \nyears;\n(b) the funds allocated and utilized for the functioning of ATFE during the said \nperiod, year-wise;\n(c) whether the Government proposes to increase the number of benches of \nATFE in view of the large number of pending cases; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the other steps taken/being taken by the \nGovernment for expeditious disposal of pending cases in ATFE?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE\n(SHRI D.V. SADANANDA GOWDA)\n****\n(a) : The number of appeals received and disposed off by the Appellate  Tribunal for \nForeign Exchange (ATFE) during each of the last three years are given below:-\nYear Appeals received Disposed off\n2015 76 77\n2014 95 49\n2013 50 27\n(b): Details of fund allocated and utilized for the functioning of ATFE during each of \nthe last three financial years is given below:-\nFinancial year Funds allocated Funds utilized\n2015-16 Rs. 9,32,00,000/- Rs. 7,79,05,000/-*\n2014-15 Rs. 8,24,50,000/- Rs. 8,09,71,000/-\n2013-14 Rs. 8,00,00,000/- Rs. 7,23,65,000/-\n* Expenditure upto 4th March, 2016. \n(c) & (d): At present there is no proposal to increase the number of benches of \nATFE. After appointments of Chairperson and two Members in the ATFE, the Tribunal \nhas become fully functional and consequently the disposal rate of Appeals in the ATFE \nhas also increased.  \n****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |709", "t": "Vadodara-Mumbai Highway", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  709 \nANSWERED ON 28TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nVADODARA-MUMBAI HIGHWAY  \n \n \n709. SHRI DILIP PATEL: \n            \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसडक़ परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has received any proposals from the Government of Gujarat for \njoining Ahmedabad-Vadodara National Highway with the newly approved Vadodara -Mumbai \nExpress Way; \n \n(b) )  if so, the details thereof and the status of preparation of feasibility report for the project; and \n \n(c) whether any timeframe has been fixed to join these two parts of the National Highways and if \nso, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to (c) Ahmedabad – Vadodara section of National Highway No -8 has already been constructed .  \nVadodara to Mumbai corridor has been selected for upgradation to Expressway.  F easibility study of \nthis corridor has already been approved under NHDP-VI.  Time frame for construction of Vadodara-\nMumbai Expressway cannot be indicated at this stage.  Ahmedabad-Vadodara National Highway No-8 \nis to be connected with the Vadodara-Mumbai Express way after construction.  \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |710", "t": "Works Stalled Under PMGSY", "head": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 710\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016\nWORKS STALLED UNDER PMGSY\n710. SHRI LAXMI NARAYAN YADAV:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the work relating to the road construction under the Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak\nYojana is stalled in Madhya Pradesh due to paucity of funds;\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken/proposed by the Government thereon; and\n(c) the efforts made by the Government to provide funds for construction of roads under\nPMGSY in Madhya Pradesh?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT)\n(a) to (c): Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) suffered from extreme paucity of\nfund during first 3 years of 12th plan period i.e. from 2012-13 to 2014-15. The budget of PMGSY\nwas substantially reduced during the year 2012-13 and 2013-14 at Revised Estimate (R.E.) stage.\nDuring the year 2014-15 an amount of Rs. 14,200 crore was allocated to PMGSY.  Consequently\na large number of pending liabilities remained at the level of the States. During the year 2015-16,\nRs. 14,291 crore was allocated to PMGSY, which was substantially enhanced to Rs. 18,291 crore\nat R.E. stage.  Rs. 19,000 crore have been allocated for PMGSY for the year 2016-17.\nWith the new funding pattern that has been brought into place from the year 2015-16, the funds\nwould be released by the Centre and the State in a 60:40 ratio in all the States except the Hill\nStates and North Eastern States where the pattern would be 90:10. This has improved the fund\navailability with the States including Madhya Pradesh, under PMGSY.\nThe funds that have been released/allocated to Madhya Pradesh during each year of the 12 th year\nplan is as follows:\n\n(Rs. in crore)\n*    As per 60:40 pattern from the year 2015-16.\n** A balance of Rs. 19.32 crore towards State’s matching Share would be released in 2016-17.\n*** Allocation of Madhya Pradesh during 2016-17.\nThe indicative allocation of the State has continuously been enhanced during the current\nplan period so that projects do not get stalled due to lack of funds.\n****\nS.No. Year Central Share* State Share* Total\n1. 2012-13 242.88 0.00 242.88\n2. 2013-14 615.00 0.00 615.00\n3. 2014-15 708.00 0.00 708.00\n4. 2015-16 1122.00 720.95** 1842.95\n5. 2016-17 1700.00*** 1133.00*** 2833.00"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |711", "t": "Setting up of Call Centres for Accident Victims on NHs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 711 \nANSWERED ON 28TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nSETTING UP OF CALL CENTRES FOR ACCIDENT VICTIMS ON NHS \n \n711.  SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARSWATI: \n SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \n \n(a)  whether the Government has asked the National Highway Authority of India (NHAI) to set up \n call centres for helping accident victims on National Highways so that timely treatment can be \n given to victims within golden hours; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether NHAI has complied with this; and \n(d) if so, the details of call centres set  up on National Highways, State -wise particularly in \n Rajasthan and Bihar? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to (d)  Yes, Sir. Govt. has decided to set up “1033” toll free national helpline for reporting road \naccidents and other issues related to National Highways. National Highway s Authority of India \n(NHAI) has mandated Indian Highway Management Company Limited (IHMCL), a Company  \npromoted by NHAI, to setup call centres for National Highway s helpline. The project has been \nstructured under following six zones - \nZones States Covered Location of Call \nCentre \nWest Goa, Gujarat, Maharashtra, Rajasthan, Dadra and Nagar \nHaveli & Daman and Diu \nAhmedabad \nSouth Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, Kerala, TamilNadu, \nTelangana, Andaman and Nicobar Island, Lakshadweep & \nPuducherry \nMysore \nDelhi Delhi, Uttar Pradesh, Uttarakhand & Madhya Pradesh Mumbai \nChandigarh Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, Jammu & Kashm ir, Punjab & \nChandigarh \nMohali \nEast Bihar, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Orissa & West Bengal  Work not awarded \nNortheast Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, \nMizoram, Nagaland, Sikkim & Tripura \nWork not awarded \n \nThe call centres are not fully operationalized due to technical issues related to declaration of “non -\nmetered” incoming calls by Department of Telecommunication (DOT). The call centre agencies are \nencountering difficulties in routing of calls by Telecom Service Providers (TSPs). IHMCL h as \ninvited fresh bids for work award. \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |712", "t": "Solar Energy System in Powerloom Industries", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.712 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nSOLAR ENERGY SYSTEM IN POWERLOOM INDUSTRIES \n \n712. SHRI S. SELVAKUMARA CHINNAYAN: \n    \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंरी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the U nion Government has received any proposal from various quarters \nincluding Powerloom Owner s’ Association of Coimbatore and Tirupur district s for \nextending 50 per cent of the cost as subsidy for installing 100 MW rooftop solar energy \nsystem for powerlooms industries.   \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof ; and  \n \n(c) the decision taken by the Union Government on the said proposal  \n \n         उत्तर \nANSWER \n  वस्त्र राज्य मंरी (स्त्वतंर प्रभार) (श्री संतोष कुमार गंगवार) \n  MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \n   IN THE MINISTRY OF TEXTILES  \n         (SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam. A proposal has been received from District Labour Weavers \nWorking Powerloom Owners Association of Coimbatore & Tirupur requesting the \nCentral Govt. for providing 50 % subsidy to small scale Powerlooms for installing solar \npower plants to overcome the problems of electricity charge hike. \n \n(c): The proposal is under examination. \n  \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |713", "t": "Transfer of Lignite Blocks", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 713 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nTransfer of Lignite Blocks \n \n713.  DR. KIRIT P. SOLANKI: \n \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Union Government has received  any request/proposal from the \nGovernment of Gujarat to transfer E,F & G Lignite Blocks in South Gujarat to \nGovernment, Public Sector Undertaking, i.e. Gujarat Mineral Development \nCorporation Limited for supplying the same to small manufacturing industries  for \nmeeting with their energy requirements; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the action taken by the Union Government thereon? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL, POWER   AND NEW & \nRENEWABLE ENERGY \n \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a)&(b) Ministry of Coal has received proposal from the Government of Gujarat  for prior \napproval of reservation of Lignite bearing area of 3015 hectares of village \nSodgam, Vitthalgam , Vandriya, Pithor, Deheli, Jambugam, Singla, Vagakhol of \nValiaTaluka of Bharuch District under section 17A (2) of Mines and Minerals \n(Development and Regulation) Act (MMDR), 1957 in favour of Gujarat Mineral \nDevelopment Corporation Limited(GMDC). The said proposals of Government \nof Gujarat w ere examined and accordingly , the Ministry accorded the approval \nto the proposal of Government of Gujarat for reserving 3015 hectare area \npertaining to the block E,F,G in ValiyaTaluka of District Bharuch under \nprovision of Section 17 (A) (2) of MMDR Act 1957 in favour of GMDC. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |714", "t": "e-Challans to Drivers", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 714 \nANSWERED ON 28TH APRIL, 2016 \nE-CHALLANS TO DRIVERS \n \n714.  MAJ GEN B C KHANDURI AVSM (RETD): \nSHRI P.R. SUNDARAM: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n  \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken a decision to prepare a national database of errant drivers and \nissue of e-challans to drivers throughout the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether there is no system at present in vogue where motor vehicle owners  could be tracked easily \nonce the vehicle is involved in an accident or challans have remained unpaid as they do not intimate the \nchange of address; and \n \n(d) if so, the corrective steps taken or proposed to be taken by the Government in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n (a) to (d)  An e-Challan software comprising a mobile app cum web application has been developed by \nNational Informatics Centre (NIC)  for M inistry of Road Transport and Highways to provide a  \ncomprehensive enforcement solution that can be used by the State Transport Enforcement cells and also \nby the Traffic Police. This application will facilitate a convenient and efficient mechanism to challan \nerrant drivers while providing a set of features and functionalities.  Currently, this mobile app cum web \napplication is under pilot implementation in two Regional Transport Offices in the State of Uttar Pradesh \nand also under advanced testing in the State of Uttarakhand and Maharashtra. Once implemented across \nthe country, this system will create a unified national database of all traffic violations, with details of \nviolators, challans, disposals, penalties, court cases, punitive actions etc. \nAt present, Transport Na tional Register (NR) comprising 19 crore vehicle details and 9 crore \ndriving license records is available with access provided to different police and transport agencies across \nthe country for tracking any vehicle or driver information.  \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |715", "t": "Water Purification Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 715 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \n Water Purification Plants   \n \n715. KUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE:  \n \nWill the Minister of DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Union Government is aware of the setting up of c ommunity water purification \nplants using R.O. technology in those villages of Karnataka where gro undwater has been \nfound to be contaminated;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(c) whether the Government is aware that the minerals essenti al for human body are also \nextracted from the water purified by R.O. technology and if so, t he details thereof, and the \nconcrete remedial steps taken or proposed to be taken by the Gove rnment to address the \nproblem? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE  \nIN THE MINISTRY OF DRINKING   WATER AND SANITATION \n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV) \n \n(a) & (b) : The Ministry of Drinking Water and Sanitation, Governm ent of India is aware \nthat Karnataka State Government is implementing Community Puri fication Plants using Reverse \nOsmosis (RO) Technology mostly in fluoride affected rural habi tations.   Rural Drinking Water \n& Sanitation Department, Government of Karnataka have informed tha t so far 2,432 community \nwater purification plants using RO Technology have been commissi oned.  Further, NITI Aayog \nhas provided Rs.59.90 crore for installation of community purification pl ants in all remaining \nfluoride affected habitations before March 2017. \n(c):  The Ministry of Drinking Water and Sanitation is aware t hat RO technology can remove \nalmost all minerals in water, and, therefore, State Governments ha ve been advised to maintain \nadequate total dissolved solids (TDS) concentration in treated wate r so that essential minerals \nlike calcium, magnesium and sodium are available for human consumpti on.  Government of \nKarnataka have informed that the water purification plants are  so designed to retain minerals \nessential for human body in the treated water. "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |717", "t": "Four Laning of NH-209", "head": " \n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 717 \nANSWERED ON 28TH  APRIL, 2016 \n  \nFOUR LANING OF NH-209 \n \n717. SHRI C. MAHENDRAN: \n   \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Union Government has any proposal for laying of 4 lanes in National \nHighway No.209 from Dindigul to Samrajnagar via Pollachi in Tamil Nadu;   \n \n(b)   if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)    the time by which the proposal is likely to be implemented? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to (c)  The Ministry has already sanction ed the 4 laning work of about 27 km. from \nPollachi to Coimbatore of NH-209 for an amount of Rs. 656.51 Crore. This work is \ntargeted for completion by June, 2018. The rema ining stretch of NH-209 is in DPR \nstage. The decision of 4 lane/2 lane of rema ining stretch will  depend on the out  \ncome  of DPR.  \n \n***** \n    \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |718", "t": "Works under MGNREGS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 718\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016\nWORKS UNDER MGNREGS\n718. SHRI A.T. NANA PATIL:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to use MGNREGS in development of the rural\nhousehold infrastructure by construction of the toilet for sanitation, creation of water\nharvesting infrastructure and development of rural school infrastructure etc.\n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the timeline for the same; and\n(c) whether the Government also proposes to provide the employment to skilled workers or\nfor creating skill workforce under this scheme?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT)\n(a)&(b): As per Schedule -I of Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee\nAct (MGNREGA), construction of the toilets for rural sanitation, creation of water harvesting\ninfrastructure are permitted under MGNREGA. The construction of play fields under rural\ninfrastructure are also permitted.\nAs regards development of rural school infrastructure, construction of Kitchen shed and\ndining space in rural schools, are proposed to include under MGNREGA.\n(c): Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA), 2005\nprovide for the enhancement of livelihood security of the households in rural areas of the country\nby providing at least one hundred days of guaranteed wage employment in e very financial year\nto every household whose adult members volunteer to do unskilled manual work. However,\nwhere ever required in creation of assets, skilled workers are provided employment and their\nwages are covered under material component.\nThe Government has formulated and launched a “Project for Livelihoods in Full\nEmployment under Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (Project LIFE-\nMGNREGA)” in April 2015. The project has been formulated in convergence with Deen Dayal\nUpadhaya Grameen Kaushalya Yojna (DDU -GKY) and National Rural Livelihood Mission\n(NRLM). Project LIFE -MGNREGA aims at promoting self -reliance and improving the skill\nbase of MGNREGA workers, thereby, converting them from being asset-less, helpless, unskilled\nworkers dependent on Government support into fully employed entities with enhanced income.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |719", "t": "Implementation of Enterprise Resource Planning System", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 719 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nImplementation of Enterprise Resource Planning System \n \n719. SHRI A. ARUNMOZHITHEVAN: \n  \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Coal India Limited (CIL) has decided to implement an Enterprise \nResource Planning (ERP) system that will help it monitor and optimize performance on a \nreal time basis and save money as the company targets production of 1 billion tonnes by \n2019-2020;  \n \n(b) if so, whether the CIL will save Rs.1000 crore every year on better resource and \ninventory management once the ERP system is fully implemented; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the advantages of an ERP system for CIL would include improved \nrevenue and financial management, introduction to International Financial Reporting \nStandards based accounting, optimization of coal production and its movement by rail, \nroad and other modes of transport, sampling, reduction in spare parts invento ry and \nbreakdown maintenance, improved governance, regulatory and environmental \ncompliance, enhanced manpower productivity and innovation; and \n     \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nCOAL, POWER AND NEW RENEWABLE ENERGY  (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (c): Coal India Limited (CIL) has decided to implement an Enterprise Resource \nPlanning (ERP)  for better resource and inventory management . Management \nDevelopment Institute, Gurgaon, has been assigned the task of selection of product & \nsystem integrator . For this, it has been directed to prepare a Detailed Project Report \n(DPR).  \n \n(d) & (e): ERP implementation is expected to facilitate improved revenue & financial \nmanagement, introduction of Internationa l Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS) based \naccounting, movement of coal by rail & other modes of transport, sampling, reduction in \nspare parts inventory and breakdown maintenance, improved governance, regulatory & \nenvironmental compliances, enhance manpower productivity and innovation. \n<><><> "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |72", "t": "Lonar Crater Tourist Centre", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†72  \nANSWERED ON 25.04.2016  \n \nLONAR CRATER TOURIST CENTRE  \n \n†72.  SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has provided an y Central \nassistance so far to the Lonar Crater tourist centre of \nBuldhana district in Maharashtra, which attracts \nthousands of foreign tourists;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \nand  \n(c)  the steps taken by the Government  for beautification and \ndevelopment of the said tourist place and to provide basic \namenities to the tourists?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)  \n \n(a) to (c):   No, Madam.  No such proposal has been received \nfrom the State Government of Maharashtra.   \n \n \n********* \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |720", "t": "Potential of Energy Generation from Biomass", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 720\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016\n \n \nPOTENTIAL OF ENERGY GENERATION FROM BIOMASS\n \n \n720.\n \nSHRI RAVNEET SINGH:\n \nSHRIMATI RAKSHATAI KHADSE:\n \n \nWill the Minister of New and Renewable Energy be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the country possess huge potential of energy generation from biomass, if \nso, the details thereof;\n \n \n(b)\n \nwhether the Government has undertaken any study to find out the same, if \nso, the \ndetails thereof;\n \n \n(c)\n \nthe fund allocated by the Government for energy generation from biomass during \neach of the last three years, States\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(d)\n \nwhether the Government proposes to establish\n \nrice husk biomass\n \npower generation \nplants by using \ng\nasifire were established in Bihar at the village levels, if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n \n(e)\n \nthe present status of these power generation plants in the country; and\n \n \n(f)\n \nthe steps being taken by t\nhe Government for promotion of g\nasifires\n \nto use widely \nas a non\n-\nconventional energy source?\n \nANSWER\n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR POWER, COAL & NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \n \n(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL)\n \n \n(a)&(b):\n \nAs \nper the National Biomass Resource Atlas prepared by Indian Institute of \nScien\nce, Bangalore under a project sponsored by Ministry of New and Renewable Energy, \nthe estimated power generation capacity from biomass is about 18,000 MW. In addition, a \nsurplus power generation capacity of about 5000 MW through bagasse based cogeneration \ni\nn sugar mills has been estimated in the country. The state\n-\nwise details of estimated power \ngeneration potential from agricultural residues and bagasse based cogeneration potential \nare given at \nAnnexure\n-\nI and II \nrespectively\n.\n \n \n(c):\n \nS\ntate\n-\nwise details of fun\nds released under biomass power and baggase cogeneration \nprogrammes, during last three years are given at \nAnnexure\n-\nIII\n.\n \n \n(d)&(e):\n \nThe Ministry of New and Renewable Energy (MNRE) is promoting \ngeneration of electricity from agro\n-\nresidues including paddy husk through biomass gasifier \nsystem for meeting unmet demand of electricity in rural areas. Sixty rice husk based \ngasifier systems of\n \n32 kW each are providing electricity in various villages in the state of \nBihar. \n \n(f):\n \nThe Ministry of New and Renewable Energy provides Central Finance Assistance \n(CFA) under biomass gasifier programme ranging from Rs. 2,500 to Rs.15,000 per\n \nkilowatt depe\nnding upon application.\n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nreferred to in reply to part (a) & (b) of the Lok Sabha Un\n-\nstarred Question \nNo. \n720 \nfor\n \n28.04.2016\n \nregarding \n“P\notential \nof Energy \nGeneration \nfrom\n \nBiomass\n”\n \n \nState\n-\nwise details of estimated power potential from \nagricultural residues\n \n \nS. No.\n \nState\n \nEstimated Power Potential from \nsurplus agricultural residues \n \n(MW)\n \n1\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n863.3\n \n2\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n9.2\n \n3\n \nAssam\n \n283.9\n \n4\n \nBihar\n \n641.1\n \n5\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n248.5\n \n6\n \nGoa\n \n20.9\n \n7\n \nGujarat\n \n1224.8\n \n8\n \nHaryana\n \n1456.9\n \n9\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n132.6\n \n10\n \nJammu &\n \nKashmir\n \n37.1\n \n11\n \nJharkhand\n \n106.7\n \n12\n \nKarnataka\n \n1195.7\n \n13\n \nKerala\n \n864.4\n \n14\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n1373.3\n \n15\n \nMaharashtra\n \n1983.7\n \n16\n \nManipur\n \n14.3\n \n17\n \nMeghalaya\n \n11.3\n \n18\n \nMizoram\n \n1.12\n \n19\n \nNagaland\n \n10\n \n20\n \nOrissa\n \n429.3\n \n21\n \nPunjab\n \n3172.2\n \n22\n \nRajasthan\n \n1126.7\n \n23\n \nSikkim\n \n2.29\n \n24\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n1160\n \n25\n \nTripura\n \n2.94\n \n26\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n1746.2\n \n27\n \nUttarakhand\n \n80.9\n \n28\n \nWest Bengal\n \n529.3\n \n \nTotal\n \n18728.7\n \n(\nMW = Megawatt)\n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nII\n \nAnnexure\n-\nII referred to in reply to part (a) & (b) of the Lok Sabha Un\n-\nstarred Question \nNo. 720 for 28.04.2016 regarding \n“Potential of Energy Generation from Biomass”\n \n \nState\n-\nwise details of estimated bagasse \nco\n-\ngeneration potential\n \n \n \nS.No.\n \nStates\n \nPotential (MW)\n \n \n1.\n \nMaharashtra\n \n1250\n \n2.\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n1250\n \n3.\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n450\n \n4.\n \nKarnataka\n \n450\n \n5.\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n300\n \n6.\n \nBihar\n \n300\n \n7.\n \nGujarat\n \n350\n \n8.\n \nPunjab\n \n300\n \n9.\n \nHaryana, Uttara\nkhand\n \n& others\n \n350\n \n \nTotal\n \n5000\n \n(\nMW = Megawatt)\n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nIII\n \nAnnexure\n-\nIII referred to in reply to part (c) of the Lok Sabha Un\n-\nstarred Question No. \n720 for 28.04.2016 regarding \n“Potential of Energy Generation from Biomass”\n \n \nDetails of funds released under biomass power and baggase\n \ncogeneration programmes, \nstate\n-\nwise during last three years\n \n \n \n(Rupees in crore)\n \nS.No.\n \nStates/UT\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2015\n-\n16\n \n1\n \nGujarat\n \n0.89\n \n0.00\n \n0.00\n \n2\n \nHaryana\n \n0.00\n \n0.00\n \n0.88\n \n3\n \nKarnataka\n \n0.90\n \n1.77\n \n0.00\n \n4\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n0.00\n \n0.89\n \n0.00\n \n5\n \nMaharashtra\n \n27.80\n \n17.12\n \n21.91\n \n6\n \nPunjab\n \n0.00\n \n0.85\n \n0.00\n \n7\n \nRajasthan\n \n0.00\n \n2.26\n \n0.48\n \n8\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n0.64\n \n0.00\n \n0.83\n \n9\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n1.12\n \n1.00\n \n1.50\n \n10\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n0.00\n \n0.00"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |722", "t": "New Discom Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.722 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nNEW DISCOM SCHEME \n \n722.  SHRI ARJUN LAL MEENA \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has launched a new discom plan/scheme; \n \n(b)  if so, the objectives thereof; \n \n(c)  the assistance to be provided under the said scheme to the States; \n \n(d)  whether the Government has held  any consultations with the States for  \nimplementing the said scheme; \n \n(e)  if so, the details and the outcome thereof; and \n \n(f)  the States that have signed an MoU in this regard? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER,  \nCOAL AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY \n \n( SHRI  PIYUSH GOYAL ) \n(a) & (b)   : The Government has launched a Scheme Ujwal DISCOM Assurance \nYojana (UDAY) on 20 -11-2015 for the operational and financial turnaround of State \nowned Power Distribution Companies (DISCOMs).  The Scheme aims to re duce \ninterest burden, cost of power and power losses in Distribution sector, besides \nimproving operational efficiency of DISCOMs. \n(c) :    The Scheme incentivizes the participating States by exempting State \ntakeover of DISCOM debts from Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management  \n(FRBM) limits for two years; increased supply of domestic coal; coal linkage \nrationalization; liberally allowing coal swaps from inefficient to eff icient plants; \nallocation of coal linkages to States at notified prices and additional/priority \nfunding in Schemes of MoP and MNRE, if they meet the operational milestones in \nthe Scheme. \n \n(d) to (f) :  The Government has held detailed deliberations regular ly with the \nstakeholders, namely State Governments, DISCOMs and financial institutions etc. \nfor development as well as implementation of the scheme.  So far , 10 States have \nalready signed the Memorandum of Understanding (MoUs) under UDAY.  \n \n********** \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |723", "t": "Axle Load Rules for Overloading", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 723 \nANSWERED ON 28TH APRIL, 2016 \nAXLE LOAD RULES FOR OVERLOADING \n \n723.  SHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n  \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has made  some changes in the Axle load rules to check  the problem of \noverloading and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government has  conducted any survey regarding the  pollution, road accidents and \ndamage caused to road due to the increase in Axle load; \n \n(c) whether the attention of  Government has been drawn towards road  accidents in 2013 wherein 95441 \nroad accidents took place due to overloading which is 19.6 per cent of total road accidents; and \n \n(d) if so, the steps taken by the  Government to implement new Axle load  rules more stringently so that \nthe recurrence of such accidents may be checked ? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) The Ministry of Road Transport and Highways issued notification S.O.41(E) dated 7 th January, \n2016 vide which the tolerance up to five per cent in the gross vehicle weight and safe axle weight has \nbeen allowed.  \n \n(b) to (d) The Ministry has not conducted survey regarding the pollution, road accidents and damage \ncaused to increase in Axle load.  The Government is aware of the road accident data which took place \ndue to overloading. The Ministry has  been sensitizing the State Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations from time to time for proper implementation of section 114, section 194 and section 200 \nof Motor Vehicles Act, 1988 to curb overloading of goods carriages.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |724", "t": "Utilization of Power by Public/Private Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.724 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nUTILIZATION OF POWER BY PUBLIC/PRIVATE SECTOR \n \n†724.  SHRI GOPAL SHETTY: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the power generation units  in the country are in a position to fully  \nutilise their installed capacity; \n \n(b)  if so, the details in this regard; \n \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d)  the details of the utilisation  difference of installed capacity between public \nsector and private sector power generation units during the last three years  till date, \nyear-wise? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER,  \nCOAL AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY \n \n( SHRI  PIYUSH GOYAL ) \n \n(a) to (d) : The utilization of power generating station is dependent on a number of \nfactors, like type / category of plant (hydro or thermal), design and age of the units, \nforced outages & planned maintenance, availability of water, availability of fuel, \nquality of fuel and schedule given by beneficiary states etc.  \n \n  The details of the Plant load factor of Thermal and Nuclear power \nstation for public sector (Central & State sector) and private sector during the last \nthree years are given at Annex-I. \n \nThe details of Hydro generation during the last three years w.r.t. target \ncomparing public sector (Central & State sector) and private sector is given at \nAnnex-II. \n \n************* \n\n \n \n \n \nANNEX-I \n \nANNEX REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n724 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 28.04.2016.     \n*********** \n \n \nSector Fuel %PLF \n2015-16* 2014-15 2013-14 \nCentral Coal / Lignite 72.48 73.96 76.11 \n  Gas / Liq Fuel 24.53 26.07 28.18 \n  Nuclear 73.65 80.74 81.74 \nState Coal / Lignite 55.41 59.83 59.06 \n  Gas / Liq Fuel 24.19 25.88 27.83 \nPvt Coal / Lignite 58.99 60.2 61.42 \n  Gas/ Liq Fuel 18.55 11.06 18.05 \nAll India Coal / Lignite 62.28 64.46 65.55 \n  Gas / Liq Fuel 22.51 20.79 24.85 \n  Nuclear 73.65 80.74 81.74 \n*Provisional figure \nGeneration from stations up to 25 MW are not being monitored in CEA.  \n \n************  \n  \n\n \n \n \n \nANNEX-II \n \nANNEX REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n724 TO BE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 28.04.2016.     \n*********** \n \nSector Capacity \nas on \n31.03.16 \n(MW) \n2015-16 2014-15 2013-14 \nTarget \n(MU) \nGeneration\n* (MU) \n% \nAchieve- \nment \nTarget \n(MU) \nGeneration \n(MU) \n% \nAchieve- \nment \nTarget \n(MU) \nGeneration \n(MU) \n% \nAchieve- \nment \nCentral 14455.72 48925 56695.85 115.88 47828 50959.81 106.55 47331 51395.73 108.59 \nState 25191.70 66505 53123.60 79.88 65142 67544.68 103.69 63259 73456.40 116.12 \nPrivate 3154.00 12570 11521.71 91.66 11327 10739.16 94.81 11673 9995.41 85.63 \nImport \nfrom \nBhutan \n \n4800 5244.74 109.27 4800 5007.74 104.33 4800 5597.90 116.62 \n*  PROVISIONAL BASED ON ACTUAL-CUM-ASSESMENT \nGeneration from stations up to 25 MW are not being monitored in CEA. \n \n**************** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |725", "t": "Amendments to National Water Policy", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 725 \nANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nAMENDMENTS TO NATIONAL WATER POLICY  \n \n725.      SHRI MULLAPPALLY RAMACHANDRAN \n \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to amend the existing National Water Policy to meet the present \nrequirements of the nation, if so, the details thereof; and  \n(b) the steps being taken to maintain water resources and eliminate surface and groundwater resources \npollution along with improving water quality? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION  \n(PROF. SANWAR LAL JAT) \n \n(a) The National Water Policy, 2012 was adopted by the National Water Resources Council at its Meeting \nheld on 28.12.2012. The Policy has made several recommendations for conservation, development and \nimproved management of water resources  in the country. There is presently no proposal to review the \nNational Water Policy, 2012. \n(b) Several measures for conservation, development and improved management of water resources are \nundertaken by the respective State Governments which inter -alia, include conservation of water resources \nin reservoirs, traditional water bodies, rain water harvesting and artificial recharge of ground water. \nCentral Government provides technical and financial assistance to the State Governments in this regard \nthrough various schemes and programs. \n \nThe Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) along with the State Pollution Control Boards (SPCB) \nissues pollution norms for water bodies and also monitors the water quality through a network of stations \nall over India. Central Wa ter Commission (CWC) also monitors water quality parameters of rivers at \nselected locations. Similarly, Central Ground Water Board (CGWB) monitors the availability as well as \nquality of ground water. The Water (Prevention & Control of Pollution) Act 1974 a nd Water (Prevention \n& Control of Pollution) Cess Act, 1977 forms the basis of water pollution regulations in the country. \nNecessary action against the polluters of water is taken by the concerned authorities under the different \nlaws/rules framed by the Government in this regard.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |726", "t": "Shortage of Power in Karnataka", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.726 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nSHORTAGE OF POWER IN KARNATAKA \n \n726.  SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the State of Karnataka is  facing shortage of power, if so, the details  \nthereof along with the power required by that State; \n \n(b)  whether the Union Government is  taking or has taken any steps to meet the  \npower requirements of the State; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER,  \nCOAL AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY \n \n( SHRI  PIYUSH GOYAL ) \n \n(a) :   As reported by the state, the power deficit in Karnataka in terms of \nEnergy and Peak was 5.2% and 6.7% respectively during the year 2015 -16. The \ndetails are given at Annex. \n \n(b) & (c) :  In response to the request of Karnataka and keeping in view the power \nshortage faced by the Southern States, Central Government allocated 200 MW of \nUnallocated power from Southern Region unallocated power Pool on 4 th November, \n2015 to Karnataka to tide over the crisis. On the request of State of Karnataka CTU , \nPGCIL had granted LTA for 281 MW of transmission capacity enabling transfer of this \npower to Karnataka from Eastern Region. \n \n************** \n  \n\n \n \n \n \nANNEX \n \nANNEX REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.726 TO \nBE ANSWERED IN THE LOK SABHA ON 28.04.2016.     \n*********** \n \nPower Supply position of Karnataka during 2015-16 \nEnergy Peak \nRequirement Availability Deficit Peak Demand Peak Met Deficit \nMU MU MU % MW MW MW % \n64,491 61,160 3,331 5.2 10,196 9,508 688 6.7 \n \n \n************ "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |727", "t": "Passage to Global Shippers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.727\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28TH APRIL, 2016\nPASSAGE TO GLOBAL SHIPPERS\n727. SHRI M. RAJA MOHAN REDDY:\nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to relax the existing rules in order to allow\npassage to global shippers to sail into Indian coasts;\n(b) if so, the details and the objectives thereof; and\n(c) the extent to which the country is likely to benefit as a result thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING\n(SHRI PON RADHAKRISHNAN)\n(a)&(b):The Government has relaxed cabotage for (i) special vessels such as Roll -On\nRoll-Off (Ro-Ro), Roll-On Roll -Off (Hybrid Ro -Ro), RO-RO-cum- Passenger\n(Ro-Pax), Pure Car Carriers, Pure Car and Truck Carriers, LNG vessels and Over-\nDimensional Cargo or Project Cargo Carriers which are in short supply in the\ncountry for a period of five years w.e.f 2 nd September, 2015 and (ii) for all ports\nwhich transship at least 50% of the EXIM and empty container handled w.e.f  7th\nMarch, 2016. The cabotage has been relaxed with the objective to promote\nCoastal Shipping in India.\n(c) Cabotage relaxation of special vessels will enable global shipping lines to\ntransport automobiles from one Indian port to other Indian port. This will\ndecongest roads and railways and also reduce carbon emissions. Cabotage\nrelaxation for container transhipment ports will enable shipping lines to\nconsolidate Indian EXIM and empty containers at transhipment ports in India for\nonward transportation to destination ports by main shipping lines.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |728", "t": "Establishment of National Water Informatics Centre", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †728 \nANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \nESTABLISHMENT OF NATIONAL WATER INFORMATICS CENTRE  \n \n†728.      SHRIMATI SAKUNTALA LAGURI  \n      SHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN  \n \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is considering to establish a National Water Informatics Centre in \nthe country for undertaking works relating to water availability and its utilisation;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the efforts made by the Government in this direction so far and the time by which the centre \nis likely to be established; and  \n(d) the progress made in this regard so far ? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND \nGANGA REJUVENATION  \n \n(PROF. SANWAR LAL JAT) \n \n(a) &  (b) Yes, Madam.  The National Water Informatics Centre (NWIC) is envisaged as an \norganization for maintaining, updation, collation and dissemination of hydro - meteorological \ndata and information. \n \n(c) & (d) NWIC is envisaged as an independent organization under the overal l supervision of \nSecretary (Ministry of Water Resources, River Development & Ganga Rejuvenation) under \nNational Hydrology Project (NHP). The Union Cabinet has approved the National Hydrology \nProject (NHP) as a Central Sector Scheme on 06.04.2016 which, inter-alia, includes setting up of \nNWIC at a cost of Rs. 39.7674 crores. To head the Center, a post of the level of Joint Secretary \nto Government of India has also been approved by the Cabinet.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |729", "t": "Graduated Drivers' Licensing System (GDLS)", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 729 \nANSWERED ON 28TH APRIL, 2016 \nGRADUATED DRIVERS' LICENSING SYSTEM (GDLS) \n \n729.  SHRI RABINDRA KUMAR JENA: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n  \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes  to adopt the Graduated Drivers' Licensing  System (GDLS) \nfollowed by many countries around the world; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n (SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to (c)  No, Madam. Driving licence in the country is granted in two stages, i.e. learner’s licence and \ndriving licence. Chapter II of the Motor Vehicles Act (M.V. Act), 1988 and Chapter II of the Central \nMotor Vehicles Rules (CMVR), 1989 lay down detailed procedure for grant  of driving licence.  Section \n7 of the M. V. Act  provides  that for getting a learner’s licence to drive a transport vehicle, the person \nmust hold a driving licence to drive a light motor vehicle for at least one year. The validity of learners’ \nlicence is for a period of six months. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |73", "t": "Kaushal Vikas Kendras", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPAERTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 73\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016\nDELAY IN DISBURSAL OF WAGES UNDER MGNREGS\n*73. PROF. SAUGATA ROY:\nSHRI K. ASHOK KUMAR:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of total number of employment provided or man days generated through\nMahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS)\nduring the last three years and the current year, State/UT-wise;\n(b) the funds released as wages during the said period, State/UT-wise and year wise;\n(c) whether the Central Government has not released funds to clear the pending wage\nliability under MGNREGS to various States/UTs, especially the drought hit\nStates/UTs;\n(d) if so, the details of delay and the reasons therefor and the time by which the funds are\nlikely to be allocated and released; and\n(e) whether there is any proposal to modify the working hours under MGNREGS in the\nbackground of high temperature in almost all parts of the country, if so, the details\nthereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI BIRENDER SINGH)\n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n*****\n\nStatement in reply to Part s (a) to ( e) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 73 for\n28.04.2016\n(a): As per the Programme Management Information System (MIS), States/UTs -wise\ndetails of persondays generated during the last three years and the current year are given in\nthe Annexure-I.\n(b): Funds under MGNREGA are released to meet the cost of unskilled wages, cost of\nmaterial including the cost of semi -skilled and skilled wages and administrative cost. State\n/UT wise release position for the last 3 years is attached at Annexure-II.\n(c)&(d): During the current financial year 2016 -17, Ministry has released\nRs.1595566.28 lakh to the States /UTs (including drought affected States) for liquidating the\npending liability of wage component of FY 2015 -16 and for running the programme during\nFY 2016-17.  State/UT wise release position is attached at Annexure-III.\n(e): The Central Government vide its Gazette Notification dated 3.1.2014 has stipulated\neight hours of work including one hour of rest for all MGNREGA workers. However, to\nfacilitate greater participation of wage seekers, the working hours of any adult worker u nder\nMGNREGA have been made flexible spreading over twelve hours on any given day.\n\nAnnexure-I referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 73 da ted\n28.04.2016\nNo. State\nPersondays generated (in Lakhs)\n2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 till\n19/04/2016\n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 3273.35 2994.70 1555.87 1965.30\n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 43.50 36.56 19.05 50.14\n3 ASSAM 314.04 298.47 210.89 491.00\n4 BIHAR 941.85 862.35 352.73 720.09\n5 CHHATTISGARH 1194.34 1298.94 555.79 1012.87\n6 GUJARAT 281.90 230.30 181.52 225.45\n7 HARYANA 128.87 117.88 61.65 48.44\n8 HIMACHAL PRADESH 262.10 282.50 190.73 176.45\n9 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 365.56 338.12 120.90 312.15\n10 JHARKHAND 566.58 436.22 453.28 587.09\n11 KARNATAKA 617.81 718.86 433.70 599.49\n12 KERALA 837.74 866.03 588.72 741.67\n13 MADHYA PRADESH 1399.47 1229.48 1172.10 1237.56\n14 MAHARASHTRA 872.39 517.36 613.88 763.59\n15 MANIPUR 285.11 113.23 101.17 74.40\n16 MEGHALAYA 174.31 215.88 167.35 198.81\n17 MIZORAM 153.56 133.65 43.60 132.95\n18 NAGALAND 245.31 183.80 89.93 218.77\n19 ODISHA 546.01 711.82 534.84 893.23\n20 PUNJAB 65.50 134.68 64.60 144.08\n21 RAJASTHAN 2203.38 1838.56 1685.46 2340.60\n22 SIKKIM 36.31 44.03 24.13 43.83\n23 TAMIL NADU 4081.44 3677.23 2679.65 3632.86\n24 Telangana 0.00 1047.27 1238.28\n25 TRIPURA 518.51 521.61 511.76 538.76\n26 UTTAR PRADESH 1411.85 1753.60 1312.72 1832.07\n27 UTTARAKHAND 192.00 165.62 147.50 223.28\n28 WEST BENGAL 2018.42 2296.34 1697.08 2865.02\n29 ANDAMAN & NICOBAR 6.61 8.03 5.11 0.47\n30 DADRA & NAGAR HAVELI NR NR NR NR\n31 DAMAN & DIU NR NR NR NR\n32 GOA 0.69 1.15 1.72 1.07\n33 LAKSHADWEEP 0.49 0.14 0.13 0.03\n34 PUDUCHERRY 8.67 8.45 3.78 5.62\nTotal 23047.67 22035.58 16628.59 23315.42\nNR=Not Reported\n\nAnnexure-II referred to in reply to part (b) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 73 dated\n28.04.2016\n(Rs. In lakh)\nState wise release position of MGNREGA (2013-14 to 2015-16 )\nS.No States 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16\n1 Andhra Pradesh 475049.00 290314.10 307380.330\n2 Arunachal Pradesh 13852.67 2704.16 4395.100\n3 Assam 57349.95 50023.46 87830.060\n4 Bihar 158070.67 95968.24 102412.260\n5 Chhattisgarh 144602.31 150570.49 106341.300\n6 Gujarat 33530.02 35442.93 30598.720\n7 Haryana 37687.81 16715.29 12470.720\n8 Himachal Pradesh 47797.09 35542.86 39610.320\n9 Jammu and Kashmir 60315.73 52171.08 55801.830\n10 Jharkhand 62143.28 72433.41 97879.900\n11 Karnataka 159606.81 171687.07 99155.270\n12 Kerala 127710.93 158758.02 152633.877\n13 Madhya Pradesh 183982.44 245163.12 236732.200\n14 Maharashtra 115292.02 79951.77 123834.730\n15 Manipur 23100.00 21997.13 25532.290\n16 Meghalaya 27106.21 27785.90 22182.560\n17 Mizoram 24474.27 11141.23 28517.360\n18 Nagaland 29214.80 11305.27 26665.950\n19 Or"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |730", "t": "Raw Material for Textile Industry", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.730 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.04.2016 \n \n RAW MATERIAL FOR TEXTILE INDUSTRY \n \n730. SHRI K.N. RAMACHANDRAN: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES  वस्त्र मंरी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to provide financial  aid and raw material to handicraft \nunits/textile industry in order to make textile industry competitive at international level and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(b)  whether the Government proposes to establish textile centres in the country so as to genera te \nemployment opportunities in textile industry; \n(c)  if so, the details of concessions and incentives proposed to be given by the Government for this \npurpose, State-wise; and \n(d)  the other steps taken or proposed to be taken by the Government in this regard? \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज् य मंरी (स्त्वतंर प्रभार) (श्री संतोष कुमार गंगवार) \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF TEXTILES \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a) to (d):  In order to make textile industry globally competitive, Governm ent is implementing \nvarious policy initiatives and schemes to provide financial assistance and raw material to textile industry \nincluding handicraft sector. These include Technology Upgradation Fund Scheme (TUFS), Scheme for \nDevelopment of Technical Textiles, Schemes for the Development of the Powerloom Sector, Scheme for \nIntegrated Textile Parks (SITP), Integrated Skill Development Scheme (ISDS), Schemes for \nDevelopment of Silk and Sericulture sectors, Baba Sahib Ambedkar Hastshilp Vikas Yojna, Design and \nTechnology Upgradation Scheme, Marketing support and services scheme, Infrastructure and \nTechnology Development Scheme, etc. The Government of India has recently notified the Amended \nTechnology Upgradation Fund Scheme (A -TUFS) for promoting modernization a nd Technology \nupgradation in textile industry. \n \n For the Handloom sector, government of India is implementing Yarn Supply scheme throughout \nthe country to make available all types of yarn at Mill Gate Price to the eligible handloom weavers so as \nto facilit ate regular supply of basic raw materials to the handloom sector and help utilize the full \nemployment potential of the sector. In the Handicrafts Sector, Raw material depots are setup in the \ncountry under National Handicrafts Development Programme to make easy availability of quality and \ngraded raw material to artisans/ enterpreneurs/ units at a reasonable rates. For the powerloom sector, a \ncorpus fund for yarn Bank is provided under the Integrated Scheme for Powerloom Sector Development, \nto provide interest free corpus to set up Yarn Banks.  So far, 13 Yarn Banks have been sanctioned and an \namount of Rs. 6.872 crores has been released as Government Assistance.    \n \nThe textile sector schemes facilitate the industry and private entrepreneurs in the setting up  of \nnew textiles units including textiles parks as well as expansion of the existing units which have potential \nfor generating large employment opportunities in all states throughout the country.  So far, 74 textile \nparks have been sanctioned under the SIT P in all the States of the country with a total employment \npotential of 4.50 lakh persons and an investment potential of Rs. 30000 crores. Scheme -wise funds spent \nby the government during the last three years are at Annexure. \n \n\n \n \n\nAnnexure \n \n \n*** \nDetails of plans funds spent in respect of schemes relating to various segments of the \nTextile sector during each of the last three financial years (2013-14 to 2015-16). \n        Rs in Crores \nSr.No. Name of the Scheme / Sector 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \n1 2 4 5 6 \n1 Handloom 550.00 397.44 549.19 \n2 Handicraft 142.13 100.99 163.66 \n3 Wool & Woollens 16.66 20.00 27.51 \n4 Sericulture / Silk 374.55 297.58 178.10 \n5 Powerlooms 13.09 51.52 101.02 \n6 Megacluster 38.75 61.79 138.75 \n7 Industry schemes including \nTechnology Upgradation Fund \nScheme (TUFS),  Scheme for \nIntegrated Textiles Park (SITP), \nIntegrated Skill Development \nSchemes (ISDS) and North East \nRegion Textile Promotion \nScheme (NERTPS) \n2029.78 2384.39 1951.27 \n  GRAND TOTAL  3164.96 3313.71 3109.50 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|8|UNSTARRED |731", "t": "Under Passages and Crossings on NHs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  731 \nANSWERED ON 28TH APRIL, 2016 \n \nUNDER PASSAGES AND CROSSINGS ON NHS  \n \n \n731. SHRIMATI  V. SATHYA BAMA: \n            \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसडक़ परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has instructed the National Highway Authority of India (NHAI) to \nprovide adequate number of under passages and crossing facilities for people along National \nHighways; and \n \n(b)  if so, the details of the under passages and crossings approved and to be provided along the \nNational Highways across Tamil Nadu? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to (b) While taking up widening of National Highways to 4 -lane, 6-lane and 2 -lane with Paved \nShoulders under various Phases of NHDP, suitable structures, viz. Vehicular Underpasses, Pedestrian \nUnderpasses, Flyovers, Grade Separators, etc. are provided for safe crossing of National Highway by \nmotorists / pedestrians.  Details of such structures already constructed and under construction / to be \ntaken up in the National Highway Projects completed/ under implementation in the State of Tamil \nNadu as on 31.03.2016 are furnished in the enclosed Annexure.  \n  \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 731 FOR ANSWER ON 28 TH \nAPRIL, 2016 ASKED BY SHRIMATI V. SATHYA BAMA REGARDING UNDER PASSAGES AND CROSSINGS ON NHS. \nDetails of Crossing facilities construction / under construction / proposed in the National Highways under the jurisdiction o f NHAI in the State of Tamil \nNadu as on 31.03.2016 \nSl. \nNo \nNH \nNo \nName of the \nstretch  \nTotal \nLength \nin  km \nStatus of \nthe Project \n(Complete\nd / \nOngoing) \nUnderpasses, Flyovers, etc. for crossing of National Highway (Nos.) \nFlyovers / VOPs  (Nos.) Grade Separators (No.) VUP (No.) \nPUP (No.) \nFOB (No.) \nCattle Crossings  \n(No.) \nCompleted  \nUnder \nconstruct\nion / to be \ntaken up Completed \nUnder \nconstruction / \nto be taken \nup Completed \nUnder \nconstruct\nion / to be \ntaken up Completed \nUnder \nconstruc\ntion / to \nbe taken \nup \nCompleted  \nUnder \nconstruc\ntion / to \nbe taken \nup \nCompleted Under \nconstr\nuction \n/ to be \ntaken \nup \n1 47 \nChengapally - \nWalayar \n(TN/Kerala \nborder) section  \n54.865 Ongoing 3 0 0 0 17 0 39 0 0 0 18 0 \n2 210 \nTrichy - \nKaraikudi section \nof NH 210 \n106.96\n8 Ongoing 0   0   1   0   0   0   \n3 7 \nKrishnagiri - \nThumbipadi \n(Km.94/0 to \nkm.180/0) \n86.000 Completed 5 0 0 0 4 0 11 0 0 0 